/* Subscriber content. */
Object.assign(window.TPR_METH=window.TPR_METH||{},{"c-m-01":{"name":"Telling a mother her son has died","why":"Gives the news clearly and kindly, answers her honestly, explains the coroner and next steps, and looks after her and the team.","frame":["Preparation","What she knows","The news","Her questions","Next steps and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Prepare and sit down","say":["Mrs Walsh, I'm one of the doctors from Jamie's community team, and this is Grace, the nurse who looked after him today. Shall we sit down?","Is anyone with you, or on the way?"],"hear":"\"My sister's parking the car. What's happening? They said he's very unwell.\"","mark":"Private room, ED nurse present"},{"h":"Find out what she knows, then a warning","say":["What have you been told so far?","I'm afraid I have some very bad news."],"hear":"\"Just that he was very unwell. Is he going to be all right?\"","mark":"Warning shot given"},{"h":"Say it clearly, then stop","say":["Jamie was found unresponsive in his flat this morning. The paramedics and the A&E team did everything they could, but I'm so sorry: Jamie died at ten past twelve.","(Silence. Stay with her.)"],"hear":"\"No. No. He texted me last night. 'Night Mum, love you.'\"","mark":"'Died' said clearly; silence allowed"},{"h":"Answer her questions honestly","say":["There were empty medicine packets near him, so it looks like an overdose, but the coroner will establish exactly what happened.","You asked how we didn't see this coming. That's a fair question, and you deserve a proper answer. There will be a review, and our consultant will meet you.","There was a note. The police and coroner hold it for now, and you'll be told when you can see it."],"hear":"\"How did the team not see this coming? Can I have the note?\"","mark":"Questions answered without speculating or defending"},{"h":"Explain what happens next","say":["Because Jamie's death was sudden, the coroner is involved. There will probably be a post-mortem, which may delay the funeral by a few days.","A coroner's officer will contact you, and you can ask to see Jamie today.","Would you like us to help with telling Connor?"],"hear":"\"I want to see him. Who's going to tell Connor?\"","mark":"Coroner process explained; viewing arranged"},{"h":"Her own safety and support","say":["Where will you be tonight? Who's with you?","Sometimes people in shock feel they don't want to go on. Have you had thoughts like that?","Here are written details: our named contact, bereavement support and your GP. I'll arrange a debrief for the team."],"hear":"\"I'll be at my sister's. Sometimes I don't want to wake up, but I'd never do anything.\"","mark":"Passive thoughts, no plan; GP and named contact given"}],"table":{"cols":["Stage","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Preparation","Sister parking; told only 'very unwell'","Private room, ED nurse, facts ready"],["What she knows","Expects him to be ill, not dead","Warning shot before the news"],["The news","'Night Mum, love you'","Clear word 'died', then silence"],["Her questions","How was it missed? The note?","Honest, no speculation; named contact and review"],["Next steps and support","Wants to see Jamie; fleeting death wish","Coroner explained; GP, written support, team debrief"]]},"key":"Say 'died', not 'passed' or 'we lost him', and then stop talking. Practical information goes in only after she has had time to react.","objmap":[[3],[4],[5],[6],[6]]},"c-m-02":{"name":"Duty of candour: telling him about the double clozapine dose","why":"Tells Mr Hollis plainly what went wrong, apologises, explains the monitoring and keeps his trust in clozapine.","frame":["Warning and facts","Apology","What was done","Clozapine plan","Review and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Warning, then the facts","say":["Mr Hollis, this is Jo, the ward manager. We need to tell you about a mistake that happened last night.","Your 300 mg clozapine night dose was given at 8 o'clock and then given again at half past nine by mistake. That's why you became drowsy and your blood pressure dropped."],"hear":"\"What exactly did you give me?\"","mark":"Error stated plainly with dose and times"},{"h":"Apologise sincerely","say":["I'm very sorry. This should not have happened.","It wasn't done on purpose; it was an error in how the dose was recorded and checked."],"hear":"\"Were you trying to poison me?\"","mark":"Apology given; deliberate harm addressed"},{"h":"What was done and how he is","say":["You went to the medical hospital for monitoring. Your heart tracing and blood tests there were normal.","How do you feel now? Any dizziness, chest pain or trouble opening your bowels?"],"hear":"\"Groggy. Dizzy getting up at seven. Bowels two days ago.\"","mark":"Symptoms checked; ECG and bloods normal"},{"h":"Clozapine continues","say":["Your clozapine will continue. This morning's dose was held on advice. If your checks are fine, you'll have your usual 300 mg tonight.","We'll check your blood pressure lying down and then standing every four hours today, do a heart tracing and a clozapine level, and keep an eye on your bowels."],"hear":"\"I was scared you'd stop it. It's the only thing that's worked.\"","mark":"Clozapine reassured; monitoring explained"},{"h":"Review, who did it, and tonight","say":["There will be a review of what went wrong. I won't name anyone, but the aim is to stop it happening again, and tonight two nurses will check your dose.","We'll share what the review finds and write to you."],"hear":"\"Who did it? Will it happen again tonight?\"","mark":"No blame; safeguard for tonight"},{"h":"Who else, and his rights","say":["Would you like Carol to be told?","If you'd like to make a complaint, PALS can help, and that won't affect your care.","Can you tell me in your words what happened, so I know I've explained it clearly?"],"hear":"\"Yes, tell Carol. I want to see the letter.\"","mark":"Written follow-up agreed; understanding checked"}],"table":{"cols":["Candour element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Warning and facts","'What exactly did you give me?'","Plain dose and times, no vague phrases"],["Apology","Fear of deliberate poisoning","Sincere apology; not deliberate"],["What was done","Groggy, dizzy, bowels 2 days","ECG and bloods normal; monitor BP and bowels"],["Clozapine plan","Fears losing clozapine","Continue; tonight's dose if stable"],["Review and follow-up","Wants Carol told, the letter, considering complaint","Review findings shared; letter; PALS"]]},"key":"An apology is not an admission of liability. Say sorry clearly, give facts not speculation, and never name or blame a colleague.","objmap":[[1],[1,2],[3,4],[4],[6]]},"c-m-03":{"name":"A wife's complaint about nine hours in A&E","why":"Hears the whole complaint, apologises specifically, fixes what can be fixed today and explains the formal routes.","frame":["Listen","Summarise","Apologise","Fix today","Formal routes"],"steps":[{"h":"Privacy and consent","say":["Mrs Doyle, I'm one of the ward doctors. Let's go somewhere private.","Brian has said I can talk with you about his care. Please tell me everything."],"hear":"\"He waited nine hours on a corridor chair. Nobody told me anything.\"","mark":"Private room; consent confirmed"},{"h":"Listen without interrupting","say":["(Nod, wait until she finishes.)","How was the night for you, at home?"],"hear":"\"No food until three. I was sent home at midnight. A nurse said he was lucky, some wait two days. I thought he'd kill himself.\"","mark":"All concerns heard; carer distress identified"},{"h":"Summarise","say":["So: Brian waited nine hours for a bed, had no food for hours, you weren't told when he moved, and a comment hurt you.","Have I missed anything?"],"hear":"\"His glasses are still in A&E, and he missed his evening tablets.\"","mark":"Every concern summarised"},{"h":"Specific apology","say":["I'm sorry. Brian should not have waited that long, and you should have been told he'd moved.","That comment was unacceptable. I'll pass it on so it's looked at."],"hear":"\"Thank you. That's all I wanted to hear at first.\"","mark":"Specific apology without blaming colleagues"},{"h":"Fix what you can today","say":["I'll get his glasses brought up this afternoon. His tablets are back on track today, and he won't get double doses.","I'll make sure you're on his notes as the person to contact."],"hear":"\"Will complaining affect his care?\"","mark":"Practical fixes with times"},{"h":"Formal routes","say":["Complaining won't affect his care. PALS can help now; a formal complaint gets a response within set timescales, and the Ombudsman is there if you're not satisfied.","I'll record this and tell the ward manager. Would you like a carer's assessment?"],"hear":"\"Yes. I want it on record so it doesn't happen to anyone else.\"","mark":"Complaint recorded, escalated; carer's assessment offered"}],"table":{"cols":["Stage","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Listen","Nine hours, no food, sent home, rude remark","Let her finish before answering"],["Summarise","Glasses, missed tablets added","Shows she was heard"],["Apologise","Wanted acknowledgment","Specific, not blaming colleagues"],["Fix today","Glasses, tablets, contact details","Concrete fixes with times"],["Formal routes","Wants it on record","PALS, complaint process, Ombudsman, carer's assessment"]]},"key":"Fix what you can today before explaining processes. A leaflet offered before she feels heard escalates; concrete actions with times calm.","objmap":[[2],[4],[5],[6],[6]]},"c-m-04":{"name":"Working with an interpreter: low mood in an Arabic-speaking woman","why":"Uses the professional interpreter well, speaks directly to Mrs Khalil, assesses mood and risk, and checks understanding.","frame":["Interpreter briefing","Seeing her alone","Mood","Risk","Plan and teach-back"],"steps":[{"h":"Decline the son kindly","say":["Thank you for offering, but we always use a professional interpreter. It protects your son from hearing difficult things and lets your mother speak freely.","He's welcome to join at the end if she wishes."],"hear":"\"(Husband) Fine. If she agrees.\"","mark":"Son declined with reason"},{"h":"Brief the interpreter","say":["Ms Saleh, please interpret everything word for word in the first person. I'll speak directly to her.","We may talk about suicide. Tell me if there's a cultural meaning I should know. We'll debrief afterwards."],"hear":"\"(Interpreter) Understood.\"","mark":"Interpreter briefed"},{"h":"Speak directly; explore her distress","say":["Mrs Khalil, what's been troubling you?","When you say your heart feels squeezed, what does that mean for you?"],"hear":"\"My mother died in Syria and I couldn't go. My heart feels squeezed. I don't sleep.\"","mark":"Grief and somatic distress identified"},{"h":"Mood symptoms","say":["Do you enjoy anything, even time with your children?","How is your sleep and appetite?"],"hear":"\"Nothing. I wake at four. My clothes are loose.\"","mark":"Depressive episode: anhedonia, early waking, weight loss"},{"h":"Risk, directly","say":["Some people feeling like this wish they were dead. Has that happened to you?","Have you made plans? Do you feel safe at home?"],"hear":"\"I ask God to take me in my sleep. No plan. I'm safe at home.\"","mark":"Passive death wish; no plan; safe at home"},{"h":"Plan and teach-back","say":["You have depression, an illness, not madness. Talking support and a medicine called sertraline can help.","Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","Ms Saleh, how are you after that? I'll book you again for next time."],"hear":"\"Tablets for my sadness, see you in two weeks. Will people say I'm mad?\"","mark":"Teach-back done; interpreter debriefed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Interpreter briefing","Family wanted son to interpret","Professional interpreter; child not used"],["Seeing her alone","Agreed with a kind reason","Allows free disclosure"],["Mood","No enjoyment, early waking, 5 kg loss","Moderate depressive episode with grief"],["Risk","Asks God to take her; no plan","Passive wish; review in two weeks"],["Plan and teach-back","Fears being called mad","Explain illness simply; confirm understanding"]]},"key":"Speak to her, not to the interpreter: 'How are you?', not 'ask her how she is'. Third-person questions make her answers shorter.","objmap":[[2],[1],[2,3],[2,5],[6]]},"c-m-05":{"name":"Explaining to a 10-year-old why her mum is in hospital","why":"Gives Lily a simple, honest explanation, lifts her guilt and agrees contact and support.","frame":["What she thinks","Simple explanation","Not her fault","Her questions","Contact and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with her","say":["Hi Lily. Your mum talks about you a lot. What do you think has happened to Mum?","I see you're drawing. Is that Biscuit?"],"hear":"\"It was my fault. We had a fight about bedtime and I said I hated her.\"","mark":"Self-blame identified"},{"h":"Simple, honest explanation","say":["Mum has an illness in her brain called mania. It made her thoughts go too fast and made her think the TV was talking to her.","The doctors and nurses are giving her medicine to help her sleep and slow her thoughts down."],"hear":"\"Is she going to die?\"","mark":"Illness explained in simple words"},{"h":"Not her fault","say":["Nothing you said or did made Mum ill. Not the fight, not anything.","Mum asked about you and wants you to know that too."],"hear":"(Cries.) \"Really?\"","mark":"Guilt lifted"},{"h":"Her questions","say":["No, Mum is not going to die. She's safe here.","The police helped get her to hospital safely. She's not in trouble.","You can't catch it, like a cold."],"hear":"\"When is she coming home?\"","mark":"Fears answered honestly"},{"h":"Honest about uncertainty","say":["We don't know yet when Mum will come home. It might be a few weeks.","We'll tell you and Dad as soon as we know more."],"hear":"\"I want to see her but I'm scared she'll be weird.\"","mark":"No false date given"},{"h":"Contact and support","say":["You could phone Mum tonight, then visit in a room like this when she's calmer.","Would you like Mrs Shah at school to know, just a little?","Mr Bennett, how are you coping? I'd like to put you in touch with Young Carers for Lily."],"hear":"\"(Dad) I'm exhausted. That would help.\"","mark":"Call and visit agreed; Young Carers referral"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What she thinks","Thinks the fight caused it","Correct guilt directly"],["Simple explanation","Saw fast talk and TV beliefs","Name it simply; link to what she saw"],["Not her fault","Cried when told","Repeat; Mum asked about her"],["Her questions","Die? Prison? Catch it?","Honest, short answers"],["Contact and support","Scared of visit; helping since summer","Phone call, planned visit, Young Carers, school"]]},"key":"Children fill gaps with blame. Say clearly, more than once, that it is not her fault, and never promise a return date you might break.","objmap":[[2],[1],[4],[6],[6]]},"c-m-06":{"name":"Faith and psychosis: finding a middle way with the family","why":"Respects the family's faith, keeps Daniel on treatment, and makes the deliverance service physically safe.","frame":["Daniel's view","Family beliefs","The service","Side effects","Shared plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Speak to Daniel first","say":["Daniel, it's your treatment, so I'd like to hear from you first. How are things?","How do you feel about the tablets?"],"hear":"\"(Daniel) Better. No voices for a month. But I can't sit still in the evenings.\"","mark":"Improving; restlessness reported"},{"h":"Explore beliefs with curiosity","say":["Mrs Mensah, please tell me how you and the family understand what happened to Daniel.","Your faith is important here. How can we work with it?"],"hear":"\"We believe it's a spiritual attack. Do you think we're backward?\"","mark":"Spiritual explanation respected"},{"h":"The service and its risks","say":["What will happen at the service?","Three days with no food or water is dangerous for anyone. Could the pastor allow water for Daniel?"],"hear":"\"Prayer and anointing. The pastor may allow water for those who are unwell.\"","mark":"Fasting without water identified as a risk"},{"h":"Common ground","say":["We both want Daniel well. Many families pray and take medicine together, like your mother with her blood pressure tablets.","Stopping the tablets now carries a high risk of the voices returning."],"hear":"\"If God heals him, why does he need tablets?\"","mark":"Shared goal found"},{"h":"Side effects","say":["The restlessness is a side effect called akathisia. I can reduce the dose to 10 mg, which often helps.","The tablet can be taken without food, so fasting from food is fine as long as he drinks and takes it daily."],"hear":"\"(Daniel) That'd help.\"","mark":"Akathisia addressed with dose reduction"},{"h":"Plan","say":["Can we agree: Daniel attends the service, keeps his tablet and drinks water.","I can link you with our chaplaincy team, who work with all faiths.","Here are warning signs, the crisis number, and a review in two weeks."],"hear":"\"Yes. I'd like to meet the chaplain.\"","mark":"Faith and medicine plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Daniel's view","Wants tablets; pacing in evenings","Adult autonomy; akathisia"],["Family beliefs","Spiritual attack; feels judged","Respect, don't label as delusion"],["The service","Three days no food or water","Dehydration risk; ask for water"],["Side effects","Restlessness","Reduce aripiprazole to 10 mg"],["Shared plan","Mother took BP tablets and prayed","Faith and medicine together; chaplaincy; review"]]},"key":"Never call the belief a delusion. A family that can say 'prayer and tablets' is likelier to keep him well than one forced to choose.","objmap":[[1,4],[2],[3,5],[6],[6]]},"c-m-07":{"name":"Speaking up about an unsafe discharge","why":"Raises the new risk information with the consultant clearly and early, agrees a safer plan and records it.","frame":["Facts","Probe","Alert","Challenge","Agreement and escalation"],"steps":[{"h":"Check facts and find a private moment","say":["Dr Fraser, have you got a few minutes privately about Mr Hughes?","Since the ward round, I've learned something new that worries me."],"hear":"\"Go on, but I've beds to free.\"","mark":"Private conversation secured"},{"h":"Probe","say":["Can I check something first?","Did you get a chance to ask about his plans for the weekend?"],"hear":"\"Not in detail. He looked brighter.\"","mark":"Gap in assessment identified"},{"h":"Alert with the facts","say":["The healthcare assistant told me he gave his dog to his brother yesterday and said, 'I've sorted everything now.'","With a planned overdose last week, that worries me a lot."],"hear":"\"Are you saying I've got it wrong?\"","mark":"New risk information shared"},{"h":"Challenge, respectfully","say":["I know you're under bed pressure, and that's real. I'm concerned this discharge isn't safe today.","Could we review him together at two o'clock before he goes?"],"hear":"\"We've three people waiting. What do you suggest?\"","mark":"Specific request made"},{"h":"Interim safety","say":["Until then, I'll ask Ade to increase his observations and keep him on the ward.","We could also ask the home treatment team to see him if discharge goes ahead."],"hear":"\"Fine. Two o'clock.\"","mark":"Joint review agreed; interim plan in place"},{"h":"Document and know the next step","say":["I'll write down the new information and our plan in his notes.","If we still disagree at two, could we ask the clinical director for a second view?"],"hear":"\"What if at two I still think he's fit to go?\"","mark":"Concern documented; escalation route known"}],"table":{"cols":["Graded step","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Facts","Dog given away, 'sorted everything'","Possible final acts; high suicide risk"],["Probe","Weekend plans not asked","Assessment gap"],["Alert","Defensive at first","State facts plainly, without blame"],["Challenge","Bed pressure","Acknowledge pressure, make a specific request"],["Agreement and escalation","Agreed 2 p.m. review","Document; clinical director if still unsafe"]]},"key":"Hints get brushed off. State the specific facts, name your concern, and ask for a specific action and time.","objmap":[[3,6],[1,4],[2,4],[5],[6]]},"c-m-08":{"name":"Saying no to diazepam safely","why":"Declines diazepam clearly and kindly, explores dependence and risk, and offers real alternatives.","frame":["His distress","Clear no","Dependence and risk","Alternatives","Complaint"],"steps":[{"h":"Validate and stay calm","say":["Mr Barker, I can see you're struggling, and I want to help.","Would you sit down so we can talk this through?"],"hear":"\"Only diazepam works. I want a month's supply.\"","mark":"Distress acknowledged"},{"h":"One clear, kind no","say":["I'm not going to prescribe diazepam. It stops working and becomes harder to stop over time.","That's not because I don't believe you're suffering."],"hear":"\"Why should I suffer when there's a tablet that works?\"","mark":"Clear refusal with a reason"},{"h":"Dependence and risk","say":["How many blues are you taking, and what happens if you miss a day?","How much do you drink? Any thoughts of not wanting to live?"],"hear":"\"Three or four a day for eight weeks. Shaky if I miss. Four cans a night. Sometimes 'what's the point', but my child keeps me going.\"","mark":"Dependence, alcohol and passive thoughts identified"},{"h":"Real alternatives","say":["Sertraline helps anxiety, even without depression. Start at 25 mg for a week, then 50 mg.","I'll refer you for CBT, and to the drug service, who can safely reduce the blues if needed.","Mixing blues and alcohol is risky. Please don't combine them."],"hear":"\"Am I an addict now?\"","mark":"Sertraline, CBT and drug service referral"},{"h":"Complaint","say":["You're entitled to complain, and I'll give you the PALS details.","Whatever you decide, it won't affect your care."],"hear":"\"I'll go to the papers.\"","mark":"Complaint threat met calmly"},{"h":"Close","say":["What matters most to you?","Let's get you to the point where you can take your child to school. I'll see you in two weeks."],"hear":"\"Taking my kid to school. I'll try it.\"","mark":"Review agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["His distress","Redundancy, panic, can't leave house","Severe GAD"],["Clear no","Angry, threatens complaint","Calm repetition, no argument"],["Dependence and risk","3–4 blues daily, shaky without, 4 cans","Dependence and overdose risk"],["Alternatives","Citalopram nausea before","Sertraline, CBT, drug service"],["Complaint","Threatens papers","PALS offered; care unaffected"]]},"key":"Say no once, kindly, with a reason, then stop arguing. Repeated justification invites negotiation.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[1,2],[5]]},"c-m-09":{"name":"His tribunal appeal has failed: the news, his rights and risk","why":"Tells him the decision honestly, responds to feelings first, explains his options and manages risk over the weekend.","frame":["The news","His feelings","His rights","Risk","Weekend plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Warning and news","say":["Mr Ellis, the tribunal decision has arrived. I'm sorry, it's not the result you wanted.","They decided you stay on Section 3."],"hear":"(Stands, shouts.) \"My solicitor said I had a good chance!\"","mark":"News given clearly"},{"h":"Feelings first","say":["I can see you're angry and disappointed. That's understandable after waiting since Wednesday.","Would you sit down so we can talk?"],"hear":"\"You told them what to say.\"","mark":"Anger acknowledged"},{"h":"His rights","say":["You can ask the hospital managers for a hearing at any time.","You can apply to the tribunal again in the next six-month period. Your mother, as nearest relative, can also ask for discharge.","An advocate, an IMHA, can help, and your solicitor will call on Monday."],"hear":"\"When can I appeal again? Is this going on forever?\"","mark":"Rights explained"},{"h":"Risk, directly","say":["Yesterday you said there'd be no point if you lost. Have you thought of ending your life?","Have you thought about leaving the ward?"],"hear":"\"I thought about tying something round my neck last night. Didn't do anything. I've thought about walking off on leave.\"","mark":"Ligature thought and absconding risk identified"},{"h":"Protective factors","say":["What's kept you going?","Any thoughts of hurting anyone?"],"hear":"\"My mum and my cat. No.\"","mark":"No current plan; no violence risk"},{"h":"Weekend plan","say":["Sam will spend time with you this evening, and we'll increase observations.","Your leave will be reviewed Monday. Can you call your mother tonight?","We'll look at your olanzapine weight gain too."],"hear":"\"OK. I'll ring Mum.\"","mark":"Observations increased; plan documented"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The news","Expected success","Clear, early, with warning shot"],["His feelings","Anger, blame","Acknowledge before facts"],["His rights","Wants to appeal","Managers, tribunal, nearest relative, IMHA"],["Risk","Ligature thought; absconding thought","Raised risk after news"],["Weekend plan","Mother and cat protect","Observations, nurse time, leave review"]]},"key":"Bad news raises immediate suicide and absconding risk. Ask about both directly before you leave him, and set a concrete weekend plan.","objmap":[[1,2],[2],[3],[4],[6]]},"c-m-10":{"name":"Urgent SBAR referral: possible lithium toxicity at home","why":"Gets a reluctant GP to visit and take bloods today with clear roles and red flags.","frame":["Situation","Background","Assessment","Recommendation","Roles and red flags"],"steps":[{"h":"Open with the concern","say":["Dr Moss, I'm a psychiatry trainee at the CMHT, calling about Mrs Margaret Doyle.","I'm worried she may have lithium toxicity, and I need your help today."],"hear":"\"Can't your team deal with it? We've no visits left.\"","mark":"Concern stated in one sentence"},{"h":"Background and assessment","say":["She takes Priadel 600 mg at night; her level was 0.7 three months ago.","For three days she's had vomiting, diarrhoea, a coarse tremor and unsteadiness, and she's been taking ibuprofen.","She's alert but refuses A&E, and her CPN thinks she has capacity for that."],"hear":"\"Why can't your team take the bloods?\"","mark":"SBAR with key facts"},{"h":"Specific request","say":["Could someone visit by 4 p.m. today to take a lithium level, U&E and calcium?","I know you're stretched. Is there a paramedic practitioner who could go?"],"hear":"\"Our paramedic could go, or I could after surgery.\"","mark":"Specific timed request accepted"},{"h":"What we'll do","say":["We'll ask her to stop lithium tonight and stop ibuprofen; paracetamol instead.","Our CPN will stay in touch and encourage fluids."],"hear":"\"Who's checking the result if it comes back late?\"","mark":"Roles agreed"},{"h":"Results and red flags","say":["Results go to the duty GP until 6:30, then out of hours. Could you flag them urgent?","If her level is 1.5 or more, her kidneys worsen or she becomes confused, she needs admission."],"hear":"\"Fine. Put it in writing.\"","mark":"Red flags agreed"},{"h":"Read back and document","say":["So: your team visits by 4, bloods urgent, lithium and ibuprofen stopped, results to duty GP, red flags agreed.","I'll email this to your urgent inbox now and record it in her notes."],"hear":"\"Thanks. That's clear.\"","mark":"Plan read back and documented"}],"table":{"cols":["SBAR","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Situation","GP reluctant, busy","Open with clear concern"],["Background","Lithium 600 mg, level 0.7; ibuprofen","Interaction and dehydration raise level"],["Assessment","D&V, coarse tremor, ataxia","Probable toxicity"],["Recommendation","Paramedic practitioner available","Visit and bloods by 4 p.m."],["Roles and red flags","Asked who checks results","Duty GP, red flags, plan in writing"]]},"key":"A vague request gets refused. One specific request with a time, plus your own offer, wins agreement.","objmap":[[1],[3],[4],[6],[6]]},"c-s-02":{"name":"What the mother sees: the depressive picture, the eating question and the risk","why":"Builds an accurate picture of a patient you have not met yet, with enough detail to judge severity and urgency.","frame":["Onset and course","Core and biological symptoms","Depression or eating disorder?","Negative thoughts, psychosis and mania","Risk and family history","Functioning and background"],"steps":[{"h":"Welcome, consent and confidentiality","say":["Thank you for coming. I'm glad you did.","Ella has told us we can talk to you, so I can listen to everything and share general information.","Anything she tells us privately, I'd check with her first. Is that OK?"],"hear":"\"Yes, of course. I just want her to get help.\"","mark":"Consent confirmed"},{"h":"Onset and course","say":["Tell me what's been happening, in your words.","When did you first notice a change, and what was going on then?","Has she ever been like this before?"],"hear":"\"About ten months ago, after Josh ended it. I thought she'd get over it, but she's got worse.\"","mark":"Worsening ten-month course identified"},{"h":"Core and biological symptoms","say":["How is her mood most days? Does she enjoy anything now?","How is she sleeping, and when does she wake?","How is her appetite? Has she lost weight? Does she seem slowed down?"],"hear":"\"Low all day. Dropped netball. Wakes at five. About six kilos gone. Yes, she talks really slowly.\"","mark":"Depressive syndrome with biological features identified"},{"h":"Depression or eating disorder?","say":["What does she eat in a typical day?","Does she talk about her weight or shape, or seem to want to be thinner?","Any sign of making herself sick, laxatives, hard exercise or secret eating?"],"hear":"\"Toast, then half a dinner. She doesn't care about her weight. She's just not hungry.\"","mark":"Primary eating disorder unlikely"},{"h":"Thinking, psychosis, mania and substances","say":["How is her concentration? What does she say about herself and the future?","Has she said anything that seems strange, or heard things others can't?","Any spells of being unusually high? Alcohol or drugs?"],"hear":"\"She can't read a page. Says it's her fault, she's useless, there's no point. Nothing odd. Hardly drinks now.\"","mark":"Negative thoughts identified; no psychotic or manic features"},{"h":"Risk and family history","say":["Has she said she wishes she wasn't here, or talked about ending her life? Any self-harm?","What medicines are in the house? Has she ever been angry towards Josh or anyone else?","Has anyone in the family had depression, or died by suicide?"],"hear":"\"Last week she said she wished she wouldn't wake up. Her dad took his own life when she was fourteen.\"","mark":"Family history of suicide identified"},{"h":"Functioning and background","say":["Has the GP done any tests or offered treatment?","How is she managing day to day: washing, getting out, work or study?"],"hear":"\"Bloods were normal. She showers once a week if I push, lost her job, hasn't been out in two weeks.\"","mark":"Self-neglect identified"},{"h":"Summarise, plan and support the mother","say":["This sounds like a fairly severe depression, not an eating disorder. Because of the risk, we'll ring her today and visit within two days.","Please lock away all medicines tonight. If she talks about ending her life, ring the crisis line, NHS 111 option 2 or 999.","Tell me back what you'll do tonight. And you're entitled to support yourself: a carer's assessment."],"hear":"\"Lock the tablets away, ring if she says anything. Thank you for listening.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Onset and course","Started after a break-up 10 months ago, steadily worse","A depressive episode, not ordinary grief over a relationship"],["Core and biological symptoms","Low all day, no interest, in bed till 2, wakes at 5, 6 kg lost, slowed","Severe depression with biological features"],["Depression or eating disorder?","Not hungry, food tastes of nothing; no concern about weight; no purging","Appetite loss from depression; primary eating disorder unlikely"],["Negative thoughts, psychosis, mania","Guilt, worthlessness, hopelessness; nothing odd; no highs","Depressive thinking without psychotic or manic features"],["Risk and family history","Wishes she wouldn't wake; paracetamol at home; father's suicide","Raised suicide risk: assess directly within days"],["Functioning","Weekly shower, lost job, housebound two weeks","Self-neglect and marked loss of function"]]},"key":"A good collateral history is a risk assessment by proxy. Ask the eating questions that rule out anorexia, ask about slowing, and always ask about family suicide: each one changes how quickly you act.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,5],[4,5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-s-03":{"name":"Is it akathisia? Timeline, inside, outside, look-alikes, risk","why":"Turns 'non-adherent' into a treated side effect and a patient who chooses to stay on treatment.","frame":["Timeline of the medicine","Inner restlessness","Visible restlessness","Look-alikes ruled out","Risk","Options: lower dose, switch, propranolol"],"steps":[{"h":"Let her move; hear her out","say":["Please don't feel you have to sit still; walk about if it helps.","The nurses said the new tablet is bothering you. What is it doing to you?","I'm not here to push you into anything. I want to understand."],"hear":"\"Nobody listens. This tablet is driving me mad. I can't keep still.\""},{"h":"The medicine timeline","say":["When did this start, and what changed with your tablets around then?","What dose are you on, and when did you last take it?","Any other medicines, new or old?"],"hear":"\"Haloperidol three weeks ago, upped to 10 last week. This started a few days later. Nothing else.\"","mark":"Dose increase identified as likely cause"},{"h":"Inside and outside","say":["What does it feel like inside? Where do you feel it most?","When is it worst?","Could you stand still for a minute while I watch, and tell me how it feels?"],"hear":"\"Like ants in my legs and a horrible tension. Worst in the meds queue. I can't stand still.\"","mark":"Akathisia identified"},{"h":"Rule out the look-alikes","say":["Any stiffness, shaking, or muscle spasms?","Is it worse at night lying down, with an urge that eases when you walk?","Are you worried about things, or is it in your body? How are the voices? Any caffeine or drugs?"],"hear":"\"No stiffness. It's all day. It's in my body. Voices have gone. Two teas a day.\"","mark":"Parkinsonism, dystonia, restless legs, anxiety, relapse and substances excluded"},{"h":"Ask about suicidal thoughts","say":["When something feels this unbearable, people sometimes think about not wanting to live. Has that crossed your mind?","Any plans, or thoughts of acting on it?","Are those thoughts coming from the restlessness, or from feeling low?"],"hear":"\"If this never stopped, I'd rather be dead. No plans. It's the restlessness.\"","mark":"Akathisia-linked suicidal thoughts identified"},{"h":"Name it and explain it","say":["I think this is akathisia, a known side effect of haloperidol, especially after a dose goes up.","It's real and physical. It's not you relapsing, and it's not in your head.","It usually settles within days of the right change."],"hear":"\"So I'm not going mad?\""},{"h":"Options and a shared choice","say":["We can lower the dose, switch to another medicine, or add propranolol, and we can combine them.","Do you have asthma or any heart problems?","One plan: haloperidol down to 5, propranolol 10 three times a day, pulse checks, and a switch if it's not better in a few days. How does that sound?"],"hear":"\"I don't want the voices back. I'll take the 5 if I get the propranolol.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"},{"h":"Check back, safety-net, involve the team","say":["Tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear.","If it gets worse, you feel faint, or the thoughts get stronger, tell a nurse straight away.","I'll talk to your consultant this afternoon and see you on Thursday."],"hear":"\"Akathisia, lower dose, propranolol, and a switch if it doesn't work. Thank you for believing me.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Timeline","Haloperidol started 3 weeks ago, raised to 10 mg; restless days later","Classic onset after a dose increase of a high-potency antipsychotic"],["Inner restlessness","Ants in the legs; indescribable tension","The subjective part of akathisia"],["Visible restlessness","Rocks foot to foot; cannot stand in a queue","The objective part; score it on the BARS"],["Look-alikes","No stiffness or spasms; daytime; no worry content; voices gone","Not parkinsonism, dystonia, restless legs, anxiety or relapse"],["Risk","'I'd rather be dead if it never stopped'; no plan","Akathisia-linked suicidal thoughts: tell the nurse in charge, review observations"]]},"key":"Refusal is the symptom, not the problem. Treat the akathisia, and adherence follows; push the tablet, and you lose her trust and miss the suicide risk.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-s-04":{"name":"Explaining OCD: the cycle, the score, and two treatments that break it","why":"Gives him a clear picture of his illness and a treatment choice he understands and owns.","frame":["Obsession","Anxiety","Compulsion and short relief","ERP","SSRI","Combined treatment"],"steps":[{"h":"Start where he is","say":["Thanks for coming back. What do you already know about OCD?","What would you like to get out of today?","I'll explain the condition, then the treatments, and you can stop me any time."],"hear":"\"I've read a lot online. I just want to know if it ever gets better.\""},{"h":"His ideas about the cause","say":["What do you think caused it?","Lots of people worry it's their personality. OCD isn't who you are; it's a condition.","Anxiety as a child can make it more likely, but it isn't your fault."],"hear":"\"I was an anxious kid. I thought this was just me.\"","mark":"Belief that OCD is fixed identified"},{"h":"Draw the cycle","say":["Almost everyone gets unwanted thoughts. In OCD a thought like 'I'm carrying germs' sticks and causes huge anxiety.","Washing brings quick relief, so your brain learns washing kept you safe.","So the thought comes back stronger and the washing grows. That's the loop we break."],"hear":"\"That's exactly it. The relief never lasts.\"","mark":"Cycle understood"},{"h":"The score and the outlook","say":["Your score was 26 out of 40, which is in the severe range.","That sounds frightening, but it tells us which treatment works best, and most people improve a lot."],"hear":"\"So it's bad, but fixable?\""},{"h":"CBT with ERP","say":["The main therapy is CBT with exposure and response prevention.","With a therapist, you build a ladder of feared situations and face them step by step without washing. The anxiety rises, then falls on its own.","Weekly sessions of about an hour, more than ten hours in total, with practice at home. It's about now, not your childhood."],"hear":"\"Good, because my childhood was fine.\"","mark":"Worry about therapy addressed"},{"h":"The SSRI","say":["The medicine is an SSRI, for example sertraline. It treats OCD directly, depressed or not.","It takes a few weeks to start and up to twelve to work fully; early nausea or jitteriness usually passes.","It isn't addictive, but we never stop it suddenly; we reduce slowly."],"hear":"\"I heard antidepressants are addictive.\"","mark":"Worry about addiction identified"},{"h":"Why both, and why not antibiotics","say":["With severe OCD, combining the two gives the best results.","Washing 'properly' once, or antibiotics 'just in case', is the OCD asking for certainty. Giving in feeds the loop.","Treatment teaches your brain to live with the uncertainty."],"hear":"\"So the antibiotics would make it worse in the long run.\"","mark":"Reassurance-seeking ideas identified"},{"h":"Check back and agree next steps","say":["Can you tell me in your words how the cycle works and what the treatments do?","What feels right for you?","I'll refer you for ERP today, give you a leaflet on sertraline, and with your agreement write to your medical officer."],"hear":"\"Both, I think. I'll read the leaflet first.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Part of the cycle","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Obsession","'I'm carrying germs that will make my baby ill'","An intrusive, unwanted thought he knows is excessive"],["Anxiety","Overwhelming dread until he washes","The driver of every compulsion"],["Compulsion and short relief","Washing hands and uniform for hours; relief fades fast","Relief rewards the ritual and keeps the loop going"],["ERP","Worried therapy means talking about childhood","Practical, step-by-step facing of fears without washing"],["SSRI and combined treatment","Fears antidepressants are addictive; Y-BOCS 26","Not addictive; severe OCD does best with both treatments"]]},"key":"Explain the cycle first and the treatments make sense on their own: ERP breaks the link between anxiety and washing; the SSRI turns the anxiety down. Every 'just in case' feeds the loop.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-s-05":{"name":"Is it panic disorder? One attack in slow motion, then everything it isn't","why":"Reaches a confident diagnosis in one sitting and shows him he has been taken seriously.","frame":["The attack itself","Out of the blue, or triggered?","Worry between attacks and agoraphobia","Other anxiety disorders","Mood and psychosis","Physical causes and substances"],"steps":[{"h":"Take him seriously from the start","say":["Thanks for coming. These attacks sound really frightening.","You said you didn't want to be fobbed off. I'll take this seriously and go through it in detail.","Tell me about them in your words."],"hear":"\"Out of nowhere I'm terrified. I thought it was my heart.\""},{"h":"One attack in slow motion","say":["Take me through the last one, from the very first thing you noticed.","How fast does it peak, how long does it last, and how often?","What else happens in your body?"],"hear":"\"Dry mouth, then pounding heart, can't breathe, dizzy, tingling lips. Peaks in minutes, lasts up to half an hour, about weekly.\"","mark":"Panic attack identified"},{"h":"The thoughts at the peak","say":["At the worst point, what do you think is happening?","Do you ever feel unreal, or as if you're watching yourself?","What do you feel the urge to do?"],"hear":"\"I think I'm dying. Sometimes the world isn't real and I think I'm going mad. I have to get out.\"","mark":"Fear of dying and going mad identified"},{"h":"Out of the blue? Then between attacks","say":["Where are you when they happen? Is there a pattern?","Between attacks, do you worry about the next one? What have you stopped doing?","Do you avoid crowds, queues, public transport, or going out alone?"],"hear":"\"At my desk, on the sofa, once in my sleep. I worry all the time about the next. I stopped the gym and turned down a promotion. Crowds are fine.\"","mark":"Panic disorder without agoraphobia identified"},{"h":"Other anxiety disorders","say":["Are you a worrier about everyday things in general?","How are you meeting people or speaking in front of others, apart from the attacks?","Any thoughts you have to neutralise, or rituals? Anything traumatic in the past?"],"hear":"\"Not a worrier. I'm fine socially. No rituals. Nothing traumatic.\"","mark":"GAD, social anxiety, OCD and PTSD excluded"},{"h":"Mood and psychosis","say":["How's your mood been? Are you still enjoying things? Sleep?","Have you ever had thoughts about ending your life?","Outside the attacks, have you heard voices or felt something strange was going on?"],"hear":"\"Mood's good. I enjoy life. Never suicidal. No voices.\"","mark":"Depression and psychosis excluded"},{"h":"Physical causes and substances","say":["What tests did your GP do?","Any medicines, inhalers or supplements?","How much coffee, energy drinks, alcohol, or other drugs?"],"hear":"\"ECG, heart monitor, bloods all normal. No medicines. Four coffees and an energy drink a day. A few beers at weekends. No drugs.\"","mark":"Physical and substance causes excluded"},{"h":"Name it and point the way","say":["This is panic disorder: your body's alarm system goes off when there's no danger.","Overbreathing causes the tingling and dizziness; the fear of a heart attack pushes the alarm higher. Awful, but not dangerous.","Cut the caffeine gradually. CBT for panic works very well; I'll refer you. Can you tell me back what's going on?"],"hear":"\"So it's an alarm going off, not my heart. And there's a therapy for it.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The attack","Sudden terror, pounding heart, breathless, tingling, peaks in minutes, up to 30 min","Classic panic attacks"],["Out of the blue?","At his desk, on the sofa, from sleep","Unexpected attacks, as panic disorder requires"],["Between attacks","Worries about the next; stopped gym; turned down promotion; crowds fine","Panic disorder without agoraphobia"],["Other disorders","Not a worrier; fine socially; no rituals or trauma; mood good","GAD, social anxiety, OCD, PTSD and depression excluded"],["Physical and substances","Normal ECG, tape and bloods; four coffees and an energy drink a day","No physical cause; caffeine is aggravating it"]]},"key":"Panic attacks happen in many disorders. Panic disorder means attacks that come out of the blue, plus fear of the next one. Always ask where they happen and what he now avoids.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-s-06":{"name":"Lithium as an add-on: why, tests, levels, safety, her life","why":"Moves from 'why me' to safe daily use, so she can make an informed choice and spot toxicity early.","frame":["Why lithium, why now","Tests before starting","Dose and levels","Side effects and toxicity","Her kidneys and a future baby"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and find out what she has heard","say":["Hi Rebecca, I'm Dr Okoro, one of the ward doctors. I'm sorry you missed the end of the ward round. How are you feeling now?","The team suggested adding lithium. What have you heard about it?"],"hear":"\"Only that it's for manic depression. Do they think I'm bipolar?\"","mark":"Worry about diagnosis identified"},{"h":"Why lithium, and why her","say":["Your diagnosis hasn't changed. Lithium is also used to strengthen an antidepressant when depression keeps returning or only partly lifts.","You've now had three episodes in three years, while amitriptyline has helped a little. Adding lithium often gives it the push it needs.","You keep taking amitriptyline. Any benefit usually shows within a few weeks."],"hear":"\"So it's like a booster? Okay, that makes more sense.\"","mark":"Rationale understood"},{"h":"Tests before starting","say":["Before the first dose we do a blood test to check your kidneys, thyroid and calcium, a blood count, your weight and a heart tracing.","We also do a pregnancy test and check every tablet you take, including anything from the chemist.","Do you take anything else, like painkillers?"],"hear":"\"Just ibuprofen for period pains, most months.\"","mark":"NSAID use identified"},{"h":"Dose and level monitoring","say":["We'd start with a low dose, one tablet at night, and check the level after a week.","The blood test is taken 12 hours after the dose, so mid-morning if you take it at ten at night.","We check weekly after any change until it's steady, and three-monthly after that, and kidneys and thyroid every six months."],"hear":"\"That's a lot of needles, but I get it.\""},{"h":"Side effects: early, long-term and her father","say":["Early on some people feel queasy, get loose stools, a metallic taste, thirst, passing more urine or a fine hand shake. These usually ease.","Over years it can slow the thyroid, cause some weight gain and, in some people, reduce kidney function. That's why we keep checking.","You look worried. What's on your mind?"],"hear":"\"My dad died of kidney failure. He had diabetes for years.\"","mark":"Kidney worry identified"},{"h":"Toxicity, interactions and stopping","say":["If the level gets too high you may get sickness, diarrhoea, a coarse shake, unsteadiness, drowsiness or slurred speech. Stop it and get a level that day.","Ibuprofen, some blood pressure tablets and water tablets push the level up, as do dehydration and stomach bugs. Paracetamol is safe instead.","Please don't stop it suddenly unless you're toxic. We reduce slowly, over at least four weeks."],"hear":"\"So no ibuprofen at all. Got it.\""},{"h":"Suicide protection and safe supplies","say":["Lithium also lowers suicide risk in people with mood problems. Are you having any thoughts about ending your life at the moment?","Both tablets are dangerous in large amounts, so after discharge we give a week at a time to begin with."],"hear":"\"Not now. I had them before I came in.\"","mark":"Current risk checked"},{"h":"Pregnancy, contraception and breastfeeding","say":["Are you planning a pregnancy, now or later?","Lithium carries a small extra risk of heart problems in the baby, so we'd plan any pregnancy together. Until then, reliable contraception matters.","If you got pregnant unexpectedly, don't stop it; ring us that day. Breastfeeding isn't advised while on lithium."],"hear":"\"We want a baby in about two years. We use condoms.\"","mark":"Pregnancy plans identified"},{"h":"Teach-back and decision","say":["To check I've explained it well, which signs would mean stopping it and having a blood test?","Here's the lithium booklet, record book and alert card. Would you like to start, or think it over?"],"hear":"\"Being sick, a bad shake, wobbly, confused. I'd like the blood tests first, then decide.\"","mark":"Decision agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Why lithium","Feared it meant bipolar disorder","Reassure early; diagnosis unchanged, augmentation for recurrent depression"],["Tests","Takes ibuprofen most months","Stop it; paracetamol instead; counsel on all NSAIDs"],["Side effects","Father died of kidney failure (diabetic)","Check her eGFR; 6-monthly monitoring; review promptly if it falls"],["Toxicity","Named four signs on teach-back","Safe to start once baseline is normal"],["Pregnancy","Wants a baby in about 2 years; uses condoms","Reliable contraception; plan pregnancy with the team; no breastfeeding on lithium"]]},"key":"Explaining lithium is easy; fitting it safely into her life is the skill. Leave time for her kidneys, her ibuprofen and her baby plans, because that is where the real risks are.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5,6],[5,8],[9]]},"c-s-07":{"name":"ECT for a man who cannot decide: fears, reasons, procedure, memory, safeguards","why":"Turns a frightened relative's 'no' into an informed partnership, while being honest about risks and clear about who decides.","frame":["Her fears and ideas","Why ECT, why now","What happens","Side effects and memory","Alternatives","Safeguards and her role"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and hear her fears first","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors looking after your dad. Thank you for coming in. How are you doing?","You've heard the team are thinking about ECT. What have you heard about it, and what worries you most?"],"hear":"\"Shock therapy. I thought they stopped that in the asylums. He'll never forgive me.\"","mark":"Fear and guilt identified"},{"h":"Why ECT, why now","say":["Your dad's depression has become very severe. For four days he's eaten nothing and had only sips to drink, and his kidney test is getting worse.","Two antidepressants and lithium haven't helped over five months. ECT is the most effective treatment we have for depression this severe, and it works faster than tablets."],"hear":"\"I didn't realise his kidneys were affected.\"","mark":"Urgency understood"},{"h":"What happens, how many, how well","say":["He'd have nothing to eat from midnight. In the ECT suite, an anaesthetist puts him fully to sleep and gives a muscle relaxant.","A brief current causes a seizure in the brain lasting under a minute; his body barely moves. He wakes in recovery within minutes.","It's usually twice a week, often 6 to 12 treatments. Most people this unwell improve, often within two or three weeks."],"hear":"\"So he wouldn't feel anything?\""},{"h":"Side effects and memory, honestly","say":["Afterwards, headache, aching muscles, sickness or confusion for an hour or so are common.","Memory for the weeks around treatment is often patchy. Most of it improves, but some people say a few older memories don't come back.","We reduce this by treating one side of the head first and checking his memory regularly. The anaesthetic risk is very small, like any short operation."],"hear":"\"Will he still know who I am?\"","mark":"Memory concern addressed"},{"h":"Alternatives and the risk of waiting","say":["We could change his medicine again, but that usually takes weeks to work.","Without effective treatment, not eating and drinking risks kidney failure, clots and infection. That's why the team feel it can't wait."],"hear":"\"So waiting isn't really safe either.\""},{"h":"Who decides, and her role","say":["At the moment your dad can't take in and weigh up this decision, so it can't be his consent.","Because he's detained, an independent doctor from the Care Quality Commission must agree ECT is appropriate. Nothing he wrote refusing it would be overruled.","Did he ever say what he'd want, or sign any lasting power of attorney?"],"hear":"\"He never talked about it. He just always wanted to be independent.\"","mark":"No advance decision or LPA"},{"h":"Time, information and close","say":["You're not deciding this or doing it to him. Your views will go to the consultant and the independent doctor.","Would it help to visit the ECT suite and read the leaflet? Could you tell me back the main points, so I know I've been clear?","Let's meet with the consultant tomorrow afternoon."],"hear":"\"Yes, I'd like to see it. And I'd like to talk to the consultant.\"","mark":"Next steps agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her fears","'Shock therapy', asylums, 'he'll never forgive me'","Correct the picture gently; lift the decision weight off her"],["Why now","Did not know his kidneys were affected","Urgency was not understood; explain it plainly"],["Memory","'Will he still know who I am?'","Honest answer; unilateral first and cognitive checks"],["His wishes","Never discussed; values independence","No advance decision or LPA; record his values for the SOAD"],["Support","Wants a visit and to see the consultant","Arrange both; offer a Carer's assessment"]]},"key":"Honesty about memory builds more trust than reassurance. And her role is to tell you what he would want, not to consent: saying so lifts her guilt and gives the SOAD what it needs.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7]]},"c-s-08":{"name":"Is rTMS right for him? History, options, the treatment, a recommendation","why":"Answers the question he came with, fully and honestly, while keeping the other options in view.","frame":["Mood now and safety","What has worked","The options","How rTMS works and how well","Practicalities and side effects","Recommendation"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and agree the agenda","say":["Hello Mr Fielding, I'm Dr Banerjee, one of the psychiatrists. I understand you'd like to talk about rTMS.","Can I spend a couple of minutes on how you are and what's helped before, then go through rTMS properly?"],"hear":"\"Fine, but I really want to hear about the magnetic treatment.\""},{"h":"Mood now and safety","say":["How's your mood been over the last couple of months?","When things are this hard, some people think about ending their life. Have you had thoughts like that?"],"hear":"\"Every day's a slog. But no, nothing like that. My wife and my work keep me going.\"","mark":"No suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"What has and hasn't worked","say":["Which treatments have helped in the past?","ECT worked well for you. What was difficult about it?"],"hear":"\"The tablets did nothing. ECT worked, but I felt rough after each one, and I couldn't drive for two days. I'm an electrician; I need my van.\"","mark":"Driving identified as key"},{"h":"The options in brief","say":["There are a few routes: adding lithium or a small dose of aripiprazole to your tablet, trying venlafaxine, ECT again, talking therapy, or rTMS.","As rTMS is what you're interested in, let me go through it properly."],"hear":"\"Yes, please.\""},{"h":"How it works and how well","say":["A magnetic coil is held against your head. It sends quick pulses to the front of the brain, which is underactive in depression. You're awake throughout.","It's less powerful than ECT: about four in ten people improve a lot. It builds over two to four weeks.","It's also used for OCD and migraine."],"hear":"\"So it's like ECT without the anaesthetic?\""},{"h":"Before starting and the sessions","say":["Do you have any metal in your head, a pacemaker, or have you ever had a fit? How much do you drink?","At the first session they find the right strength by making your thumb twitch. Then you'd go five days a week for four to six weeks.","You sit in a chair; it clicks and taps on your scalp. Sessions take a few minutes to about forty, depending on the type."],"hear":"\"No metal, never had a fit. Maybe six pints a week.\"","mark":"Safety screen clear"},{"h":"Side effects, tablets, driving, top-ups","say":["Headache and scalp discomfort are common at first; paracetamol helps. Fits are very rare. It doesn't affect memory.","Keep taking your trazodone. Most people drive themselves to and from sessions.","Most people don't need more afterwards, but top-ups are possible if mood dips."],"hear":"\"I can drive? That changes everything.\""},{"h":"Recommendation, check and close","say":["I think rTMS is a sensible option for you. Tablets haven't worked, ECT helped but was hard, and rTMS works in a similar way without an anaesthetic.","Can you tell me back the main points, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","I'll refer you today and see you in four weeks. If you have any thoughts of hurting yourself, ring the crisis line."],"hear":"\"Daily for about a month, I can drive, keep my tablet. Let's do it.\"","mark":"Referral agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mood and safety","Daily slog, no suicidal thoughts","Moderate episode; outpatient rTMS is reasonable"],["Past treatments","Four antidepressants failed; ECT worked","Treatment-resistant; likely to respond to brain stimulation"],["ECT downsides","Post-anaesthetic sickness, two days off driving","rTMS avoids both; this drives his preference"],["Screening","No metal, implants or seizures; modest alcohol","No barrier to referral"],["Goal","Wants to keep working and driving","Plan sessions around work; continue trazodone"]]},"key":"He came for rTMS; give it to him in full. Keep the history to the points that shape the choice (safety, ECT response, its downsides), name the alternatives briefly, and make an honest recommendation with reasons.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-s-09":{"name":"First-rank symptoms: voices, thoughts, control, sudden meanings","why":"Finds each first-rank symptom reliably by moving from his own story to precise questions that test outside origin.","frame":["Voices: echo, discussion, commentary","Thought alienation: insertion, withdrawal, broadcast","Passivity: made feelings, impulses, acts","Somatic passivity","Delusional perception"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and let him tell it","say":["Hello Callum, I'm Dr Adeyemi from the early intervention team. Thanks for coming in. Your GP said things have felt odd lately. Can you tell me what's been happening?"],"hear":"\"My mind's being taken over. The network. They know everything.\"","mark":"Experiences of influence suggested"},{"h":"Voices: echo, discussion, commentary","say":["Do you ever hear your own thoughts spoken out loud, a moment after you think them?","Do you hear voices talking about you to each other, calling you he or him?","Do they ever describe what you're doing as you do it?"],"hear":"\"Two men in the vent. 'He's useless.' 'He's going to the kitchen now.' Sometimes they say my thoughts just after me.\"","mark":"Thought echo, discussion and commentary present"},{"h":"Thought insertion and withdrawal","say":["Do thoughts that aren't yours come into your head, that someone else put there?","How do you know they aren't your own thoughts?","Do your thoughts ever stop suddenly, as if someone took them out of your head?"],"hear":"\"They're the network's thoughts, not mine. And mid-sentence my thoughts get pulled out. Just gone.\"","mark":"Insertion and withdrawal present"},{"h":"Thought broadcast","say":["Do you feel your thoughts escape from your head, so other people know them without you saying anything?","Is that from your face or what you say, or do the thoughts themselves leave you?"],"hear":"\"They leak out through the walls. The neighbours know what I'm thinking.\"","mark":"Broadcast present"},{"h":"Passivity: made feelings, impulses, acts; somatic","say":["Do you ever feel your emotions are put there by someone else, not your own?","What about urges, or movements: does anything outside you control what your body does?","Any sensations in your body that you feel are being done to you?"],"hear":"\"My feelings are mine. But my right hand taps by itself. They're working it. And there's a buzzing in my stomach from their machines.\"","mark":"Made acts and somatic passivity present; made feelings absent"},{"h":"Delusional perception","say":["When did you first realise what was happening? Was there a moment it became clear?","Did you see or hear anything ordinary just before? What made it mean that?"],"hear":"\"I saw a red van parked outside, and I just knew I'd been chosen for their experiment.\"","mark":"Delusional perception present"},{"h":"Summarise, check and next steps","say":["Can I check I've got it right? You hear voices discussing you and describing what you do, and your thoughts feel put in, taken out and known by others.","Your hand moves by itself, there's a buzzing put in your stomach, and seeing the red van made it all clear. Have I missed anything?","My colleague will see you next, and then we'll plan help together."],"hear":"\"That's it. It's a relief someone gets it.\"","mark":"Summary agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["First-rank symptom","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Thought echo, discussion, commentary","Two men in the vent; 'he's going to the kitchen'; repeat his thoughts","Three auditory first-rank symptoms present"],["Insertion and withdrawal","Thoughts 'from the network'; pulled out mid-sentence","Thought alienation present, outside origin confirmed"],["Broadcast","Thoughts leak through walls; neighbours know","Broadcast present, not just face-reading"],["Passivity","Hand taps by itself; stomach buzzing from machines; feelings his own","Made acts and somatic passivity present; made feelings absent"],["Delusional perception","Red van, then sudden certainty of being chosen","Two-step delusional perception present"]]},"key":"A 'yes' is not a symptom. For each item, ask for an example and test outside origin: whose thought, who moves the hand, how do others know. That separates real first-rank symptoms from look-alikes.","objmap":[[1],[2],[3,4],[5,6],[7]]},"c-s-10":{"name":"Will my baby get it? Fear, genes, numbers, choice and her own health","why":"Turns a frightening online claim into clear, honest numbers, hands the decision back to her and keeps her well through pregnancy.","frame":["Her fear and beliefs","How it is inherited","The numbers","Testing and other causes","Her decision","Her own health"],"steps":[{"h":"Confidentiality and her fear","say":["Hello Hannah, I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatrists. Everything we talk about today stays between us and your care team. How are you feeling?","What have you read, and what worries you most?"],"hear":"\"That schizophrenia is genetic. I can't give this to my baby. I'm thinking of ending it.\"","mark":"Fear of inheritance identified"},{"h":"How it is inherited","say":["Schizophrenia isn't caused by one gene. Lots of genes each add a tiny bit of vulnerability.","What's passed on is a higher chance, not the illness itself. Whether it develops depends on much more."],"hear":"\"So it's not definitely going to happen?\""},{"h":"The numbers, both ways","say":["In everyone, about 1 in 100 people develop schizophrenia. When one parent has it, it's about 13 in 100.","That means about 87 in every 100 children like yours won't develop it. Does Imran's family have any mental illness?"],"hear":"\"No, none. I thought it was more like half.\"","mark":"Risk overestimated"},{"h":"Testing and other causes","say":["There's no test, before or after birth, that predicts it.","Other things add to the risk: heavy cannabis as a teenager, serious childhood stress or trauma, and some problems around birth. Some of those you can influence."],"hear":"\"So I could protect them a bit?\""},{"h":"Her decision and her faith","say":["How do you feel about the pregnancy itself, apart from this worry?","This is your decision. Whatever you choose, we'll support you, and I won't share this with your family.","If you'd like to talk it through with someone independent, I can give you the NHS pregnancy counselling details. There's time to think."],"hear":"\"We wanted this baby so much. But it's against my faith, and my family would disown me.\"","mark":"Values conflict identified"},{"h":"Her own health","say":["Can I ask about you? Are you taking any medication at the moment?","How are your sleep and thoughts? Any of the old experiences, or thoughts of hurting yourself?","Pregnancy and the weeks after birth bring a higher risk of becoming unwell, especially off medication."],"hear":"\"I stopped my tablets six months ago. I didn't tell anyone. I'm sleeping okay, no voices, no thoughts like that.\"","mark":"Off medication; no current symptoms or self-harm thoughts"},{"h":"Plan and teach-back","say":["I'd like to refer you to the perinatal mental health team; they specialise in pregnancy. They'll talk with you about medication.","Can you tell me back what the chance is for your baby?","Let's meet again next week; here's our duty number."],"hear":"\"About 13 in 100, so most won't get it. And there's no test.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her fear","'Schizophrenia is genetic'; thinking of ending the pregnancy","Information gap driving a major decision"],["Numbers","Thought the risk was about half","Correct with 13 in 100, and 87 in 100 unaffected"],["Family history","None on her husband's side","One-parent figure applies"],["Her decision","Wanted baby; faith and family conflict","Non-directive support; confidentiality; counselling routes"],["Her health","Off aripiprazole 6 months, well now","High relapse risk; urgent perinatal referral and medication review"]]},"key":"The numbers alone do not finish the job. Give the risk both ways, hand the choice back to her without steering, and then turn to her own relapse risk, which is the danger she has not yet seen.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5],[6,7]]},"c-s-13":{"name":"Consent first, treatment protected, a lawful route to compete","why":"Answer an employer's demand to stop a medicine without breaching confidentiality, and turn the problem into an exemption and workplace support.","frame":["Consent and its limits","Why it is adult ADHD","Stopping and alternatives","The ban and the TUE","Workplace adjustments"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and agenda","say":["Hello Mr Morgan, I'm Dr Bello. Tom has agreed I can talk about his ADHD, his treatment and the competition.","Anything else about his life I can't discuss, though I'm happy to listen. What would help most today?"],"hear":"\"He needs to come off the Ritalin. He's been picked for the nationals.\"","mark":"Consent limits stated; manager's goal identified"},{"h":"Why it is ADHD","say":["What do you notice about Tom at work and on the mat?","Those are the three parts of ADHD: attention, restlessness and acting on impulse.","It started when he was small, and it's still there; that's adult ADHD."],"hear":"\"Careless mistakes, never follows instructions, can't sit still, temper. But I thought kids grew out of it.\"","mark":"Inattention, overactivity and impulsivity identified"},{"h":"Stopping and the alternatives","say":["Stopping isn't dangerous, but his symptoms return within a day: on the mat, at work and on the road.","The non-stimulant he tried at 16 made him worse and affected his liver.","The other first-choice medicines are also stimulants, and also banned in competition."],"hear":"\"So there's nothing else?\"","mark":"Stopping advised against; alternatives unsuitable"},{"h":"Herbal remedies and the 'performance' worry","say":["There's no good evidence for herbal calming remedies, and supplements can contain banned substances.","In ADHD, the medicine brings focus to an ordinary level. It doesn't give him an edge."],"hear":"\"I'd found one online. I'll bin it.\""},{"h":"The exemption","say":["The proper route is a Therapeutic Use Exemption from UK Anti-Doping.","It needs a specialist report: the diagnosis, what's been tried, and the dose. We can write that this week.","Apply now; it can take weeks. I can't issue a certificate myself, but our report is the key evidence."],"hear":"\"Right. So he applies, and you back it up.\"","mark":"TUE route agreed"},{"h":"Drug use raised","say":["Thank you for telling me. I can't discuss anything Tom hasn't agreed to share.","Any drug like that can mean a positive test, and it's risky alongside his medicine. I'd encourage you to talk to him."],"hear":"\"Fair enough. I just worry about him.\"","mark":"Confidentiality held"},{"h":"Work adjustments","say":["At work, short written task lists and one job at a time help a lot.","A quick daily check-in, and breaks to move, cut the mistakes.","Access to Work can fund an ADHD coach."],"hear":"\"We can do that. The lists especially.\"","mark":"Adjustments agreed"},{"h":"Check and close","say":["What will you take back from today?","Shall we bring Tom in now to agree the plan together?"],"hear":"\"Keep the tablets, apply for the exemption, lists at work. Yes, get him in.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Consent","Written consent: ADHD, treatment, competition","Discuss those only; listen to anything else"],["Symptoms now","Mistakes, missed instructions, restless, temper","Adult ADHD with all three domains active"],["Alternatives","Atomoxetine worse and liver problem at 16","No suitable non-stimulant; keep methylphenidate"],["The ban","Picked for nationals; told it is banned","Apply for a TUE now with specialist evidence"],["Work","Forgets instructions, gets frustrated","Task lists, check-ins, breaks, Access to Work"]]},"key":"The manager's goal and the patient's health are both met by the exemption, not by stopping the drug. Excellent practice names the consent limits first and holds them when the drug disclosure comes.","objmap":[[1,6],[2],[3,4],[5],[7,8]]},"c-s-15":{"name":"Real, not faked: name it, show how we know, then the way out","why":"Replace the 'faking' story with a clear, positive diagnosis, so the family supports treatment and the patient comes back.","frame":["The 'faking' fear","What they are and how we know","Why: mind and body","Mood, risk and stressors","Treatment and outlook","First aid and next step"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and his story","say":["Mr Turner, I'm Dr Huang from the neuropsychiatry team. Chloe has agreed I can talk to you about her diagnosis and treatment.","What has she told you about what the neurologists said?"],"hear":"\"That they think she's making it up. She's devastated. So am I.\"","mark":"Fear of being disbelieved identified"},{"h":"Answer the fear first","say":["I'm sorry it came across like that. Chloe isn't making this up.","These attacks are real, and she isn't doing them on purpose."],"hear":"\"Then why send her to a psychiatrist?\""},{"h":"What they are and how we know","say":["They're called functional, or dissociative, seizures. The brain is healthy, but the way it's working goes wrong for a while.","In hospital, her usual attacks were filmed while her brain waves were recorded. The brain waves stayed normal: that's how we know.","It's common: about one in five people seen in seizure clinics have these."],"hear":"\"So it's not epilepsy. Then what is it?\"","mark":"Diagnosis and evidence explained"},{"h":"Mind and body","say":["Think of how embarrassment makes us blush, or nerves make the heart race. The body reacts without our choosing.","Functional seizures work a bit like that: an automatic shutdown, often linked to stress, though not always."],"hear":"\"She was fine until her mum died.\""},{"h":"Mood, risk and stressors","say":["I'm so sorry about her mum. How has Chloe been since?","Has she seemed low, or talked about wishing you weren't here, or harming herself?","Have you two been able to talk about her mum?"],"hear":"\"Anxious, withdrawn, not sleeping. Never said anything about hurting herself. We don't really talk about her mum.\"","mark":"Anxiety, low mood and unresolved grief; no known self-harm"},{"h":"Treatment and outlook","say":["The epilepsy tablets won't help; her neurologist will reduce them slowly. Please don't stop them suddenly.","The main treatment is a talking therapy designed for these seizures, about 12 sessions, and learning to spot and ride out warning signs.","Many people improve a lot; understanding and accepting the diagnosis early is the best sign."],"hear":"\"So she could get better?\"","mark":"Treatment and outlook explained"},{"h":"First aid and a way back","say":["During an attack: keep her safe, don't hold her down or put anything in her mouth, talk calmly, and time it.","Call 999 if she's injured or it's different from usual.","What would help Chloe come and talk to us: with you, or by video?","Can you tell me how you'll explain this to her?"],"hear":"\"Real, not her fault, the brain's software, therapy helps. I think she'd do a video call.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["'Faking' fear","'They think she's making it up'","Say early: real, involuntary, common"],["Diagnosis","Normal scans and EEG; attacks filmed","Positive diagnosis from video-EEG, not 'nothing found'"],["Stressors","Mother died 18 months ago; not talked about","Possible contributor; offer bereavement support"],["Mood and risk","Anxious, withdrawn, poor sleep; no self-harm talk","Assess fully when she attends; low current risk"],["Medicines","On epilepsy tablets","Won't help; neurology to taper"]]},"key":"Explaining the diagnosis well is itself treatment. The excellent doctor answers 'faking' in the first minute, shows how the diagnosis was made, and leaves a door open that Chloe can walk through on her own terms.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-s-16":{"name":"Periods gone on risperidone: confirm, exclude, explain, choose","why":"Confirms antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinaemia, rules out pregnancy, other drugs and the pituitary, and agrees a change she can live with.","frame":["Symptoms of high prolactin","Pregnancy","Other drugs and medical causes","Pituitary red flags","Explanation and long-term risk","Options and shared choice"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, and find out what she knows","say":["I'm one of the doctors in the team. Thanks for coming back for your result.","What did you understand that blood test was for?","Before I explain it, may I ask some personal questions about your periods and your body?"],"hear":"\"Something about my periods? Nobody really said.\""},{"h":"Ask for the effects of high prolactin","say":["Tell me what's been happening with your periods since the summer.","Any breast soreness, swelling, or milky fluid from the nipples?","This can make some people lose interest in sex. Has that happened to you?","Any spots or extra hair growth?"],"hear":"\"Lighter, then stopped last month. Sore breasts, no milk. I've been avoiding sex.\"","mark":"Amenorrhoea, breast tenderness and low libido identified"},{"h":"Exclude pregnancy","say":["I ask everyone this: could you be pregnant at the moment?","We'll do a urine test today anyway, just to be sure."],"hear":"\"No, not for two months. But we want a baby soon.\"","mark":"Pregnancy unlikely; test today; pregnancy wish identified"},{"h":"Exclude other drugs and medical causes","say":["Anything else you take: contraception, sickness or indigestion tablets, antidepressants, herbal remedies?","Any tiredness, feeling the cold or weight change? Any kidney problems?","Were your periods regular before risperidone?"],"hear":"\"Only the risperidone. I feel well. They were always regular.\"","mark":"Other drugs, thyroid, kidney and PCOS made unlikely"},{"h":"Screen for a pituitary cause","say":["Any headaches, especially new or worse in the morning?","Any change in your vision, like bumping into things at the side?"],"hear":"\"No headaches. My eyes are fine.\"","mark":"No pressure symptoms: prolactinoma unlikely"},{"h":"Explain the result and the cause","say":["Prolactin is a hormone from a small gland under the brain. High levels switch periods off.","Yours was normal before risperidone and is now 1,100, about twice the top of normal.","Risperidone blocks dopamine, which is the brake on prolactin. That's the most likely cause.","Over years it can thin bones and make pregnancy harder, so it's worth treating."],"hear":"\"So it's the tablets, not a tumour?\"","mark":"Result explained and linked to risperidone"},{"h":"Lay out the options and agree one","say":["Option one: a gradual switch to aripiprazole. Option two: keep risperidone and add aripiprazole 5 mg.","Option three: a lower dose, with some relapse risk. Option four: carry on and monitor.","Keeping you well matters most. Which feels right to you?"],"hear":"\"Adding the small one feels safer. I don't want the voices back.\"","mark":"Shared decision: add aripiprazole"},{"h":"Plan for pregnancy, tests and follow-up","say":["Fertility can come back quickly as prolactin falls, so use condoms until you're ready.","I'll refer you to the perinatal team for advice before you try. Folic acid 400 micrograms when you start trying.","Repeat prolactin, thyroid and kidney tests today; prolactin again at three months; see you in two weeks.","What will you tell Tom tonight?"],"hear":"\"Hormone up from the risperidone, small extra tablet, bloods, condoms, baby team.\"","mark":"Plan agreed and understood"}],"table":{"cols":["Check","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Symptoms of high prolactin","Periods lighter then absent; sore breasts; low libido","Symptomatic, so treat rather than just watch"],["Pregnancy","No sex for two months; wants a baby soon","Test anyway; plan for fertility returning"],["Other drugs and medical causes","Risperidone only; no thyroid or kidney symptoms; periods regular before","Risperidone is the likely cause; bloods to confirm"],["Pituitary red flags","No headache or visual change; level 1,100","Prolactinoma unlikely; scan only if it stays high after a change"],["Options and shared choice","Fears relapse and weight gain; prefers to keep risperidone","Add aripiprazole 5 mg now; aim for one medicine before conception"]]},"key":"A good doctor lowers the prolactin. An excellent one also spots that fertility returns quickly once it falls, so contraception and preconception advice are part of the same plan.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5],[6],[7,8],[8]]},"c-s-17":{"name":"From tablets to a depot: what, how often, what risks, what law","why":"Gives a worried father an accurate, jargon-free picture of depot treatment and how consent and the Mental Health Act apply.","frame":["Why a depot (adherence)","What it is","Choice and schedule","Test dose and side effects","Consent and the MHA","After discharge"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, confirm consent, hear his worry","say":["I'm one of the ward doctors. Daniel has agreed we can talk to you about his treatment.","What have you heard about injections so far?","What worries you most?"],"hear":"\"Someone said 'depot'. I don't want to hurt him.\"","mark":"Father's main concern identified"},{"h":"Establish the adherence pattern","say":["What happens with his tablets at home?","How is he when he takes them? Any side effects?","Why do you think he stops?"],"hear":"\"Takes them, feels better, stops. Unopened boxes. Third relapse this year.\"","mark":"Non-adherence from lack of insight; good past response"},{"h":"Explain what a depot is","say":["It's the same kind of medicine as a tablet, given into a muscle.","It releases slowly over weeks, so the level stays steady without daily tablets.","It isn't stronger; it's a different way of getting the same medicine in."],"hear":"\"So it's not a stronger drug?\"","mark":"Concept understood"},{"h":"Explain the choices and the schedule","say":["Because risperidone worked, paliperidone is the natural choice: it's what risperidone becomes in the body.","Two starting injections a week apart in the upper arm, then monthly; three-monthly once he's stable.","Nurses give it on the ward; the depot clinic or care coordinator at home."],"hear":"\"How often? Who'd give it?\"","mark":"Frequency question answered"},{"h":"Explain test doses, side effects and monitoring","say":["Older injections start with a small test dose. His months on risperidone already show he tolerates paliperidone.","Side effects are the tablet's: weight, sleepiness, stiffness, restlessness, hormone changes. Plus a sore arm.","Because it lasts weeks, side effects last too, so we check weight, bloods and blood pressure."],"hear":"\"And if it doesn't suit him, it's stuck in him?\"","mark":"Side effects question answered"},{"h":"Explain consent and the Mental Health Act","say":["We'll seek his agreement first and offer him choices.","On Section 3, for the first three months of treatment his consultant can authorise medicine if he refuses.","After three months, he must consent with capacity, or a second-opinion doctor must approve it."],"hear":"\"So they'd hold him down?\"","mark":"MHA position explained"},{"h":"Answer 'what if he refuses at home?'","say":["Nobody can give an injection at home against his will.","A Community Treatment Order can make the injection a condition of living at home.","If he refuses and becomes unwell, he can be recalled to hospital for up to 72 hours."],"hear":"\"That might be the only thing that works.\"","mark":"Refusal question answered"},{"h":"Check understanding and close","say":["Can I check I've explained it properly? How would you tell his mum?","Here's a leaflet. The ward will offer you a carer's assessment.","The consultant will discuss it with Daniel at ward round; come to the family meeting."],"hear":"\"Same medicine, monthly injection, sore arm, treatment order if needed.\"","mark":"Plan understood"}],"table":{"cols":["Question","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Why a depot","Third relapse this year after stopping; responds well; no side effects","A strong case for a long-acting injection"],["Choice and schedule","Did well on risperidone","Paliperidone monthly after two starting doses; three-monthly later"],["Test dose and side effects","Fears hurting him; worried it stays in his body","Past tolerance of risperidone replaces a test dose; monitor closely"],["Consent and the MHA","Refusing all medicine on Section 3","Three-month rule applies; seek agreement first; second opinion later"],["After discharge","Will not attend a clinic","Home visits; consider a CTO with recall"]]},"key":"Good doctors explain the injection. Excellent ones match the depot to the drug he already responded to, and answer the father's real question: what happens when he refuses at home.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-s-18":{"name":"Stopped going out: event, panic, places, and what it isn't","why":"Reaches a confident diagnosis of agoraphobia with panic attacks by mapping the fear and actively ruling out its look-alikes and the risks.","frame":["Panic attack","Agoraphobic situations","Panic disorder","Depression and psychosis","PTSD, social anxiety, OCD","Self-neglect and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle in and open gently","say":["Thank you for letting me into your home. Your GP asked us to visit.","Your daughter's worried you're not going out. How have things been?","Has anything changed in the last couple of months?"],"hear":"\"I'm fine. I work from home anyway.\""},{"h":"Get past the minimising","say":["Things people feel embarrassed about are often what helps us most.","Whatever it is, I won't judge."],"hear":"\"Two months ago, a group of lads outside the supermarket.\"","mark":"Precipitating event identified"},{"h":"Elicit the panic attack","say":["What happened in your body at the time?","What went through your mind?","And when you got home?"],"hear":"\"Heart banging, couldn't breathe, thought I was dying. It melted away at home.\"","mark":"Panic attack with catastrophic thoughts; relief at home"},{"h":"Map the agoraphobic situations","say":["Which places bring that feeling back?","What's the worst thing you fear might happen there?","What about crowds, queues, being out alone elsewhere?"],"hear":"\"Supermarket, bus, church. I'll be stuck, collapse, no one will help.\"","mark":"Agoraphobia: several situations, fear of being trapped or without help"},{"h":"Separate panic disorder","say":["Have you ever had an attack out of the blue, at home, for no reason?","Do you worry between times about having another attack?"],"hear":"\"Never at home. Home is safe.\"","mark":"Attacks situational only: panic disorder not supported"},{"h":"Screen the differentials","say":["How's your mood at home? Enjoyment, sleep, appetite?","Nightmares, flashbacks, jumpiness? Worries about being judged? Checking or washing?","Any sense those lads are watching or following you on purpose?","Alcohol, caffeine, drugs? Shakiness, sweating, weight loss?"],"hear":"\"Mood's good, no flashbacks, they don't know me. Small whisky twice a week.\"","mark":"Depression, PTSD, social anxiety, OCD, psychosis and substances made unlikely"},{"h":"Assess self-neglect and risk","say":["How are you managing for food?","Washing, clothes, keeping the house?","Any thoughts of not wanting to live, or of harming anyone?"],"hear":"\"Daughter shops online. Clothes starting to smell. No thoughts like that.\"","mark":"Mild self-neglect (laundry); no suicidal or violent thoughts"},{"h":"Summarise and close","say":["That day was a panic attack, and your mind learned that going out is dangerous.","It's called agoraphobia, it's common and very treatable.","Your GP will do routine bloods, including thyroid, and we'll talk about therapy."],"hear":"\"So it can be fixed? I can go back to church?\"","mark":"Diagnosis shared"}],"table":{"cols":["Diagnostic question","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Panic attack","Racing heart, breathlessness, fear of dying; relief at home","Panic attack triggered by intimidation"],["Agoraphobic situations","Supermarket, bus, church, crowds; fears being trapped and collapsing","Agoraphobia: fear in several places where escape or help is hard"],["Panic disorder","No attacks out of the blue at home","Situational attacks only: not panic disorder"],["Depression and psychosis","Mood, sleep and enjoyment normal; fear of youths is realistic, not persecutory","Neither present"],["PTSD, social anxiety, OCD","No flashbacks, nightmares, fear of judgement or compulsions","Not present"],["Self-neglect and risk","Daughter shops; clothes unwashed; no suicidal thoughts","Mild self-neglect; low risk"]]},"key":"Good doctors spot the avoidance. Excellent ones test whether the fear is realistic or persecutory, ask about attacks at home, and note it is only two months old, so the diagnosis is provisional.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5,6],[6,7],[8]]},"c-s-19":{"name":"The scan wasn't normal: SPIKES with his sister","why":"Breaks unexpected news of a meningioma kindly and clearly, answers her real questions honestly, and sets up urgent next steps without false promises.","frame":["Setting","Perception","Invitation and knowledge","Emotion","Strategy and summary"],"steps":[{"h":"Setting: room, consent, support","say":["We won't be disturbed here. Samuel asked us to tell you everything.","Would you like anyone with you, or on the phone?"],"hear":"\"No. Just tell me.\"","mark":"Setting and consent confirmed"},{"h":"Perception: what she knows","say":["What's your understanding of why we did the scan?","What were you expecting today?"],"hear":"\"To rule things out. Then he'd start medicine for psychosis.\"","mark":"Expects normal result: high shock risk"},{"h":"Invitation and knowledge: the news","say":["I'm afraid the scan has shown something we weren't expecting.","It's a growth in the front of his head, called a meningioma: a lump from the lining around the brain."],"hear":"\"A brain tumour? Oh my God.\"","mark":"News delivered in plain words"},{"h":"Emotion: stop and stay","say":["I'm so sorry. That's a huge shock.","Take your time. There's no rush.","Shall I tell you a bit more about what it means?"],"hear":"\"Brain tumours kill people. How long has he got?\"","mark":"Fear of death identified"},{"h":"Knowledge, led by her questions","say":["Most meningiomas are not cancer and grow slowly; many can be removed or controlled.","I can't say exactly until the MRI and specialists; I won't guess.","A growth at the front can cause headaches, personality change and suspiciousness."],"hear":"\"So it's why he's been so strange?\"","mark":"Honest prognosis; symptoms linked"},{"h":"Strategy: urgent next steps","say":["MRI is booked for Friday; an urgent referral to the brain surgery team has gone today.","Treatment may be surgery, radiotherapy or watching; Samuel decides with them.","He must stop driving now and tell the DVLA."],"hear":"\"Can they save him? Can he drive? Will I decide for him?\"","mark":"Next steps, driving and decisions covered"},{"h":"Practical worries and safety-netting","say":["A fit note means at least statutory sick pay; our support worker can help.","He makes his own decisions unless he lacks capacity for a specific one.","Worse headache, vomiting, drowsiness, a fit or new weakness: 999 or A&E."],"hear":"\"Okay. Worse headache, sick, drowsy, fit, weakness.\"","mark":"Safety-net understood"},{"h":"Summary: telling Samuel and close","say":["Shall I see him at home tomorrow with you, so you don't carry it alone?","We'll stay involved and hold the antipsychotic until the specialists advise.","What will you take away from today?"],"hear":"\"Growth on the lining, probably not cancer, MRI Friday, no driving, you'll tell him tomorrow.\"","mark":"Plan to tell Samuel agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["SPIKES stage","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Setting","Samuel asked her to come; mother doesn't know","Consent confirmed; she is alone, so pace carefully"],["Perception","Expects a normal scan and tablets","News will be a total shock; warn first"],["Invitation and knowledge","'A brain tumour? Brain tumours kill people.'","Explain 'meningioma' plainly; correct the fear honestly"],["Emotion","Crying, apologising","Silence and permission to feel; continue only when she asks"],["Strategy and summary","Fears about driving, work, decisions; can't tell him herself","MRI, urgent referral, DVLA, fit note, capacity; tell Samuel together"]]},"key":"Good doctors give the news clearly. Excellent ones let her questions set the pace, stay inside what is known, and offer to tell Samuel together so she is not left carrying it.","objmap":[[1,2,3,4],[5],[6,7],[7],[8]]},"c-s-20":{"name":"Slow, blank and shuffling: ask all four, examine in order","why":"Identifies drug-induced parkinsonism from a dose increase, actively excludes the other three EPSEs and NMS, and agrees a safe plan.","frame":["Parkinsonism","Acute dystonia","Akathisia","Tardive dyskinesia","NMS screen"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, confirm and acknowledge","say":["I'm one of the ward doctors. Your mum's noticed you're slower and stiffer.","Can I ask about that, and then examine you?","Can you confirm your date of birth?"],"hear":"\"I feel like I'm made of wood.\""},{"h":"History: the four EPSE groups","say":["Stiffness, slowness, shuffling, falls? Shaking hands?","Sudden spasms of the neck, eyes or tongue?","Restless inside, can't sit still?","Mouth or tongue movements you can't control?"],"hear":"\"Stiff, shuffling, one fall, tremor watching telly. No spasms, not restless, nothing in my mouth.\"","mark":"Parkinsonism identified; no dystonia, akathisia or TD symptoms"},{"h":"Timeline and safety screen","say":["When did it start? Any dose change?","Any other new tablets?","Any fever, sweating or feeling muddled?"],"hear":"\"Two weeks, since haloperidol went to twice a day. No fever.\"","mark":"Onset linked to dose increase; NMS unlikely"},{"h":"Prepare to examine","say":["I'll look at your face, hands, arms and walking. Nothing painful. Is that okay?","Alice will be with us. I've cleaned my hands.","Any pain anywhere?"],"hear":"\"Yes, fine. Just the hip from the fall.\"","mark":"Consent and chaperone"},{"h":"Seated: rest, face and mouth","say":["Sit back, hands on knees, feet flat. I'll watch for a few seconds.","Open your mouth and rest your tongue. Now stick it out."],"hear":"(Right hand tremor at rest; reduced facial expression; tongue still.)","mark":"Resting tremor and hypomimia; no orofacial dyskinesia"},{"h":"Arms: posture, tone and speed","say":["Hold your arms out, palms down.","Let your arm go floppy; I'll move your wrist and elbow.","Tap thumb and finger together quickly."],"hear":"(Cogwheel rigidity at the wrist; finger taps slow and shrinking.)","mark":"Rigidity and bradykinesia"},{"h":"Standing and walking","say":["Stand up without pushing on the chair if you can.","Arms out to the side, then let them drop.","Walk to the door, turn, come back."],"hear":"(Slow to rise; no restlessness; shuffling steps; reduced arm swing; turns in several steps.)","mark":"Parkinsonian gait; no akathisia"},{"h":"Explain, options and close","say":["This is parkinsonism, a side effect of the higher haloperidol dose. It isn't Parkinson's disease.","Options: back to the lower dose, switch, or short-term procyclidine.","Physio and lying and standing blood pressure because of the fall. Can you tell me what you understood?"],"hear":"\"The stiffness is from the haloperidol. It should get better if you change it.\"","mark":"Plan understood"}],"table":{"cols":["EPSE","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Parkinsonism","Stiffness, cogwheeling, resting tremor, slow taps, shuffling, reduced arm swing, flat face","Present: drug-induced parkinsonism"],["Acute dystonia","No spasms of neck, eyes or tongue","Absent"],["Akathisia","No inner restlessness; still when standing","Absent"],["Tardive dyskinesia","Tongue still at rest and protruded; no lip or jaw movements","Absent: baseline recorded"],["NMS screen","No fever, sweating or confusion","Unlikely; safety-net"]]},"key":"Good doctors find the stiffness. Excellent ones ask about all four EPSE groups, tie onset to the dose change, screen for NMS, and act on the fall.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,5,6,7],[8],[8]]},"c-s-21":{"name":"Liver check in a drinker: stigmata, the flap and the abdomen","why":"Finds every sign of chronic liver disease and encephalopathy in a fixed order, keeps him informed, and turns the findings into tests that stage the liver.","frame":["Peripheral stigmata","Encephalopathy signs","Abdomen and organs","Portal hypertension","Cerebellar and Wernicke's signs"],"steps":[{"h":"Hands, consent and limits","say":["Hello Mr Pendleton, I'm Dr Okafor. I hear you'd like your liver checked.","Any pain, yellow eyes, swelling or itching that you've noticed?","A full check would include the back passage and genitals, which we don't need to do today. Is that all right?"],"hear":"\"No pain, nothing I've spotted. Go for it.\"","mark":"Consent given; no symptoms"},{"h":"Position, then look from the end of the bed","say":["Would you like a nurse here as a chaperone?","Top off and lie back on one pillow; I'll cover your legs.","First I'm just looking at your colour, alertness and any shaking."],"hear":"\"Steve can stay. Do I look all right?\"","mark":"Alert, no jaundice, no fetor"},{"h":"Hands, arms and pulse","say":["Hands out flat, then palms up for me.","I'm feeling your pulse at the wrist now."],"hear":"\"They used to shake first thing, but not now.\"","mark":"Palmar erythema found"},{"h":"The flap, coordination and thinking","say":["Arms out, wrists bent back like stopping traffic, and hold it while I count to fifteen.","Touch your nose, then my finger, and keep going back and forth.","What's today's date and where are we? Could you copy this star?"],"hear":"\"Tuesday the 10th, Willow Ward. Here's your star.\"","mark":"No asterixis; orientation and star copy normal"},{"h":"Eyes, mouth, neck and chest","say":["Look up while I pull down your lower lid.","Keep your head still and watch my finger.","I'm looking at your chest for small red spots."],"hear":"\"So I've done damage? My dad died of his liver.\"","mark":"Four spider naevi; no nystagmus"},{"h":"Abdomen: look, then feel","say":["Any sore spots before I press? I'll watch your face.","Big breath in, and out, as I feel for your liver.","One hand behind your back now to feel each kidney."],"hear":"\"No, go ahead. Is the liver meant to be there?\"","mark":"Smooth liver edge 2 cm below the ribs"},{"h":"Tap and listen","say":["I'm tapping down your ribs to size the liver.","Could you roll onto your side towards me?","Now I'll listen to your tummy."],"hear":"\"Rolling over, right.\"","mark":"No shifting dullness; spleen not felt"},{"h":"Legs, reflexes and gait","say":["I'm pressing on your shins and ankles for swelling.","Let your legs go floppy while I tap your reflexes.","Walk to the door and back, then feet together, eyes closed."],"hear":"\"Straight line, see? No wobble.\"","mark":"No oedema; gait and Romberg normal"},{"h":"Findings, tests and safety net","say":["No signs your liver is failing or reaching your brain, but some signs of strain.","Bloods today, then an ultrasound and a FibroScan to measure stiffness.","Tell the nurses at once about yellow eyes, a swollen tummy, muddled thinking, or blood in vomit or stools."],"hear":"\"Bloods, a scan, and stay off the booze.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Sign group","What you heard or saw","What it means"],"rows":[["Peripheral stigmata","Red palms, four spider naevi, no jaundice","Chronic liver disease likely; stage it with bloods and elastography"],["Encephalopathy signs","No flap over 15 seconds, oriented, star copied, no fetor","No encephalopathy now; recheck if he becomes drowsy"],["Abdomen and organs","Smooth liver edge 2 cm below the ribs, non-tender","Hepatomegaly, typical of fatty liver or alcohol-related hepatitis"],["Portal hypertension","No spleen, no ascites, no dilated veins","No sign of decompensation"],["Cerebellar and Wernicke's signs","No nystagmus, normal finger-nose, gait and Romberg","No cerebellar damage or Wernicke's features"]]},"key":"Confusion in a drinker has three bedside causes: withdrawal, Wernicke's and hepatic encephalopathy. A flap, fetor and poor star copy point to the liver; nystagmus and ataxia point to Wernicke's. Look for both every time.","objmap":[[1,2],[2,3,5],[2,4,5,8],[6,7,8],[9]]},"c-s-35":{"name":"Three domains, three settings: an ADHD collateral history","why":"Gathers everyday evidence of ADHD, rules out the main look-alikes and leaves the parent with a clear, honest next step.","frame":["Inattention","Hyperactivity","Impulsivity","Onset and settings","Development and family history","Differentials, risk and impact"],"steps":[{"h":"Start with her worry","say":["What's been worrying you most about Callum?","I'll ask about his whole life, not just school. Is that alright?"],"hear":"\"He's in detention three times a week. I'm terrified he'll be excluded.\"","mark":"Main concern: exclusion"},{"h":"Inattention, in everyday terms","say":["How long can he stick at homework before he's off?","Does he lose things or leave jobs half done?","What about getting ready in the morning?"],"hear":"\"Ten minutes. Loses his PE kit every week. I tell him five times to put his shoes on.\"","mark":"Inattention identified"},{"h":"Hyperactivity and impulsivity","say":["Can he stay sitting through a meal or a lesson?","Does he blurt out, push in, or act first and say sorry later?","Any risky things without thinking, or injuries?"],"hear":"\"Up and down at dinner, shouts out, then apologises. Two trips to A&E.\"","mark":"Hyperactivity and impulsivity identified"},{"h":"Onset and settings","say":["When did you first notice this? Was it there at primary school?","Is it the same at football, at his gran's, at his dad's?"],"hear":"\"Since nursery. The football coach and my mum say the same.\"","mark":"Early onset; pervasive across settings"},{"h":"Development and family","say":["How was the pregnancy and birth? Did he walk and talk on time?","Anyone in the family the same?"],"hear":"\"Five weeks early, emergency Caesarean. Milestones fine. His dad never sat still.\"","mark":"Prematurity and family history identified"},{"h":"Look-alikes and co-occurring problems","say":["Any trouble with reading, spelling or maths?","How are friendships? Any fixed routines or trouble with noise? Is he defiant on purpose?","How's his sleep and hearing? Any staring spells? Any alcohol, vaping or drugs?"],"hear":"\"Reads fine, lots of mates, not defiant, just forgets. Xbox till eleven. Nothing else.\"","mark":"Main differentials screened; late screens noted"},{"h":"Impact and risk","say":["How is this affecting him in himself? Any talk of hurting himself?","Has he lashed out, or been picked on?","When he won't listen, how do you handle it at home?"],"hear":"\"He says he's stupid. Some lads wind him up. We shout, but we've never hit him.\"","mark":"Low self-esteem; peer risk; no physical punishment"},{"h":"Her concerns and next steps","say":["What are you hoping we can do?","ADHD is a real possibility, but I'll need the school's view and to see Callum before deciding.","Medicine is never automatic; we'd decide together."],"hear":"\"So you'll get the school forms and see him. That's a relief.\"","mark":"Concerns addressed; plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["ADHD domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Inattention","10 minutes on homework, loses kit, forgets daily","Marked inattention"],["Hyperactivity and impulsivity","Up and down at meals, shouts out, two A&E visits","Combined presentation likely; injury risk"],["Onset and settings","Since nursery; home, school, football, grandmother's","Childhood onset, pervasive"],["Development and family","Born at 35 weeks; father similar","Two recognised risk factors"],["Differentials and impact","No learning, autism or defiance features; late gaming; low self-esteem","ADHD most likely; sleep and mood need watching"]]},"key":"A parent's story is one setting's view. The excellent doctor gets examples, not labels, screens the look-alikes, asks how home discipline works, and promises a school report and seeing the child before any diagnosis.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-s-36":{"name":"Five things a parent needs about ADHD","why":"Turns a new diagnosis into understanding and a shared home, school and medicine plan.","frame":["What ADHD is","Why it happens","Home","School","Medicine and monitoring"],"steps":[{"h":"Open: what he knows and fears","say":["Thanks for coming back in. What have you made of it all since we last met?","What's the main thing you'd like to leave with today?"],"hear":"\"Honestly? Half the family think it's just a label. And I want to know about these tablets.\"","mark":"Scepticism and medicine worry identified"},{"h":"What ADHD is","say":["ADHD affects three things: keeping attention, controlling activity, and stopping to think before acting.","Every 9-year-old is lively. With Jake it's far more than his age, at home and at school, and it's causing harm, like running into the road.","Does that match the Jake you know?"],"hear":"\"That's him. He doesn't mean it, he's just off before you can stop him.\"","mark":"Core features linked to his son"},{"h":"Why it happens","say":["The parts of the brain that act as brakes develop differently. Genes play the biggest part.","Being born early, or smoking or alcohol in pregnancy, add a little risk. Parenting, sugar and screens don't cause it.","Was anyone in the family like Jake as a boy?"],"hear":"\"Me, if I'm honest. I was always in trouble at school.\"","mark":"Heritability discussed; father's history"},{"h":"Home: parent training","say":["We'll offer you both a parent group for ADHD, about 10 to 12 weekly sessions.","It's not a parenting test. It teaches tricks that work for ADHD brains: short instructions, quick praise, routines."],"hear":"\"Would his mum and I both have to go?\"","mark":"Parent training agreed"},{"h":"School support","say":["With your permission I'll write to the SENCO, the teacher who leads on special needs.","We'll ask for a written support plan: seat near the teacher, short tasks, movement breaks, and a rating form to track progress."],"hear":"\"They've moved his seat already. It's not been enough.\"","mark":"School plan agreed"},{"h":"Medicine: how it helps, checks first","say":["Because he's still struggling and at risk near roads, medicine makes sense. The first choice is methylphenidate.","It boosts the brain's brakes for the school day. It works within an hour, and we find the right dose step by step.","Before starting: has he ever fainted while running or had chest pain? Any sudden deaths in the family under 40?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. Is it addictive?\"","mark":"Cardiac history clear"},{"h":"Side effects and monitoring","say":["Common: smaller appetite at lunch, trouble getting to sleep, headaches or tummy aches. Usually mild.","We check height, weight, pulse and blood pressure at every dose change, then every few months, and review every year.","Taken as prescribed it isn't addictive, but keep it locked away."],"hear":"\"So you'd keep an eye on his growth?\"","mark":"Monitoring explained"},{"h":"Teach-back and close","say":["What will you tell Jake's mum tonight?","Here's a leaflet. If he faints, gets chest pain or seems very low, stop it and call us or 111. I'll see you both in six weeks."],"hear":"\"It's how his brain's wired, not us. A parent group, school plan, and the tablets with checks.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What ADHD is","'He's off before you can stop him'","Impulsivity causing real danger: impairment, not naughtiness"],["Why it happens","'I was the same at school'","Strong family link; offer father a GP route to adult assessment"],["Home","'Do we both have to go?'","Parent group works best with both carers; arrange it"],["School","'They moved his seat; not enough'","Ask SENCO for a written SEN support plan"],["Medicine and monitoring","No fainting, no sudden deaths","Stimulant safe to start with routine pulse, BP and growth checks"]]},"key":"Good explains ADHD. Excellent links every fact to Jake's own life, asks the cardiac questions before naming a dose, and leaves the father able to explain it to the mother himself.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-s-37":{"name":"From the start: building Noah's developmental story","why":"A dated timeline with real examples gives the team the backbone of an autism assessment.","frame":["Birth and milestones","Social communication","Repetitive behaviour and sensory","Course and regression","Differentials and co-occurring","Impact"],"steps":[{"h":"Open: her concern in her words","say":["Tell me what you've noticed about Noah.","I'll go through his story from pregnancy to now, then how he talks, plays and copes with change."],"hear":"\"His brother was chatting at two. Noah just isn't the same.\"","mark":"Concern: language and difference from sibling"},{"h":"Pregnancy, birth, motor milestones","say":["How was the pregnancy? Was he born on time? Any special care after birth?","When did he sit and walk?"],"hear":"\"All normal. He walked at about thirteen months.\"","mark":"Normal birth and motor milestones"},{"h":"Language in order","say":["Did he babble as a baby? When were his first words, and when did he put two words together?","How does he ask for a drink now? Does he ever repeat things from TV?"],"hear":"\"First words were about two and a half. He says lines from his cartoons.\"","mark":"Language delay with echolalia"},{"h":"Social communication and play","say":["If you call his name from across the room, what happens?","Does he point to show you something, just to share it?","How does he play: with other children, or on his own? Any pretend play?"],"hear":"\"He doesn't turn. He pulls my hand to the fridge. He plays on his own, lining things up.\"","mark":"Social communication difference identified"},{"h":"Repetitive behaviour and sensory","say":["What does he love most? What happens if a routine changes?","Is he bothered by noise, clothes or food textures?"],"hear":"\"Trains, all day. Same route to school or he melts down. Hand dryers terrify him.\"","mark":"Restricted, repetitive and sensory features identified"},{"h":"Course, regression, hearing","say":["When did you first sense something was different? Has he ever lost skills or words?","Has his hearing been tested? When?"],"hear":"\"Around two. He's never lost anything, just slow. Hearing was fine last spring.\"","mark":"No regression; hearing normal"},{"h":"Co-occurring and safety","say":["How are sleep and eating? Is he very active or clumsy? Any fits or blank spells?","Does he ever hurt himself when upset?","Anyone in the family with autism, late speech or learning difficulties?"],"hear":"\"He bangs his head in meltdowns. Takes two hours to sleep. His dad's cousin is autistic.\"","mark":"Self-injury and sleep problem; family history"},{"h":"Impact, summary and next steps","say":["How is all this affecting him, you and Alfie?","So: walked on time, talked late, doesn't share attention easily, loves routine and trains, and finds some sounds hard. Have I got that right?","Next, our team assesses him properly with school's input and a speech and language check."],"hear":"\"Yes, that's him. So you'll see him yourselves?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Birth and milestones","Term birth, walked 13 months, words at 2.5 years","Isolated language delay, motor normal"],["Social communication","No name response, leads by hand, no pointing to share, plays alone","Social communication difference across settings"],["Repetitive behaviour and sensory","Lines up cars, trains, same route, hand dryers","Restricted interests, sameness, sensory sensitivity"],["Course and regression","Noticed at 2, no loss of skills, hearing normal","Developmental pattern; deafness and regression unlikely"],["Differentials and co-occurring","Head-banging, poor sleep, family history","Self-injury and sleep need a plan; genetic loading"],["Impact","Meltdowns shape family life; isolated at school","Impairing; supports referral for full assessment"]]},"key":"A list of autism symptoms is not a history. Excellent pins each feature to an age and a real example, asks about loss of skills and hearing, and keeps the diagnosis for the assessment.","objmap":[[2,3],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-s-38":{"name":"Telling a parent: what autism means for Isla","why":"Gives a clear diagnosis, answers why now, screens what else to watch, and turns the label into support.","frame":["What autism is","Why now","What causes it","Co-occurring conditions","Support and next steps"],"steps":[{"h":"Open: consent, knowledge, questions","say":["Isla's happy for me to share everything with you today. What do you already know about autism?","What do you most want to know?"],"hear":"\"Not much. I want to know why it's taken so long, and what happens now.\"","mark":"Questions agreed"},{"h":"The headline and what it means","say":["The assessment shows Isla is autistic.","Her brain works differently in how she reads people and copes with change. She'll also have strengths, like focus and honesty.","How does that sit with you?"],"hear":"\"Honestly? Relief. And a bit sad.\"","mark":"Diagnosis given; emotion named"},{"h":"Why now, and Asperger's","say":["Many girls copy others and hide difficulties. Secondary school friendships are harder to copy.","Asperger's was the old name for autism without language delay; now there's one term, autism."],"hear":"\"My friend's boy was diagnosed at four. That's why I didn't think of it.\"","mark":"Late recognition explained"},{"h":"What causes it","say":["It's mostly genetic, from many genes together. It's not parenting, vaccines or diet.","Is anything like this in the family?"],"hear":"\"Her dad's very like her, now I think of it.\"","mark":"Cause explained; blame lifted"},{"h":"Co-occurring conditions: screen","say":["Autistic young people more often have anxiety, low mood, sleep and eating difficulties. How is Isla with those?","Has she ever talked about hurting herself or wishing you weren't here?"],"hear":"\"She's anxious, she refuses school some Mondays, sleeps late. No self-harm that I know of.\"","mark":"Anxiety, school avoidance and sleep identified"},{"h":"What helps","say":["There's no tablet for autism itself. Medicine can help things alongside it, like sleep or anxiety.","We'll offer CBT adapted for autism for her anxiety, and a sleep plan."],"hear":"\"So no medication for the autism. OK.\"","mark":"No medicine for core autism understood"},{"h":"School and family","say":["With your and Isla's consent, I'll write to the SENCO: a time-out card, a quiet space, warning of changes.","There's a parent group, and you can ask the council for a carer's assessment."],"hear":"\"School would listen if it came from you.\"","mark":"School and family support agreed"},{"h":"Telling Isla, teach-back, close","say":["How would Isla like to hear this, and who should be there?","What will you tell Isla's dad tonight?"],"hear":"\"She'd want to read it herself, then talk. I'll tell him it explains a lot.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What autism is","Relief and sadness","Name both; give strengths alongside difficulties"],["Why now","'Why did it take ten years?'","Masking and rising social demands at secondary school"],["What causes it","'Her dad's very like her'","Genetic loading; no blame"],["Co-occurring conditions","Anxiety, Monday refusal, late sleep","Adapted CBT, sleep plan, school adjustments; keep screening mood and eating"],["Support and next steps","'She'd want to read it herself'","Isla-led feedback; consented SENCO letter; parent group"]]},"key":"Good explains autism. Excellent screens for what comes with it, especially anxiety, mood and eating in girls, and keeps Isla in charge of her own diagnosis.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-s-39":{"name":"Medically fit, not yet safe: agreeing the night plan","why":"Keeps a young person safe overnight while protecting the working relationship with the ED team.","frame":["Medical safety","Risk tonight","Where she stays","Consent and confidentiality","Safeguarding","Tomorrow and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Acknowledge and align","say":["I know you're full and need cubicles. I want to help you with that.","Can I check the medical side first? Dose per kilo, and the 4-hour paracetamol level?"],"hear":"\"About 58 milligrams per kilo. Paracetamol and salicylate both negative.\"","mark":"Medical clearance confirmed"},{"h":"Why not home tonight","say":["She still says she'd be better off dead, she's been low for weeks, and this is her first presentation.","We've nothing yet from home, school or social care, and our senior team aren't in until morning."],"hear":"\"But mum's on her way. She could watch her.\"","mark":"High-risk features named"},{"h":"Where she stays","say":["The right place is a paediatric bed tonight, with her mum.","An adult psych ward isn't safe or lawful for a 15-year-old, and she doesn't need a mental health bed now."],"hear":"\"OK. I can get her a bed on the observation unit.\"","mark":"Paediatric bed agreed"},{"h":"Keeping her safe overnight","say":["Can we agree observations: within eyesight when alone, at least every 15 minutes when settled with mum?","Room cleared of cords and medicines; belongings checked with her agreement."],"hear":"\"Fine. Mum can stay on the chair-bed.\"","mark":"Observation level agreed"},{"h":"Consent and leaving","say":["If she wants to leave, please keep her safe and bleep me. I'll check whether she understands the decision.","If she's competent and still refuses, I'll get the on-call CAMHS consultant. We don't just override her with mum's consent."],"hear":"\"And what do we tell mum?\"","mark":"Gillick plan agreed"},{"h":"Safeguarding and confidentiality","say":["Mum will be told about the overdose and the safety plan; Chloe will know what's shared.","Has CP-IS been checked? I'll tell the safeguarding team in the morning."],"hear":"\"Not known to social care. I'll note it.\"","mark":"Safeguarding checks done"},{"h":"Tomorrow and follow-up","say":["Our liaison team will see Chloe and her mum, and speak to school and the GP.","Most go home with a safety plan and CAMHS follow-up within 7 days. If risk stays high or home isn't safe, we look at CAMHS inpatient care."],"hear":"\"So likely home tomorrow with follow-up.\"","mark":"Likely outcome explained"},{"h":"Summary and escalation","say":["To recap: paediatric bed, mum staying, observations as agreed, call me if anything changes.","I'll write it up now. My bleep is 3361."],"hear":"\"Got it. Thanks for being straight with me.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Medical safety","58 mg/kg, paracetamol negative","No medical reason to stay; risk is psychiatric"],["Risk tonight","'Better off dead', weeks of low mood, first presentation","Not safe to discharge without collateral and senior review"],["Where she stays","'Can she go to your psych ward?'","Paediatric bed; s131A age-appropriate care"],["Consent and confidentiality","'What if she walks out?'","Gillick competence; senior input; no override via mother"],["Safeguarding","Alone at home, CP-IS not yet checked","Check, document, inform safeguarding team"],["Tomorrow and follow-up","'How do you decide?'","Morning psychosocial assessment; follow-up within 7 days"]]},"key":"Good knows the guideline. Excellent hears the nurse's pressure first, gives a plan that makes his night easier, and leaves clear written instructions for every likely problem before 08:00.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6],[7]]},"c-s-40":{"name":"Is this psychosis, and is anything else causing it?","why":"Establishes psychotic symptoms safely while excluding organic and drug causes in a young person.","frame":["Delusions and conviction","Perception and first-rank symptoms","Form of thought","Organic red flags","Substances","Mood, background and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Open: safety and his concern","say":["This is a private room. I'm one of the doctors, here to help.","Tell me about the headache, and what you think is causing it."],"hear":"\"It's the device. They put it in my head while I was asleep.\"","mark":"Delusional explanation of headache"},{"h":"The belief and its conviction","say":["Who put it there, and why you?","How did you first realise? How sure are you, out of 100?","Could there be any other explanation?"],"hear":"\"The government. I've known for months. A hundred percent. There's no other way.\"","mark":"Persecutory delusion, fixed conviction"},{"h":"Voices and thought interference","say":["Do you ever hear voices when nobody's there? What do they say? To you or about you?","Can people know your thoughts? Put thoughts in or take them out? Control your body?"],"hear":"\"They talk about me, say I'm watched. The device sends my thoughts out.\"","mark":"Third-person voices; thought broadcast"},{"h":"Form of thought","say":["I don't know that word, 'zintered'. What does it mean?","(Note: glancing behind, muttering.) Are you hearing something now?"],"hear":"\"It's when the signal goes into you. Obviously.\"","mark":"Neologism; responding to voices"},{"h":"Organic screen","say":["Is the headache worse lying down or in the morning? Any vomiting, blurred vision, weakness?","Any head injury, fits, fever, or new medicines?"],"hear":"\"No. Just a dull ache since yesterday.\"","mark":"No organic red flags"},{"h":"Substances","say":["What have you used: cannabis, skunk, spice, vapes, cocaine, ketamine, alcohol?","When did you last use?"],"hear":"\"Weed at weekends, but I stopped about four months ago.\"","mark":"Cannabis stopped 4 months ago"},{"h":"Mood, prodrome, background","say":["How's your mood and sleep? Any times you've felt very high or full of energy?","When did things change at college? Any problems learning to talk or walk as a child?","Anyone in the family had problems like this?"],"hear":"\"Scared, not sleeping. Stopped college three weeks ago. My dad was in hospital once, paranoid.\"","mark":"Prodrome; paternal family history"},{"h":"Risk, insight, next steps","say":["Have you thought of doing anything about the people tracking you? Do you carry anything to protect yourself?","Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","Could stress or strain on the brain be part of this? I'd like blood tests and to bring in a team for young people with these experiences."],"hear":"\"No. I just want it out. OK, if my mum's there.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Delusions and conviction","Government implanted a device; 100% sure","Persecutory delusion with bizarre content"],["Perception and first-rank symptoms","Voices about him; thoughts broadcast","Third-person voices and thought broadcast: first-rank symptoms"],["Form of thought","'Zintered'","Neologism: formal thought disorder"],["Organic red flags","Dull ache, no red flags, normal neuro","Organic cause less likely; bloods still needed"],["Substances","Cannabis stopped 4 months ago","Drug-induced psychosis less likely; screen anyway"],["Mood, background and risk","Prodrome, father's illness, no intent to harm","First-episode psychosis likely; EIP today"]]},"key":"Good finds the delusion. Excellent tests conviction without arguing, asks every first-rank symptom, screens headache red flags and synthetic drugs, and asks whether he would act against his persecutors.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-s-41":{"name":"Explaining psychosis and the law to a frightened father","why":"Turns a shocked parent into an informed ally who understands the illness, the plan and why the law is being used.","frame":["What psychosis is","Cause and tests","Treatment and early intervention","Section 2 and rights","Outlook and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent, safety and his story","say":["I'm Dr Hassan, one of the child and adolescent psychiatrists. Josh is safe and settled with a nurse, and he's fine with me talking to you.","Before I explain, what have you noticed in Josh over the last few months?","And what's worrying you most right now?"],"hear":"\"He's been in his room for months, muttering. I thought it was just being a teenager. Why's a psychiatrist involved?\"","mark":"Prodrome identified"},{"h":"Tests done, then name it","say":["The medical team checked bloods, a drug screen, a heart tracing and a brain scan. All normal.","That tells us this is coming from his mental health. The name for it is psychosis.","Is it all right if I explain what that means?"],"hear":"\"Psychosis? Are you saying he's mad?\"","mark":"Organic causes excluded and explained"},{"h":"Psychosis in his own symptoms","say":["No. He's unwell, and it's treatable. Psychosis means the mind struggles to tell what's real.","For Josh, that's hearing voices no one else hears and believing classmates are filming him through his phone.","Can you tell me back, in your words, what psychosis is?"],"hear":"\"So his brain's playing tricks on him and he believes it.\"","mark":"Diagnosis understood"},{"h":"Cause and danger, honestly","say":["There's rarely one cause: genes, stress and sometimes cannabis can all add up. Nothing you did caused this.","Is he dangerous? Mostly he's frightened, and the main risk tonight is to himself, like stepping into traffic."],"hear":"\"He did smoke weed last year. And my mum's sister was never right.\"","mark":"Risk factors identified"},{"h":"Treatment and early intervention","say":["On the ward we start a low-dose medicine called an antipsychotic, increase slowly and check side effects weekly.","We'll also offer talking therapy for Josh and sessions for the whole family.","An early intervention team will stay with him for up to three years after he leaves hospital."],"hear":"\"I don't want him like a zombie.\"","mark":"Treatment plan accepted in principle"},{"h":"Section 2 and his rights","say":["Josh doesn't think he's ill and won't stay. At 16, your consent can't lawfully keep him in, so we use the Mental Health Act.","Section 2 lasts up to 28 days. A specialist social worker and two doctors decide together, and they'll talk to you.","He can appeal within 14 days, he gets a free advocate, and as his nearest relative you can ask for discharge."],"hear":"\"So it's not for ever? And I get a say?\"","mark":"Legal framework understood"},{"h":"Teach-back, support and close","say":["What will you tell Josh's mum tonight?","Here are leaflets on psychosis and Section 2, and the ward number. Ring any time.","The consultant will call you both tomorrow at nine and arrange a family meeting this week."],"hear":"\"Psychosis, treatable, 28 days not for ever, and I can appeal with him. I'll ring his mum now.\"","mark":"Understanding confirmed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What psychosis is","Voices, belief he is filmed, muttering for months","First-episode psychosis with a prodrome; explain in his words"],["Cause and tests","Normal bloods, scan; past cannabis; family history","Organic cause excluded; vulnerability plus triggers"],["Treatment and early intervention","Fears he'll be 'zombified'","Low dose, slow increase, weekly checks; EIP for three years"],["Section 2 and rights","Lacks capacity, refuses, stepped into traffic","Parent cannot consent; Section 2 with appeal, IMHA, nearest relative rights"],["Outlook and support","'Will he be normal again?'","Many recover well; early treatment helps; family meeting booked"]]},"key":"Good doctors explain psychosis. Excellent ones also explain why a parent's consent cannot replace the Act at 16, and give the father his own rights as nearest relative, so the law feels like protection, not punishment.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-s-42":{"name":"Mental state in mania: observe, ask, test, present","why":"Turns a chaotic, overfamiliar interview into a complete mental state and risk picture you can present in 90 seconds.","frame":["Appearance, behaviour and speech","Mood and affect","Thought form and content","Perception and cognition","Insight","Risk to, by and from"],"steps":[{"h":"Alone, confidential, and watching","say":["Hi Maya, I'm Dr Okoro, one of the doctors for young people. Is it okay if we chat on our own for a bit?","What you say stays private, unless I'm worried someone could get hurt. Then I'd tell you first.","So, what's been happening this week?"],"hear":"\"Oh, this week's been epic, electric, eclectic! Doc, you look tired. Late night?\"","mark":"Pressured speech with rhyming noted; overfamiliar"},{"h":"Mood, energy, sleep and appetite","say":["I'll keep the focus on you. How's your mood, from 0 to 10?","How much sleep have you needed? And eating?","Do your thoughts feel faster than usual?"],"hear":"\"Ten! Three hours is loads. Food's boring. My thoughts are like rockets.\"","mark":"Elated mood, reduced need for sleep, racing thoughts"},{"h":"Grandiosity, gently tested","say":["Tell me about the physics theory. What does it mean about you?","How sure are you, from 0 to 100?","I can see that question annoyed you; let's move on."],"hear":"\"It's a revolution. You wouldn't get it. A hundred percent. Stop asking!\"","mark":"Grandiose belief, firmly held; irritability when challenged"},{"h":"Perception, passivity and cognition","say":["Have you been hearing voices or seeing things others couldn't?","Does anyone control your thoughts or put ideas in your head?","Where are we, and what's today's date?"],"hear":"\"No voices. I'm in control. A&E, Saturday the 7th, obviously.\"","mark":"No hallucinations or passivity; oriented"},{"h":"Low mood and risk: self, others, from others","say":["Have you ever felt very low? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","Have you been spending, or met people online you don't know?","Has anyone been hurt when you've felt irritable?"],"hear":"\"Never low. I spent £400 on Mum's card. There's a 19-year-old who wants to meet me. I threw my phone at my sister.\"","mark":"Overspending, exploitation risk and aggression identified"},{"h":"Insight and close with her","say":["Do you think anything might be wrong at the moment?","I'm going to talk to my consultant and your parents. We'll explain the plan before anything happens."],"hear":"\"Nothing's wrong. I'm the best I've ever been.\"","mark":"No insight"},{"h":"Present to the consultant","say":["Maya Rossi, 15, one week of elated mood, three hours' sleep and poor intake.","Pressured speech with rhyming, flight of ideas, grandiose belief held with full conviction, no hallucinations, oriented, no insight.","Risks: overspending, a 19-year-old online asking to meet, threw a phone. Impression: first manic episode; differentials drug or thyroid cause, schizoaffective."],"hear":"\"Good. Let's admit to the adolescent unit and raise safeguarding about the online contact.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["MSE domain","What you heard or saw","What it means"],"rows":[["Appearance, behaviour and speech","Heavy make-up, restless, overfamiliar, rapid rhyming speech","Disinhibition and pressured speech of mania"],["Mood and affect","'Ten out of ten', irritable when challenged","Elated, labile affect"],["Thought form and content","Rockets of ideas; revolutionary theory, 100% sure","Flight of ideas; grandiose belief, possibly delusional"],["Perception and cognition","No voices or visions; oriented","No hallucinations; cognition grossly intact"],["Insight","'Nothing's wrong'","No insight; affects consent to admission"],["Risk to, by and from","£400 spent, phone thrown, 19-year-old online","Financial, aggression and exploitation risk; safeguarding"]]},"key":"Good doctors record elation and grandiosity. Excellent ones also ask about past low mood, suicide and online exploitation, and quote observed signs (the rhymes, the overfamiliarity) when presenting.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4,5],[6],[2,4],[7]]},"c-s-43":{"name":"Explaining a stimulant with learning disability: small chunks, pictures, teach-back","why":"Gives Bethany enough clear information, in her words, to agree to the ECG, the checks and the medicine herself.","frame":["What she knows and wants","The medicine and how we start","Pulse, blood pressure and weight","The ECG","Follow-up and what-ifs"],"steps":[{"h":"Her understanding first","say":["Hello Bethany, I'm Dr Patel. Thanks for coming back. Is it okay to talk on our own, with Dawn outside?","What do you remember about what we found last time?","What's hardest for you at your cooking class?"],"hear":"\"You said I've got ADHD. I can't sit still all day and I forget what the teacher said.\"","mark":"Understanding and goals identified"},{"h":"What the medicine does","say":["There's a medicine that can help your brain focus and help you sit still for longer.","It's called methylphenidate. Lots of people with ADHD take it.","Can you tell me what the medicine is for?"],"hear":"\"To help me focus at cooking. Is it drugs, though?\"","mark":"Purpose understood"},{"h":"Start small, go slowly, then one a day","say":["It's a medicine from doctors. We start with a small tablet at breakfast and lunch.","Each week we might make it a bit bigger, if you're okay.","When we find the right amount, you can have one tablet in the morning that lasts all day."],"hear":"\"So one in the morning would last all my class?\"","mark":"Plan understood"},{"h":"Pulse, blood pressure and weight","say":["Before starting, we check your heartbeat, your blood pressure and your weight.","The medicine can make your heart go a bit faster and make you less hungry.","We check again every six months. If anything goes too high or your weight drops, we change it."],"hear":"\"So you check it's not making my heart go too fast?\"","mark":"Reason for monitoring understood"},{"h":"The ECG, and her fear","say":["We'd also like you to have an ECG. It's a heart picture. Can I show you this picture of one?","Small stickers go on your chest, arms and legs. They just listen. Nothing goes in, and it doesn't hurt.","Your uncle has a heart problem, so we check your heart is safe before you start. Would you like Dawn with you?"],"hear":"\"I don't like wires. But if Dawn comes and it's a lady nurse, okay.\"","mark":"ECG agreed"},{"h":"What-ifs and side effects","say":["You might feel less hungry or find it hard to sleep, or get a headache. Tell Dawn or ring us.","If it doesn't work, we change the amount or try a different medicine.","You take it for as long as it helps, and we check with you every few months."],"hear":"\"What if I get a racing heart?\"","mark":"Side effects and alternatives understood"},{"h":"Teach-back and close","say":["Can you tell me the three things we're doing before you start?","Here's a leaflet with pictures to take home.","The ECG is this week, then I'll ring you, and I'll see you in four weeks."],"hear":"\"Heart picture, blood pressure, weigh me. Then small tablets. Ring you if my heart races.\"","mark":"Understanding confirmed; consent given"}],"table":{"cols":["Part of the plan","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What she knows and wants","Can't sit still, forgets instructions at class","Clear goal to link the medicine to"],["The medicine and how we start","'Is it drugs?'","Explain prescribed medicine; small dose, weekly increase, then once-daily"],["Pulse, blood pressure and weight","'You check it's not making my heart go too fast'","Monitoring understood; repeat every 6 months"],["The ECG","Scared of wires; uncle has heart problem","Concrete explanation plus support person; ECG agreed"],["Follow-up and what-ifs","'What if it doesn't work?'","Dose change or another medicine; phone review and clinic dates"]]},"key":"Good doctors simplify the words. Excellent ones also chunk the information, check each chunk by teach-back, and solve her fear with practical choices (Dawn, a female nurse, a picture) rather than reassurance alone.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5],[6,7]]},"c-s-44":{"name":"Morbid jealousy with his partner: name it, link the past, protect her","why":"Helps a loyal partner see the illness and the risk clearly, accept admission and leave with a plan to keep herself safe.","frame":["What it is","Risk to her","Risk to the rival","Admission and treatment","Her safety and separation"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent and what she has seen","say":["I'm Dr Mensah, one of the psychiatrists. Callum's safe, and he's agreed I can talk to you.","What have you noticed about him over the last few months?","Has he checked your phone, or kept track of when you come home?"],"hear":"\"He's a bit jealous. He reads my messages and writes down my times. He's just protective.\"","mark":"Checking and tracking identified"},{"h":"Ask the hard questions kindly","say":["I have to ask this, and I'm not judging: has anything happened with your colleague?","Has he ever threatened or hurt you? Did you know about the knife?","Do any children live with you, or come to visit?"],"hear":"\"No! I hardly know the man. Callum's never touched me. I saw the knife; he said it's for protection.\"","mark":"No affair; knife known; no children"},{"h":"Name the illness","say":["Callum has an illness called morbid jealousy. His brain has fixed on a false belief that you're unfaithful.","It started from one photo. No proof will change his mind, because the illness is driving it.","Can you tell me in your words what that means?"],"hear":"\"So he really believes it, even though it isn't true. Is it psychosis?\"","mark":"Diagnosis understood"},{"h":"Link the past to now: risk","say":["Yes, it's a kind of psychosis. Six years ago this same belief led him to hurt a partner.","Now he's bought a knife and followed your colleague home. I'm worried for you and for him.","Because your colleague is at risk, we've informed the police."],"hear":"\"That was years ago. He was stressed.\"","mark":"Risk to partner and rival made clear"},{"h":"Why hospital, why now","say":["Tablets alone take weeks to work, and he says he won't take them. Home is where the belief lives.","In hospital we can keep everyone safe, restart the medicine that helped before and assess him properly.","He's refused to come in, so a team is assessing him under the Mental Health Act. He can appeal."],"hear":"\"Can't you just give him his tablets and let me take him home?\"","mark":"Admission explained; pressure to send home met"},{"h":"Treatment, outlook and separation","say":["Many people improve with treatment, but it can come back, so follow-up will be long-term.","Whether you stay together is your decision. If treatment doesn't work, living apart is sometimes the only safe option.","For now, please don't be alone with him while he's this unwell."],"hear":"\"I love him. But I didn't know about any of this.\"","mark":"Separation discussed honestly"},{"h":"Her safety plan and close","say":["Is there somewhere safe you can stay tonight?","Here's the 24-hour domestic abuse helpline, and I'd like to refer you to an adviser who supports partners.","What will you take away from today? The ward will ring you within two days."],"hear":"\"My sister's. It's an illness, he's dangerous right now, hospital's safest, and I can call that line.\"","mark":"Safety plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What it is","One photo, fixed certainty, phone checking","Delusional jealousy; evidence won't shift it"],["Risk to her","Past assault on an ex over the same belief; tracks her","High risk; she minimises it as 'protective'"],["Risk to the rival","Followed colleague home; flick knife","Third-party risk; police informed"],["Admission and treatment","Refuses help; risperidone worked before","MHA assessment; restart antipsychotic in hospital"],["Her safety and separation","'Should I leave him?'","Her choice; no time alone with him now; helpline, IDVA, safe place tonight"]]},"key":"Good doctors explain the diagnosis. Excellent ones hold firm, kindly, when she pushes to take him home, link today's knife to the past assault, and leave her with a concrete safety plan, not just reassurance.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,6],[4],[5],[7]]},"c-s-45":{"name":"Presenting erotomania: structure, diagnosis, risk, plan","why":"Lets the consultant grasp the case and the risk in 2 minutes and agree a safe plan for tonight and beyond.","frame":["Summary and risk headline","Diagnosis and features","Differentials","Immediate management","Long-term management"],"steps":[{"h":"One-line opener with the risk","say":["Dr Mensah, it's Dr Ali in A&E. Can I present a new patient with significant risk to others?","Rachel Doyle, 38, dental nurse, brought by police after being found in her employer's garden with a kitchen knife."],"hear":"\"Go ahead. Keep it brief.\"","mark":"Headline risk given"},{"h":"History, MSE and risk","say":["Seven months' fixed belief that Mr Hale, her employer, secretly loves her, since he praised her at a party. Over 200 texts, four home visits, police warning, suspended.","She thinks his wife intercepts his replies; the knife was 'in case his wife attacks me'.","MSE: euthymic, no thought disorder or hallucinations, grossly intact cognition, no insight. Hypothyroid on levothyroxine; weekend wine; no psychiatric or forensic history."],"hear":"\"What other information would you want?\"","mark":"History and MSE presented"},{"h":"Extra sources","say":["Police records and the warning, GP summary, her sister, and her workplace through the police.","Tonight I've sent thyroid tests, routine bloods and a drug screen."],"hear":"\"Good. What do you think is going on?\"","mark":"Collateral sources listed"},{"h":"Diagnosis with reasons, then differentials","say":["Delusional disorder, erotomanic type, de Clérambault's.","Fixed belief over three months, higher-status object, rejection reinterpreted, stalking with a weapon, no insight.","Differentials: schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, substance-induced psychosis, and organic causes such as thyroid disease."],"hear":"\"Why not schizophrenia?\"","mark":"Erotomania identified"},{"h":"Immediate plan","say":["She's medically cleared pending thyroid tests and not intoxicated. She's on 1:1 with security, and police have the knife.","I'd offer informal admission; if she refuses, request an MHA assessment for Section 2.","A general adult ward should be fine if she's settled; PICU if she tries to leave or becomes agitated. Forensic advice in the morning."],"hear":"\"She's polite and calm. Why not discharge to the CMHT?\"","mark":"Immediate plan proposed"},{"h":"Long-term plan and risk","say":["Bio: an antipsychotic such as risperidone, monitoring, and cutting alcohol.","Psych: stalking risk assessment and HCR-20, CBT once engaged. Social: no contact, police and a Stalking Protection Order, work and finances.","Police informed to protect Mr Hale and his wife; they're told before any leave or discharge."],"hear":"\"Good. Anything else?\"","mark":"Long-term plan proposed"},{"h":"Confirm and close","say":["So: offer admission, MHA assessment if she refuses, police informed, bloods, forensic advice in the morning.","I'll document it and ring you if anything changes."],"hear":"\"Agreed. Call me after the assessment.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Summary and risk headline","Employer's garden at night with a knife","Lead with the risk; it sets the urgency"],["Diagnosis and features","Seven months, higher-status object, rejection reinterpreted, stalking, no insight","Delusional disorder, erotomanic type"],["Differentials","Normal mood, no hallucinations; hypothyroid; weekend wine","Schizophrenia, bipolar, substance-induced, thyroid: exclude"],["Immediate management","Calm, refuses help, knife held","1:1, informal admission offered, MHA assessment if refused"],["Long-term management","Wife blamed as the obstacle; job suspended","Antipsychotic, stalking risk tools, police and SPO, work and money support"]]},"key":"Good doctors say 'delusional disorder'. Excellent ones name the erotomanic type, list the features that prove it, and put the wife, as the perceived rival, at the centre of the risk plan.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6],[7]]},"c-s-46":{"name":"From the compass to the fire: psychopathology, then the ABC of fire-setting","why":"Shows exactly which psychotic experiences drove a planned fire, so the repeat risk and the safety plan rest on evidence.","frame":["Delusional perception and mission","Voices and commands","Passivity of thought","Mood and insight","Fire: antecedents, behaviour, consequences","Other risks"],"steps":[{"h":"Check he is safe, then open wide","say":["Ryan, I'm Dr Okafor, one of the ward doctors. Are you hurt at all? Any trouble breathing?","I'd like to hear how things have been for you lately. Is that OK?","What was going on before your parents brought you in?"],"hear":"\"I'm fine. Mum and Dad panicked because I was out all night, looking for where to start.\"","mark":"No injury; story opened"},{"h":"Find the moment it began","say":["When did things first start to feel different?","What happened when you found the compass? What did you realise?","How did your thinking change after that, and what did you do?"],"hear":"\"The needle swung round and pointed at me. I just knew I'd been chosen.\"","mark":"Delusional perception identified"},{"h":"The mission and other beliefs","say":["What is it you've been chosen to do?","How would you go about it?","Do you get messages or signs meant just for you?","Is anyone working against you?"],"hear":"\"Switch off the phone masts so people can think again. The radio adverts tell me which ones. Nobody's against me.\"","mark":"Grandiose and referential delusions; no persecutory ideas"},{"h":"The implant and the voices","say":["How do you know what to do next?","Do you hear anyone when no one else is there? Speaking to you, or about you?","Do they tell you to do things? Do you do what they say?"],"hear":"\"The device in my tooth. A man says 'go now'. Two others talk about me, say I'm too slow. I do what he says.\"","mark":"Implant delusion; 2nd- and 3rd-person voices; obeyed commands identified"},{"h":"Thoughts, mood and insight","say":["Does the device affect your thinking? Do thoughts get put in or taken out?","What thought was put in today?","Can others read your thoughts? Is anything controlling your body or feelings?","How's your mood, sleep and energy? Could this be an illness?"],"hear":"\"It put in 'light the way out' and took the fear away. Mood's fine, sleep's fine. I'm not ill.\"","mark":"Thought insertion and withdrawal linked to fire; not elated; no insight"},{"h":"The fire: ABC","say":["Tell me about the fire. What did you want to happen?","How did you plan it? Where did the lighter come from?","Why that room? Did you want anyone hurt?","Might you light another one? What, if anything, would stop you?"],"hear":"\"Alarm goes, doors open, I walk out. Kept a lighter from the smoking shelter. Paper and paint by the fire door. I'd do it again.\"","mark":"Planned instrumental fire with intent to repeat"},{"h":"Other risks","say":["Would you try to leave the ward any other way?","Would you hurt anyone who got in the way?","Have you had thoughts of hurting yourself?","Would you take medicine to help?"],"hear":"\"Any way I can. I wouldn't hurt anyone, or me. No tablets.\"","mark":"High absconding risk; refuses treatment; no SH or harm intent"},{"h":"Summarise, act, close","say":["Let me check I've got this right. The voices and the thought told you to light the way out, and you'd do it again?","I'm worried someone could get hurt, so staff will stay within sight of you and we'll check for lighters.","I'll speak to your consultant today about treatment. You can see an advocate and appeal your section; the nurses will help."],"hear":"\"I don't like it, but fine.\"","mark":"Safety actions agreed and RC informed"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Delusional perception and mission","Compass needle pointed at him; mission to switch off masts; radio messages","First-rank symptom; grandiose and referential delusions drive his behaviour"],["Voices and commands","Man says 'go now'; two discuss him; he obeys","Third-person and command hallucinations; obedience raises risk"],["Passivity of thought","'Light the way out' inserted, fear removed","Inserted thought directly lowered the barrier to the fire"],["Mood and insight","Mood 'fine', sleep normal; 'not ill'","Mania less likely; no insight, so will not engage voluntarily"],["Fire: ABC","Kept lighter, chose fuel and fire exit, to escape; would repeat","Planned, instrumental, current intent: high imminent risk"],["Other risks","Will abscond any way; refuses medicine; no SH","Absconding and untreated psychosis feed the fire risk"]]},"key":"Naming first-rank symptoms is not enough: link each one to the act. The inserted thought that removed fear and the obeyed command are why this fire will happen again.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,5],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-s-47":{"name":"After the assault: before, during, after, then past, present, future","why":"Pins down the belief that drove the attack and the factors that make another one likely, so the safety plan fits the real risk.","frame":["Safety first","The incident: before, during, after","Mental state","Historical factors","Clinical and situational factors","Immediate measures"],"steps":[{"h":"Make it safe and introduce yourself","say":["Is the other man being looked after? How long was the restraint, and are his obs normal?","Mr Holloway, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors. I'm not police.","I'd like to hear what happened this morning from your side."],"hear":"\"You lot dragged me here. Now you want to talk?\"","mark":"Victim and restraint checked; role clear"},{"h":"The incident in his words","say":["Walk me through what happened in the corridor.","What made you think he was dangerous?","What did you want to happen when you hit him?","Did you have anything in your hands?"],"hear":"\"He was an assassin. I could tell by his voice. It's kill or be killed.\"","mark":"Intent to kill in delusional self-defence; no weapon"},{"h":"The beliefs behind it","say":["Who is behind this? How sure are you?","Is there anyone else here you're worried about?","Why do you think they want to harm you?"],"hear":"\"MI5. I know too much. There are more of them, dressed as patients and staff. The rooms are bugged.\"","mark":"Persecutory delusions with ward staff and patients involved"},{"h":"Perceptions, mood and insight","say":["Have you heard anything when nobody was around?","How's your mood? Any low spirits or feeling on top of the world?","Has anyone said this could be an illness? What do you think?"],"hear":"\"Whispering outside my door all night. It's real, not in my head. I'm scared, not depressed. I'm not ill.\"","mark":"Possible auditory hallucinations; fearful mood; no insight"},{"h":"The past","say":["Have you been in trouble for fighting before? Ever been to prison?","How many times have you been in hospital? Ever on an intensive care ward?","What medicine were you on, and when did you stop?","Any cannabis, alcohol or other drugs?"],"hear":"\"Prison twice, for fights. Loads of admissions, PICU too. Stopped my tablets months ago. Cannabis every day at home.\"","mark":"Historical risk factors identified"},{"h":"Now and next","say":["How do you feel about the man you hit?","If someone came up to you today, what would you do?","Any thoughts of self-harm or of leaving?","Who do you have outside?"],"hear":"\"No regrets. If another one comes near me, I'll finish the job. No, I'm not suicidal. I live alone.\"","mark":"No remorse; intent to repeat; low risk to self"},{"h":"Summarise, agree measures, close","say":["You've been terrified since you came in, and you'd act again to protect yourself. Is that right?","To keep you and others safe, staff will stay close and you'll have a quieter area.","I'd like to offer you a tablet to ease the fear. I'll speak to your consultant today about the safest place for you."],"hear":"\"Fine. Just keep them away from me.\"","mark":"Measures agreed; RC and PICU raised"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Incident","Attacked a greeting patient; intended to kill; no weapon","Delusion-driven, impulsive, serious intent"],["Mental state","MI5 plot, assassins on the ward, whispering, no insight","Active persecutory psychosis targeting people around him"],["Historical","Two prison terms, PICU, stopped medication, daily cannabis, isolated","High baseline risk of violence"],["Clinical and situational","No remorse, would repeat, refusing medicine, busy ward","Risk is current and imminent"],["Self and absconding","No suicidal ideas; resents detention","Low self-harm risk; watch for absconding"]]},"key":"The risk lives in the belief, not the behaviour. A calm patient who still thinks the ward is full of assassins, and would act again, is as dangerous as he was at 10:05.","objmap":[[1],[3,4],[2,6],[5],[7]]},"c-s-48":{"name":"Safe to see him: her understanding, his signature, her plan","why":"Turns a frightened mother's worries into a plan she can follow at each stage of a possible relapse.","frame":["Her understanding","Discharge plan","Relapse signature","Risk factors","Visits and home risks","Crisis plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Consent, purpose and acknowledgement","say":["Mrs Price, I'm Dr Bose, one of Callum's doctors. He's asked us to talk openly with you.","Today is about spotting early signs and keeping you safe when he leaves.","These months must have been very hard, as his mum and as the person he hurt."],"hear":"\"They have. I want to help him, but I'm scared.\"","mark":"Consent confirmed; her dual role acknowledged"},{"h":"Her understanding","say":["What have you been told about Callum's illness?","What do you think the cannabis had to do with it?"],"hear":"\"Schizophrenia. Will it go away? I knew about the weed, I never thought it mattered.\"","mark":"Gaps identified: long-term course and cannabis risk"},{"h":"Her and that night","say":["Only as much as you want to share: how have you been since?","Who supports you?"],"hear":"\"I still jump at noises. My sister's been good.\"","mark":"Trauma symptoms; some support"},{"h":"The discharge plan","say":["He'll live in supported accommodation with staff day and night.","He stays on clozapine with regular blood tests, and a care coordinator visits him weekly at first.","He'll keep seeing the drug service. Anything you'd add?"],"hear":"\"That's more than I expected. What if he stops his tablets?\"","mark":"Discharge plan understood"},{"h":"His relapse signature and risk factors","say":["Last time, what was the first thing you noticed changing?","Then what? And just before that night?","The things that make relapse more likely are stopping clozapine, cannabis, a change in his smoking, and stress."],"hear":"\"Sleeping all day, ignoring my texts. Then watching the windows. Then he wouldn't eat my food.\"","mark":"Early, middle and late signs agreed"},{"h":"Visits and home risks","say":["How are you hoping to see him?","Is there anything at home that could be a weapon? Did his fears ever involve your home?","Some parents feel angry after this. Have you ever thought of harming him?"],"hear":"\"At his place sounds safer. Just kitchen knives. Never, I love him.\"","mark":"Visits at accommodation; no weapons; no harm intent from her"},{"h":"Crisis plan, teach-back, close","say":["Early signs: ring his care coordinator. Worse, or out of hours: the crisis line or 111. If you feel in danger: leave and call 999.","Can you tell me what you'd do if he refused food you'd made?","Is there anything that would stop this plan working for you?"],"hear":"\"I'd leave and ring 999, then you. My phone's always on.\"","mark":"Plan understood and workable"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her understanding","Thought it would go away; underrated cannabis","Needs clear, simple facts about long-term treatment and cannabis"],["Relapse signature","Sleeping late, ignoring texts; watching windows; refusing her food","Early, middle, late stages she can spot"],["Risk factors","Stopping clozapine, cannabis, smoking change, stress","What the team and she watch for"],["Visits and home","Wants visits at his place; kitchen knives only; no anger towards him","Supervised visits first; home visits after review"],["Her health","Startle, poor sleep; sister supports","Trauma symptoms: GP and carer's assessment"],["Crisis plan","Can repeat the three steps","Plan is usable and owned by her"]]},"key":"Ask her what she saw first before you teach anything. Her observations become his signature, and a plan built from her words is one she will actually use.","objmap":[[2],[4],[5],[3,6],[7]]},"c-s-49":{"name":"Teaching DTs at the bedside: what it is, how we know, what we do","why":"Gives the nurse a clear picture of the diagnosis and a plan she can carry out safely on her shift.","frame":["What DTs is","Who gets it and when","How we made the diagnosis","Immediate treatment","Monitoring and nursing care","Escalation and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Set up and find her starting point","say":["Chloe, the medical registrar is on the way. Is someone with Mr Mehta?","Have you looked after anyone in alcohol withdrawal before?","What would be most useful for me to go through?"],"hear":"\"Not really. I've never heard of DTs. What causes it, and how do you know he's got it?\"","mark":"Knowledge gap and priorities identified"},{"h":"What DTs is and who gets it","say":["DTs is the most severe kind of alcohol withdrawal: withdrawal plus delirium.","Alcohol slows the brain down. After years of heavy drinking, the brain revs up to compensate, and when the alcohol stops suddenly it goes into overdrive.","It's most likely after long, heavy drinking, past withdrawal fits or DTs, older age and physical illness."],"hear":"\"So it's like his brain's lost its brakes?\"","mark":"Mechanism and risk factors understood"},{"h":"How we made the diagnosis","say":["Three things together. First, he drinks a bottle of whisky a day, and he's had none for about two days. DTs usually starts two to four days after the last drink.","Second, delirium: he can't hold attention, he's muddled, and he's seeing things that aren't there.","Third, withdrawal signs: tremor, sweating, fast pulse, high blood pressure, slight temperature.","We've also checked for other causes: the head scan is normal, and we're checking sugar, infection, salts and liver."],"hear":"\"Could it be a head injury from the fall?\"","mark":"Diagnostic reasoning and differentials explained"},{"h":"Immediate treatment","say":["Lorazepam is the main treatment; it calms the overdrive. We give it by mouth and repeat it to keep him calm but rousable.","Pabrinex, a high-dose vitamin B drip, protects his brain from Wernicke's. It goes in before or with any glucose.","Drip fluids for dehydration and the muscle damage from lying on the floor, and we'll replace any low salts.","We avoid haloperidol on its own, because it can make fits more likely."],"hear":"\"So no haloperidol, even though he's hallucinating?\"","mark":"Benzodiazepine, thiamine and fluids plan understood"},{"h":"Monitoring and nursing care","say":["Obs and NEWS2 at least hourly, plus how sleepy he is, fluid balance and blood sugar.","A quiet side room by the nurses' desk, lights on, a clock he can see, the same faces, and someone with him.","We'll book a Gujarati interpreter, in person or by phone, so he understands what's happening."],"hear":"\"Can I use the CIWA score?\"","mark":"Monitoring and environment agreed"},{"h":"Escalation triggers and the risks of missing it","say":["Untreated, DTs can cause fits, heart rhythm problems, choking and death.","Call the medical team at once if he fits, becomes very drowsy, or his NEWS2 is 5 or more.","If he fits: recovery position, protect his head, time it, emergency call."],"hear":"\"OK. Fit, drowsy, NEWS2 five. I'll call.\"","mark":"Escalation triggers clear"},{"h":"Longer term and check understanding","say":["Once he's clear, the alcohol care team will see him about stopping drinking and support at home.","Can you talk me through what you'll do over the next few hours?","Any questions? You can bleep me any time today."],"hear":"\"Lorazepam as charted, Pabrinex first, fluids, hourly obs, side room, interpreter, call if he fits or gets drowsy.\"","mark":"Plan repeated back accurately"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Drinking and timing","Bottle of whisky a day; ~2 days without","Fits the usual window for DTs"],["Delirium","Inattentive, disorientated, picking at the air","Acute confusion with visual hallucinations"],["Withdrawal signs","Tremor, sweating, HR 112, BP 156/94, T 37.7","Autonomic overactivity"],["Differentials","CT normal; raised CK, GGT; dehydrated","Head injury unlikely; rhabdomyolysis and liver strain to manage"],["Treatment","Lorazepam, Pabrinex, fluids","Treats withdrawal, protects brain and kidneys"],["Escalation","Fit, oversedation, NEWS2 5 or more","Medical team now; consider HDU"]]},"key":"A good explanation names DTs; an excellent one gives the nurse what to do at 3 a.m.: the triggers that mean call now, and why CIWA won't work while he is delirious.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7]]},"c-s-50":{"name":"After the G coma: last night, the pattern, the body, the risks","why":"Separates a one-off accident from dependence with dangerous withdrawal, and catches the time-critical sexual health risks.","frame":["Last night","GBL pattern and dependence","Withdrawal","Other drugs and injecting","Intent and mood","Chemsex and sexual health"],"steps":[{"h":"Set the tone","say":["Tom, I'm Dr Adeyemi from the mental health team. We see everyone after a serious overdose; it's routine.","What you tell me stays within the health team unless someone's at serious risk.","I see a lot of people after G. Nothing will shock me."],"hear":"\"I'm so sorry for taking up a bed. It was just a stupid night.\"","mark":"Rapport and confidentiality set"},{"h":"Last night","say":["What do you remember about last night?","What did you take, and what did you drink with it?","Did you mean to take that much?"],"hear":"\"A party. G in my drink, a few beers. Then nothing. I didn't mean it.\"","mark":"GBL with alcohol; says unintended"},{"h":"The pattern and dependence","say":["When did you start using G? How has that changed?","How often now, and how much each time?","Do you need more for the same effect? Do you use it to sleep or first thing?","Have you ever passed out on it before?"],"hear":"\"Five years. Every couple of hours at weekends. And a bit most nights for months, to sleep. I've gone under twice before.\"","mark":"Tolerance and round-the-clock dosing: dependence identified"},{"h":"Withdrawal now","say":["What happens if you miss your night dose?","How do you feel right now? Shaky, sweaty, panicky?","When was your last dose?"],"hear":"\"Shaky, sweaty, can't sleep. Like now. Last dose about midnight.\"","mark":"Early GBL withdrawal identified; escalate"},{"h":"Other drugs and injecting","say":["What else do you use, at parties or other times?","Do you ever inject? Do you always use new equipment?","How much do you drink in a usual week?"],"hear":"\"Crystal once a month. I inject it. New needles, but I shared a spoon once.\"","mark":"Methamphetamine injecting; equipment sharing"},{"h":"Mood and intent","say":["How's your mood been, especially after weekends?","Have you ever wished you wouldn't wake up, or thought about killing yourself?","What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"Low on Tuesdays after a session. But never suicidal. My job, my sister.\"","mark":"No suicidal intent; comedown low mood"},{"h":"Chemsex and sexual health","say":["Was last night sex as well as drugs? Did you use condoms?","When were you last tested for HIV and hepatitis? Are you on PrEP?","Has anything ever happened at a party you didn't agree to?"],"hear":"\"No condoms. Never tested, I'm scared. I can't remember the end of the night.\"","mark":"Condomless sex, untested, possible non-consensual sex: PEP and SARC needed"},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["Your shakes are early withdrawal, so the medical team will start treatment now.","I'd like you seen by sexual health today for PEP and tests. There's also a chemsex support service I can refer you to.","Can you tell me back the main things we've agreed?"],"hear":"\"Treatment for the shakes, sexual health today, and the support service.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Last night","G in drink plus beers, collapse, says unintended","Accidental overdose from combining depressants"],["Pattern and dependence","Every 2 hours at weekends; nightly to sleep; tolerance; two previous collapses","GBL dependence with repeated overdose"],["Withdrawal","Shaky, sweaty, HR 104, last dose midnight","Early withdrawal: treat now, can escalate fast"],["Other drugs","Monthly injected methamphetamine; shared spoon","BBV risk; needle exchange"],["Intent and mood","No suicidal thoughts; low after weekends","Low suicide risk; monitor comedown mood"],["Sexual health","Condomless sex, never tested, memory gap","PEP today, BBV screen, SARC information"]]},"key":"The overdose is yesterday's emergency; the withdrawal and the PEP window are today's. Ask about night-time dosing and condoms, because both have a clock running.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,8],[2,5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-s-51":{"name":"Which came first? Mood, drink and the timeline","why":"Separates alcohol dependence from an independent depressive episode with a dated timeline, while catching the risks that cannot wait.","frame":["Depressive syndrome","Drinking pattern in units","Dependence features","Withdrawal and its complications","Timeline: which came first","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Open with his story","say":["I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatrists. Your GP asked me to see you because your mood has been low. How have things been for you?","You were hoping for antidepressants. I want to understand the whole picture first, so we get it right."],"hear":"\"I'm just flat. Nothing's worth doing. I thought I'd get tablets.\""},{"h":"The depressive syndrome","say":["When did you last feel like your usual self?","What do you still enjoy? How are sleep, appetite and energy?","Do you blame yourself for things? How is your concentration?"],"hear":"\"Six weeks maybe. I don't enjoy anything. I sleep badly, I'm not eating, I'm shattered.\"","mark":"Depressive syndrome for six weeks"},{"h":"Drinking in numbers","say":["Talk me through yesterday from when you woke, and every drink.","What do you drink, and what strength? How many on a usual day?","Where do you drink, and with whom?"],"hear":"\"Four to six pints of lager, at home in the day, then the pub at night. On my own mostly.\"","mark":"About 80 units a week"},{"h":"Dependence features","say":["How strong is the urge? Can you stop once you start?","Does it take more drink these days to feel it?","What's dropped away because of drinking? You were in hospital with your pancreas; did that change it?"],"hear":"\"I'm craving every couple of hours. Once I start I can't stop. Even after the pancreas I carried on.\"","mark":"Alcohol dependence identified"},{"h":"Withdrawal and complications","say":["What happens if you go a morning without a drink?","Have you tried to stop? What happened?","Have you ever had a fit, been confused, or seen things others couldn't when stopping?"],"hear":"\"Shakes and the runs within hours. Two days off once, I was confused and saw things on the walls.\"","mark":"Complicated withdrawal identified"},{"h":"Which came first","say":["When did the drinking become daily, and when did your mood drop?","Since it got heavy, have you had any sober weeks? How was your mood then?","Before drinking, any depression, anxiety, or hearing or seeing things? Anyone in the family with mental illness or drink problems?"],"hear":"\"It got heavy months ago, before the low mood. Never been sober since. My dad and nan drank.\"","mark":"Drinking preceded low mood"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Do you ever think about ending your life? Have you thought how?","How likely are you to act on it? What keeps you going?","Has anything risky happened while drunk, like cooking, driving or falls?"],"hear":"\"I think about getting drunk and going in the river. Only hope is getting my wife back. I started a pan fire once.\"","mark":"High suicide risk identified"},{"h":"Name it and close","say":["I think the drink has a hold on you: that's alcohol dependence. Heavy drinking itself causes this kind of low mood.","Once you're safely off alcohol for 3 to 4 weeks, we check your mood again. Today, I want to get you safe and stopping safely.","So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up today's plan?"],"hear":"\"So the beer might be what's making me low. I'll see the crisis team today.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Depressive syndrome","Six weeks of low mood, anhedonia, poor sleep and appetite, fatigue","Meets depressive symptoms, but cause not yet clear"],["Drinking pattern","4-6 pints daily, alone, day and night; about 80 units a week","Heavy, solitary, continuous drinking"],["Dependence features","Craving, loss of control, tolerance, carried on after pancreatitis","Alcohol dependence (ICD-11)"],["Withdrawal","Morning shakes and diarrhoea; confusion and visions after 2 days off","Complicated withdrawal: supervised, likely inpatient"],["Timeline","Heavy drinking first; no sober period since","Probable alcohol-related depressive symptoms; reassess when sober"],["Risk","River plan when drunk, pan fire, fragile protective factor","High risk: same-day crisis input"]]},"key":"The diagnosis turns on the timeline, not the symptom count. Depressive symptoms that began after heavy drinking, with no sober period since, cannot be called an independent depression until reassessed after 3 to 4 weeks of abstinence.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5],[6],[7],[8]]},"c-s-52":{"name":"Look, walk, speak, eyes, arms, legs: then act","why":"Covers every cerebellar sign in a fixed order on both sides, keeps a forgetful, unsteady man safe, and turns the findings into same-day action.","frame":["Posture and gait","Romberg's test","Speech","Eye movements","Upper limb coordination","Lower limb coordination and reflexes"],"steps":[{"h":"Prepare and introduce","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors. Can I check your full name and date of birth?","I'd like to check your balance and coordination. It won't hurt. Is that alright? Sam will stay with us.","Any pain anywhere before we start?"],"hear":"\"I'm only here to drop this letter off. But go on, I don't mind.\"","mark":"Confabulation noted"},{"h":"Look first","say":["Is that your stick? Do you use it all the time?","Can you sit forward on the chair without holding on, arms folded?"],"hear":"He sways to the side when sitting unsupported.","mark":"Truncal ataxia noted"},{"h":"Gait and Romberg","say":["Please walk to the door and back at your normal pace. I'll walk beside you.","Now heel to toe, like on a tightrope. Watch me first.","Feet together, arms by your sides. I'm here. Now close your eyes."],"hear":"Wide-based, short steps; cannot heel-toe; unsteady with eyes open, worse with eyes closed.","mark":"Ataxic gait identified"},{"h":"Speech and eyes","say":["Please say after me: British constitution. And: baby hippopotamus.","Keep your head still; just your eyes follow my finger. Say if it goes double."],"hear":"Slightly slurred speech; jerky beats of the eyes on looking to each side.","mark":"Gaze-evoked nystagmus identified"},{"h":"Arms","say":["Let your arm go loose and heavy; I'll do the moving.","Pat one palm with the other hand, flipping front and back, fast as you can. Watch me first. Now the other hand.","Finger to your nose, then out to my finger, and keep going. Now swap hands."],"hear":"Tone normal; slightly clumsy alternating movements; mild overshoot on reaching for the finger.","mark":"Mild upper limb ataxia"},{"h":"Legs and reflexes","say":["Let your legs hang loose; I'm going to tap your knees and ankles.","Lying back, put this heel on the other knee and slide it down your shin. Like this. Now the other side."],"hear":"Knee jerks present; ankle jerks absent; heel wobbles off the shin on both sides.","mark":"Lower limb ataxia identified"},{"h":"Close with him","say":["Thank you, that's all the tests. You did really well.","Your balance and eyes show me your body is short of a vitamin called thiamine. A drip today can help stop things getting worse."],"hear":"\"A drip? I've got things to do. But if it helps my legs...\""},{"h":"Interpret and act","say":["Sam, he has a wide-based ataxic gait, leg ataxia worse than arm, new nystagmus and confabulation.","I'm treating this as possible Wernicke's on top of Korsakoff's. He needs IV Pabrinex today; I'm phoning the medical registrar."],"hear":"\"I'll go with him to medical admissions.\"","mark":"Possible Wernicke's encephalopathy"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Posture and gait","Sways seated; wide-based, short steps; cannot heel-toe","Midline (vermis) cerebellar damage"],["Romberg's test","Unsteady eyes open, worse eyes closed","Cerebellar plus position-sense loss (neuropathy)"],["Speech","Mildly slurred","Minor; vermis damage often spares speech"],["Eye movements","Horizontal gaze-evoked nystagmus, new","With ataxia and confusion: possible Wernicke's"],["Upper limbs","Mild dysdiadochokinesis and past-pointing","Arms less affected than legs: typical of alcohol"],["Lower limbs and reflexes","Heel-shin poor both sides; ankle jerks absent","Leg ataxia plus peripheral neuropathy"]]},"key":"Confabulation alone suggests chronic Korsakoff's; new nystagmus, ataxia or confusion alongside it means possible acute Wernicke's, which needs IV thiamine today, not a letter.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4,5,6],[3,5],[7,8]]},"c-s-53":{"name":"What came back, why he stopped, the cannabis, then a plan he owns","why":"Links a relapse to its two causes in under four minutes, then builds a short-term plan he will actually follow.","frame":["Return of symptoms","Stopping the medicine","Cannabis","Risk and support","Medication choice","Cannabis plan and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and find what came back","say":["I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatrists. I'd like to hear what's happened, then plan with you. Is that alright?","When did you first notice something was off? What was it?","Where does the voice seem to come from? How frightening is it?"],"hear":"\"People at work are against me. The parked cars mean something. A voice above my head, for two weeks.\"","mark":"Relapse features identified"},{"h":"Why he stopped","say":["Side effects are a very common reason to stop tablets. What was it for you?","How were you in the weeks after stopping?"],"hear":"\"I couldn't get an erection. I was fine for two weeks, then the worry started.\"","mark":"Sexual side effect identified"},{"h":"The cannabis","say":["How much are you smoking, and since when?","What does it do for you?","Was cannabis around the last two times you were unwell? Any alcohol or other drugs?"],"hear":"\"Three or four joints most nights. It calms me. I was smoking loads both times before.\"","mark":"Cannabis at every episode"},{"h":"Risk and support","say":["Have you thought of hurting anyone, or felt you need to defend yourself?","Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","Who's around you, and how is work going?"],"hear":"\"No. I just keep my head down. My manager asked if I was OK. Mum's nearby.\"","mark":"No harm thoughts; support nearby"},{"h":"Summarise and share a view","say":["So two weeks after stopping, the worry and the voice came back, and you've been smoking to cope. Have I got that right?","I think the illness is returning, and both stopping the tablets and the cannabis have played a part."],"hear":"\"Makes sense. I just want to feel normal again.\""},{"h":"Choose the medicine together","say":["Olanzapine worked twice. Restart it now, then switch to something kinder on sex once you're settled. Or aripiprazole now, which may take longer.","Olanzapine: drowsiness and hunger, so take it at night; we'll watch weight, sugar and cholesterol.","Erection problems have other causes too, so we'll check a hormone called prolactin."],"hear":"\"Olanzapine for now, but not long term.\"","mark":"Medication agreed"},{"h":"Cannabis: advice and help","say":["I'd advise stopping completely. It makes the voice worse and the medicine work less well.","The drug service can help, at your pace. I'll refer now so it's ready."],"hear":"\"I'll see them, but sort the voice first.\""},{"h":"Support, safety net and close","say":["The crisis team can visit at home this week, and your care coordinator weekly. Hospital is an option, but I don't think you need it.","Would a week off work and your mum in the evenings help?","Can you tell me what we've agreed? We'll see you within a week."],"hear":"\"Olanzapine tonight, no weed, crisis team visits, drug service when I'm ready.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Return of symptoms","Colleagues against him, parked cars meaningful, voice above head","Relapse matching past episodes"],["Stopping the medicine","Stopped for erection problems; well 2 weeks then unwell","Relapse timed to stopping; side effect must be solved"],["Cannabis","3-4 joints nightly; present at both past episodes","Worsens psychosis and relapse risk"],["Risk and support","No harm thoughts; mother nearby; still working","Safe to treat at home"],["Medication choice","Olanzapine now, not long term","Restart, then planned switch to aripiprazole"]]},"key":"Restarting the medicine is easy; keeping him on one is the job. A plan that does not answer why he stopped will fail again, so name the side effect and plan the switch on day one.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-s-54":{"name":"Spot it, test it, name it: his thinking errors","why":"Turns his own words into evidence he can weigh, so he sees the distortions himself and understands what CBT would do.","frame":["Personalisation","Minimisation and maximisation","Selective abstraction","Catastrophising and black-and-white thinking","Arbitrary inference"],"steps":[{"h":"Open slowly","say":["I'm Dr Brennan, one of the psychiatrists. Your GP tells me things have been hard since a match. Can you tell me about it?","Take your time; there's no rush."],"hear":"\"(long pause) I messed up. Big match, terrible shot. We lost.\""},{"h":"The match","say":["What went through your mind as you walked off?","How did the rest of the team score that day?"],"hear":"\"It was my fault we lost. I got 22; nobody else got 10.\"","mark":"Personalisation identified"},{"h":"The season","say":["How's your season been overall?","What does your coach say about you?"],"hear":"\"Average of 74, but that doesn't count. Coach says clubs want me, but he's just being nice.\"","mark":"Minimisation and maximisation identified"},{"h":"The future","say":["Where does this leave your cricket?","Picture yourself back at the crease next week: what happens?"],"hear":"\"I'll never be good again. Either you're great or you're nothing. I should retire.\"","mark":"Catastrophising identified"},{"h":"Home","say":["How are things at home?","What makes you feel you're failing as a dad? Has anyone said so?"],"hear":"\"Nobody's said it. I just know. I'm a useless husband and father.\"","mark":"Arbitrary inference identified"},{"h":"Test and screen","say":["How sure are you, out of 100, that you lost the match? You scored more than double anyone else; is it possible it wasn't you?","Have you heard voices or had experiences others found strange?","When you feel like this, do you ever think about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"Ninety-five... maybe sixty now. No voices. No, I wouldn't. My kids.\"","mark":"Belief bends to evidence: not delusional"},{"h":"Explain in plain words","say":["Low mood bends thinking in typical ways. You blamed yourself for a team loss: that's personalisation.","You shrank your average and blew up one shot: minimisation and maximisation; picking out one bit of evidence is selective abstraction.","'Never good again' is catastrophising; great-or-nothing is black-and-white thinking; deciding you're failing your family with no evidence is jumping to conclusions. Does that fit?"],"hear":"\"Yeah. That's me. I do that all the time.\""},{"h":"Hope and next step","say":["This is common in depression and very treatable. CBT helps you catch these thoughts and test them.","Before we meet again, could you jot down one bad moment a day, the thought, and the evidence for and against?"],"hear":"\"I can try that.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Thinking error","What he said","What it means"],"rows":[["Personalisation","\"It was my fault we lost\"","Takes blame for a shared outcome"],["Minimisation and maximisation","\"Average of 74 doesn't count\"; one shot defines him","Shrinks positives, magnifies a single negative"],["Selective abstraction","Focuses on one dismissal, ignores the season","Filters out contrary evidence"],["Catastrophising and black-and-white thinking","\"Never good again\"; \"great or nothing\"","Worst-case, all-or-nothing predictions"],["Arbitrary inference","\"Failing as a dad\" with no evidence","Conclusion without support: depression, not delusion"]]},"key":"Naming distortions is not enough; testing them is the point. If his conviction falls when he weighs his own evidence, it is a distortion of depression; if it does not shift at all, think delusion.","objmap":[[2,3,4,5],[7],[6],[6],[8]]},"c-s-55":{"name":"Ride the wave: the hierarchy, step by step","why":"Explains why exposure works, builds her own ladder of steps, and brings therapy to her door so she can start.","frame":["What she has tried","Risk","The anxiety curve","Graded exposure","Systematic desensitisation","The hierarchy"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and review past treatment","say":["Thank you for letting me visit. I'm Dr Adeyemi; I've taken over your care. It's been a hard year; I'm not here to blame you.","What did you make of the online course? Where did it get stuck?","How's the sertraline? Do you take it every day?"],"hear":"\"The tasks were confusing and I panicked just thinking about them. I take the sertraline religiously; it helped a bit.\"","mark":"Past treatment unsupported and too fast"},{"h":"Current picture","say":["What happens when you try to leave the house?","What can you do with your husband that you can't do alone?"],"hear":"\"I stand at the door shaking, heart pounding, like I'll die. With Mark I can get to the car.\"","mark":"Agoraphobia with panic; safety person"},{"h":"Risk","say":["On the worst mornings, do you ever think about ending your life? Any plans?","Ever tried to harm yourself? How much do you drink?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think they'd be better off. No plans; I'd never do that to Mark. Two glasses of wine a week.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts, no plan"},{"h":"The anxiety curve","say":["Anxiety is like a wave. If you stay put, it rises, peaks and falls by itself, usually within the hour.","When you go back inside, relief comes fast, but your brain learns the door is dangerous. Each time you ride the wave out, the next one is smaller."],"hear":"\"So going back in keeps it going?\""},{"h":"Two ways to climb the ladder","say":["Graded exposure: we list situations from easiest to hardest and you face them in order, staying until the anxiety drops.","Systematic desensitisation is the same ladder, but first you learn a relaxation skill and use it at each step, often starting in your imagination. It's hard to be relaxed and panicky at once.","Never thrown in at the deep end."],"hear":"\"I like the relaxation idea. I'd want that.\""},{"h":"Build her hierarchy","say":["What's the smallest step you could imagine? And the hardest?","Rate each from 0, no anxiety, to 100, the worst."],"hear":"\"Imagining going out, 20. Opening the door, 30. Gate, 55. Corner shop with Mark, 80. Supermarket alone, 100.\"","mark":"Hierarchy agreed"},{"h":"Agree the plan","say":["A therapist will see you here weekly, about an hour, for the first steps; later sessions move outside, and eventually to clinic.","You'd practise daily between sessions; Mark comes for early steps, then steps back.","I'd also like to raise your sertraline to 100 mg. If anxiety ever overwhelms you, slow breathing, phone Mark, or call the number on this plan."],"hear":"\"Here at home? Then yes, I can do that.\""},{"h":"Check and close","say":["Can you tell me how you'd explain the wave to Mark?","I'll refer you today and call in four weeks."],"hear":"\"If I stay, it goes down by itself. Small steps, with relaxation.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What she has tried","Online self-help overwhelming; sertraline 50 mg slight help","Needs therapist-led, smaller steps; dose not optimised"],["Risk","Passive thoughts on bad mornings, no plan, husband protective","Low-moderate; safety plan and review"],["The anxiety curve","Panics at the door, goes back in","Escape keeps the fear alive"],["Graded exposure","Willing if steps are small","Real-life ladder, stay until anxiety falls"],["Systematic desensitisation","Likes the relaxation idea","Relaxation paired with each step, starting in imagination"],["The hierarchy","Imagining going out 20 to supermarket alone 100","Start at 20 to 40; move up when rating halves"]]},"key":"Both methods climb the same ladder; desensitisation adds a relaxation skill paired with each step. Either way, she must stay until anxiety falls: leaving at the peak teaches her brain the danger was real.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-s-56":{"name":"Should she quit? Finding the feeling behind leaving","why":"Turns a request to quit into a shared understanding she can take back to her therapist tomorrow.","frame":["Practical and service reasons","The therapist's conduct","Feelings in the room","Past relationships","Present relationships","The urge to leave first"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and name the embarrassment","say":["Hello Erin, I'm Dr Siddiqui, one of the psychiatrists. I've read your therapy notes.","You look a bit uncomfortable. Talking about therapy to a stranger at this hour isn't easy.","I won't go over your low mood again tonight. I'd like to understand what's happening in therapy."],"hear":"\"It's mortifying, honestly. I just want someone to tell her I'm done.\""},{"h":"Her reasons, and the practical side","say":["What has made you want to stop now, three months in?","What did you want therapy to help with?","Is anything practical getting in the way: times, travel, work?"],"hear":"\"No, it's easy to get to. I just feel worse, not better.\"","mark":"No practical barrier"},{"h":"The therapist: conduct and trust","say":["Has she done anything that worried you, like being late, cancelling or overstepping?","Has she said she is leaving, or seemed inexperienced?","Do you trust her with what you tell her?"],"hear":"\"No. She's always on time. She's professional. She just doesn't care.\"","mark":"No concern about conduct"},{"h":"The feeling in the room","say":["What is it like sitting with her, especially in the silences?","What do you imagine she is thinking about you then?","That sounds a lonely place to be every week."],"hear":"\"That I'm boring. That she'd rather see someone else. That she's waiting for me to go.\"","mark":"Feels uncared for and about to be dropped"},{"h":"Who else makes her feel this way","say":["Does that feeling remind you of anyone, now or when you were younger?","What happened with your dad when he left?","And at the moment: your partner, work?"],"hear":"\"Dad moved to Spain when I was seven. And I keep thinking Jack will get bored of me too.\"","mark":"Abandonment pattern identified"},{"h":"Name the link and explain transference","say":["I wonder if the feeling with your dad and Jack is turning up with your therapist too. What do you think?","There is a name for it: transference. Feelings from an earlier relationship get carried into a new one.","In your kind of therapy it is expected. It often means you've reached something important."],"hear":"\"So I'm leaving her before she can leave me?\"","mark":"Leave-first urge recognised"},{"h":"Her choice, and taking it back","say":["It's your decision whether to carry on.","Could you tell her tomorrow exactly what you've told me, including that you want to stop?","Would you like me to email her tonight so she knows you came in?"],"hear":"\"Yes, email her. I'll try saying it myself.\""},{"h":"Safety net and close","say":["Before you go, any thoughts of hurting yourself tonight?","Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","If things get worse: the therapy service in the day, your GP, or 111 option 2."],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. Go at ten, tell her the truth, and you'll email her.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Practical and service","Easy to attend, no cost or time problem","Not a practical drop-out"],["Therapist's conduct","Punctual, professional, not leaving","No real-world reason to stop"],["Feelings in the room","Silences feel like boredom and rejection","The feeling the therapy can work with"],["Past","Father left when she was 7","Likely source of the expectation of being left"],["Present","Fears partner and manager prefer others","Same pattern, active now"],["Urge to leave first","Wants to quit before she is dropped","Transference being acted out; worth taking back"]]},"key":"Transference is a hypothesis, not a verdict: check the therapist's real conduct first, offer the link tentatively, and send the feeling back to the therapy rather than resolving it yourself.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,5],[6],[7],[8]]},"c-s-57":{"name":"Family therapy for a sceptical father: no blame, clear format, real fit","why":"Explains family therapy in his terms and finds out, without blame, whether this family can use it.","frame":["His view and the home pattern","Parental conflict and safety","What family therapy is","How it runs","Why it may help after CBT","Fit and decision"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and hear his view","say":["Mr Kowalski, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors in the team. Thanks for coming. Maya is with her therapist.","How do you see Maya at the moment, and what do you make of her treatment so far?"],"hear":"\"She's thirteen. I think she's just being a teenager. The CBT hasn't done much.\""},{"h":"The pattern at home, without blame","say":["What happens at home when Maya is low? How do you and her mum each respond?","Maya mentioned the thought diary evening. How was that from your side?","Lots of parents disagree about how to help. That's normal."],"hear":"\"I told her not to worry so much. Her mum went mad at me. It turned into a row.\"","mark":"Conflicting parental responses identified"},{"h":"The separation and safety","say":["How are things between you and Maya's mum at the moment?","When you argue, does it ever get physical, or does anyone feel frightened?","How is her mum herself?"],"hear":"\"We're separating. We haven't told Maya. It's just shouting. Her mum's on antidepressants from the GP.\"","mark":"Hidden separation identified; no violence reported"},{"h":"What family therapy is","say":["Family therapy sees the family as a team where everyone's reactions affect each other.","The therapist asks how each of you notices and responds, so nobody is the problem.","Some teams have colleagues watching on video who share ideas, but only if you agree."],"hear":"\"So you think we're the problem?\""},{"h":"Defuse blame and answer 'why not CBT?'","say":["No. Nobody caused this, and you clearly love her.","CBT gave Maya skills. She says they get undone at home. Family therapy works on that part.","Children usually sense tension, even when nothing is said."],"hear":"\"She did ask me if we're splitting up. I said no.\"","mark":"Father links home tension to Maya"},{"h":"How it runs","say":["Sessions are often every two to three weeks, about an hour, for a few months.","Not everyone has to come every time. Separated parents can come together or apart.","The family therapist will agree the details with you at the first meeting."],"hear":"\"I do twelve-hour shifts. And I'm not sure her mum would sit in a room with me.\""},{"h":"Fit and shared decision","say":["Would you be willing to give it a try?","Could you talk to Maya's mum about it? We'll also ask Maya who she wants there.","We can ask for evening slots."],"hear":"\"I'll give it a go. If it helps her.\"","mark":"Willing to engage"},{"h":"Check and close","say":["Can you tell me in your words what family therapy would involve?","Any other questions?"],"hear":"\"Meetings every few weeks, not always all of us, working on how we handle things at home.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Home pattern","Dad dismisses, Mum defends, row follows","Parents pulling in opposite directions; Maya caught between"],["Parental conflict","Separating, hidden from Maya; shouting only","Unspoken tension she senses; no abuse reported"],["Mother's health","On antidepressants, too low to attend","Needs her own support and an invitation"],["Why not CBT alone","Skills undone at home","Family work targets what CBT cannot reach"],["Fit","Sceptical but willing; shifts","Suitable if flexible; Maya's view to be sought"]]},"key":"Name the family pattern without assigning fault: 'nobody caused this' and 'families are part of recovery' must both be said, or a defensive parent will hear blame and not come.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4,5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-s-58":{"name":"Is CBTp right for him? The 5Ps, then fit","why":"Builds a shared story of why psychosis happened, then tests whether he can use CBT to stay well.","frame":["Presenting problem","Predisposing","Precipitating","Perpetuating","Protective","Suitability and motivation"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and set the aim","say":["Hi Callum, I'm Dr Afolabi, one of the doctors in the EIP team.","The team has recommended CBT for psychosis, a talking therapy. I'd like to understand how things came about, then see if it suits you."],"hear":"\"Sure. I got a bit paranoid at uni, I suppose.\""},{"h":"The episode and now","say":["Can you take me back to when things started to feel wrong?","What happened next, and how long before you felt better?","How are you now: any whispering, feeling watched, low mood or thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"I thought they'd put cameras in the smoke alarm. Heard whispering. I'm fine now.\"","mark":"Presenting problem clear; currently well"},{"h":"Predisposing: birth, family, childhood","say":["Any problems in your mum's pregnancy, or when you were born?","Is there any family history of mental health problems?","What was growing up like? Was there a time someone badly let you down?"],"hear":"\"I was born early, a few weeks in special care. My uncle has schizophrenia. A mate shared my private messages at fifteen.\"","mark":"Predisposing influences identified"},{"h":"Precipitating: what tipped it","say":["What changed in the months before you became unwell?","Were you using cannabis or anything else then? How much?"],"hear":"\"New shared house, deadlines, and I was smoking skunk most days.\"","mark":"Precipitants identified"},{"h":"Perpetuating: what keeps the risk up","say":["How about cannabis now?","How do you feel about the risperidone? Some men notice sexual side effects.","How did it feel being told it was psychosis?"],"hear":"\"Weekends only. The tablets cause problems in bed. I'm not sure it was psychosis.\"","mark":"Perpetuating influences identified"},{"h":"Protective: what keeps him well","say":["What stops you from stopping the tablets?","What matters most to you, and who's in your corner?"],"hear":"\"I'm scared of it coming back. I want to finish my degree. Mum and my sister.\"","mark":"Protective influences identified"},{"h":"Share the formulation","say":["Can I tell you how I'm making sense of it? Tell me if it doesn't fit.","Your uncle's illness and being born early may have made you more sensitive. The betrayal at fifteen left you wary of people.","The move, deadlines and strong cannabis then pushed that wariness into feeling watched."],"hear":"\"I've never linked the school thing before. That fits.\"","mark":"Formulation accepted"},{"h":"Explain CBT for psychosis","say":["CBT looks at how thoughts, feelings, actions and body sensations affect each other.","If you think 'they're filming me', you feel scared, stay in your room, and never get to test it.","With the psychologist you'd map this, test thoughts gently, and build a relapse plan. At least 16 sessions, usually weekly."],"hear":"\"So it's like debugging my own thinking. I'd try that.\""},{"h":"Suitability, decision and close","say":["You're curious, you can see links, and you want to stay well. That makes you a good fit.","Shall I refer you to the psychologist? I'll also ask about the side effects.","Can you tell me in your words what CBT would do?"],"hear":"\"Yes. Help me understand it and spot it early.\"","mark":"Suitable and motivated"}],"table":{"cols":["5P","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Predisposing","Born at 33 weeks; uncle with schizophrenia; messages shared at 15","Biological and psychological vulnerability; lasting mistrust"],["Precipitating","Shared house, deadlines, daily skunk","Stress plus cannabis tipped the balance"],["Perpetuating","Weekend cannabis; doubts diagnosis; sexual side effects","Relapse risk; targets for CBT and medication review"],["Protective","Wants degree; family; takes tablets; fears relapse","Motivation to engage"],["Suitability","Curious; accepts formulation; links thoughts and feelings","Good fit for CBT for psychosis"]]},"key":"The formulation is the bridge: link his old mistrust to the paranoia and he will see what CBT is for. Suitability rests on curiosity and motivation, not on accepting the word psychosis.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5,6],[7],[8],[9]]},"c-s-59":{"name":"Explaining anorexia to a frightened parent: six questions in order","why":"Answers what she needs to know in a sequence she can follow, and leaves her with a role.","frame":["What it is","Why it happens","Physical risks and checks","Treatment","Outlook","How family can help"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, consent and her worries","say":["Mrs Bennett, I'm Dr Rahman, the doctor who saw Chloe last week. She's fine with me talking to you.","What do you already know, and what's worrying you most?"],"hear":"\"Only what I've read online. I'm terrified she'll die.\""},{"h":"What anorexia is, and why Chloe has it","say":["Anorexia is an illness where someone eats far too little and is very frightened of gaining weight.","Chloe's weight is well below healthy. She counts calories, runs to burn it off, and her periods have stopped.","Does that match what you've seen?"],"hear":"\"Yes. She runs even when it's dark and icy.\"","mark":"Diagnosis understood"},{"h":"Why it happens; lifting guilt","say":["There's no single cause. Genes, personality like perfectionism, stress and pressure about looks all play a part.","Starting university, new routines and weight gain worries may have tipped it."],"hear":"\"I had problems with food at sixteen. Did I give her this?\"","mark":"Maternal guilt identified"},{"h":"Respond to guilt, then the physical risks","say":["You didn't cause this. Genes play a small part, but so do many other things.","Low weight strains the heart; her slow pulse and low blood pressure show that. It thins the bones and lowers the infection-fighting white cells.","That's why we'll check her weight, pulse, blood pressure and bloods every week."],"hear":"\"So her heart could stop?\"","mark":"Physical risk understood"},{"h":"Treatment","say":["Most people are treated as outpatients. A dietitian gives a clear meal plan, and a therapist offers a talking therapy for eating disorders.","There isn't a tablet for anorexia itself.","If her checks get worse, a hospital stay may be needed to keep her safe."],"hear":"\"How long does therapy take?\""},{"h":"Outlook","say":["Many people recover. About half recover fully, and many more improve a lot.","Starting early, as Chloe has, and having family support both help."],"hear":"\"That's more hopeful than I thought.\""},{"h":"How she can help","say":["Keep meals regular and calm, and avoid talk of diets, weight or shape.","See the illness as separate from Chloe.","Look after yourself too: Beat's helpline, our carers' group, and a Carer's assessment."],"hear":"\"I'd like to join the group.\"","mark":"Carer role agreed"},{"h":"Check and close","say":["Can you tell me the main points in your words?","Ring us or 111 if she faints, has chest pain or struggles to stand."],"hear":"\"Serious but treatable, weekly checks, meal plan and therapy, and I keep meals calm.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Question","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["What it is","Calorie counting, daily running, no periods, BMI 15","Meets the diagnosis of anorexia nervosa"],["Why","Perfectionist; university move; mother's past eating difficulty","Many causes together; mother not to blame"],["Risks","Pulse 54, BP 92/64, WCC 3.4, ECG normal","Moderate physical risk; weekly monitoring"],["Treatment","Frightened of weight gain but recognises problem","Outpatient meal plan and CBT-ED; admission if worse"],["Help at home","Meals end in tears","Calm, regular meals; carer support"]]},"key":"Fear blocks learning: name her fear and guilt first, then explain in short chunks and check after each. A parent who leaves with a clear job at home helps more than one who leaves with every statistic.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-s-60":{"name":"Weighing the outlook: the illness story, then the person","why":"Sorts her history into poor and favourable signs so the team can judge prognosis and safe discharge.","frame":["Course of the illness","Poor prognostic signs","Favourable prognostic signs","Personality traits","Family and early life","Current mood and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Open gently with a quiet patient","say":["Hello Leah, I'm Dr Novak, one of the doctors. I'd like to understand your journey with food and what has helped you.","Some of this may be difficult to talk about. Take your time, and we can pause any time."],"hear":"\"Okay. I'm not sure where to start, really.\""},{"h":"How it began","say":["Can you take me back to the time eating first became hard?","What was happening in your life at that point?","How did people treat you?"],"hear":"\"About fifteen. I was bigger and kids called me names.\"","mark":"Bullying as precipitant"},{"h":"What she did and didn't do; the delay","say":["What did you start doing with food and exercise?","Did you ever binge, or make yourself sick, or take laxatives or pills?","How long before anyone knew, or you got help?"],"hear":"\"Tiny portions, eating slowly, running every day. Never sick. About three years before help.\"","mark":"Restricting only; long untreated illness"},{"h":"Treatment and recovery","say":["What treatment did you have, and did you finish it?","How have food, weight and exercise been since?","Any alcohol or drugs?"],"hear":"\"Day programme at eighteen, finished it. Stable weight four years. I drink a bit socially.\"","mark":"Completed treatment; sustained recovery"},{"h":"Depression, overdoses, self-harm: then and now","say":["Your notes mention two overdoses. What was happening each time?","Have you hurt yourself in other ways? When did it stop?","How's your mood now? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?"],"hear":"\"I got really depressed twice. I used to cut when I thought people would leave. Not for six years. I'm okay now.\"","mark":"Comorbid depression and self-harm; no current risk"},{"h":"Personality","say":["How would you describe yourself as a person?","How do you react when you think someone might leave you?","Are you someone who needs things just right?"],"hear":"\"Quiet. Perfectionist. Feelings hit me hard. I panic about people leaving.\"","mark":"Perfectionism and fear of abandonment"},{"h":"Family and early life","say":["What was growing up like? How are things with your parents?","Has anyone ever hurt you, physically, sexually or emotionally?"],"hear":"\"They weren't really there emotionally. Nothing like abuse.\"","mark":"Distant parents; no abuse"},{"h":"Strengths and support now","say":["Who is there for you now?","What are you proud of?"],"hear":"\"Two close friends, my psychologist, and I've worked full time for three years.\"","mark":"Current protective supports"},{"h":"Summarise the balance and close","say":["So it was a long, hard road, but you finished treatment and have stayed well for four years, with work, friends and support. Is that fair?","I'll talk to my consultant, and nothing will be decided without you."],"hear":"\"Yes. I'd want a plan in case it comes back.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Course","Onset 15; 3 years untreated; day programme at 18, completed","Adolescent onset (favourable); long delay (poor)"],["Behaviours","Restriction, exercise, body checking; no binge, purge or pills","Restricting type: favourable"],["Comorbidity","Two depressions with overdoses; cutting until 19; EUPD","Poor signs, now historical"],["Personality and family","Perfectionist, intense, fears abandonment; distant parents","Vulnerabilities to watch at times of loss"],["Now","BMI stable 4 years; work; friends; therapist; mood good","Strong current protection; good outlook"]]},"key":"Separate past from present: most of her poor signs are historical, most of her favourable signs are current. Say which side carries more weight now, and still write a relapse plan for times of loss or rejection.","objmap":[[2,3,4],[3,5],[4,8],[6,7],[5,9]]},"c-s-61":{"name":"Lithium and a baby: weigh both risks, offer three routes","why":"Turns a frightening internet fact into an honest comparison of risks, so she chooses a route and leaves with a written plan.","frame":["Her worries and wishes","Her relapse risk","Lithium's risk to the baby","Three options","Monitoring around the birth","Feeding and the written plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and find the fear","say":["Thanks for coming in before making any changes; that was a good call.","What have you read, and what worries you most?","And what are you hoping for from today?"],"hear":"\"That lithium damages the baby's heart. I want to know if I can stay on it.\"","mark":"Main fear identified: heart defect"},{"h":"Pin down her relapse risk","say":["Tell me about the times you were unwell before lithium.","Were you taking the earlier medicines when you relapsed?","Have you ever stopped or missed lithium? What happened?"],"hear":"\"Three times, even on the tablets. Two admissions. Nothing since lithium.\"","mark":"High relapse risk identified"},{"h":"Check now: pregnancy, health, family","say":["Could you be pregnant now, and what contraception are you on?","Any other medicines, ibuprofen, or folic acid?","Has anyone in the family been unwell after a birth?"],"hear":"\"Not pregnant, on the mini pill. Mum had some kind of breakdown after my brother.\"","mark":"Family history of postpartum illness identified"},{"h":"Give the baby's risk honestly","say":["Lithium early in pregnancy slightly raises the chance of a heart problem, more at higher levels.","The well-known one, Ebstein's anomaly, is rare, and most babies exposed have normal hearts.","It doesn't affect fertility. A detailed heart scan in mid-pregnancy would check."],"hear":"\"So it's not as bad as the website said?\"","mark":"Teratogenic risk explained"},{"h":"Give her own risk honestly","say":["The highest risk for you is in the weeks after birth.","Women with bipolar disorder have a high chance of becoming unwell then, including a serious illness called postpartum psychosis.","Stopping medicine raises that risk a lot."],"hear":"\"I couldn't go through hospital again, not with a baby.\"","mark":"Postpartum relapse risk explained"},{"h":"Three options, each with a cost","say":["One: stay on lithium with close monitoring. Two: switch to an antipsychotic before trying, though those didn't hold you before.","Three: taper slowly over at least four weeks and restart after birth, with a high chance of relapse.","Whatever you choose, never stop suddenly."],"hear":"\"Staying on it feels safest for me. What does monitoring mean?\"","mark":"Option chosen: continue lithium"},{"h":"Monitoring, feeding, plan and close","say":["Levels every four weeks, weekly from 36 weeks and within a day of birth; doses change as your body does.","Breastfeeding usually isn't advised on lithium, so let's plan feeding early.","Folic acid from today, a perinatal referral, and if you're pregnant, keep taking it and ring us. Can you tell me the plan back?"],"hear":"\"Folic acid now, don't stop it, blood tests, bottle-feed, ring you if I'm pregnant.\"","mark":"Plan agreed and checked by teach-back"}],"table":{"cols":["Decision factor","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her relapse risk","Three relapses on antipsychotics; five well years on lithium","High risk off lithium; lithium is her proven drug"],["Family history","Mother unwell after a birth","Raises her postpartum risk further; clarify the details"],["Risk to the baby","Fears heart damage","Small, real rise in heart defects; rare Ebstein's; scan in mid-pregnancy"],["Options","Prefers to stay on lithium","Informed choice; monitoring plan and perinatal input needed"],["Feeding","Hoped to breastfeed","Usually not advised on lithium; plan bottle-feeding and protected sleep"]]},"key":"Good doctors list lithium's risks. Excellent ones place them beside her own relapse risk after birth, give three real options and leave the choice with her, in writing.","objmap":[[2],[4,5],[5,6,7],[7],[3,7]]},"c-s-62":{"name":"Valproate to a safer route: risks, rules, then a stepped switch","why":"Moves her off the riskiest mood stabiliser before conception without a relapse, and answers the fears that stop women asking for help.","frame":["Her hopes and fears","Valproate's risks","The safety rules","Relapse risk after birth","The alternatives","The stepped switch"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, skip the history, find the fears","say":["I've read your history, so I won't make you repeat it all.","What are your thoughts about a pregnancy, with your illness and medicines in mind?","What worries you most?"],"hear":"\"Whether I can have a baby at all, and if social services would take it off me.\"","mark":"Fears identified: parenthood and social services"},{"h":"Answer the big fear first","say":["Wanting a family is completely reasonable; your diagnosis doesn't stop that.","Social services wouldn't be involved just because of your illness. If they ever were, it would be to support you.","Does that help?"],"hear":"\"It does. I've been dreading asking.\"","mark":"Parenthood and social services addressed"},{"h":"Valproate's risks in numbers","say":["Valproate is the medicine we worry about most in pregnancy.","About 11 in 100 babies have a major birth defect, such as spina bifida or a heart problem, against 2 to 3 normally.","And 30 to 40 in 100 children have learning or developmental problems."],"hear":"\"I knew it was bad. I didn't know it was that bad.\"","mark":"Teratogenic and developmental risks explained"},{"h":"The rules and the 'never stop suddenly' message","say":["That's why you're on the implant and sign a form every year.","The implant stays until the switch is done and you're settled.","If you ever found you were pregnant on valproate, keep taking it and ring us that day."],"hear":"\"So I mustn't take the implant out yet.\"","mark":"Pregnancy Prevention Programme understood"},{"h":"Her relapse risk and sertraline","say":["The highest risk for you is the weeks after birth: a serious chance of becoming unwell, including postpartum psychosis.","Pregnancy itself isn't usually the riskiest time, but stress can play a part.","Sertraline can carry on; it's well studied and fine for breastfeeding."],"hear":"\"What happens to the baby after birth?\"","mark":"Postpartum risk explained"},{"h":"Alternatives, matched to her history","say":["Olanzapine kept you well before and is often used in pregnancy. The downside is weight.","Lithium and aripiprazole didn't suit you, and lamotrigine alone won't prevent mania.","We'd get the dietitian involved before starting. How do you feel about that?"],"hear":"\"If you help with the weight, I'll try it.\"","mark":"Olanzapine agreed"},{"h":"The stepped switch and close","say":["Olanzapine first, building up over a week or two; then valproate comes down slowly over about two months.","Folic acid 5 mg while you're still on valproate; we'll see you every two weeks.","Can you tell me the plan back, so I know I've been clear?"],"hear":"\"New tablet first, valproate down slowly, implant stays, don't stop anything suddenly.\"","mark":"Switch plan agreed by teach-back"}],"table":{"cols":["Decision factor","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her fears","Can she have a baby; will social services intervene","Answer early and honestly; it frees her to hear the rest"],["Valproate","Knows it is 'bad', not the numbers","Give absolute figures; switch before conception"],["Contraception","Wants the implant out soon","Implant stays until stable on the new medicine alone"],["Alternatives","Olanzapine worked; lithium and aripiprazole failed","Olanzapine is the logical choice, with weight support"],["Relapse risk","Bipolar I with past psychosis","High postpartum risk; plan by 32 weeks"]]},"key":"Good doctors say valproate must go. Excellent ones sequence the switch: new drug up, valproate down slowly, contraception off last, with weight support arranged before the first olanzapine dose.","objmap":[[1],[3,4,7],[5],[6,7],[2]]},"c-s-63":{"name":"Why isn't she better? Rule out, name the drivers, act in three domains","why":"Turns a worried colleague's question into a clear list of what is keeping the depression going and who will do what about it.","frame":["Course so far","What is not the problem","Perpetuating factors","Risk","Biopsychosocial next steps","Plan B"],"steps":[{"h":"Thank, confirm consent, get the summary","say":["Thanks for ringing; I'm glad you did. She's agreed we can share information, so let's go through it.","Can you give me the picture now compared with when she was referred?","Which treatments has she had, at what dose and for how long?"],"hear":"\"Sertraline 200 for eight weeks, CBT finished. She's still flat and low.\"","mark":"Adequate failed trial confirmed"},{"h":"Rule out the usual culprits","say":["Is she taking it every day, as far as you know?","Any alcohol or drugs?","How are things with her husband, family, money and housing?"],"hear":"\"All solid. Her husband's great and her mum's round the corner.\"","mark":"No adherence, substance or social problems"},{"h":"Look for what keeps it going","say":["How does she talk about herself as a mum and a partner?","What does a normal day look like for her now?","She stopped breastfeeding: how does she feel about that?"],"hear":"\"She says she's a rubbish mum. She barely leaves the house. She still gets upset about the feeding.\"","mark":"Negative beliefs, low activity and feeding regret identified"},{"h":"Check risk and the baby","say":["How is she with Isla, and how is Isla doing?","Has she mentioned harming herself or the baby, or anything odd in her thinking?"],"hear":"\"Lovely with her, Isla's thriving. She said no to all that last week.\"","mark":"No current risk to mother or baby reported"},{"h":"Name the drivers and the biological step","say":["Lots is in her favour, so I think her harsh view of herself and how little she's doing are keeping it going.","Sertraline has had a fair trial, so I'd switch, perhaps to mirtazapine or venlafaxine, crossing over gradually.","And I'd like her thyroid and blood count checked."],"hear":"\"So a different tablet, and bloods via the GP?\"","mark":"Switch and physical checks agreed"},{"h":"Psychological and social steps","say":["I'll ask our psychologists about interpersonal therapy or parent-infant work, aimed at those beliefs.","Our OT and nursery nurse can help her get out with Isla, and there are good peer support groups.","Her husband could have a carers' assessment, and Early Help is there if they want it."],"hear":"\"That's much more than I thought there'd be.\"","mark":"Named biopsychosocial plan agreed"},{"h":"Plan B, escalation and close","say":["If that doesn't work: add a second medicine, rethink the diagnosis, ECT or magnetic stimulation, or a mother and baby unit.","Please ring us the same day if risk changes.","Can you read back what you'll tell her and what you'll watch for?"],"hear":"\"Review this week, new tablet, bloods, therapy, OT, group. I'll ring if anything changes.\"","mark":"Escalation agreed and read back"}],"table":{"cols":["Factor","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Treatment so far","Sertraline 200 mg for 8 weeks; CBT completed","Adequate trial failed; switch antidepressant"],["Support and stability","Supportive husband and mother; stable home; no substances","Strong protective factors; not the problem"],["Beliefs","Calls herself a rubbish mum and partner","Key perpetuating factor; target in therapy"],["Activity","Barely leaves the house","Isolation keeps mood low; OT, groups, behavioural activation"],["Feeding","Still upset about stopping breastfeeding","Possible guilt; explore and address in therapy"],["Risk","No harm thoughts; Isla thriving","Continue monitoring; same-day escalation if this changes"]]},"key":"Good doctors say 'change the medicine'. Excellent ones name the factors keeping her stuck, check the thyroid and diagnosis, and give every professional a specific job, including a plan B.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,5],[4,7],[5,6],[7]]},"c-s-64":{"name":"Quetiapine at 15 weeks: separate the drug risk from her own risk","why":"Helps her decide on evidence, not fear, while making sure the real diabetes risk is tested for and managed with obstetrics.","frame":["Her story and worries","Quetiapine's risks","The diabetes question","Alternatives","Postpartum relapse risk","Recommendation and shared plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and hear the last pregnancy","say":["Congratulations. You asked us to look at your medicine; can you tell me how your first pregnancy went?","What worries you most this time?","How are you in yourself at the moment?"],"hear":"\"I stayed well, but I got diabetes and ended up with a caesarean. I don't want that again.\"","mark":"Previous GDM and birth distress identified"},{"h":"Check now: health, sickness, support","say":["How's the sickness, and are you managing the tablets?","Has anyone checked your blood sugar yet?","Who's around to support you and Lily?"],"hear":"\"No sugar test yet. My partner's left and my parents are two hours away.\"","mark":"No early glucose test; limited support identified"},{"h":"Quetiapine's risks honestly","say":["Quetiapine isn't linked with a clear rise in birth defects.","Some babies are unsettled or sleepy for a few days after birth, and the team watches for that.","Breastfeeding is generally fine; we'd watch that the baby isn't too drowsy."],"hear":"\"So it's mainly the diabetes I need to worry about?\"","mark":"Pregnancy and breastfeeding risks explained"},{"h":"The diabetes question","say":["The evidence is mixed: quetiapine may add a little risk, partly through appetite and weight.","The biggest factor is that you've had it before; it comes back in about 1 in 3 or more.","So we test early and repeat at 24 to 28 weeks, and get a dietitian involved now."],"hear":"\"So changing tablets wouldn't make me safe anyway.\"","mark":"GDM risk and screening explained"},{"h":"Alternatives with their costs","say":["Aripiprazole affects weight less, but it's untested for you and can reduce milk supply.","Olanzapine has similar weight effects. Lamotrigine won't prevent mania, and valproate isn't used in pregnancy.","Switching mid-pregnancy also risks a relapse and means two drugs for the baby."],"hear":"\"And if I just came off everything?\"","mark":"Alternatives compared"},{"h":"Her own risk after birth","say":["With bipolar I, the weeks after birth carry a high risk of becoming unwell, including postpartum psychosis.","An effective preventive medicine roughly halves that risk.","Being well matters for you, the baby and Lily."],"hear":"\"I can't be ill with a newborn and Lily on my own.\"","mark":"Postpartum relapse risk explained"},{"h":"Recommend, agree, share with obstetrics","say":["My recommendation is to stay on quetiapine and tackle the diabetes risk head-on. How does that sit with you?","I'll write to the obstetric team today for an early sugar test and diabetes clinic, and ask about a birth choices appointment.","Can you tell me the plan back?"],"hear":"\"Stay on it, early sugar test and again later, dietitian, plan who helps after the birth.\"","mark":"Recommendation agreed; obstetric plan set"}],"table":{"cols":["Decision factor","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Response to quetiapine","Well 4 years and through the first pregnancy","Proven drug for her; switching risks relapse"],["Diabetes history","GDM last time; no test yet this time","High recurrence risk; early OGTT and repeat at 24 to 28 weeks"],["Birth experience","Distressing caesarean at 38 weeks","Birth choices referral; include her wishes in the plan"],["Support","Separated; parents two hours away; toddler","Higher postpartum risk; plan help for the first weeks"],["Feeding","Wants to breastfeed","Compatible with quetiapine; watch for infant sleepiness"]]},"key":"Good doctors list quetiapine's risks. Excellent ones show that previous diabetes, not the drug, is the main driver, give a clear recommendation and get the early glucose test booked through obstetrics.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6],[7],[4,7]]},"c-s-65":{"name":"Six times: obsessions, form, rituals, avoidance, risk","why":"Builds a full picture of a postnatal OCD relapse, separates intrusive thoughts from real risk, and helps her feel safe enough to tell you everything.","frame":["Onset and link to the birth","Obsessions: content and form","Compulsions and rituals","Avoidance","Impact and risk to her and the baby","Other illness"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, normalise, hear the story","say":["Thank you for coming. Lots of new mums have worries like these, so please don't feel embarrassed.","Tell me about your little one, and what's been happening.","When did things change, and how has it been since?"],"hear":"\"Leo's six weeks. It started straight after he was born and it's worse every week.\"","mark":"Postnatal onset, progressive course identified"},{"h":"Past OCD and treatment","say":["Have you had worries like this before Leo?","What treatment helped then?","What do you think has set it off now?"],"hear":"\"OCD since 29. CBT helped. Clomipramine made me sleepy, so sertraline for three years.\"","mark":"Relapse of known OCD identified"},{"h":"The obsessions: content and form","say":["What goes through your mind before you wash the bottle again?","Do these feel like your own thoughts? How sensible do they seem to you?","What happens if you try to push them away?"],"hear":"\"That germs will make him ill. It's my own silly thought, but if I fight it I panic.\"","mark":"Contamination obsession: own, senseless, resisted"},{"h":"Harm thoughts, gently","say":["Some new mums get sudden, horrible thoughts or pictures of the baby being hurt. Has that happened to you?","How do you feel when that comes? Do you ever want to act on it?"],"hear":"\"A picture of dropping him on the stairs. It's horrible. I'd never.\"","mark":"Ego-dystonic harm image; no intent"},{"h":"Every compulsion and the avoidance","say":["Tell me about the washing: how many times, and what happens if it isn't right?","Is there anything else you have to check, count or repeat?","Is there anything you avoid now?"],"hear":"\"Six times each. Now locks and switches too, six times. Dan isn't allowed to make bottles.\"","mark":"Washing, checking, counting and avoidance identified"},{"h":"Impact and the baby's care","say":["How much of the day does it take?","Has it ever delayed a feed or stopped you doing something for Leo?","How are you sleeping and eating, and how are your hands?"],"hear":"\"About three hours. One feed was late. Leo's gaining weight. My hands are raw.\"","mark":"Significant impairment; baby's care intact"},{"h":"Screen, summarise and close","say":["How's your mood been? Any thoughts about ending your life?","Any experiences that seemed strange, like hearing things others don't? Any alcohol?","Can I sum up what I've heard, and then talk about next steps?"],"hear":"\"Anxious, not low. No to the rest. Yes, please.\"","mark":"No depression, psychosis or substance use"}],"table":{"cols":["OCD domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Onset","Worse since birth, six weeks ago; OCD since 29","Postnatal relapse of established OCD"],["Obsessions","Germs will make Leo ill; image of dropping him","Contamination and harm obsessions; ego-dystonic"],["Compulsions","Washes bottles six times; checks locks and switches six times","Washing, checking and counting rituals"],["Avoidance","Partner not allowed to make bottles; avoids visitors","Avoidance maintains anxiety; target in ERP"],["Impact and risk","Three hours a day; one late feed; hands raw; no intent","Significant impairment; indirect risk only; no safeguarding concern"],["Other illness","Anxious, not depressed; no psychosis or substances","OCD is the main diagnosis; monitor mood"]]},"key":"Good doctors document the washing. Excellent ones normalise and then ask directly about harm thoughts, separate them from intent and psychosis, and measure the real impact on the baby's care.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[4,6],[7]]},"c-s-66":{"name":"Obsession or wish? Insight over time","why":"Proves the thoughts are obsessions by tracing how they began and changed, then rules out the three conditions they get mistaken for.","frame":["Obsession","Compulsion","Insight over time","Not a wish","Not psychosis","Not depression"],"steps":[{"h":"Open without judgement","say":["I'm Dr Mensah, one of the psychiatrists. Your GP told me a little.","People bring all sorts of unwanted thoughts here. Nothing you tell me will shock me.","Can you tell me about the thoughts in your words?"],"hear":"\"I keep thinking people believe I'm a paedophile. Then I wonder if they're right.\""},{"h":"The thought now: intrusive, own, resisted","say":["How do these thoughts arrive: do you choose them, or do they push in?","Whose thoughts are they: your own mind's, or put there from outside?","When one comes, what do you do with it?"],"hear":"\"They jump in. They're mine, that's what scares me. I push them away, then go over and over them.\"","mark":"Obsession identified"},{"h":"Onset, course and insight then and now","say":["When did it start, and what was going on in your life?","How often were they at first, and how often now?","Back then, what did you make of them? And how true do they feel today?"],"hear":"\"After my girlfriend left. Once a day, I knew they were stupid. Now it's all day and I'm fifty-fifty.\"","mark":"Insight reduced with severity"},{"h":"Compulsions and impact","say":["Tell me about checking your messages.","Is there anything else you must check or repeat, over and over?","What has all this stopped you doing?"],"hear":"\"Texts five or six times, sockets, the cooker, doors. I'm off work and I don't see anyone.\"","mark":"Compulsions identified"},{"h":"Is it a wish? Ask plainly","say":["I ask this of everyone with these thoughts, and I ask it without judgement.","Have you ever felt sexually drawn to children, had urges to act, or looked at images of children?","Do you ever check your body for a reaction when a child is near?"],"hear":"\"Never. The idea makes me feel sick. I do check myself, and I avoid kids completely.\"","mark":"Ego-dystonic: no interest, urge or behaviour"},{"h":"Psychosis screen","say":["Do you feel people are plotting against you or trying to harm you?","Do you get signs or messages meant only for you, on TV, online or from strangers?","Any voices, or a sense your thoughts are put in, taken out or broadcast?"],"hear":"\"No. It's not signals. It's just in my head.\"","mark":"Psychosis excluded"},{"h":"Mood, risk and the past","say":["How is your mood, and do you still enjoy anything? Sleep, appetite, the future?","When it's this bad, some people start to wish they weren't alive. Has that happened to you?","Has anything like this happened before? Any drink or drugs?"],"hear":"\"Low because of this, but I still enjoy football. Never suicidal. At nineteen I checked the gas for months.\"","mark":"Past episode identified"},{"h":"Name it, explain it from his story, close","say":["I think this is OCD, and a severe episode.","The thoughts push in, they horrify you, you fight them, and at first you knew they were over the top. That's an obsession. When OCD gets severe, the doubt grows until it feels true.","It's very treatable: a therapy called exposure and response prevention, and a tablet. So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up what we've found?"],"hear":"\"So it feels real because it's severe, not because it is. And the checking keeps it going.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Obsession","Own thought, pushes in, pushed away, then ruminated on","Core of OCD"],["Compulsion","Rereads texts, checks sockets, doors, body; avoids children","Neutralising and avoidance keep it going"],["Insight over time","Absurd at first, now '50:50'","Insight lost through severity, not a new delusion"],["Not a wish","Horror, no arousal, urges, images or contact","No paedophilic interest; no safeguarding concern"],["Not psychosis","No reference, persecution, voices or passivity","Psychosis excluded"],["Not depression","Low because of symptoms, still enjoys things, eats and sleeps","Secondary low mood; no depressive episode"]]},"key":"Poor insight now does not mean delusion. Always ask how the thought first felt: if he once called it absurd and fought it, it is an obsession that has worsened, and you must commit to OCD.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5,6,7],[8]]},"c-s-67":{"name":"Cycle first, then clear the mimics","why":"Finds the menstrual link that the referral missed, then rules out bipolar disorder and the other conditions that look like it.","frame":["The spells","Link to the cycle","Not bipolar or psychosis","Not EUPD","Not physical or substances","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Name her fear, set the aim","say":["I'm Dr Iqbal, one of the psychiatrists. Your GP mentioned bipolar disorder. That's a big word to hear.","Let's find out together what's going on. Tell me about these mood changes."],"hear":"\"I just get really low and snappy, then I'm fine again. I'm scared it's bipolar.\""},{"h":"What a spell is like, and between spells","say":["Talk me through a spell: mood, interest, sleep, appetite, energy, how you see yourself.","How does it affect work and your relationship?","And between spells, how are you? How would friends describe you then?"],"hear":"\"Tired, eating everything, crying, hating myself. Between, I'm completely back to normal.\"","mark":"Episodic depressive spells identified"},{"h":"Course and why now","say":["When did these start, and how have they changed?","What made you go to your GP now?"],"hear":"\"About six years. Much worse this last year. And I've started having dark thoughts.\""},{"h":"How spells start and end: the cycle","say":["How does a spell start, and how does it end? Does it link with anything?","Where in your cycle does it come? Does it lift once your period starts? Is that most months?","Were you ever on the pill or another hormone? Did that change things?"],"hear":"\"Now you say it, about a week before my period. Gone a day or two after it starts. Better when I was on the pill.\"","mark":"Premenstrual link identified"},{"h":"Body symptoms; screen highs and psychosis","say":["In that week, any bloating, breast tenderness, aches?","Have you ever had the opposite: full of energy, needing little sleep, racing thoughts, spending?","Have you ever heard or seen things others couldn't, or believed things others found strange?"],"hear":"\"Bloated and sore, yes. Never high. Nothing like that.\"","mark":"Bipolar and psychosis excluded"},{"h":"Traits, health and substances","say":["Outside that week, does your mood swing within a day? Temper, impulsive decisions, how you feel about yourself, relationships, self-harm?","Any health problems: thyroid, irregular periods, acne or extra hair?","What about alcohol, and anything recreational?"],"hear":"\"No, I'm steady otherwise. Regular periods. A few drinks at weekends, no drugs.\"","mark":"EUPD, physical causes and substances excluded"},{"h":"Risk","say":["You mentioned dark thoughts. Tell me about them.","Has it ever gone as far as a plan, or acting on it?","What keeps you going through that week?"],"hear":"\"That everyone would be better off without me. No plans, I'd never. My family, my boyfriend.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts, no plan"},{"h":"Name it, explain it, plan","say":["I don't think this is bipolar disorder. It fits premenstrual dysphoric disorder, PMDD.","Most women notice some premenstrual change; in PMDD the brain is extra sensitive to normal hormone shifts, so the mood change is severe. It's recognised and treatable.","I'd like you to rate symptoms daily for two cycles. So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up what we've found?"],"hear":"\"So it's tied to my cycle, it's not bipolar, and there's treatment.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The spells","Low, tearful, irritable, tired, eats and sleeps more, self-critical","Depressive-type symptoms, at least five"],["Link to the cycle","Starts a week before period, gone 1 to 2 days after; better on the pill","Luteal timing: core of PMDD"],["Not bipolar or psychosis","Never elevated, no reduced need for sleep, no unusual experiences","Bipolar disorder not supported"],["Not EUPD","Steady outside that week, stable self-image, no self-harm","Personality disorder not supported"],["Not physical or substances","Regular cycles, no illness, light alcohol, no drugs","TFT and FBC to complete the screen"],["Risk","Passive thoughts that week, no plan, strong protective factors","Safety plan for the premenstrual week"]]},"key":"The diagnosis is in the timing, not the symptom list. Ask how spells begin and end and whether mood is normal in between; then confirm with two cycles of daily ratings rather than recall.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-s-68":{"name":"What's new, and why now: four causes to sort","why":"Sorts relapse, perimenopause, lithium or thyroid problems and life stress by asking what is new and what is not.","frame":["Depressive relapse","Perimenopause","Lithium and thyroid","Life stresses","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Name her fear, set the aim","say":["I'm Dr Adeyemi. You've been well a long time, so I imagine you're worried it's coming back.","Let's find out what's causing this. What made you go to your GP?"],"hear":"\"I've just not been myself. Snappy, crying at nothing, and my head's all over the place.\""},{"h":"Time course and character","say":["When did you first notice it, and has it changed?","When do you get irritable or tearful? What sets it off?","How is your concentration, and your energy?"],"hear":"\"About four months. Little things set me off. I can't focus at work and I'm exhausted.\""},{"h":"Life stresses, illness and medicines","say":["Has anything changed at home or work, or been stressful?","Any new illness, or new tablets, including from the chemist?"],"hear":"\"My daughter left for university. My husband and I are tense, sex hurts. I take ibuprofen for headaches sometimes.\"","mark":"Stress and NSAID use identified"},{"h":"Is it depression?","say":["How is your mood underneath? Do you still enjoy things?","What do you think of yourself these days, and of what lies ahead?","Is this like your depression before?"],"hear":"\"Not low, really. I still love my garden. Before, I couldn't get out of bed and I thought I was evil. This is different.\"","mark":"No depressive relapse"},{"h":"Sleep and the body","say":["What's getting in the way of your sleep? What happens when you wake?","Do you feel anxious or panicky then, or hot?","Any hot flushes in the day?"],"hear":"\"I wake up drenched, but not panicky. And at work my face goes bright red.\"","mark":"Vasomotor symptoms identified"},{"h":"Vaginal symptoms and periods","say":["You mentioned sex hurts. Any dryness or soreness?","What have your periods been doing this last year? When was the last one?"],"hear":"\"Dry, yes. Periods got lighter and all over the place, then nothing for three months.\"","mark":"Perimenopause identified"},{"h":"Lithium and thyroid","say":["Any shaky hands, diarrhoea, sickness, unsteadiness or thirst?","Any feeling cold, weight gain, dry skin or constipation?","When was your last lithium blood test?"],"hear":"\"None of that. Last blood test was about four months ago, all fine.\"","mark":"No toxicity symptoms"},{"h":"Risk, work and driving","say":["Any thoughts lately of harming yourself, or anyone else?","Any sense that people are against you, or odd experiences like last time?","Has your concentration affected work or driving?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. I've stopped using the motorway, I don't trust my focus.\""},{"h":"Likely cause, reasons, plan","say":["I don't think this is your depression coming back. The sweats, flushes, dryness and periods stopping point to the perimenopause.","Sweating can push lithium up, so I'll check your level, kidneys, thyroid and calcium today. Swap ibuprofen for paracetamol.","Your GP can talk to you about HRT. Can you tell me what we've agreed?"],"hear":"\"It's probably the menopause, not a relapse. Bloods today, no ibuprofen, and see the GP about HRT.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Possible cause","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Depressive relapse","Mood not low, enjoys garden, no guilt; 'different from before'","Relapse unlikely"],["Perimenopause","Night sweats, flushes, dryness, periods stopped 3 months ago","Most likely cause; no FSH needed at 49"],["Lithium and thyroid","No toxicity or hypothyroid symptoms; sweating and ibuprofen","Check level, U&E, TFT, Ca; stop NSAID"],["Life stresses","Daughter left home, strain with husband","Adds to it; painful sex feeds the strain"],["Risk","No harm to self or others; avoiding motorway","Low risk; driving advice"]]},"key":"Once she clearly denies low mood and says this feels different, stop hunting for relapse. Ask what wakes her at night: that one question opens the perimenopause history, and the lithium bloods close the loop.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7,8],[9]]},"c-s-69":{"name":"Brief history, honest explanation: collapse, tests, shock, meaning","why":"Gathers just enough history to make the link, then answers every question a frightened parent has, including the hardest one.","frame":["The collapse and since","Tests","Stressor","Background","Meaning and plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Introduce, acknowledge, agree what you can share","say":["I'm Dr Patel from the psychiatry team. You've been here all night; that must have been frightening.","Sophie can't tell us her wishes at the moment, so I'll tell you what you need to know to support her.","May I start with some questions, then go through what I think is going on?"],"hear":"\"Please. I just want to know what's wrong with her.\""},{"h":"The collapse and since","say":["Who saw it happen, and what did they describe?","Any shaking, wetting herself, tongue biting or injury?","Since then, has she moved, spoken, eaten or drunk anything?"],"hear":"\"Her friends said she just went down mid-sentence onto the grass. No shaking. Nothing since, not even water.\"","mark":"Stupor with no seizure features"},{"h":"Tests and health","say":["What tests has she had, and what were you told?","Any illness or medicines? Alcohol or drugs, as far as you know?"],"hear":"\"Brain scans, the brainwave test, bloods, a drug test. All normal. She's never ill.\"","mark":"No physical cause"},{"h":"The stressor and her reaction","say":["Has anything been worrying Sophie lately?","How did she react, compared with the rest of the family?","How close has she been to her uncle?"],"hear":"\"My sister's husband got arrested last week over indecent images of children. She took it worse than any of us. They were close when she was little.\"","mark":"Acute stressor identified"},{"h":"Background","say":["Has she ever had problems with her mood or nerves, or harmed herself?","Anything like this before? Anyone in the family?"],"hear":"\"Never. Nothing like this. No one in the family.\""},{"h":"Name it and explain the tests","say":["I think Sophie has a dissociative stupor. After a big shock, the mind can shut down movement and response for a while.","The normal tests are good news: her brain and body are healthy. It's still real; it's how the brain is working, not damage.","She isn't choosing this and can't snap out of it."],"hear":"\"So it's because of the news about Paul?\""},{"h":"Link to the shock; the hardest question","say":["The timing fits: she was shaken by the news and withdrawn all week.","Whether he harmed her, I don't know. It's possible, and it's just as possible the shock alone did this.","Please don't question her. Once she's recovered, she can talk in her own time, and we'll stand by her whatever she says."],"hear":"\"I couldn't bear it if he had.\"","mark":"Abuse question answered honestly"},{"h":"Plan and outlook","say":["She stays here for fluids and possibly a feeding tube; we'll see her every day.","We'll also check for a related condition called catatonia, which responds to a medicine.","Most people recover over days to weeks; then talking therapy helps with what's behind it."],"hear":"\"What can I do?\""},{"h":"Her role, teach-back, close","say":["Sit with her, talk normally, keep things calm. Don't fuss over the symptoms or push her.","And rest yourself. So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up what's happening to Sophie?"],"hear":"\"The shock has made her mind switch off for now. It's real, she'll likely come out of it, and I should keep things normal.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The collapse and since","Sudden, mid-conversation, no jerking or injury; mute and still since","Stupor; seizure unlikely"],["Tests","CT, MRI, EEG, bloods, drug screen normal","No physical cause found"],["Stressor","Uncle arrested a week ago; Sophie hit hardest, withdrawn","Clear precipitant"],["Background","No past history, no self-harm, no family history","First episode; good outlook"],["Meaning and plan","Mother fears abuse and wants to help","Honest answer; medical care; calm normal support"]]},"key":"Answer 'Did he harm her?' directly: you don't know, it is possible, it is not assumed, and it will be explored only when she is well. Pair that honesty with a clear physical-safety plan on the medical ward.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,5],[6,7],[8],[9]]},"c-t-01":{"name":"Teaching from a real case: story, diagnosis, why it matters, plan","why":"Turns your assessment into a clear teaching case and a specific, safe plan the student can repeat.","frame":["The presentation","Depressive symptoms","Diagnosis and differentials","Why it matters after MI","Biopsychosocial plan, now and later"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening and structure","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Is it Priya?","Great. I've just seen Mr Whitfield on cardiology. I'll present him, then talk through diagnosis and plan.","Stop me with questions at any point."],"hear":"\"Perfect. I'm with the team this week.\""},{"h":"Presenting complaint","say":["Mr Whitfield is 63, an estate agent, three weeks after an NSTEMI.","His heart is recovering well, but the ward is worried he's not engaging with cardiac rehab.","Since the infarct he's felt persistently low."],"hear":"\"So the cardiology side is fine, it's his mood holding him back?\"","mark":"Reason for referral stated"},{"h":"Depressive symptoms","say":["He's lost all enjoyment: he misses playing his guitar.","He wakes at 4 a.m., eats only when the nurses insist, and thinks he's lost weight.","He blames himself for the heart attack and feels guilty about his wife's long journeys."],"hear":"\"Could the early waking just be the ward noise?\"","mark":"Core and biological symptoms presented"},{"h":"Background, risk and collateral","say":["He has hypertension and type 2 diabetes, and he's on aspirin, clopidogrel, bisoprolol, amlodipine, metformin and atorvastatin.","He drank 3 to 5 pints a night and smokes 30 a day. He denies any suicidal thoughts.","Before I'm sure, I'd speak to his wife with his permission, the nurses about sleep and eating, and his GP records."],"hear":"\"That's a lot of alcohol. Could that be part of it?\"","mark":"Risk stated and collateral sources named"},{"h":"Diagnosis and differentials","say":["My working diagnosis is a moderate depressive episode.","Differentials: an adjustment disorder, alcohol-related low mood, hypothyroidism, and hypoactive delirium, though he's fully oriented.","I'd check thyroid function, B12, folate and liver tests to be sure."],"hear":"\"How do you tell it apart from an adjustment disorder?\"","mark":"Diagnosis justified"},{"h":"Why it matters","say":["Depression after a heart attack is common.","It's linked with poorer rehab uptake, worse adherence and worse heart outcomes.","So treating his mood is part of treating his heart."],"hear":"\"I hadn't thought of it as part of the cardiac care.\""},{"h":"Management plan","say":["Biologically: sertraline 50 mg in the morning, the safest after a heart attack, with lansoprazole because of the antiplatelets, and a sodium check.","Psychologically: behavioural activation linked to rehab now, then CBT through Talking Therapies to work on his guilt.","Socially: rehab nurses set small goals, his wife brings his guitar, a fit note and occupational health for work, and alcohol and smoking support.","Longer term: GP follow-up, review of the antidepressant at 4 to 6 weeks, and continuing it for at least six months after he's well."],"hear":"\"Why lansoprazole and not omeprazole?\"","mark":"Biopsychosocial plan, short and long term"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: a moderate depressive episode after NSTEMI, treat actively, watch the interactions.","Can you tell me the three things you'd check before starting sertraline?","Have a look at NICE guidance on depression with a chronic physical health problem tonight."],"hear":"\"QTc, sodium and bleeding risk. Thanks, that was really useful.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mood and enjoyment","Low since the infarct; misses his guitar","Core symptoms of depression"],["Biological symptoms","4 a.m. waking, eats when told, weight loss","Moderate severity; check ward noise and physical causes"],["Thinking","Self-blame for the MI, guilt about his wife","Depressive cognitions: a target for CBT"],["Risk","No suicidal thoughts; heavy drinking; job fear","Low now, but review: several risk factors"],["Physical context","Aspirin, clopidogrel, bisoprolol, diabetes","Sertraline with a PPI; check QTc and sodium"]]},"key":"A good presenter names the diagnosis and gives a plan. An excellent one picks a drug for this heart, names the interaction it creates and how to guard against it, and links mood treatment to cardiac recovery.","objmap":[[2,3,4],[5],[6],[7],[8]]},"c-t-02":{"name":"Explaining clozapine to a carer: why, how, safety, her role","why":"Turns a frightened, frustrated mother into an informed partner who can spot warning signs early.","frame":["Why now: treatment resistance","Starting and cross-titration","Monitoring: blood, heart, bowels","Side effects: common and serious","The carer's role"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening and consent","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on Daniel's ward. Are you Mrs Mensah?","Thank you for coming in. Daniel has said I can talk to you about his treatment.","I'm sorry it's felt that way. Can I hear your worries first, then explain the new plan?"],"hear":"\"Abena. Good, because nobody tells me anything.\"","mark":"Consent confirmed and frustration acknowledged"},{"h":"Her understanding and worries","say":["What do you understand about where things are with Daniel?","How has he seemed when you've visited?","What worries you most about the new medicine?"],"hear":"\"He shouted at me last time. I read clozapine is the last resort and can kill your blood.\"","mark":"Main fears identified"},{"h":"Why a change","say":["Daniel has tried three antipsychotics properly, and his illness hasn't settled.","When two haven't worked, we call it treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","Clozapine is the medicine that works best in that situation, which is why the team chose it."],"hear":"\"If it's so good, why didn't you try it first?\"","mark":"Treatment resistance explained"},{"h":"Starting clozapine","say":["We start very low, 12.5 mg, and increase slowly over two to three weeks.","As clozapine goes up, haloperidol comes down and then stops.","Some people improve within weeks; for others it takes a few months."],"hear":"\"So he won't be on both forever?\"","mark":"Cross-titration explained"},{"h":"Blood tests and checks","say":["Before starting, he's registered with a monitoring service and has blood tests and a heart tracing.","Then blood tests weekly for 18 weeks, every two weeks until a year, then monthly.","In the first weeks we check his pulse, blood pressure and temperature daily, and do heart blood tests weekly."],"hear":"\"Every week? What are you looking for?\"","mark":"Monitoring schedule given"},{"h":"Side effects","say":["Common ones: drooling at night, constipation, sleepiness, dizziness, a fast pulse and weight gain. Most can be treated or improve.","Rare but serious: a drop in infection-fighting white cells, heart inflammation, a blocked bowel, and fits.","That's why we check so closely, and why we give a laxative from the start."],"hear":"\"Won't that mean he dies young?\"","mark":"Common and serious side effects explained"},{"h":"Her role","say":["You may spot things before we do: fever, sore throat, flu-like symptoms, chest pain, or no bowel movement for three days. Ring us straight away.","If he starts or stops smoking, tell us: it changes the level of clozapine in his blood.","You can also have a carer's assessment, and there are carers' groups that help."],"hear":"\"I didn't know smoking mattered. He's been cutting down.\"","mark":"Carer's role agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: clozapine because three medicines haven't worked, a slow start, close blood and heart checks, and you as an extra pair of eyes.","Can you tell me which signs would make you ring the ward?","Here's a leaflet and the ward number. Shall we meet with Daniel and the pharmacist next week?"],"hear":"\"Fever, sore throat, chest pain, constipation. Yes, I'd like that.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Why now","Three antipsychotics, still unwell after eight weeks","Treatment-resistant: NICE says offer clozapine"],["Her fears","'Last resort', 'kills your blood', 'zombie'","Correct the myths with balanced facts"],["Monitoring","Asks how often the blood tests are","Weekly 18 weeks, fortnightly to a year, then monthly"],["Safety signs","Didn't know about constipation or fever","She becomes an early-warning system"],["Smoking","Daniel is cutting down","Levels may rise: tell the team, recheck level"]]},"key":"Many people stop at blood tests. Excellent explanations give equal weight to constipation, fever and chest pain, and to smoking changes, and turn the carer into someone who knows exactly when to ring.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6,7],[7,8]]},"c-t-03":{"name":"Is this PTSD? Trauma outline, the clusters, then what else","why":"Reaches a confident PTSD diagnosis and a safe risk picture without re-traumatising a reluctant patient.","frame":["The trauma (outline only)","Re-experiencing","Avoidance and numbing","Hyperarousal","Comorbidity and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Major Owen Fairbairn?","Thank you for coming, Owen. Lots of people feel like that at first.","You only need to share what you're comfortable with. Could you tell me what's been troubling you?"],"hear":"\"Owen's fine. I'm not sure I need to be here.\""},{"h":"The trauma, outline only","say":["You don't need to go into detail. Can you give me a rough idea of what happened, and when?","Were you or others in danger of being killed or badly hurt?","Do you remember all of it, or are there gaps?"],"hear":"\"An IED on a convoy. Two of my lads were killed. I don't remember the next few hours properly.\"","mark":"Traumatic event and memory gap identified"},{"h":"Re-experiencing","say":["Do memories come back when you don't want them to?","When they come, does it feel like remembering, or as if it's happening again now?","What are your nightmares like? What sets the memories off?"],"hear":"\"Same dream most nights. Fireworks and I'm back there, heart pounding, sweating.\"","mark":"Re-experiencing identified"},{"h":"Avoidance and numbing","say":["Are there places, sounds or conversations you avoid because of it?","Do you feel close to your wife and children, or cut off?","What do you enjoy now?"],"hear":"\"I won't watch the news. I don't get in other people's cars. With my wife it's like I'm behind glass.\"","mark":"Avoidance and numbing identified"},{"h":"Hyperarousal","say":["How do you react to a door slamming?","Do you find yourself on guard, checking exits?","How are your sleep, temper and concentration?"],"hear":"\"I hit the deck at a car backfiring. I always sit facing the door. Can't sleep before two.\"","mark":"Hyperarousal identified"},{"h":"Comorbidity","say":["Some people blame themselves after something like this. Do you?","How much do you drink now, and what does it do for you? Any drugs?","Is your mood low, or more numb? Did you lose consciousness or hit your head in the blast?"],"hear":"\"Half a bottle of whisky most nights, to sleep. I chose the route. Numb, not sad. No head injury.\"","mark":"Guilt and harmful alcohol use identified"},{"h":"Risk and function","say":["Has it ever got so bad you've thought of ending your life?","Do you have access to any weapons, at home or at work?","Has your temper ever spilled over at home? How is work?"],"hear":"\"Thoughts they'd be better off, but no plans. No guns at home now. I shout, never hit. I'm on sick leave.\"","mark":"Risk assessed: no current intent, no firearms access"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["What you've described is post-traumatic stress disorder, alongside drinking that's become a way of coping.","It's a normal brain's response to an abnormal event, and it's very treatable with a specific talking therapy.","With your agreement, I'll arrange that and write to your GP. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"So is there anything that actually works, or do I just live with it?\""}],"table":{"cols":["PTSD cluster","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Trauma","IED killed two of his soldiers; memory gaps","Catastrophic threat: meets the event criterion"],["Re-experiencing","Nightmares most nights; fireworks put him back there","Core feature: reliving in the present"],["Avoidance and numbing","No news, no being driven; 'behind glass' with wife","Avoidance and detachment maintain the PTSD"],["Hyperarousal","Hits the deck at noises, faces doors, sleeps at 2 a.m.","Ongoing sense of current threat"],["Comorbidity and risk","Half a bottle of whisky nightly; guilt; no plans; no guns","Harmful alcohol use; risk lower but needs review"]]},"key":"Diagnosing PTSD needs the event in outline, not detail. What separates excellent: checking flashbacks feel like the present, and asking a serving soldier plainly about weapons and drink.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4,5],[6],[7],[8]]},"c-t-04":{"name":"After a near-fatal attempt: before, during, after, now, then plan","why":"Builds a full, defensible picture of risk and a plan he can agree to, rather than a tick-box score.","frame":["The attempt: intent and planning","Current intent and means","Lead-up: mood and triggers","Background risk factors","Protective factors and formulation"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Lukas Petrov?","Thank you for talking with me, Lukas. I hear the police brought you in last night.","Could you help me understand how things got to that point?"],"hear":"\"Yes. I guess so.\""},{"h":"Last night","say":["Take me back to yesterday evening. When did you decide to head for the railway?","Did you do anything to prepare, like writing a note or saying goodbye?","Had you been drinking? What did you think would happen?","What stopped it?"],"hear":"\"I wrote a note on my phone. I'd had half a bottle of vodka. I was waiting for the express. A woman spoke to me and I just froze.\"","mark":"Planned, highly lethal attempt identified"},{"h":"Current intent","say":["How do you feel now about still being here?","Are you still thinking about ending your life? Any plan?","Is there anything at home you could use to hurt yourself?"],"hear":"\"I don't know if I'm glad. No new plan. There's paracetamol at the flat.\"","mark":"Ongoing ambivalence and means at home identified"},{"h":"Lead-up and mood","say":["What's been happening in the last few weeks?","How's your mood been, and your sleep and appetite? Do you feel there's any hope?","Any voices when no one's around, or a sense that people are out to get you?"],"hear":"\"Lost my job five weeks ago, then Kasia left. Low every day. Can't sleep. No voices.\"","mark":"Depressive symptoms and hopelessness identified"},{"h":"Background risk","say":["Have you ever hurt yourself or tried to end your life before?","How much do you drink on a normal day? Any drugs?","Would you say you're an impulsive person? Anyone in the family died by suicide?"],"hear":"\"I cut my arms once, years ago. Eight cans a night since the job. I do things without thinking.\"","mark":"Alcohol, impulsivity and past self-harm identified"},{"h":"Protective factors","say":["Who's important to you, and who knows how you're feeling?","Is there anything that keeps you going, even a little?","Is there anyone you'd worry about if you died, or anyone you'd want to hurt?"],"hear":"\"My sister, Elena, in Leeds. No one round here. I don't want to hurt anyone.\"","mark":"Sister identified as main protective factor"},{"h":"Risk formulation","say":["Last night was a serious attempt: you planned it, wrote a note and chose a method that could easily have killed you.","You're still unsure you're glad to be alive, you've had two big losses, you're on your own, and the drink makes it worse.","On your side: your sister, and the fact you let someone help. So I'm very worried about you going home alone tonight."],"hear":"\"So what happens to me now? Are you going to lock me up?\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["You're not in trouble. My advice is a few days in hospital, as an informal patient, so you're safe while we treat your mood and the drinking.","Can I call Elena tonight to let her know?","We'll write a safety plan together, and here are the numbers: NHS 111 option 2 and Samaritans on 116 123. Can you tell me the plan back?"],"hear":"\"OK. Ring Elena. I'll stay.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The attempt","Note on phone, vodka, waited for an express, froze","Planned, highly lethal attempt interrupted by chance"],["Now","Not sure he's glad; paracetamol at home","Current risk remains high"],["Lead-up","Job lost, partner left, low for weeks, hopeless","Likely depressive episode; acute losses"],["Background","Eight cans nightly, impulsive, past cutting","Alcohol and impulsivity raise risk"],["Protective","Sister in Leeds; no local friends","Thin support: plan must add safety"]]},"key":"A good assessment lists risk factors. An excellent one turns them into a formulation (why this man, why now, what protects, what would change it) and links that directly to the level of care offered.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-05":{"name":"Is this treatment-resistant? Symptoms, each treatment, other factors, then options","why":"Proves or disproves treatment resistance with evidence, keeps her safe, and agrees a clear next step she believes in.","frame":["Current symptoms and severity","Each treatment: dose, duration, adherence, response","Other factors and missed diagnoses","Risk","Options: optimise, augment, switch, combine, therapy, ECT"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Clarissa Oduya?","I'm sorry, that sounds really disheartening. Thank you for coming.","I'd like to go through how you've been and what you've tried, then talk about next steps."],"hear":"\"Yes. I'm starting to think nothing works.\""},{"h":"Current symptoms","say":["How's your mood been, day to day? Does it change through the day?","What's your sleep like, and your appetite, weight and energy? What do you still enjoy?","Has there ever been a stretch when you felt unusually high, or barely needed sleep? Any voices or strange beliefs?"],"hear":"\"Low every day, worse in the morning. Awake at five. Lost a stone. Some days I don't get up. Never high.\"","mark":"More severe depressive episode; bipolar and psychosis screened out"},{"h":"Treatments so far","say":["Let's go through each tablet: the dose, how long, whether you took it every day, and whether it helped at all.","Any side effects that made you skip doses?","How did the talking therapy go? Has anything helped in the past?"],"hear":"\"Fluoxetine 20, then citalopram 20, three months each, every day. Mirtazapine 30 for two months. Nothing. I stopped therapy after four sessions; I was too tired. Fluoxetine and CBT worked in my thirties.\"","mark":"Adequate trials confirmed; therapy incomplete"},{"h":"Other factors","say":["How much alcohol do you drink? Any drugs?","Any pain, health problems, or recent blood tests?","What's been happening at home and at work? Who supports you?"],"hear":"\"A glass at weekends. GP bloods were fine. Off work three months. My husband's been wonderful.\"","mark":"No alcohol, physical or social cause found"},{"h":"Risk","say":["When things are this hard, some people feel life isn't worth living. Do you?","Has it gone further, to thoughts or plans of ending your life? Any tablets saved up?","What stops you acting on those thoughts?"],"hear":"\"Some nights I hope I won't wake up. I'd never do anything; my faith, and my husband. No tablets saved.\"","mark":"Passive death wishes; no plans; strong protective factors"},{"h":"Diagnosis","say":["You've taken three antidepressants properly, at proper doses, and they haven't worked.","We call that treatment-resistant depression. It's common, and it doesn't mean nothing will work.","It means we change the approach."],"hear":"\"So it's not that I'm doing it wrong?\""},{"h":"Next steps","say":["There are a few routes: increase the mirtazapine; add a second medicine, like aripiprazole or lithium, to boost it; or switch or combine antidepressants.","Alongside, therapy again, CBT or behavioural activation, at a pace that fits your energy.","ECT is an option for very severe depression, but I'd keep it in reserve. Which of these feels right for you?"],"hear":"\"I don't want regular blood tests. The aripiprazole, I think.\"","mark":"Shared decision made"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: keep mirtazapine, add a small dose of aripiprazole, baseline checks today, and a referral for therapy.","Can you tell me back what you'll look out for, and who you'd call if the thoughts got worse?","I'll ring in two weeks and see you in four. NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans if you need someone sooner."],"hear":"\"Restlessness, and I'd tell my husband and ring 111. Thank you, that's the first hope I've had.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Symptoms","Daily low mood worse mornings, 5 a.m. waking, lost a stone, days in bed","More severe depressive episode"],["Treatments","Fluoxetine 20, citalopram 20 (3 months each), mirtazapine 30; taken daily","Adequate trials: treatment-resistant"],["Missed diagnoses","Never high; no voices; bloods normal; little alcohol","No bipolarity, psychosis or physical cause"],["Therapy","Stopped CBT after four sessions, too tired","Therapy not yet given a fair trial"],["Risk","Hopes not to wake; faith and husband; no stockpile","Passive risk: safety plan and close review"]]},"key":"Three failed drugs is not proof of resistance until you know the dose, duration and adherence for each and have excluded bipolarity. Excellent practice proves it, then lets her choose the next step.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-06":{"name":"Can he refuse insulin? The four abilities, then the cause","why":"Turns a 'does he have capacity?' referral into an evidenced, decision-specific answer and a plan he might accept.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use and weigh","Communicate","Impairment and causal link"],"steps":[{"h":"Open honestly","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Geoffrey Amankwah?","The diabetes team asked me to talk with you about your decision. I'll be honest: they've asked whether you're able to make it.","It's still your decision. I'd just like to understand your reasons."],"hear":"\"So you think I'm mad. I've already told them no.\""},{"h":"His understanding, then fill the gaps","say":["Tell me what happened when you collapsed, and what caused it.","What have they told you about insulin, and why now?","Let me add a bit: the sugar got so high it dried you out badly. Insulin brings it down most reliably now."],"hear":"\"Sugar went sky-high. They said tablets aren't enough and I need injections.\"","mark":"Understanding present"},{"h":"Plant the facts to check retention","say":["Without insulin, the team's worry is the sugar climbs again: another collapse, coma, even death.","Over years, high sugar also harms the eyes, kidneys, nerves and heart.","Could you say that back to me? I'll ask again before I go."],"hear":"\"Sugar goes up, I could collapse or die, and my eyes and kidneys get worse.\""},{"h":"Using and weighing","say":["With all that, what makes you say no?","Tell me more about the chips. What do they do? When did you first notice them?","How sure are you? So how do you weigh the risk of dying against the chips?","Would a sealed pen, or tablets, be different?"],"hear":"\"The nurses put tracking chips in the vials to control my thoughts. A hundred per cent. The chip's worse than dying. Tablets, maybe.\"","mark":"Unable to use and weigh because of a fixed belief"},{"h":"A clear decision","say":["So what's your decision about insulin today?","Has that been the same each day?"],"hear":"\"No insulin. Same as yesterday, same tomorrow.\"","mark":"Communicates a clear, consistent choice"},{"h":"What is driving it: mind or brain","say":["What's today's date, and where are we?","And your mood lately? Any thoughts about ending your life?","Have you been taking your olanzapine? Anything like this before you came in?"],"hear":"\"Tuesday, Ward 7. Fed up, not depressed. I want to live. Tablets on and off; at home it was the TV.\"","mark":"Psychosis identified as the cause; delirium and depression excluded"},{"h":"Retention check and conclusion","say":["Before I sum up, tell me again what could happen without insulin.","You've understood and remembered it all. But the worry about chips outweighs everything, and I think it comes from your illness.","So for this one decision, today, I think you can't weigh it up. That's what I'll write."],"hear":"\"So that's it. You've decided I'm mad.\"","mark":"Retains; lacks capacity for this decision now"},{"h":"Least restrictive plan and close","say":["Nobody will hold you down. I'll ask the diabetes team today whether tablets could be safe for now.","Can I ring your sister so her view counts? And I'll come back tomorrow: my answer may change.","What's your understanding of what happens next?"],"hear":"\"You ask about tablets, ring Abena, and come back tomorrow.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Ability","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","Explains high sugar, collapse and why insulin is advised","Present, once gaps were filled"],["Retain","Repeats the risks correctly ten minutes later","Present"],["Use and weigh","'The chip's worse than dying'; would refuse a sealed pen","Absent: a fixed belief overrides every risk"],["Communicate","'No insulin', same each day","Present"],["Impairment and causal link","Chip belief since admission, TV before; olanzapine irregular; oriented, mood not low","Psychotic relapse causes the failure to weigh; no delirium or depression"]]},"key":"Understanding and repeating the risks is not enough. Capacity fails only where a belief from the illness stops him weighing them; a reasoned refusal you disagree with must be respected.","objmap":[[2,3],[3,4],[6],[5,7],[8]]},"c-t-07":{"name":"Hear her, then explain: psychosis, causes, treatment, safety, outlook","why":"Turns an angry, frightened mother into an informed ally who knows what Owen has, what will help, and what to do at home.","frame":["Her account and fears","What psychosis is (and is not)","Causes","Treatment and checks","Safety at home","Outlook and support"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, consent, and let her speak","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists looking after Owen. Are you his mum, Mrs Pritchard?","I'm sorry it has felt that way. Owen has said I can talk openly with you.","Please tell me everything that's worried you first."],"hear":"\"Nobody's told me anything, and then a nurse says 'schizophrenia'. He's depressed, not schizophrenic.\"","mark":"Anger and misconception identified"},{"h":"Her account and worries","say":["What have you noticed at home since he left university?","What about eating, sleeping, the way he talks? And the night with the knife?","Has he used cannabis? Anyone in the family had serious mental health problems?"],"hear":"\"Stayed in his room, wouldn't wash, only ate food he'd made, muttered. Weed most days at uni. My brother had a breakdown.\"","mark":"Prodrome, cannabis and family history identified"},{"h":"What psychosis means, using his experiences","say":["What do you understand by schizophrenia, and what worries you about it?","Owen told us he cut his arm to remove a microchip, and that Gabriel's voice has spoken to him for months.","Hearing a voice nobody else hears, or believing firmly something that isn't true, is called psychosis."],"hear":"\"He never told me any of that.\""},{"h":"Not depression; psychosis versus schizophrenia","say":["Withdrawal can be depression, and early on it looks the same. The voice and the beliefs point to psychosis.","Psychosis is the symptoms. Schizophrenia is one cause, usually if it lasts or returns; it's too early to say.","Does that make sense so far?"],"hear":"\"So psychosis is what's happening, and schizophrenia might be the reason, later.\"","mark":"Understanding checked"},{"h":"Causes, without blame","say":["It's a mix of vulnerability and stress: genes, heavy cannabis, and pressure like falling behind at uni.","You didn't cause this, and early on it's very hard to spot."],"hear":"\"I should have seen it sooner.\""},{"h":"Checks and olanzapine","say":["We're checking bloods, a urine drug screen and a heart tracing; a brain scan if anything is unusual.","Olanzapine turns down the voice and loosens the beliefs over a few weeks. Main side effects: sleepiness, hunger, weight gain; we'll monitor.","Usually at least one to two years after he's well."],"hear":"\"Will he be on it forever?\""},{"h":"Safety at home","say":["Before any leave we'll review risk with you. Please lock away or remove knives and sharps for now.","If he talks of hurting himself or acting on the voice, ring the ward, the crisis line or 999."],"hear":"\"I'll put the knives in the car. My husband will help.\"","mark":"Home safety plan agreed"},{"h":"Early intervention, outlook and close","say":["The Early Intervention team will support him for up to three years: care coordinator, CBT for psychosis, family sessions.","Many recover well from a first episode; some have more episodes; fewer keep symptoms.","What will you tell your husband tonight? Here's a leaflet, the carers' support details, and I'd like to book a family meeting."],"hear":"\"It's an illness, not our fault, the treatment helps, and we lock the knives away.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Topic","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Her account and fears","Withdrawn, unwashed, sealed food, muttering; fears violence","Prodrome then psychosis; fear fuelled by stigma"],["Psychosis or depression","Mood not clearly low; microchip and Gabriel's voice","Psychosis is the main problem, not depression"],["Causes","Daily cannabis at uni; uncle's breakdown; stress of failing","Stress-vulnerability; cannabis is a changeable factor"],["Treatment","Olanzapine started; worried about 'forever'","Antipsychotic plus monitoring; continue 1-2 years after recovery"],["Safety at home","Knife, cut arm, leave coming","Remove sharps, review before leave, crisis numbers"],["Outlook","'Will he go back to uni?'","EIP care; many recover well; realistic, hopeful message"]]},"key":"Hearing her first is the treatment. Then explain with Owen's own words and answer her depression theory directly; a generic lecture on schizophrenia leaves her frightened and unconvinced.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-t-08":{"name":"Why we die younger: confirm, map his risks, then a safe plan","why":"Turns a frightening headline into an honest explanation and a personal, safe plan he owns.","frame":["Smoking","Weight, diet and activity","Blood pressure, glucose and lipids","Family and personal history","Clozapine effects","Monitoring and who does it"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, validate, agree the plan","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist in the outpatient team. Are you Desmond Achterberg?","I hear you read something worrying. I'm really glad you came to ask us.","Could you tell me what you read, then we'll go through it together?"],"hear":"\"It says we die twenty years early. I do everything I'm told. Nobody ever said.\""},{"h":"What he read and how it hit him","say":["What part worries you most?","How have you been since you read it: sleep, mood?","I'm sorry nobody raised it with you. You were right to ask."],"hear":"\"I've not slept much. I'm frightened, and a bit angry.\"","mark":"Worry and mood explored"},{"h":"Is it true?","say":["Yes, the gap is real. On average, people with illnesses like yours live 15 to 20 years less.","Mostly it's heart disease, stroke, lung disease and diabetes, not the illness itself.","The good news: much of it comes from things we can change."],"hear":"\"So it's not certain. There's something I can do.\""},{"h":"Why it happens, and his own risks","say":["Do you smoke? How many, and have you thought about stopping?","How has your weight changed on clozapine? What's a usual day's eating and activity?","Has anyone mentioned your blood pressure, sugar or cholesterol? Any heart attacks or strokes in the family?"],"hear":"\"Twenty a day. Three stone heavier, always hungry, hardly go out. GP says BP's high. Dad died of a stroke.\"","mark":"Multiple risk factors identified"},{"h":"Clozapine's own risks","say":["How are your bowels? How often do you open them?","Clozapine slows the gut, and if it stops completely that's an emergency."],"hear":"\"Every three or four days. Hard. I didn't know it mattered.\"","mark":"Constipation identified"},{"h":"What he can change","say":["Stopping smoking is the single biggest win. How were you planning to do it?","Here's the catch: smoke speeds up how fast you clear clozapine. Stop suddenly, and your level can climb high within days.","So we set a date, check your level before and a week after, and adjust the dose. Patches or varenicline help with cravings.","What else would you like to work on? A walking group, help with cooking?"],"hear":"\"I was going cold turkey Saturday. I'll do it properly. And I'd try walking with my key worker.\"","mark":"Safe quit plan agreed"},{"h":"Monitoring we do","say":["Every year we'll check weight, waist, blood pressure, sugar, cholesterol and a heart tracing, plus your blood counts every four weeks.","I'll write to your GP about your blood pressure, sugar and a heart risk score.","And regular laxatives starting today."],"hear":"\"OK. I'll talk to the GP about the blood pressure.\""},{"h":"Mood and risk","say":["Has reading this made you feel hopeless, or like giving up?","Please don't stop clozapine: it keeps you well and protects your life too."],"hear":"\"No, it's made me want to live longer.\"","mark":"No hopelessness"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["What are the two or three things you'll do first?","If you feel drowsy, drool, get muddled or have no bowel opening for three days, ring us the same day.","Here's a leaflet. I'll see you in four weeks."],"hear":"\"Stop smoking with patches after the blood test, walk with Leon, see the GP.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Smoking","20 a day; planning cold turkey","Biggest changeable risk; quitting needs a clozapine level plan"],["Weight, diet and activity","Three stone gain, hungry, takeaways, hardly goes out","Metabolic risk; small, supported goals"],["BP, glucose and lipids","BP high, untreated; HbA1c 44; lipids unknown","GP to confirm and treat BP, recheck HbA1c, lipids, QRISK3"],["Family history","Father died of stroke","Adds to cardiovascular risk"],["Clozapine effects","Bowels every 3-4 days, hard stools","Risk of fatal hypomotility; start laxatives now"]]},"key":"Encouraging him to stop smoking is good; doing it without a clozapine level plan is dangerous. The excellent doctor links the two in the same breath.","objmap":[[1,2,3],[4,5],[6],[6,7],[8,9]]},"c-t-09":{"name":"Changed after a crash: injury, change, baseline, alcohol, other causes, risk","why":"Separates treatable delirium and withdrawal from brain-injury change, using the person who knows him best.","frame":["Injury severity","The change","Previous personality","Alcohol and drugs","Other causes","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, consent, purpose","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Liam's mum, Mrs Doherty?","Liam's happy for us to talk. The surgeons asked us to see him because of his behaviour.","Could you tell me what he was like before the crash, and what's different now?"],"hear":"\"He's not himself at all. He was always so gentle.\""},{"h":"The accident","say":["What do you know about the crash? Was he wearing a helmet?","Was he knocked out, and for how long?","What's the last thing he remembers before, and when did his memory come back? Any scans, intensive care?"],"hear":"\"Car pulled out. Out for several minutes. Doesn't remember the day, or the first two days here. Scan was clear, a night in intensive care.\"","mark":"Significant head injury identified"},{"h":"What has changed","say":["What's different? Can you give me an example?","Can he follow a conversation? Does he remember what you told him yesterday?","Is he worse at any time of day?"],"hear":"\"Swearing, crude to the young nurses, laughs at nothing. Asks for Jess, who left months ago. Worse at night; tried to leave last night.\"","mark":"Disinhibition and fluctuating confusion identified"},{"h":"Before the crash","say":["What was he like before: personality, work, friends?","Any mental health problems before? How had his mood been?"],"hear":"\"Easy-going, partner in a building firm. Low since Jess left.\""},{"h":"Alcohol and drugs","say":["How much does he drink, roughly, and how often?","When was his last drink before the crash?","Any drugs? And since he's been here: shaking, sweating, seeing things?"],"hear":"\"Most evenings, six or eight pints, vodka some nights. Night before. He's been shaking and picking at the sheets.\"","mark":"Heavy drinking and possible withdrawal identified"},{"h":"Other causes and the crash itself","say":["Has he had a temperature, or new pain? He's on strong painkillers too.","I have to ask: could the crash have been anything other than an accident?"],"hear":"\"Nurses said a temperature last night. No, a car pulled out on him.\"","mark":"Infection and opioids flagged; no evidence of intent"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Has he hurt anyone, or tried to leave?","Has he said anything about hurting himself?","He mustn't drive or ride until he's cleared."],"hear":"\"Pulled his drip out and headed for the door. Never said anything like that.\""},{"h":"Her concerns and the possible causes","say":["What worries you most?","There are three likely reasons, maybe together: confusion from being unwell, coming off alcohol, and the head injury. The first two are very treatable.","Brain injury changes often improve over months; it's too early to say how much."],"hear":"\"Is this brain damage? Will I get my son back?\""},{"h":"Next steps and close","say":["Today: blood tests and a chest X-ray, treatment for withdrawal and vitamins, a review of his painkillers, and we'll discuss another scan.","Can you tell me what you'll say to your husband? Here's the Headway leaflet and the ward number.","I'll see Liam again on Monday."],"hear":"\"Confusion, drink and the head. Treat the first two, wait for the third.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Injury severity","Out several minutes; no memory for two days; still forgetting","At least moderate TBI; needs follow-up imaging"],["The change","Crude remarks, irritability, laughing, worse at night, pulled cannula","Delirium plus possible frontal disinhibition"],["Previous personality","Gentle, sociable, working; low after break-up","A true change from baseline; mood before the crash matters"],["Alcohol and drugs","6-8 pints most nights, vodka; last drink night before; shaking","Alcohol withdrawal, possible early delirium tremens"],["Other causes","Temperature, morphine PCA, rib fractures","Chest infection and opioids may add to delirium"],["Risk","Tried to leave; aggression to staff","Capacity assessment, observation, no driving"]]},"key":"Do not stop at 'head injury'. Night-time worsening and heavy drinking on day 3 to 4 make delirium and withdrawal the urgent, treatable causes; judge personality change once they have cleared.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6],[7],[8,9]]},"c-t-10":{"name":"CPN handover: change, background, meds, substances, risk, social, plan","why":"Captures the freshest picture of a relapse in ten minutes, so the ward plan starts from evidence.","frame":["Recent change","Background and relapse pattern","Medication","Substances","Risk","Social situation"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and set the structure","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Paul Henshaw, Darren's CPN?","Thanks for coming over. Darren arrived an hour ago, so your view is really valuable.","Could you give me the main picture first, then I'll ask some specific questions?"],"hear":"\"Sure. I've known him two weeks, and he's gone downhill fast.\""},{"h":"Recent change","say":["What was he like when you first met him, and what's changed since?","What does he believe is happening? Any voices? How's his sleep, eating and self-care?","What has he done because of it?"],"hear":"\"Neighbours filming him through the smoke alarm. Voices commenting. Sleeping in his car a week. Not eating.\"","mark":"Persecutory relapse with behavioural change"},{"h":"Background","say":["When was he diagnosed, and how many admissions?","What happened at previous relapses? What sets them off?"],"hear":"\"Diagnosed 2018. Section 2 then, Section 3 in 2022 after stopping meds and smoking weed.\"","mark":"Relapse signature identified"},{"h":"Medication","say":["What's he prescribed, and when did he stop?","Why did he stop? What has worked best before?","Has a depot ever been discussed?"],"hear":"\"Olanzapine 10 mg, stopped three weeks ago: weight gain, felt flat. It worked well. He refused a depot before.\""},{"h":"Substances","say":["What's he using, how much and how often? How much does it cost him?","Alcohol? Anything else?"],"hear":"\"Skunk daily, about twenty quid a day. Four cans of strong lager. Nothing else I know of.\""},{"h":"Risk","say":["Has he threatened or hurt anyone, now or before? Any weapons? Police?","Any thoughts of suicide or self-harm?","Any children in his life? Has he been driving?"],"hear":"\"Threatened the neighbour last week, police came. Punched a stranger in 2022. Said once 'might as well end it'. Daughter, seven. Driving, yes.\"","mark":"Risk to others, self, child and on the road identified"},{"h":"Social and physical","say":["Where does he live? Money, benefits, support?","Any physical health problems? Any forensic history beyond the caution?"],"hear":"\"Council flat, alone, benefits. His mum's supportive. No physical problems.\""},{"h":"Ward plan","say":["Here's my plan: 15-minute observations, no leave until the RC sees him, restart olanzapine 10 mg tonight, bloods, drug screen and ECG.","I'll talk to the ward social worker about his daughter and about housing.","We'll advise him not to drive."],"hear":"\"Sounds right. His mum will want to know.\""},{"h":"Summary back and close","say":["Let me summarise to check I've got it right.","Could you come to the weekly ward review? We'll plan a CPA review before discharge.","You spotted this and escalated it. Thank you."],"hear":"\"Yes, I'll be there Thursday.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Recent change","Neighbours filming, voices commenting, sleeping in car, not eating","Acute persecutory relapse with self-neglect"],["Background and relapse pattern","Two admissions; last one after stopping meds and cannabis","Same trigger again: predictable relapse signature"],["Medication","Stopped olanzapine 3 weeks ago: weight, flatness; worked well","Restart; tackle side effects; discuss depot later"],["Substances","Daily skunk, 4 cans strong lager","Drives relapse; withdrawal monitoring on the ward"],["Risk","Threat to neighbour; past assault; passive SI; daughter; driving","High risk to others while unwell; safeguarding; DVLA advice"],["Social situation","Lives alone, benefits, supportive mother","Housing move and family involvement in discharge plan"]]},"key":"A handover is only as good as its structure. Asking why he stopped olanzapine, and who else is at risk (neighbour, daughter, road users), turns a story into a plan.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5,7],[6],[8,9]]},"c-t-11":{"name":"Is he safe to go? The attempt, the calm, the cause, the risk","why":"Turns a calm man who wants to leave into a clear picture of intent, illness and risk, and a safe plan he can accept.","frame":["The attempt: planning and precautions","Final acts and method","The calm: regret, relief, hopelessness","Illness: depression, psychosis, alcohol","Risk and protective factors"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: his wish to leave","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Roger Whitfield?","Thank you for waiting, Roger. I know you'd like to head home. I'd like to understand what happened first.","Could we start with how you came to be at the hotel?"],"hear":"\"Fair enough. I'm fine now, honestly. It was a stupid moment.\""},{"h":"Why the hotel","say":["Your home is a long way from that hotel. Why there?","When did you book it, and who knew where you were?","Were you hoping not to be found?"],"hear":"\"I booked it last week. Told Denise I was at a supplier do. I didn't want her or the boys to be the ones who found me.\"","mark":"Planned attempt and avoidance of discovery identified"},{"h":"The attempt: plan, final acts, method","say":["Take me through yesterday evening, step by step.","Did you leave a note, or sort out money or your will?","How did you choose the method? Had you looked it up?","Had you been drinking? And what happened when it failed?"],"hear":"\"Letters in my desk for Denise and the boys. I changed my will Monday. I'd read up on it. Half a bottle of whisky. The rail snapped and staff heard the crash.\"","mark":"Final acts, researched violent method and chance discovery identified"},{"h":"Now, and the brightness","say":["How do you feel about still being here?","You seem calm this morning. What's changed since last night?","Do you still have thoughts about ending your life?"],"hear":"\"Honestly? I'm sorry it didn't work. I feel at peace. Nothing's changed, I just know there's a way out.\"","mark":"Regret at surviving and relief of a decision identified"},{"h":"Mental state: depression, psychosis, alcohol","say":["How's your mood been over the last few months? What do you still enjoy?","How are your sleep, appetite and energy?","Do you feel you deserve to be punished, or hear voices when no one is there?","How much have you been drinking lately?"],"hear":"\"Low since the summer. Stopped golf. Awake at four. Lost a stone. I feel a failure, but no voices. Half a bottle a day lately.\"","mark":"Depressive episode and escalating alcohol use identified"},{"h":"Life stresses","say":["What's been happening in your life that led to this?","Who else knows about it?","Is there anything else: health, the law, other losses?"],"hear":"\"The firm went bust a month ago. Ninety grand of debt, some against the house. Denise has no idea.\"","mark":"Debt, business loss and secrecy identified"},{"h":"Risk and protective factors","say":["Has anyone in your family ever taken their own life?","If you went home today, what's there that you could use to harm yourself?","What, if anything, keeps you going? Would you accept help from us?"],"hear":"\"My dad did, when I was nineteen. There's rope and tools in the garage. The boys, I suppose. I've never seen anyone like you before.\"","mark":"High risk with some protective factors identified"},{"h":"Plan: stay, informal admission, legal route","say":["Roger, I'm really glad you told me all this. I'm worried enough that I think you need to stay in hospital for now.","I'd like you to come in voluntarily, to keep you safe and start treating the depression; it does get better.","If you decided to leave, I'd have to ask colleagues to assess you under the Mental Health Act. I'd much rather it didn't come to that."],"hear":"\"I don't want to be locked up. But I don't trust myself at home, to be honest.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: a planned attempt, a deep low with drinking, a debt you've carried alone, and you're still hopeless. That's why we're admitting you.","A nurse will stay with you while we find a bed near home. Can I tell Denise you're safe here?","My consultant will see you today. Any questions for me?"],"hear":"\"Tell her I'm in hospital. I'll tell her about the money myself.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Planning and precautions","Hotel booked a week ahead, 80 miles away; cover story to his wife","High intent: planned and designed not to be found"],["Final acts and method","Letters, new will, researched hanging, whisky first; rail gave way","Violent, lethal method; survival by chance"],["The calm","'At peace', sorry it failed, 'nothing's changed'","Relief of a decision made, not recovery; intent persists"],["Illness","3 months low, anhedonia, early waking, 6 kg weight loss, guilt; half a bottle a day","Severe depressive episode with alcohol misuse; no psychosis"],["Risk and protective factors","Father's suicide, secret debt, rope at home; two sons, accepts help","High, ongoing risk; admission needed"]]},"key":"The question that changes the decision is 'What has changed since last night?' If his problems are the same and he regrets surviving, his calm is a warning, not reassurance.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7],[8,9]]},"c-t-12":{"name":"Ready for hormones? Story, life now, goals, health, risk","why":"Gives a full, respectful picture of her gender history and readiness, rules out other needs, and leaves her with a clear, safe path.","frame":["Gender history","Social transition and support","Goals and expectations","Mental and physical health","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: name, pronouns, aim","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the gender clinic. What name and pronouns would you like me to use?","Thank you, Nadia. Your GP referred you to talk about hormones and surgery. I'm here to understand your journey.","Could you tell me about how you've felt about your gender over time?"],"hear":"\"Nadia, and she/her. I've waited a long time for this appointment.\""},{"h":"Gender history","say":["What's your earliest memory of feeling your gender didn't match your body?","How was puberty for you?","Has that feeling ever wavered, or has it stayed the same?"],"hear":"\"I knew I was a girl at five. I wore my sister's clothes. When my voice broke I wanted to disappear. It's never changed.\"","mark":"Persistent gender incongruence since childhood identified"},{"h":"Social transition","say":["What does your life look like now: name, clothes, work?","Who knows, and how have they responded?","How does living as Nadia feel, compared with before?"],"hear":"\"Two years as Nadia. Changed my name. Work were fine. Marcus has been amazing. I finally feel like me.\"","mark":"Established social transition with support identified"},{"h":"Goals of treatment","say":["What are you hoping hormones and surgery will change?","Is there anything you think they might change that they won't? Voice and facial hair are common ones.","Have you thought about children, now or later?"],"hear":"\"Breasts, my shape, and surgery later. I thought my voice would go higher. Children with Marcus, maybe; I hadn't thought about that.\"","mark":"Goals clear; voice and fertility expectations need work"},{"h":"Mental and physical health","say":["I ask everyone these, and it's not because being trans is an illness. How have your mood, sleep and appetite been?","Have you had experiences like hearing voices, or thoughts that won't leave you alone, or worries about one part of your body?","Any medical problems, smoking, alcohol or drugs?"],"hear":"\"Mood's good. No voices, nothing like that. It's not one feature; it's my whole body being male. I smoke five a day.\"","mark":"No psychosis, OCD or body dysmorphia; smoking identified"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Have you ever wanted to end your life, or thought of harming parts of your body?","Have you ever bought or been offered hormones online?","Has anyone hurt you or treated you badly because you're trans? And can I ask about sexual health?"],"hear":"\"No self-harm. A friend offered online oestrogen; I haven't. I was punched on a night bus last year. Just Marcus, and I've not had a check for a while.\"","mark":"Assault and online hormone risk identified"},{"h":"Support and consent","say":["Who supports you, and who could you turn to if things got hard?","Hormones bring changes that may be permanent, like fertility. How do you feel about weighing those up?","Is it alright if I write to your GP with what we've discussed?"],"hear":"\"Marcus, my sister and a friend from work. I understand some things can't be undone. Yes, write to my GP.\""},{"h":"Next steps","say":["Next come baseline blood tests and a second appointment; then hormones can be recommended, usually prescribed by your GP with us.","Before hormones, I'll refer you to the fertility clinic to store sperm, and to a speech and language therapist for voice.","Stopping smoking really matters, because oestrogen and smoking together raise clot risk. Please don't use online hormones; tell us if you're tempted."],"hear":"\"So bloods, fertility, voice therapy, stop smoking, and see you again. That's more than I expected.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: a lifelong, settled sense of being a woman, two years living as Nadia, good support, and no other health problems to treat.","What will happen next, as you understand it?","Here's our number and a peer support leaflet. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"Bloods, sperm storage, voice therapy, second appointment. Thank you for listening properly.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Gender history","Knew she was a girl at five; puberty unbearable; never wavered","Marked, persistent gender incongruence"],["Social transition and support","Two years as Nadia; name changed; out at work; partner supportive","Well-established transition and support"],["Goals and expectations","Wants oestrogen and later surgery; expects a higher voice","Clear goals; correct voice and fertility expectations"],["Mental and physical health","Mood good; no voices, obsessions or single-feature focus; smokes","No mental disorder; smoking to address before hormones"],["Risk","Assaulted on a bus; offered online oestrogen; no self-harm","Safety advice, support, sexual health check"]]},"key":"Screen for other conditions, but say why first. The difference between excellent and poor here is that she leaves feeling assessed, not judged, and knows exactly what happens next.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,7],[4],[5,6],[8,9]]},"c-t-19":{"name":"Why has Josh changed? Baseline, body, mind, world, then risk","why":"Turns a worried carer's account into a clear differential, catches the physical cause, and gives the home a safe plan.","frame":["Baseline","Biological: physical health and pain","Psychological: mood and mental illness","Social: losses and environment","Risk: to self, to others, from others"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and acknowledge","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the learning disability team. Are you Declan?","Thank you for coming in. Josh has agreed we can talk about him. You know him well, so your view really matters.","Tell me what you've noticed, and then I'll ask some more detailed questions."],"hear":"\"I've been really worried about him.\""},{"h":"Baseline Josh","say":["What is Josh like when he's well?","How does he communicate, and what does he enjoy?","What can he do for himself?"],"hear":"\"Cheerful, always singing. Short sentences. Loves the day centre and helping in the kitchen.\"","mark":"Baseline established"},{"h":"What has changed","say":["When did you first notice a change, and what was it?","How is he sleeping and eating? Has he lost weight?","Is there anything he still enjoys?"],"hear":"\"About six weeks. In his room, crying at night, skipping meals, lost weight. Nothing seems to cheer him up.\"","mark":"Depressive features identified"},{"h":"The body first","say":["Any signs of pain: holding his tummy, jaw or ears?","How are his bowels? Any vomiting?","Has he seen the GP, or had any new tablets?"],"hear":"\"Actually, the GP saw him two days ago. Very constipated. He's on laxatives but hasn't been yet.\"","mark":"Severe constipation identified: urgent physical cause"},{"h":"Mind and world","say":["Has he heard voices, seemed unusually high, or done anything odd?","Has anything changed around him: staff, visitors, the day centre?","Has anyone new come into his life?"],"hear":"\"No voices. His favourite worker at the day centre left. His mum visits monthly now, not weekly.\"","mark":"Losses identified as triggers"},{"h":"Risk three ways","say":["Tell me about the head-banging and the time he hit out. What happened just before?","Has he ever said he doesn't want to live, or tried to harm himself another way?","I ask everyone this: any bruises, fear of a particular person, or anything that worries you about how he's treated?"],"hear":"\"He hit out when we tried to get him up. Head-banging a few times, no injury. Nothing about dying. No bruises, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Risks to Josh, to staff and from others assessed"},{"h":"Background and his concerns","say":["Any epilepsy, past mental health problems or similar spells?","What worries you most?","How are you and your colleagues doing?"],"hear":"\"No epilepsy. My worry is he'll hurt someone again, and what do we do tonight?\"","mark":"Carer concerns heard"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["This sounds most like depression, possibly set off by those losses. But the constipation needs sorting today; it can cause real pain and become dangerous.","For now: gentle prompts, not pulling him out; keep a chart of mood, sleep, bowels and incidents; call us the same day if things get worse.","We'll do bloods, see him in two weeks, and look at a behaviour support plan. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"That helps. I'll tell the team.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Baseline","Cheerful, sociable, short sentences","A clear change from his usual self"],["Biological","Severe constipation, weight loss, poor sleep","Urgent physical cause; may drive pain and distress"],["Psychological","Crying, withdrawal, no enjoyment; no psychosis or mania","Probable depressive episode"],["Social","Favourite worker left; mother visits less","Losses that may have triggered it"],["Risk","Head-banging, one assault on staff, no abuse signs","Moderate risk; behaviour support and monitoring needed"]]},"key":"Depression may well be the answer, but the excellent doctor finds the constipation first. One plausible explanation is not enough in someone who cannot easily say what hurts.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[4,5],[6],[8]]},"c-t-20":{"name":"Depression, dementia or something treatable? Baseline, course, causes, risk","why":"Uses the mother's knowledge to separate a treatable cause from early dementia, without labelling too soon.","frame":["Baseline","Memory and function","Mood","Treatable causes: thyroid, senses, sleep, infection, medicines","Seizures and psychosis","Risk and carer coping"],"steps":[{"h":"Settle and set up","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette, Owen's mum?","That's completely understandable. Owen is happy for us to talk. You know him better than anyone.","Let's start with what you've noticed, and I'll ask more as we go."],"hear":"\"I just want to know what's happening to him.\""},{"h":"Usual Owen","say":["What was Owen like before this, at his best?","What could he do for himself?","Has he had a memory or skills assessment before, maybe around 30?"],"hear":"\"Chatty, cheerful. Dressed himself, loved football. Yes, they did some tests when he was thirty.\"","mark":"Baseline established"},{"h":"Memory and function","say":["What has he been forgetting? Since when?","Has he got lost, or struggled with things like dressing?","Does he still recognise family and friends? Sudden or gradual?"],"hear":"\"About a month. Muddles the days, loses his blanket, can't do buttons. He got lost once. He knows us all.\"","mark":"Rapid cognitive and functional decline identified"},{"h":"Mood and biology","say":["How has his mood been? Does he still enjoy football?","How is he sleeping and eating?","Has he cried, or said anything sad?"],"hear":"\"Low. Doesn't want to go. Up walking at night. Eating less.\"","mark":"Depressive features identified"},{"h":"Treatable causes","say":["Any change in hearing or eyesight? When were they last checked?","Does he snore or stop breathing at night? Any recent illness, falls or new medicines?","When was his thyroid last checked? Any changes at home or the day centre?"],"hear":"\"Not checked for two years. He snores terribly. No new tablets, nothing's changed.\"","mark":"Thyroid, senses and sleep apnoea to exclude"},{"h":"Seizures and psychosis","say":["Any fits, sudden jerks, staring spells or wetting himself?","Has he talked to people who aren't there, or seemed frightened of things?"],"hear":"\"No fits, nothing like that. No voices.\"","mark":"Seizures and psychosis screened"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Tell me about the time he got lost. What's happened since?","Any worries in the kitchen, crossing roads, or with his self-care?","Any concerns about how anyone treats him? Any thoughts of harming himself?"],"hear":"\"A neighbour brought him back. I lock the door now. No one would hurt him. He's never said anything like that.\"","mark":"Wandering risk identified"},{"h":"Her coping","say":["How are you managing?","Who else helps you?","What worries you most?"],"hear":"\"My arthritis is bad. It's just me. What happens when I can't cope?\"","mark":"Carer strain identified"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["You're right that people with Down's syndrome are at higher risk of dementia. But this has come on fast and his mood is low, so depression or a physical cause is just as likely.","We'll check his thyroid and other bloods, hearing, eyes and sleep, and compare his memory with the tests at thirty.","We'll arrange a carer's assessment and a door alarm, and see you both in four weeks. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"That's a relief. Thank you.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Baseline","Cheerful, independent dressing, assessment at 30","A clear change; a baseline to compare with"],["Memory and function","Days muddled, blanket lost, buttons, lost once; knows faces","Real decline, but fast for dementia"],["Mood","Low, no enjoyment, night walking, eating less","Depressive episode likely"],["Treatable causes","Snores and pauses; hearing and eyes unchecked","Thyroid, sensory loss, sleep apnoea to exclude"],["Risk and coping","Got lost; door locked; mother exhausted","Wandering risk and carer strain need action"]]},"key":"In Down's syndrome, dementia is common but never a diagnosis of one visit. A fast change with low mood means thyroid, senses, sleep and depression first, and decline proven against his own baseline.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6],[7,8],[9]]},"c-t-21":{"name":"Garden to risk: the account, the arousal, the pattern","why":"Turns an angry, guarded man into an honest account that shows whether this is exhibitionism and how far it is escalating.","frame":["Sexual motivation","Past pattern and paraphilias","Erotomania and illness screen","Risk to the neighbour and children","Protective factors"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: role and limits","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with the custody team. Are you Gareth Mulvaney?","I'm not police. My job is to check how you are and whether you need any support.","Anything you tell me may be shared if there's a risk to someone. Is that okay? Have you seen your solicitor?"],"hear":"\"Fine. Go on. I've spoken to the solicitor on the phone.\""},{"h":"The incident in his words","say":["Take me through what's been happening in the garden these last two weeks.","Where could your neighbour see you from? Did you know she was at home?","What did you expect would happen if she saw you?"],"hear":"\"Every afternoon for two weeks. Her kitchen looks right on to it. She works from home. I thought she might like what she saw.\"","mark":"Deliberate visibility to a known woman identified"},{"h":"Sexual motivation","say":["I know this is embarrassing, but I need to ask some personal questions.","When you were out there, did the thought of her seeing you excite you? Did you have an erection?","Have you touched yourself or masturbated in the garden? Do you fantasise about her, or about going round?"],"hear":"\"Yes, it turned me on. A few times, more lately. I've thought about knocking on her door. I haven't.\"","mark":"Sexual arousal, masturbation and contact fantasies identified"},{"h":"Past pattern and psychosexual history","say":["Has anything like this happened before, at any age? Ever been in trouble with the police?","Tell me about your relationships and sex life over the years. Any difficulties?","Any other sexual interests: watching people, pain, younger people or children? Do you use pornography, and what kind?"],"hear":"\"As a teenager I flashed women in the street a few times. Never caught. Nothing with kids. Normal porn. Fine with my wife until we split.\"","mark":"Earlier exhibitionism identified; no interest in children"},{"h":"Mental state and the belief about her","say":["Do you believe she's interested in you? How sure are you? Could she just be upset?","How's your mood been since the separation? Sleep, appetite, enjoyment?","Any times of feeling high and racing? Hearing things others don't? Memory trouble? How much do you drink, any drugs?"],"hear":"\"I hoped she'd be interested. I suppose she might be scared. Mood's flat but I get on with it. Drink hardly at all.\"","mark":"Erotomania, depression, mania and psychosis screened out"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Have you ever approached her, watched her or followed her?","If you're released today, what would you do about her?","Do you spend time around children? Any thoughts of hurting yourself, now or once you're home?"],"hear":"\"Never been near her. I'd keep away now. No kids around me. I'm not going to do anything stupid.\"","mark":"Escalating risk to neighbour; no current child risk; no suicidal intent"},{"h":"Protective factors","say":["How do you think she felt seeing you?","Who's around to support you? What do you do with your days?","If I put you in touch with help for these urges, would you go?"],"hear":"\"I suppose she'd be frightened. I see my brother. I'd go, if it's private.\"","mark":"Some victim empathy and willingness to engage"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Let me sum up: being seen excited you, it's happened before years ago, and you've thought about going round. Is that fair?","I don't think a mental illness is causing this, but there is help to manage these urges. I'll tell the sergeant you're fit to be interviewed.","I'll share the risk to your neighbour with the police and write to your GP. If you feel low, call 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123."],"hear":"\"Fair enough. I didn't think it'd come to this.\"","mark":"Opinion and plan given"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Sexual motivation","Aroused by her seeing him; erections; masturbating in garden","Exhibitionistic behaviour, not naturism"],["Past pattern and paraphilias","Exposed to women in street as a teenager; nothing since until separation; no other paraphilias","Long-standing pattern unmasked by loss"],["Erotomania and illness screen","Hoped she was interested; accepts she may be scared; no mood, psychotic or cognitive symptoms","Not erotomania; no mental illness"],["Risk to the neighbour and children","Knows she is home alone; fantasises about going round; no child interest","Escalating, targeted risk; police must know"],["Protective factors","Some empathy, brother's support, will accept help, no previous convictions","Engagement with treatment is realistic"]]},"key":"Asking about arousal is what separates good from excellent. 'Was it sexual for you?' turns a naturism argument into a risk assessment; without it you cannot rate the danger to the neighbour.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-22":{"name":"From poor sleep to planned force: the belief, its strength, the ladder","why":"Separates morbid jealousy from ordinary suspicion and measures how close he is to hurting his partner.","frame":["Belief and evidence","Conviction and alternatives","Behaviour ladder","Other psychosis and alcohol","Risk factors"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: from sleep to stress","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Wesley Thorne?","I understand. Your GP wanted us to look at the stress behind the poor sleep. Can you tell me about it?","Go on. I'm listening, and I'd like the whole picture."],"hear":"\"It's my partner, Shona. She's been sleeping around.\""},{"h":"The stress: how it began","say":["When did you first think this? What happened?","What else have you noticed since?","How many men do you think there have been?"],"hear":"\"Six months ago, men's aftershave on her coat. Now she flirts with everyone. The postman this morning. Hundreds of men.\"","mark":"Jealous belief on trivial evidence identified"},{"h":"Belief quality","say":["Is there any other way to explain the aftershave, or the way she spoke to him?","On a scale of nought to a hundred, how certain are you?","Do you have any other worries: people watching you, plotting, messages meant for you?"],"hear":"\"There's no other explanation. A hundred per cent. No, nothing else.\"","mark":"Fixed delusion; no other delusions"},{"h":"Behaviours","say":["What have you done to find out the truth?","Do you check her phone, or follow her?","When you've confronted her, what happened?"],"hear":"\"I go through her phone. I follow her after work. We row; she denies it. I've taken time off sick to watch her.\"","mark":"Checking, stalking and confrontation identified"},{"h":"Mental state","say":["How's your mood? Any times you feel low, or unusually high?","Do you hear or see things others don't?","How much are you drinking now, and when did that go up? Any drugs? Any problems in your sex life?"],"hear":"\"Stressed, not down. No voices. Five or six cans a night, more since all this. No drugs. That side's fine.\"","mark":"Mood disorder and other psychosis screened out; drinking rose after the belief"},{"h":"Risk to partner","say":["Where do you see this going?","You said 'one way or another'. What do you mean?","Would you use force? Have you looked into ways to make her talk? Do you have weapons?"],"hear":"\"If she won't tell me, I'll make her. I've read about injections, truth serum. No weapons.\"","mark":"Planned force and researched method: high risk"},{"h":"Other risks and history","say":["Have you ever hurt a partner before? Been in trouble with the police?","Any thoughts of harming the men you suspect, or yourself?","Are there children in the house or who visit?"],"hear":"\"I did two years for hitting my ex. There's a restraining order. Not bothered about the blokes. No kids. I'd never hurt myself.\"","mark":"Past partner violence identified"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["I can see how tormented you've been. I think an illness is driving this belief, called morbid jealousy, and it can be treated.","I'm worried for Shona's safety, so I can't keep this confidential. She needs to know, and I want my consultant to see you today.","Tonight, will you stay somewhere other than home, with your brother perhaps?"],"hear":"\"I'm not ill. But fine, I'll go to my brother's.\"","mark":"Concern shared; same-day plan to protect partner"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Belief and evidence","Aftershave on coat; greeting to postman; hundreds of lovers","Trivial evidence, grossly implausible conclusion"],["Conviction and alternatives","No other explanation; 100% sure","Delusion, not an overvalued idea"],["Behaviour ladder","Phone checks, following, rows, plans to force a confession, truth serum research","Escalating; now planning and method"],["Other psychosis and alcohol","No other delusions or voices; not depressed; drinking rose after onset","Delusional disorder, jealous type; alcohol an amplifier"],["Risk factors","ABH on ex-partner, off work to watch her, no insight","High, imminent risk to Shona"]]},"key":"Testing the belief diagnoses it; asking what 'one way or another' means measures the danger. The excellent doctor does both, then acts the same day for the partner rather than writing a letter.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,5],[4,6],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-23":{"name":"Calm, observe, test, then ask the drink question","why":"Gets a terrified, hallucinating woman calm enough to assess, and reaches the diagnosis that changes her treatment today.","frame":["Appearance and behaviour","Mood and risk","Perception","Cognition","Alcohol and physical causes"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: calm and low stimulation","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Margaret Penrose?","I can see you're very frightened, Margaret. You're safe here, and I'm staying with you.","Can you look at me for a moment? I'd like to ask you a few questions."],"hear":"\"On the floor. Look. Can't you see them?\""},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","say":["I notice you're shaking and sweating, and you keep stamping your feet.","You seem to find it hard to sit still. Is that right?","You're speaking quickly; take your time."],"hear":"\"I have to squash them before they get me.\"","mark":"Tremor, sweating, agitation and distractibility observed"},{"h":"Mood and thoughts","say":["How are you feeling in yourself right now?","Do you feel something terrible is about to happen?","Any urge to hurt yourself, or to run away?","Is anyone trying to harm you, or control your thoughts?"],"hear":"\"Terrified. Like I'm going to die. I don't want to hurt myself. Nobody's controlling me.\"","mark":"Fear and sense of doom; no SI; no passivity"},{"h":"Perception","say":["Tell me what you're seeing. Where are they coming from?","Can you feel anything on your skin?","Do you hear anything others can't? I can't see them, but I believe you can."],"hear":"\"Beetles, pouring out of the walls. They're crawling up my legs. No voices.\"","mark":"Visual and tactile hallucinations; no auditory"},{"h":"Cognition","say":["What's the date today? And what is this place?","Who do you think I am?","Starting from December, can you go back through the months? What do you remember since the operation?"],"hear":"\"I don't know... a hospital? Are you a policeman? December, November... I lose track. It's all a blur.\"","mark":"Disoriented, inattentive: delirium"},{"h":"Alcohol history","say":["When did you last have a drink?","How much would you usually drink on a normal day?","Have you ever had the shakes, fits or seen things when you stopped before?"],"hear":"\"The night before the operation. A few bottles of red a day, since I retired. Never stopped before.\"","mark":"Heavy dependence, abrupt stop 4 days ago"},{"h":"Physical","say":["Any pain in your back or tummy? Trouble passing water or opening your bowels?","Nurse, could you get a full set of obs and a glucose now?","Any falls, headaches or problems with your eyes or walking?"],"hear":"\"My back's sore. I haven't been to the loo for days.\"","mark":"Contributing causes to check"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Margaret, I think your body is reacting to suddenly stopping the wine. It's serious, but it's treatable.","We'll give you medicine to calm the shaking and vitamins through a drip, and a nurse will stay close.","Staff nurse, this is delirium tremens; please bleep the orthopaedic registrar now and I'll write the plan."],"hear":"\"Will they go away?\" Nurse: \"Bleeping now.\"","mark":"Delirium tremens named; urgent plan started"}],"table":{"cols":["Mental state domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Appearance and behaviour","Sweating, tremor, stamping, restless, distractible","Autonomic overactivity and agitation of withdrawal"],["Mood and risk","Terrified, sense of doom; no SI; wants to escape","Fear-driven risk of falls and leaving, not suicide"],["Perception","Insects from walls; crawling up legs; no voices","Visual and tactile hallucinations typical of DTs"],["Cognition","Disoriented, cannot do months backwards, 'a blur'","Delirium, not a primary psychosis"],["Alcohol and physical causes","Bottles of wine daily, last drink 4 days ago; constipated, in pain","Delirium tremens; other causes to treat too"]]},"key":"A clear-headed psychosis and delirium look alike for ten seconds. Inattention and disorientation make it delirium; the last-drink question makes it delirium tremens, which is a medical emergency, not a psychiatric admission.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-24":{"name":"The voice, the drink, the timeline","why":"Shows the voices began only after heavy drinking stopped, so she gets the right diagnosis and avoids a wrong long-term label.","frame":["The voice itself","Other psychotic and mood symptoms","Alcohol history","Withdrawal and delirium tremens","Other causes and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: why she is here","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Natalia Petrenko?","Your GP was concerned about how you've been sleeping and some worries about your neighbour. Can you tell me about that?","Thank you. I'd like to hear it all, and then ask about some other parts of your life."],"hear":"\"He sings to me at night.\""},{"h":"The experiences","say":["What exactly do you hear, and when?","Where does it come from? Is it as clear as my voice now?","When you open the door, what do you find?"],"hear":"\"Nursery rhymes, through the keyhole, every night. Clear as you. I open the door and no one's there.\"","mark":"True auditory hallucination identified"},{"h":"Nature of voices","say":["How do you know it's him? Why would he do that?","Do you hear any other voices, or see or feel things others don't?","Is anyone plotting against you, or that your thoughts are interfered with? When did this start?"],"hear":"\"I know his voice. I don't know why; we got on. Nothing else. It started a few nights ago.\"","mark":"No other psychotic features; recent onset"},{"h":"Alcohol history","say":["Your GP mentioned your liver tests. What do you usually drink, and how much on a typical day?","How long has it been that much? What changed?","Did you ever drink first thing to steady yourself, or struggle to cut down?"],"hear":"\"Two bottles of red a day, for about four years, since my husband died. I never felt drunk.\"","mark":"Alcohol dependence (about 20 units a day) identified"},{"h":"Stopping drinking","say":["When did you last have a drink?","When you stopped, did you have shakes, sweats, fits or feel muddled?","How long did that last, and how are you now?"],"hear":"\"A week ago today. Shaky and sick for two or three days, then fine. Never confused.\"","mark":"Abrupt stop; withdrawal settled; no DTs"},{"h":"Mental state and causes","say":["How's your mood been? Still enjoying things? How are you coping with the loss of your husband?","Do you know today's date and where we are? Any memory problems?","Any illnesses, medicines, drugs, or family history of mental illness?"],"hear":"\"Mood's fine, work is good. I miss him in the evenings. I know where I am. No medicines, nothing else.\"","mark":"Mood disorder, delirium and secondary causes screened out"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Have you thought about having it out with him?","Any thoughts of harming him, or yourself?","Any worries about managing at work?"],"hear":"\"No, I hate arguments. Never. Work's fine.\"","mark":"Low risk to neighbour and self"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Let me put it together: heavy drinking for years, stopping a week ago, and the singing started a few days later.","I think this is an effect of stopping alcohol on the brain, called alcoholic hallucinosis. You're not going mad, and it usually fades if you stay off drink.","I'll ask your GP to start vitamin B1 and check bloods, refer you to the alcohol service, and see you in two weeks. If you get shaky or muddled, go to A&E."],"hear":"\"So it's the wine. That makes sense, strangely.\"","mark":"Diagnosis explained; plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Framework item","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The voice itself","Neighbour singing nursery rhymes at night, clear, from outside, no one there","True auditory hallucination"],["Other psychotic and mood symptoms","None; mood fine; explanation built around the voice","Primary psychosis and mood disorder unlikely"],["Alcohol history","Two bottles daily for four years, since bereavement","Alcohol dependence, about 140 units a week"],["Withdrawal and delirium tremens","Stopped a week ago; shaky 2–3 days; oriented; no tremor now","Withdrawal settled; not delirium tremens"],["Other causes and risk","No illness, medicines, drugs or family history; won't confront him","Alcohol-induced psychotic disorder; low risk"]]},"key":"The diagnosis lives in the timeline. Hearing the voice well gets you to 'psychosis'; asking when she stopped drinking, and checking she is clear-headed, gets you to hallucinosis and away from a wrong schizophrenia label.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,6],[4,5],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-25":{"name":"From cracked hands to a fear ladder: the OCD cycle, ERP and CBT","why":"Turns an embarrassed, frightened man into an informed partner in a step-by-step plan he believes can work.","frame":["Trigger and obsession","Compulsion and relief","Exposure","Response prevention","Cognitive work"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tobias Mensah?","Thanks for coming, Toby. Your GP told me a bit about the cleaning and your hands. That must be really hard to talk about.","You're not alone in this. Can we go over how things are now, then talk through treatment?"],"hear":"\"It's embarrassing, to be honest. And this clinic feels dirty.\""},{"h":"Obsessions and compulsions","say":["What goes through your mind when you look at a counter after a batch?","What do you do then, and how long does the relief last?","Part of you thinks it's over the top. How likely is HIV from a counter, really, out of ten?"],"hear":"\"That it's got germs and someone'll get HIV. I wipe twice, wash my hands. Relief for a minute. I know it's silly but I can't risk it.\"","mark":"Contamination obsession, cleaning compulsion, partial insight"},{"h":"Impact and risk","say":["How many hours a day does it eat up? How are your hands?","How's your mood been? Sleep and appetite?","Any times you've felt life isn't worth it, or wanted to hurt yourself?"],"hear":"\"Two hours extra a day. They bleed. I've been down for a couple of months, but I'd never hurt myself.\"","mark":"Moderate-severe impairment; low mood; no suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Name it and explain","say":["What you're describing is OCD. It's common, and it's very treatable.","Here's the cycle: you see the counter, the HIV thought comes, anxiety shoots up, you clean, it drops, so next time the urge is stronger.","Does that fit with how it feels?"],"hear":"\"That's exactly it. It never stops.\"","mark":"OCD cycle understood"},{"h":"How ERP works","say":["ERP means exposure and response prevention: facing the worry, without doing the cleaning.","We'd make a ladder: for example, touching the counter once and waiting five minutes before washing, near the bottom; leaving a colleague's area unscrubbed, higher up.","You stay with the anxiety and it falls on its own. Each time, the fear gets weaker."],"hear":"\"You want me to touch dirty things on purpose? That sounds mad.\"","mark":"ERP and fear ladder explained"},{"h":"CBT around it","say":["Alongside ERP, CBT looks at the thoughts: how responsible you feel, and the facts about HIV.","HIV isn't passed on through worktops or food. We'd test your predictions with small experiments.","There's homework daily, and you'd see a therapist weekly for more than ten hours in all."],"hear":"\"Can it be at home or by video? I can't focus here.\"","mark":"CBT elements explained; practical concern raised"},{"h":"Medication and skin","say":["Yes, video sessions and some at work are possible, and ERP at work suits you well.","Keep taking fluoxetine 40 mg; it's had only a few weeks at that dose. We can raise it to 60 mg later.","Your GP can give an emollient and a soap substitute, and steroid cream if needed; healthy skin will also ease the fear."],"hear":"\"Good. I want to stay on it.\"","mark":"Medication continued; skin plan"},{"h":"Work and support","say":["Would it help if occupational health agreed what normal cleaning looks like in your kitchen?","Gloves while your hands heal could help too.","OCD-UK has good information; would you like your family to know about the plan?"],"hear":"\"Yes, a clear standard would help. My sister knows.\"","mark":"Work and support agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Just to check I've explained it well, how would you describe ERP back to me?","So: therapy referral with ERP, some sessions at work or video, fluoxetine continued, skin cream, occupational health.","I'll see you in six weeks. If your mood drops or you think of harming yourself, call us or 111 option 2."],"hear":"\"I face it in small steps and don't clean, and the fear drops. OK.\"","mark":"Teach-back and plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Part of the OCD cycle","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Trigger and obsession","Counters after each batch; thought that customers will get HIV","Contamination obsession with inflated responsibility"],["Compulsion and relief","Wipes twice, stays late, repeated handwashing; relief for a minute","Ritual reinforces the fear: target for change"],["Exposure","Can touch a counter at 3/10 fear; colleagues' areas at 8/10","Ladder ready to build with the therapist"],["Response prevention","Urge 9/10; partial insight","Good predictor of response to ERP"],["Cognitive work","Unsure about HIV risk; cracked skin raises fear","HIV facts, experiments and skin care"]]},"key":"Explaining ERP is not enough: build the first rungs of his ladder with him, and admit anxiety will rise before it falls. That honesty is what keeps people in therapy.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5],[6],[7,8,9]]},"c-t-26":{"name":"Name it, draw the cycle, then break it","why":"Turns shame into understanding, and understanding into a treatment plan she believes in.","frame":["Binge with loss of control","Compensation (vomiting and other purging)","Self-worth tied to weight and shape","The restrict-binge-purge cycle","Physical consequences"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and reduce shame","say":["Thanks for coming, Hannah. Your GP shared a bit about the bingeing and being sick. I know that can be hard to talk about.","There's nothing to be embarrassed about here. This is common and treatable.","Can I check a few things, then explain what I think is happening?"],"hear":"\"OK. I've never told anyone except my GP.\""},{"h":"Brief check of the pattern","say":["Take me through a typical weekday in terms of food.","During a binge, how much do you feel in control?","Apart from being sick, do you use laxatives, water tablets, fasting or extra exercise?","What's different at weekends?"],"hear":"\"No breakfast, gym at lunch, then I eat everything in sight at night and make myself sick. Weekends I eat normally and I'm fine.\"","mark":"Binge, loss of control and compensation identified"},{"h":"Name the diagnosis","say":["How much does your weight or shape affect how you feel about yourself?","What you've told me fits bulimia nervosa: binges, feeling out of control, then being sick to make up for it, with weight affecting how you see yourself.","It isn't anorexia, as your weight is healthy, and it's more than binge eating, because of the vomiting."],"hear":"\"So it has a name. I thought I was just disgusting.\"","mark":"Bulimia nervosa explained; differentials excluded"},{"h":"Draw the cycle","say":["Skipping food all day leaves your body starving by evening, so it pushes you to eat a lot, fast.","Then the guilt comes, you're sick, and you promise to eat less tomorrow, which starts it again.","Your weekends prove it: when you eat regularly, the binges stop."],"hear":"\"That's literally my week.\"","mark":"Maintaining cycle understood"},{"h":"Physical effects","say":["Being sick often can lower your potassium, which the heart needs to keep a steady rhythm.","Stomach acid wears teeth and the throat; the swelling in your cheeks is from your salivary glands.","Have you had palpitations, dizziness or cramps? I'd like a blood test today."],"hear":"\"Heartburn, and my cheeks look puffy. No palpitations.\"","mark":"Physical risks explained; potassium check agreed"},{"h":"Treatment options","say":["The first step is guided self-help: a book-based programme with short sessions with a therapist over about four months.","The core is eating regularly: three meals and two or three snacks, no long gaps, and a food and mood diary.","If it isn't helping after a month, we move to a longer therapy called CBT for eating disorders, about 20 sessions."],"hear":"\"But if I eat breakfast and lunch I'll put weight on.\""},{"h":"Medication and checks","say":["That's the commonest fear. Treatment aims for regular eating, not weight change, and most people's weight stays fairly steady.","Tablets aren't the first treatment. Fluoxetine can be added later if therapy alone isn't enough.","After being sick, rinse with water and wait an hour before brushing. Please see a dentist."],"hear":"\"OK. I'd rather not take tablets anyway.\"","mark":"Treatment plan agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Could you sum up, in your words, what's going on and what we'll do first?","I'll refer you for guided self-help, write to your GP, and give you the Beat helpline.","If you get palpitations, faint or vomit blood, call 111 or 999. We'll review in four weeks."],"hear":"\"Bulimia, the starving makes the bingeing worse, so I start with breakfast and lunch.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Binge with loss of control","Dinner then cereal, crisps, chocolate; 'I can't stop'","Objective binges"],["Compensation","Vomits with fingers every weekday; gym and fasting","Purging plus restriction: bulimia, not binge eating disorder"],["Self-worth and shape","Mirror checking, avoids meals out, 'still too big'","Core overvaluation of weight and shape"],["The cycle","Weekends with regular meals: no binges","Restriction drives binges: regular eating is the lever"],["Physical consequences","Heartburn, puffy cheeks, knuckle calluses; BMI 22","Purging effects; check potassium; anorexia excluded"]]},"key":"The treatment target is regular eating, not stopping the vomiting by willpower. Break the daytime restriction and the binges, then the vomiting, usually fall away.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5,7],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-27":{"name":"Mental state in the place of safety: from tonight back to the first sign","why":"Gives a complete, structured picture of a first psychotic episode that the AMHP and admitting team can act on.","frame":["Appearance and behaviour","Thought content: persecution, reference, delusional perception","Perception: voices and other senses","Cannabis and substances","Cognition and insight","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: name, injuries, section 136","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Can I check your name?","The police brought you here because they were worried about your safety on the road tonight. Are you hurt anywhere?","You're on a section called 136. It means a doctor and a social worker check how you are. You haven't done anything wrong."],"hear":"\"Declan. No, I'm not hurt. They were shouting at me.\""},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","say":["(Observe: clothing, self-care, eye contact, restlessness, glancing round as if listening.)","You keep looking at the door. Is something worrying you right now?","Could you tell me about tonight?"],"hear":"\"I'm checking they're not coming. I had to get out of the flat.\"","mark":"Hypervigilance and possible response to unseen stimuli noted"},{"h":"Speech and mood","say":["How have you been feeling in yourself, in your mood?","How have you been sleeping? Any times you've felt unusually high or full of energy?"],"hear":"\"Scared. Not low, just scared. I haven't slept properly in weeks.\"","mark":"Anxious, not depressed or elated"},{"h":"Thought form and content","say":["Who do you think is behind this, and what do they want?","When did it start? What was the very first thing that made you realise?","Do things on the TV or online seem to be about you? How sure are you about all this?"],"hear":"\"A car flashed its lights outside and I just knew they'd found out I'm a grass. The news sends me warnings. I'm a hundred percent sure.\"","mark":"Persecutory and referential delusions; delusional perception"},{"h":"Perception","say":["Tell me about the shouting. Whose voices, how many, and where do they come from?","Are they speaking to you, or discussing you? Do they give you orders?","Can you hear them now, here? Any strange smells, tastes, sights or feelings on your skin?"],"hear":"\"Men's voices, calling me a grass, saying they'll get me. I can hear them now, faintly. Nothing else.\"","mark":"Second-person hallucinations, heard in the suite"},{"h":"Cannabis and substances","say":["How much cannabis do you smoke a day, and what kind?","When did you last have some? Has it gone up recently?","Any alcohol, cocaine, spice or other drugs?"],"hear":"\"Skunk, about five joints a day, more since I lost my job. Last one around ten. Few cans a week, nothing else.\"","mark":"Escalating daily high-potency cannabis"},{"h":"Cognition and insight","say":["What's the date today, and what is this place?","Could anything else explain the voices, like stress, lack of sleep or the cannabis?","Would you accept help to feel safer?"],"hear":"\"Sunday, the hospital. It's not in my head, it's real. But I'd take help to sleep.\"","mark":"Orientated; insight absent; open to help"},{"h":"Risk","say":["When you ran into the road, were you trying to hurt yourself?","Any thoughts of wanting to be dead?","Have you thought of protecting yourself from them? Do you have anything at home for that?"],"hear":"\"No, I was just getting away. I'd never kill myself. There's a hammer by my door in case they come.\"","mark":"Accidental harm risk; defensive weapon"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Thank you, Declan. I believe this has been terrifying.","I think your mind is under a lot of strain, possibly made worse by the cannabis, and you need more help than you can get at home tonight.","A social worker and another doctor will see you this morning to decide the best plan. You'll stay here, safe, until then."],"hear":"\"I just want them to stop.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Thought content","Gang think he's a grass; car flashed lights and he 'just knew'; TV warnings","Persecutory and referential delusions; delusional perception"],["Perception","Men's voices calling him a grass, threatening; heard in the suite","Second-person auditory hallucinations, extracampine quality"],["Cannabis","Skunk five joints a day, rising since job loss","Contributing factor; duration points beyond intoxication"],["Cognition and insight","Orientated, attentive; 'it's real' but wants sleep","Not delirium; no insight, partial acceptance of help"],["Risk","Ran into road to escape; hammer by door; no suicidal ideas","Accidental harm, vulnerability, defensive violence risk"]]},"key":"Follow the story back to the first odd moment and ask if he hears them now. The delusional perception and voices in the suite separate an evolving psychosis from a bad night on cannabis.","objmap":[[1],[2,4],[5],[6,7],[8,9]]},"c-t-28":{"name":"Since discharge: the relapse signature check","why":"Catches an early relapse, finds its cause, and turns partial insight into a plan he will follow.","frame":["Stress and routine since discharge","Medication adherence and side effects","Early warning signs versus last relapse","Positive symptoms","Mood and negative symptoms","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Marcus Adeyemi?","Thanks for seeing me, Marcus. The staff here say you've mostly stayed in your room lately, and they wanted to check you're alright.","Could you tell me how things have been since you left the ward?"],"hear":"\"Fine at first. Then it got noisy.\""},{"h":"Since discharge","say":["What's a normal day like here?","How are you getting on with the other residents and staff?","How are you sleeping and eating?"],"hear":"\"They're loud, the kitchen's filthy. I stay in. I get takeaways. Sleep's bad.\"","mark":"Stressors, withdrawal and poor sleep identified"},{"h":"Medication and use","say":["How have you been getting on with your olanzapine?","Lots of people stop medicines when side effects bother them. Has that happened?","Any cannabis, alcohol or other drugs?"],"hear":"\"I stopped a month ago. I put on two stone. A few beers a week, no weed.\"","mark":"Non-adherence due to weight gain"},{"h":"Early warning signs","say":["What were the first signs last time you got unwell?","Have any of those come back?","What do you make of the changes yourself?"],"hear":"\"Last time I stopped sleeping and stayed in. Then I thought the neighbours were watching. Maybe I'm a bit stressed.\"","mark":"Relapse signature matches; partial insight"},{"h":"Psychotic symptoms","say":["Has anyone been bothering you or doing things behind your back?","How sure are you of that? What have you done about it?","Do you hear anyone talking when no one is around? What do they say, and do they tell you to do things?","Do you feel your thoughts or actions are controlled by anyone?"],"hear":"\"Staff read my post. A man's voice says what I'm doing: 'He's getting up now.' It doesn't tell me to do things.\"","mark":"Persecutory belief and third-person commenting voice"},{"h":"Mood and negative symptoms","say":["How's your mood been? Any feeling that things are hopeless?","Do you still enjoy anything? Do you feel you've lost your get-up-and-go?","Is staying in your room more about feeling safe, or not having the energy?"],"hear":"\"Not depressed. I stay in to be safe. I can't be bothered with much, though.\"","mark":"Withdrawal mostly fear-driven; some loss of drive"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Any thoughts of self-harm or of not wanting to live?","Have you felt like getting back at the staff, or anyone here?","Are you smoking in your room? Have you thought of leaving the hostel?"],"hear":"\"No. I'd never hurt anyone. I smoke by the window. I've thought about leaving.\"","mark":"Self-neglect, fire and leaving risk; no intent to harm"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["I think stopping the olanzapine, plus the stress here, has brought some of the old symptoms back early.","There's a medicine called aripiprazole that's much kinder to weight. Would you try it?","Your care coordinator will visit twice a week, and here's the crisis number. We'll catch it early this time."],"hear":"\"OK. As long as it's not hospital.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Medication","Stopped olanzapine a month ago; gained two stone","Non-adherence from a side effect: the trigger"],["Early warning signs","Poor sleep, staying in, then suspicion: same as last time","Relapse signature repeating"],["Positive symptoms","Staff read his post; male voice commenting on his actions","Persecutory delusion; running commentary"],["Mood and drive","Not depressed; stays in to be safe; less motivation","Fearful withdrawal with some negative symptoms"],["Risk","Poor intake, smoking in room, may leave; no intent to harm","Self-neglect, fire and vulnerability risks"]]},"key":"Ask why he withdrew, not just whether he did. Fear, low mood and negative symptoms look the same from the corridor but need different responses.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-30":{"name":"The safety plan, step by step, in his words","why":"Gives him a concrete, personal plan he can follow in the hardest hour, with the main means removed.","frame":["Warning signs and triggers","Own coping steps","People and places","Professional contacts","Making it safer"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Rhys Hollingworth?","Thanks for coming in, Rhys. I know you've lived with these thoughts for a long time, and coming back takes effort.","Today I'd like us to write a plan together for the hard moments. Would that be okay?"],"hear":"\"Go on then.\""},{"h":"Current risk check","say":["Are the thoughts with you today? Any plan or intent to act on them?","When they're at their worst, what do you think about doing?","What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"Not today. When it's bad I think about taking tablets. My lad keeps me here.\"","mark":"No current intent; overdose thoughts; son protective"},{"h":"Warning signs","say":["What usually sets the thoughts off?","What's the first thing you notice: thoughts, feelings, what you do?","Do you drink when they come?"],"hear":"\"Rows with my ex about seeing my son. Then I'm pacing, ignoring my phone, opening cans by six.\"","mark":"Triggers and warning signs written"},{"h":"Own coping steps","say":["When the thoughts come, what have you done that helps, even a bit?","Some people use a delay rule: wait 30 minutes and do something physical. Would that work for you?","There are apps like Stay Alive with grounding and reasons for living. Worth a try?"],"hear":"\"Walking the dog. A hot shower. Fishing videos. I could try the waiting thing.\"","mark":"Personal coping steps written"},{"h":"People and places","say":["Who could you be with, or where could you go, to take your mind off things?","Who could you tell if it gets bad? What would you send them?","What stops you reaching out?"],"hear":"\"Gaz, maybe Tom. My sister. I don't want to be a burden.\"","mark":"Named contacts; burden belief addressed"},{"h":"Professional contacts","say":["In hours, your care coordinator and GP. Out of hours, the crisis line or 111, option 2.","Samaritans is free, any time, on 116 123. If you'd rather text, SHOUT is 85258.","If you feel you can't keep safe: 999 or A&E."],"hear":"\"Texting's easier than talking, to be honest.\"","mark":"Crisis contacts written"},{"h":"Making it safer","say":["What do you have at home that you might use on a bad night?","Could you take the old tablets to the pharmacy today?","Could we agree no drinking alone after a row?"],"hear":"\"There's a drawer of old pills. I'll drop them at the chemist on the way home.\"","mark":"Means reduction agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Let's read it back together. Would you change anything?","Take a photo on your phone and keep the paper copy by the kettle.","Your care coordinator will review it with you in two weeks, and we can talk then about longer-term help."],"hear":"\"It actually feels like mine this time.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Safety plan part","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Warning signs and triggers","Rows with ex about son; pacing, ignoring phone, cans by six","Predictable crisis pathway he can spot early"],["Own coping steps","Dog walk, shower, fishing videos; will try 30-minute delay","Personal, realistic first steps"],["People and places","Gaz, Tom, sister; football with son","Distraction and support, once burden fear is addressed"],["Professional contacts","Prefers text: SHOUT; Samaritans, crisis line, 111 option 2","Contacts that suit how he copes"],["Making it safer","Drawer of old tablets; drinking alone","Means reduction today; alcohol plan"]]},"key":"A plan he wrote with you, in his words, with a specific action on the tablets today, beats any leaflet. Ask what has helped before suggesting anything.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-t-32":{"name":"Getting to yes: fear first, then reasons, then the plan","why":"Turns a defensive refusal into an informed choice, while keeping him safe whatever he decides.","frame":["His fears","Why the test matters","Honesty about leave and police","What happened on leave","Psychosis and physical risk","The plan either way"],"steps":[{"h":"Calm opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward doctors. The nurses are worried about you since you came back.","I'm not here to tell you off. Can we sit down for a few minutes?"],"hear":"\"I'm fine. Leave me alone.\""},{"h":"Why he is refusing","say":["The nurses say you don't want to give a urine sample. That's your right. Can you tell me why?","What do you think would happen if it came back positive?","What's the worst bit of that for you?"],"hear":"\"Last time I got sent home straight away. And you'll call the police.\"","mark":"Fears identified: leave, discharge, police"},{"h":"Home and voices","say":["Being sent home: what's making that so hard right now?","What are the voices saying about the staff, or the sample?"],"hear":"\"No electric at the flat. And the voices say you'll use my piss against me.\"","mark":"Psychosis driving refusal identified"},{"h":"Why the test matters, and honesty","say":["Those jerky movements worry me. Some drugs can cause them, and so can your medication. The test helps us know which.","The result stays with your care team; we don't give it to the police.","I'll be honest: your consultant will look at your leave either way. Being open counts for you."],"hear":"\"So you won't grass me up?\"","mark":"Purpose, confidentiality and leave explained"},{"h":"What happened on leave","say":["Talk me through your leave. Where did you go?","Did you take anything? What, and when?","Did anyone ask you for money, or to bring anything back?"],"hear":"\"Smoked a bit of spice with Dean. Now he says I owe him forty and wants my tablets.\"","mark":"Exploitation and debt identified"},{"h":"Ask, offer choices","say":["With all that in mind, how do you feel about doing the test?","You could do it in private, and watch the nurse seal the pot.","If you'd like a few minutes to think, that's fine too."],"hear":"\"Fine. But I'm doing it on my own.\"","mark":"Agreement reached"},{"h":"Plan either way, and close","say":["The nurses will check your pulse, temperature and blood pressure, and do a heart tracing and a blood test.","I'll tell the nurse in charge about Dean, so we can keep you safe from him.","If you feel chest pain, very hot or shaky, tell staff straight away."],"hear":"\"Am I in trouble?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Fears","Losing leave, police, sent home","Answer each honestly; correct the police myth"],["Home","No electricity at the flat","Care coordinator task; not a reason to stay unwell"],["Voices","'They'll use my urine against me'","Psychosis is driving refusal: explain the process concretely"],["Leave","Spice with Dean; owes £40; asked for tablets","Intoxication plus exploitation: safeguarding"],["Physical","Jerks, fast pulse","NEWS2, ECG, CK; tell dystonia from intoxication"]]},"key":"Persuasion comes after understanding: name his fear, answer it honestly, and never trade a promise about leave or a threat for a sample.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5],[6,7]]},"c-t-33":{"name":"Behaviour change in LD: what's new, why now, how risky","why":"Finds the illness, the triggers and the risks behind a change in behaviour, in Callum's own words.","frame":["What has changed","Voices and beliefs","Mood, sleep and eating","Body and pain","Life changes and substances","Risk and vulnerability"],"steps":[{"h":"Adapted opening","say":["Hello Callum, I'm Dr Chinonso. I'm a doctor who helps with worries and feelings.","I'll ask short questions. If one is confusing, tell me. We can have breaks.","Would you like Gemma in here, or just us for now?"],"hear":"\"Just us. OK.\""},{"h":"What has changed","say":["Gemma says things have been different lately. What has been different for you?","When did it start? Was it before or after the garden centre stopped?","What happens at night?"],"hear":"\"The man talks through the wall. I can't sleep.\"","mark":"Onset about five weeks ago identified"},{"h":"The voice and the belief","say":["Tell me about the man. Where did you first see him?","When he talks, do you hear it with your ears, like my voice, or is it a thought?","How sure are you that he put a chip in your phone? A bit sure, or completely sure?","What have you done because of the man?"],"hear":"\"With my ears. I'm completely sure. I don't use the kitchen, he listens through the cooker.\"","mark":"Auditory hallucinations and fixed persecutory belief identified"},{"h":"Mood, anxiety, sleep, eating","say":["Do you feel sad, or scared, or something else?","Do you still like your train videos?","How many hours do you sleep? What do you eat each day?"],"hear":"\"Scared. Not sad. I still watch trains. I eat crisps.\"","mark":"Fear without depressive symptoms"},{"h":"Body and pain","say":["Does anything hurt? Your teeth, your tummy, your head?","Are you going to the toilet OK?","Any new tablets, or any fits or funny turns?"],"hear":"\"Nothing hurts. No new tablets.\"","mark":"No physical cause found"},{"h":"Life changes, substances, exploitation","say":["Has anything changed at your flat or in your week?","Has anyone new come to your flat?","Do you smoke anything or drink alcohol? Who gives it to you?","Who looks after your bank card?"],"hear":"\"Garden centre stopped. Kai comes round, gives me smoke, uses my card.\"","mark":"Routine loss, cannabis and possible exploitation identified"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Have you hurt anyone, or wanted to, because of the man?","Have you hurt yourself, or wanted to die?","Do you go out at night to get away?"],"hear":"\"I punched the wall once. I don't want to hurt anyone.\"","mark":"Risk to others low; vulnerability high"},{"h":"Summary, consent for collateral, close","say":["Let me say back what I heard. Tell me if I get it wrong.","Can I talk to Gemma about how you were before?","Thank you for answering so many questions."],"hear":"\"Yes, Gemma can come in.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Voices and belief","Man's voice through walls, chip in phone, completely sure","Hallucinations and a persecutory delusion: psychosis"],["Mood and basics","Scared, not sad; sleeps 3-4 hours; crisps only","Anxiety from psychosis; self-care slipping"],["Body","No pain, normal bowels, no fits","Physical cause less likely; still do bloods"],["Life and substances","Placement ended; Kai gives cannabis, uses his card","Triggers plus financial exploitation"],["Risk","Punched wall; no intent to harm","Low risk to others; high vulnerability"]]},"key":"What separates excellent from good is asking who comes to the flat and who has his money: exploitation often sits behind the substance use and the relapse.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-35":{"name":"OCD history: thought, ritual, cost, risk","why":"Builds a complete, evidenced picture of OCD and its severity while making it safe for her to disclose.","frame":["Obsessions","Compulsions and avoidance","Course and triggers","Impact and severity","Comorbidity","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and normalise","say":["Thanks for coming, Priya. Your GP said some worries have got much harder to manage lately.","Some questions may feel personal. Many people have thoughts they find upsetting, and nothing you say will shock me."],"hear":"\"It's taking over. I feel stupid even saying it.\""},{"h":"Obsessions","say":["Do upsetting thoughts or pictures keep coming into your mind, even when you try to push them away?","Where do they come from: your own mind, or somewhere outside?","Do they seem sensible to you, or over the top?"],"hear":"\"Pictures of Anaya getting really ill from germs I bring home. They're mine. I know it's over the top.\"","mark":"Contamination obsessions identified"},{"h":"Compulsions and avoidance","say":["What do you do to make the worry settle?","How many hours a day does it take altogether?","What happens if you try not to do it? What do you avoid?"],"hear":"\"Hour-long showers, bleaching handles, asking Dev if she's OK. Four or five hours a day.\"","mark":"Washing, cleaning and reassurance compulsions identified"},{"h":"Course and triggers","say":["When did this first start?","What changed in the last few months?","Have you had any help for it before?"],"hear":"\"Checking since I was 19. Worse since I moved onto the wards and she started nursery.\"","mark":"Recent triggers identified"},{"h":"Impact","say":["How is it affecting work?","How is it affecting you and Dev, and caring for Anaya?","What about your sleep, and your hands?"],"hear":"\"I've been late twice. We argue. Bed after one. My hands bleed.\"","mark":"Severe functional impact"},{"h":"Comorbidity","say":["What's your mood been like lately? Are you still enjoying things?","Any twitches or movements you can't stop?","How much do you drink? Any other drugs?"],"hear":"\"Low some days, but I still love being with Anaya. No twitches. A glass or two at weekends.\""},{"h":"Harm thoughts and suicide risk","say":["Some people with OCD get unwanted thoughts of harming someone they love. Has that happened to you?","Has it ever got so bad you've thought you'd be better off dead?"],"hear":"\"Pictures of giving her the wrong medicine. It horrifies me. No, I'd never hurt myself.\"","mark":"Ego-dystonic harm thoughts; no suicidal ideation"},{"h":"Summary and explanation","say":["Let me check I've understood.","This sounds like obsessive-compulsive disorder. It's common and very treatable.","Next time we'll plan treatment together; here's a leaflet in the meantime."],"hear":"\"Can this actually get better?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Obsessions","Images of daughter ill from germs; own, resisted, excessive","Contamination obsessions with insight"],["Compulsions","Showers, bleaching, hand-washing, reassurance, 4-5 h/day","Severe compulsions plus family accommodation"],["Harm thoughts","Images of giving the wrong medicine; horrified; avoids","Ego-dystonic harm obsessions: not a risk"],["Impact","Late for work, arguments, bed after 1 am, bleeding hands","Major interference: severe OCD"],["Comorbidity","Low some days, still enjoys daughter; no tics","Mild low mood; monitor"]]},"key":"Ask about harm thoughts and respond calmly: naming them as a common OCD symptom, not a danger, is what makes her disclose fully and trust the plan.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-36":{"name":"Explaining ERP: the cycle, the ladder, the fear","why":"Turns a frightened, uninformed patient into one who understands why facing fears works and turns up to therapy.","frame":["The OCD cycle","Exposure","Response prevention","The fear ladder","Homework and relapse prevention"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","Thanks for coming, Tom. I understand you've been referred for CBT and want to know what it involves.","Could we start with how the OCD is affecting you now? Then I'll explain the therapy and answer your questions."],"hear":"\"Yeah, no idea what it is. Someone said it means touching dirty stuff.\""},{"h":"His OCD now","say":["How many times a day are you washing, and how long does it take altogether?","What do you use on your hands? How is it affecting work?","And your mood lately? Ever feel like hurting yourself, or that you'd be better off dead?"],"hear":"\"Up to sixty times. Bleach sometimes. Pallets at work are the worst. Mood's fine, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Contamination washing confirmed; mood and risk checked"},{"h":"How OCD works","say":["CBT is the main talking treatment for OCD. It works on the here and now: how your thoughts, feelings and actions feed each other.","Let me draw it. You touch a pallet, the thought comes, 'germs', anxiety shoots up, you wash, and it drops.","That relief teaches your brain washing kept you safe, so next time the urge is stronger. Does that fit?"],"hear":"\"That's exactly it. It's like a loop.\"","mark":"Cycle mapped to his washing"},{"h":"What CBT with ERP is","say":["The main part is called exposure and response prevention. Exposure means facing a trigger on purpose.","Response prevention means not washing afterwards. The anxiety rises, peaks, then falls by itself, usually within the hour.","Each time you do it, the anxiety falls faster. Your brain learns it can cope without washing."],"hear":"\"So you want me to touch dirty things and not wash? How does that help?\"","mark":"ERP principle explained"},{"h":"How sessions run","say":["A therapist from psychology sees you weekly, about an hour, usually 12 to 20 sessions, as an outpatient.","First you build a fear ladder together, rating each step out of ten, starting low.","Between sessions you practise most days and rate your anxiety. You set the goals with the therapist."],"hear":"\"Who decides what I do each week?\""},{"h":"His worries","say":["You look worried. What's going through your mind?","That fear is normal. You won't start with pallets; the first step might be a door handle, and you choose when.","Normal hygiene stays. On the question of whether it works: most people who finish it improve a lot."],"hear":"\"I don't think I could not wash. The anxiety would be too much.\"","mark":"Main fear identified: the anxiety itself"},{"h":"Other options","say":["Some people also take a medicine called sertraline. It's optional and works alongside therapy.","It starts at 50 mg and can go up; in OCD it can take up to 12 weeks to work.","If OCD is severe, using both together works better than either alone."],"hear":"\"I'd rather try the therapy first.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["How would you explain to Kasia how the therapy will help?","Here's the OCD-UK leaflet and the NHS page. Use a moisturiser after washing, and please stop the bleach.","I'll see you in six weeks. If your mood drops or you feel hopeless, call your GP or 111 option 2."],"hear":"\"Face it bit by bit, don't wash, and the anxiety goes down on its own. Start small.\""}],"table":{"cols":["ERP element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The OCD cycle","Pallet, 'germs', panic, wash, relief, then the urge returns","Washing is maintained by the relief it brings"],["Exposure","Avoids pallets; washes up to 60 times a day","Avoidance and washing stop him learning the fear is false"],["Response prevention","'I couldn't not wash'","The core skill to build; fear of anxiety is the main barrier"],["The fear ladder","Door handle feels manageable, pallets feel impossible","A graded start at about 3/10 will engage him"],["Homework and relapse prevention","Partner checks his hands","Daily practice and family involvement to stop reassurance"]]},"key":"Response prevention is what makes exposure work: touching the trigger and then washing teaches nothing. Breathing skills can help him start a step but must not become a new ritual during it.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-37":{"name":"Behind 'work stress': event, three clusters, then what else","why":"Finds the trauma hidden behind a vague referral and builds an evidenced PTSD diagnosis with comorbidity and risk.","frame":["The event","Re-experiencing","Avoidance and numbing","Sense of threat","What else: mood, alcohol, risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Adebayo Coker?","Thanks for coming, Bayo. Your GP said work has been very stressful and the tablets haven't helped.","I want to understand what's been going on. Where would you like to start?"],"hear":"\"Not at all. I can't concentrate, I'm snapping at everyone. I just want to feel normal.\""},{"h":"Stress and trigger","say":["When did all this begin? Did anything happen around then?","Only what you're comfortable with: what happened that day?","Were you hurt? Did you hit your head? Are there gaps in what you remember? Has there been a police statement or an inquest?"],"hear":"\"Five months ago a man stepped in front of my train. I saw his face. Then the brakes, and nothing I could do.\"","mark":"Traumatic event identified"},{"h":"Re-experiencing","say":["Does it come back when you don't want it to, like you're seeing it again?","What about dreams about it?","What happens when something reminds you, like a horn or a brake?"],"hear":"\"Every day I see his face. Nightmares most nights. A horn and I'm back there.\"","mark":"Re-experiencing identified"},{"h":"Avoidance and arousal","say":["Do you keep away from anything that reminds you: the line, the cab, talking about it?","Have your feelings changed? Do you feel cut off or numb, even with family?","Are you jumpier, more on guard? How is sleep, concentration and your temper?"],"hear":"\"I swap shifts to avoid that line. I feel flat with the kids. I jump at everything, sleep three hours, and I froze on a platform last week.\"","mark":"Avoidance and sense of threat identified"},{"h":"Comorbidity","say":["Is your mood low most of the day, every day? Can you still enjoy anything?","How much are you drinking: how many cans, what strength? Any other drugs? Drinking before a shift?","Any panic attacks? Any voices or feeling watched?"],"hear":"\"Low sometimes, but the kids still make me smile. Four cans most nights to sleep. No drugs, no voices.\"","mark":"Harmful alcohol use identified; no depressive episode"},{"h":"Treatment so far","say":["How are you taking the paroxetine? Any doses missed?","Any side effects? Has anyone offered you talking therapy?"],"hear":"\"Most mornings. Felt sick the first two weeks. Nobody's mentioned therapy.\""},{"h":"Risk and work","say":["Do you ever feel life's not worth it, or think about ending your life? Any plans?","What keeps you going?","With the sleep, the startle and the drinking, how safe do you feel driving right now?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think what's the point. I'd never do it, my kids. Honestly, I'm scared I'll freeze again.\"","mark":"Passive thoughts, no plan; not safe for safety-critical work"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Let me summarise. Since the incident you've been reliving it, avoiding reminders and living on high alert.","That's post-traumatic stress disorder. The main treatment is trauma-focused talking therapy, and it works well.","Please don't drive trains until occupational health sees you, and let's cut the drinking down. Can you tell me the plan back?"],"hear":"\"So it's PTSD, I need the therapy, and no driving for now. That's a relief, actually.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Cluster","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The event","Man stepped in front of his train 5 months ago; saw his face","A trauma involving death, witnessed close up"],["Re-experiencing","Daily images of the face, nightmares most nights","Core PTSD feature in the present"],["Avoidance and numbing","Swaps shifts, won't talk about it, flat with children","Avoidance with emotional numbing"],["Sense of threat","Startle, scanning platforms, 3 hours' sleep, froze at work","Hyperarousal, now affecting safety"],["What else","4 cans nightly; passive thoughts, no plan; still enjoys kids","Harmful alcohol use; no depressive episode; low current suicide risk"]]},"key":"Reliving is not remembering: PTSD intrusions feel as if it is happening now, with fear and body sensations. Ordinary distressing memories are recalled as past events.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-t-39":{"name":"Is it dependence? Drug by drug, then the six features","why":"Builds a complete, evidenced drug history that confirms dependence and finds the risks that need action today.","frame":["Craving","Loss of control","Tolerance","Withdrawal","Priority over other things","Carrying on despite harm"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Liam O'Connor?","Thanks for coming, Liam. Your GP mentioned you've been using a few different drugs.","I won't judge. What you say stays with our team and your GP, unless someone, including the baby, is at serious risk. Shall we start?"],"hear":"\"Mainly gear, a bit of other stuff. Yeah, go on.\""},{"h":"Drugs used","say":["Let's go through each one: how much heroin, how often, and what does it cost you?","Do you smoke it or inject? Where do you inject? Ever shared needles, spoons, filters or water?","What else: tablets, crack, alcohol, cannabis? When did you last use?"],"hear":"\"About a gram a day, forty quid. Smoke it, inject my arms when I'm skint. Blues, four to six a day. Crack at weekends. Last had some at seven this morning.\"","mark":"Heroin, street benzodiazepine and crack use identified"},{"h":"Dependence features","say":["How strong is the urge when you wake? Have you tried to cut down, and what happened?","Do you need more now than at first to get the same effect?","What happens if you go a day without? Tell me in your words.","What's it pushed out of your life? Have you kept going even when it was clearly harming you?"],"hear":"\"First thing I think of. Can't cut down. Need four times what I used to. Without it: sweats, aches, the runs, can't sleep. It comes before food. Even after the abscess I kept going.\"","mark":"All six dependence features present"},{"h":"Complications","say":["Have you ever overdosed? What happened?","Any abscesses, clots, or infections? When were you last tested for hepatitis and HIV?","How's your mood? Ever hear things others don't? Any trouble with money or the police?"],"hear":"\"Overdosed last year, heroin and blues. Groin abscess. Tested three years ago, scared to go back. Mood's up and down. Owe my dealer. Got done for shoplifting.\"","mark":"Overdose, abscess and BBV risk identified"},{"h":"Past treatment","say":["What treatment have you had before? What helped most?","What's the longest you've been off heroin, and what brought you back?","Have you got a naloxone kit?"],"hear":"\"Methadone at 27, clean eight months. Then three months inside, and back on it the week I got out. No naloxone.\"","mark":"Relapse after release from prison identified"},{"h":"Background","say":["When did you first try drugs, and how did it build up?","How were things growing up? Anything difficult?","How are you and Chloe? Does she use? What's the plan for the baby?"],"hear":"\"Weed at 14, heroin at 19 with mates. Dad drank. Chloe doesn't use; she's five months gone. I want to sort it before the baby comes.\""},{"h":"Risk","say":["Do you use with blues or drink at the same time? Do you ever use alone?","Have you ever thought of ending your life, or was the overdose an accident?","Where do you keep drugs in the flat? The midwife and children's services need to know so they can support you both. Can we do that together?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes alone, yeah. The overdose was an accident. In a drawer. Will they take the baby?\"","mark":"High overdose risk; safeguarding concern for unborn baby"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Let me sum up. Heroin every day, street diazepam, cravings, withdrawal, more needed, and it comes first despite harm. That's opioid dependence.","It's a health condition with good treatment: methadone or buprenorphine, a keyworker, and support for you and Chloe.","Take this naloxone kit; I'll show you how. Urine test today, blood tests offered, and I'll see you next week."],"hear":"\"OK. If it's for the baby, I'll do it.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Dependence feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Craving","'First thing I think of when I wake'","Strong compulsion to use"],["Loss of control","Tries to cut down, can't","Impaired control over use"],["Tolerance and withdrawal","Needs four times the first amount; sweats, aches, diarrhoea within 12 h, uses to relieve it","Physiological dependence"],["Priority over other things","Comes before food, bills, work","Drug use has taken over"],["Carrying on despite harm","Abscess, overdose, debt, prison, still using","Persistence despite clear harm"]]},"key":"Count the features over the past year, with his own examples. Daily use alone is not dependence; three or more features, including withdrawal and loss of control, are.","objmap":[[1,2],[2,5],[3],[4,6,7],[8]]},"c-t-41":{"name":"Memory or worry? Sort attention from loss, then explain the results","why":"Turns a frightened, self-testing patient into someone who understands why this is anxiety and not dementia.","frame":["Pattern of forgetting","Generalised anxiety features","Other causes: mood, substances, body","Daily function","Test results"],"steps":[{"h":"Name the fear, promise the answer","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Linda Okoro?","I can hear how worried you are. I will go through all your results with you today.","First, could you tell me what changes you've noticed in your memory and how you've been feeling?"],"hear":"\"Yes. Is it dementia? I've been so frightened.\""},{"h":"Memory: examples, course, fluctuation","say":["Give me a couple of recent examples of things you've forgotten.","Is it more that things don't go in, or that things you knew have gone?","Has it got steadily worse, or does it come and go with stress?"],"hear":"\"I walk into a room and forget why. I lose my place in a book. It's worse when I'm wound up; on holiday it was fine.\"","mark":"Attention-type, fluctuating complaints identified"},{"h":"Anxiety: worry, body, sleep, focus","say":["What do you find yourself worrying about, and can you switch it off?","How does it feel in your body: tense, restless, on edge?","How long has the worrying been this bad, and how is your sleep?"],"hear":"\"Money, my health, my son's job, everything. My shoulders are like rocks. I lie awake for hours. It's been over a year.\"","mark":"Generalised anxiety identified"},{"h":"Mood, panic, substances, body","say":["Have you been low or lost interest in things for weeks at a time? Any sudden panic attacks?","What medicines do you take, including for pain? How much do you drink?","Any hot flushes or menopause symptoms, or changes in weight, heat or cold tolerance?"],"hear":"\"Some low days but I still enjoy my grandson. Codeine most days for my back. A glass or two of wine in the evening.\"","mark":"Depression and psychosis screened; codeine and alcohol noted"},{"h":"Function, family, background","say":["Do you still manage money, cooking, driving and getting about on your own?","Tell me about your mum's illness and your part in her care.","What else has changed in the last couple of years?"],"hear":"\"I do everything myself. I looked after Mum until she died last year. I retired early with my back and money's tight.\"","mark":"Function intact; carer stress and retirement identified"},{"h":"Brief risk","say":["When things feel at their worst, has life ever felt not worth living?","Are you managing to eat and look after yourself?"],"hear":"\"No, never. I'm eating fine.\""},{"h":"Explain each result","say":["Your CT scan showed no shrinkage or stroke damage, and the blood tests ruled out the treatable causes of memory problems.","Your memory score has gone up from 82 to 88. Dementia doesn't usually get better like that.","Most of the points you lost were on concentration tasks, the ones anxiety affects most."],"hear":"\"So the score going up is a good thing?\""},{"h":"Name the diagnosis and close","say":["Putting it together, this is generalised anxiety disorder, not dementia. The worry is taking up the space your attention needs.","It's very treatable, with talking therapy or medication, and your concentration should improve as it settles. I'll write to your GP.","Could you sum up for me what we've found? What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"So it's my nerves, not my brain going. I feel I can breathe again.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Pattern of forgetting","Forgets why she enters rooms, loses thread of books; worse with stress","Attention failure that fluctuates, not progressive memory loss"],["Anxiety features","Worry about money, health, son; tension, restlessness, poor sleep, over a year","Generalised anxiety disorder"],["Other causes","Codeine most days, wine most evenings; no depressive episode or psychosis","Contributors to address, not the main cause"],["Daily function","Drives, manages money, cooks, never gets lost","Does not meet dementia's requirement of functional decline"],["Test results","Normal CT and bloods; ACE-III 82 to 88, losses on attention","Improving, attention-weighted profile consistent with anxiety"]]},"key":"Ask whether information never went in or has gone missing. Anxiety blocks attention, so new things don't stick; dementia erodes what was stored and steadily erodes daily function.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-42":{"name":"Risk in every direction, then a plan she helps build","why":"Keeps a frightened mother on side while protecting a baby who has already been hurt.","frame":["Her mood and her own risk","Risk to Alfie from Leah","Risk to Alfie from Dean","Risk to Leah from Dean","Her fears and what might derail the plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Greet both, agree the plan","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Leah Brennan?","Hello Alfie. Thank you for coming, Leah. Your midwife asked me to see you because things have felt hard since he arrived.","I'd like to hear how you're both doing, including how things are at home, and then we'll plan together. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Yes, and this is Alfie. I suppose so.\""},{"h":"Her mood, sleep, bonding, past episode","say":["How have you been feeling since he was born?","How is sleep when Alfie is asleep? How do you feel when you hold him?","You had depression at 19. What helped then, and why did you stop it?"],"hear":"\"Low and crying most days. Even when he sleeps, I can't. I love him. Sertraline helped last time; I stopped when I felt better.\"","mark":"Probable postnatal depression identified"},{"h":"Risk to Leah and from Leah","say":["Has it ever felt like life isn't worth going on with, or that you'd harm yourself?","Many new mums get frightening thoughts about the baby. Have you had any thoughts of harming Alfie?","Any alcohol or drugs? Any times you've felt high, racing, or heard things others don't?"],"hear":"\"No, never. I'd never hurt him. I don't drink. Nothing like that.\"","mark":"Low risk to self and to Alfie from Leah"},{"h":"The shaking: facts and Alfie now","say":["Can you tell me exactly what happened when Dean shook him?","How long for, and what state was Dean in? How was Alfie afterwards?","Has anyone checked Alfie since? Any sleepiness, being sick, poor feeding or fits?"],"hear":"\"Last Thursday at night. Dean was drunk, Alfie wouldn't stop crying. He shook him for a few seconds till I grabbed him. Nobody's seen him.\"","mark":"Serious risk to Alfie identified"},{"h":"Dean and home: every kind of abuse","say":["How much is Dean drinking and smoking? Is he around Alfie when he's like that?","Has he ever pushed, hit or hurt you? Does he control money, your phone or who you see?","Has he ever pressured you into sex or anything sexual? Have you ever wanted to hurt him?"],"hear":"\"Every night. He pushed me into the door when I was pregnant. He takes my card and checks my phone. Nothing sexual. I'd never hurt him.\"","mark":"Domestic abuse and coercive control identified"},{"h":"Her fears and her supports","say":["What worries you most about what happens next?","Who could you and Alfie stay with if you needed to?"],"hear":"\"That they'll take him. My mum's twenty minutes away; Dean hates me going there.\"","mark":"Fear of removal and a safe place identified"},{"h":"Honest safeguarding and a shared plan","say":["Because Alfie was shaken, I have to tell children's social care today. The aim is support for you both, and I'll tell them how protective you are.","A children's doctor should check Alfie today; I'll ring ahead. Could you and Alfie stay with your mum tonight?","I'd like to link you with an IDVA, a specialist in domestic abuse, and see you within a week. Medication is your choice; here's a leaflet on sertraline."],"hear":"\"Okay. If it keeps him safe. Mum will have us.\""},{"h":"What could get in the way, and close","say":["What might stop this plan working, like Dean finding out?","How can we reach you safely?","Tell me in your words what we've agreed. If Dean turns up or gets violent, call 999."],"hear":"\"He checks my phone, so use Mum's. A&E today, Mum's tonight, the IDVA, and you next week.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Direction of risk","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Leah to herself","Low and tearful; no suicidal thoughts, no self-harm, no substances","Low risk today; treat the depression"],["Leah to Alfie","Loves him, breastfeeds, no thoughts of harm, moves him away from smoke","Protective parent; a strength to build on"],["Dean to Alfie","Shook him while drunk, intolerant of crying; Alfie not yet checked","Significant harm: paediatric check and social care today"],["Dean to Leah","Shouting, pushed in pregnancy, controls money and phone, isolates her","Domestic abuse with coercive control: DASH and IDVA"],["Barriers to the plan","Fear of removal; Dean checks her phone","Frame referral as support; agree a safe contact route"]]},"key":"Her own risk is low; Alfie's is high. Excellent practice says so honestly, makes the referral with her rather than about her, and checks what could derail the plan.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-44":{"name":"Their picture first, then the facts: an ECT conversation","why":"Replaces a frightening film image with an accurate picture so he can make his own decision.","frame":["His ideas and fears","Why ECT for him now","What happens","Memory and side effects","His physical health","His choice and alternatives"],"steps":[{"h":"Name the worry, ask first","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Desmond Clarke?","Thank you, Des. Your consultant mentioned ECT, and I hear you're worried about your memory.","That's a fair worry. Could I hear what you've heard first, then explain it properly?"],"hear":"\"Yes. Des. I am. I've heard terrible things.\""},{"h":"His ideas, fears and hopes","say":["What have you heard about ECT, and where from?","What frightens you most about it?","If a treatment did work, what would you hope to get back?"],"hear":"\"An old film. They held a man down and he screamed. I do the crossword every morning; I'm scared I'll lose my mind. I just want to feel like me.\"","mark":"Beliefs, fears and expectations elicited"},{"h":"Why ECT, why now","say":["You've had two good courses of antidepressants and you're still very unwell, not eating, feeling a burden.","Nothing we have works better than ECT for depression this severe, and older people tend to respond especially well.","Most people improve, often within a few weeks."],"hear":"\"Why should it work when the tablets didn't?\""},{"h":"What actually happens","say":["First some checks: blood tests, a heart tracing and a visit from the anaesthetist.","On the morning you don't eat, you have a short anaesthetic and a muscle relaxant, so you're asleep and barely move. A small current causes a brief, controlled seizure; a guard protects your teeth.","You wake within minutes, have a cup of tea, and come back to the ward. Twice weekly as a rule, commonly six to twelve sessions."],"hear":"\"So I'd be asleep. That's not what I pictured.\""},{"h":"Memory and side effects, honestly","say":["Afterwards some people have a headache, aching muscles or feel muddled for an hour or so.","Memory: many people have gaps for events around the weeks of treatment. These usually improve over weeks to months; a few people say some gaps last.","We test your memory at the start and as we go, and we can give it to one side of the head to reduce the effect."],"hear":"\"Would I still be me afterwards? Could I still do my crossword?\"","mark":"Memory concern addressed specifically"},{"h":"Blood pressure and kidneys","say":["Your blood pressure rises briefly during treatment, and the anaesthetist watches and manages it.","We'll check your kidney blood tests first, and the anaesthetist will tell you whether to take your ramipril on treatment mornings."],"hear":"\"I was worried about my blood pressure. Good.\""},{"h":"His choice and alternatives","say":["This is your decision. You can say no, and if you say yes you can stop at any point.","If you'd rather not, we could add lithium or another medicine, though those usually take longer to work.","You don't need to decide today."],"hear":"\"I'd want to talk to my wife and daughter.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Could you tell me what you'll take from today?","Here's a leaflet and a short video; the ECT nurse can show you the suite.","Your consultant will talk with you about consent once you've had time. What other questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"I'm asleep, it's twice a week, my memory may be patchy but usually comes back, and it's my choice.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Topic","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["His ideas","Film image of screaming and restraint","Correct the picture first: anaesthetic, relaxant, no pain"],["His fear","Crossword every morning; fears losing his mind","Give specific, honest memory information and safeguards"],["His hope","To feel like himself again","Link ECT's purpose directly to this"],["Physical health","Blood pressure and kidneys","Workup and anaesthetic review; ramipril plan"],["His choice","Wants to talk to family","Time, leaflet, video, family meeting, consultant consent talk"]]},"key":"Reassurance is not information. Excellent practice says plainly that memory gaps around treatment are common and usually recover, explains the safeguards, and still leaves the decision with him.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-45":{"name":"Lead the conversation: name the high, then shape the plan","why":"Keeps a fast, expansive patient on track so he leaves with a diagnosis he accepts and a plan he will follow.","frame":["Manic symptoms","Why lithium stopped","Risks","Diagnosis","Treatment and setting"],"steps":[{"h":"Greet, give him a voice, set the agenda","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jamal Whitfield?","Good to meet you too, Jamal. Your GP and family have been worried, and I want to hear it from you.","Let's talk about how things have been, then what might help. Okay?"],"hear":"\"Yes! Good to meet you, doc. I'm brilliant. They worry too much.\""},{"h":"Symptoms, with a firm steer","say":["How much are you sleeping, and do you feel tired?","Tell me about the film and the people you've approached.","Can I pause you there? I want to make sure we cover what matters to you. What have you bought recently?"],"hear":"\"Two hours and I'm buzzing. I'm making a film that'll win awards. I bought a three-grand camera and a sound deck.\"","mark":"Manic symptoms identified"},{"h":"Stopping lithium","say":["When did you stop lithium, and what made you decide to?","What did you understand about why you were on it?"],"hear":"\"Two months ago. The shakes, the thirst, the blood tests. I was discharged, so I thought I was cured.\"","mark":"Trigger identified: abrupt lithium stop"},{"h":"Risk","say":["How are you paying for things, and how much do you owe?","Where and when have you been approaching people? Any trouble? Are you driving?","Any plans to quit work? Any voices, or special powers or missions?"],"hear":"\"On my credit card. Town centre, late; one guy got angry. I drive everywhere. I'm going to hand my notice in. No voices.\"","mark":"Financial, occupational, driving and vulnerability risks identified"},{"h":"Name it: mania, bipolar disorder","say":["The little sleep, the energy, the big plans and spending: this looks like the high you had two years ago, coming back.","Two episodes like this mean the diagnosis is bipolar disorder. It's common and treatable.","Stopping lithium suddenly is probably what tipped it."],"hear":"\"Okay, say I believe you. What do you want me to do about it?\""},{"h":"Treatment and setting","say":["I'd suggest starting a medicine today to bring the high down and help you sleep, such as olanzapine at night. It can make you sleepy and hungry, so we'll watch your weight.","Lithium protects best long term, but we can decide that when you're settled; there are other options.","I'd like the home treatment team to visit daily, and for you to stay with your parents. If that doesn't work, hospital would be the next step."],"hear":"\"No lithium today, fine. Mum's is okay. Just not hospital.\""},{"h":"His concerns and safety limits","say":["I know you're worried about losing your creativity. The aim is a film that actually gets finished.","For now: no driving, and you'll need to tell the DVLA. Let's pause big purchases and quitting your job. Could your parents look after the credit card?","I'll write a fit note for two weeks."],"hear":"\"The DVLA? Seriously? Fine, Mum can drive me.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Could you sum up what we've agreed?","May I share the plan with your parents?","If things get worse, call the crisis line; I'll see you next week."],"hear":"\"Tablet tonight, team tomorrow, stay at Mum's, no driving or big buys. Yes, tell them.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Manic symptoms","2 h sleep, no tiredness, award-winning film, gym twice daily","Manic episode"],["Lithium","Stopped abruptly 2 months ago: tremor, thirst, tests; thought he was cured","Likely trigger; gap in understanding"],["Risks","£4,500 on credit card, late-night approaches, driving, planning to resign","Financial, vulnerability, driving and work risks"],["Diagnosis","Second manic episode","Bipolar I disorder"],["Plan","Agrees tablet and home treatment, not lithium yet, stays with parents","Least restrictive care with admission as fallback"]]},"key":"With mania, warmth is not enough: you must lead. Interrupt kindly, give choices within limits, and pair the diagnosis with concrete curbs on driving, spending and work decisions.","objmap":[[1,2,3],[4],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-46":{"name":"He understands but can't weigh it: capacity and best interests, explained to his carer","why":"Turns a worried carer into an informed partner in a lawful, gentle, urgent plan.","frame":["Impairment of mind or brain","Understand","Retain","Use or weigh","Communicate","Best interests"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the liaison psychiatrists. Are you Tessa, Liam's support worker?","He's still bleeding and still refusing tests. I've just assessed whether he can make this decision.","You know him well, so I'd value your view. Can I share what I found and then plan with you?"],"hear":"\"Of course. I've never seen him this frightened.\""},{"h":"Her view of Liam","say":["What's Liam like on a good day?","When did the worries about the police start?","Has he been taking his risperidone? How do you know?"],"hear":"\"He's usually chatty. About three weeks ago he started saying police come in at night. I found tablets in his bin.\"","mark":"Recent change and missed risperidone identified"},{"h":"Capacity findings","say":["For this decision, today, I don't think he has capacity. Here's why.","He understands, remembers and tells us his choice clearly.","But he can't weigh it up, because he's sure the tests are a police plot to harm him."],"hear":"\"But he's always been stubborn. Can't he make a bad choice?\"","mark":"Unable to use or weigh identified"},{"h":"What it means","say":["He's allowed an unwise choice if he can weigh it up. This is different.","It's only this decision, and only now. He can still decide other things.","We'll check again once he's safer and record it each time."],"hear":"\"So it's not forever. That's a relief.\"","mark":"Decision- and time-specific explained"},{"h":"Best interests plan","say":["So the team must act in his best interests: what he'd want, his values, the gentlest way.","First we persuade, with you there, using pictures and one step at a time.","Bloods and a drip now; the camera test as soon as he's stable, with sedation."],"hear":"\"And if he still says no? Will they hold him down?\"","mark":"Least restrictive route agreed"},{"h":"Safeguards and people","say":["Nobody can sign for him, not you and not his mum, but both your views count.","I'll phone his mum now; an advocate is only needed if nobody can be consulted.","Any holding is a last resort, the minimum needed, and written down; the court only if there's a serious dispute."],"hear":"\"Is it legal to go against what he says?\"","mark":"Proxy consent, legality and restraint clarified"},{"h":"Mental health","say":["The police beliefs may be a return of psychosis after missed risperidone.","Once he's stable I'll review him and his medicine, and tell his own psychiatrist.","The Mental Health Act isn't used for his stomach; this is the Mental Capacity Act."],"hear":"\"That makes sense. It's not like him at all.\"","mark":"Possible psychotic relapse flagged"},{"h":"Hand over and close","say":["How would you sum up what happens now?","What else is worrying you?","I'll write this in his notes and come back tonight or first thing tomorrow."],"hear":"\"He can't decide this one right now, the doctors act in his best interests, I help keep him calm, and you ring his mum.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Capacity test","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Impairment of mind or brain","Mild learning disability; three weeks of new police beliefs after missed risperidone","Probable psychotic relapse on top of LD: the cause of the inability"],["Understand","Repeats that he may be bleeding and could die","Passed"],["Retain","Recalls it later in the conversation","Passed"],["Use or weigh","Says the blood test is a police injection and the camera is for them","Failed: belief stops him balancing risk of death"],["Communicate","Clearly says no","Passed"]]},"key":"Understanding and repeating back is not capacity. If a belief rooted in his mental state stops him weighing the risk of dying, he lacks capacity for this decision, however well he talks and remembers.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-47":{"name":"Not her burden: a sterilisation request answered fear by fear","why":"Turns a demand you must refuse into a plan that meets every real worry underneath it.","frame":["Consent and rights","Inheritance","Parenting support","The exposing","Carer strain"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Okoye, Callum's mum?","Thank you for coming, Patricia. It sounds like you've carried a lot for many years.","I'd like to hear your worries, then talk about what can realistically be done. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Fine. I want him sterilised. He can't cope with a baby and neither can I.\""},{"h":"Her concerns","say":["Tell me what's worrying you most.","So the baby's health, who raises it, money and tiredness. Anything else?"],"hear":"\"The baby'll be like him, and they'll dump it on me. And he's done that thing again.\"","mark":"Inheritance fear and fear of being left with the baby identified"},{"h":"Callum's situation","say":["Can you tell me what happened with the police?","Was it an adult or a child, and has he ever touched anyone?","Who supports him and Jade at the flat now?"],"hear":"\"A grown woman at a bus stop. He panicked about them taking the baby. Never touched anyone.\"","mark":"Stress-linked exposure without contact offending identified"},{"h":"Her wellbeing","say":["You've been looking after everyone. How are you sleeping, and how's your mood?","Have you ever felt so low you've thought of harming yourself?"],"hear":"\"I cry in the car before work. Nobody's ever asked. No, I'd never do anything.\"","mark":"Carer strain identified"},{"h":"Sterilisation explained","say":["I can't arrange that operation. It's permanent, so it's Callum's own decision.","If he couldn't understand it even with help, a court would have to decide, and only for his benefit.","What we can offer is help with contraception choices and easy-read relationships education."],"hear":"\"So you're saying no.\""},{"h":"Baby and parenting","say":["What were you told about why Callum has a learning disability?","Lack of oxygen at birth is an injury, not genes, so it isn't passed on.","Social care will assess before the birth and support them to parent. You won't be expected to raise this baby."],"hear":"\"Really? I thought it'd be me by default.\"","mark":"Heritability and parenting fears answered"},{"h":"Managing the offending","say":["Our team can assess the exposing and how likely it is to happen again.","A psychologist can help him handle stress in safer ways.","We'll make sure the police know about his disability, and he has an appropriate adult."],"hear":"\"Money's what sets him off.\"","mark":"Plan for the exposing agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["I'd like to refer you for a carer's assessment, and you to see your GP about sleep and mood.","What would you tell a friend about today?","I'll see Callum with you in two weeks."],"hear":"\"That it's not all on me. He gets help, and so do I.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Concern","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Sterilisation","'Stop it happening again'","His decision alone; court only if he lacked capacity, and only for his benefit"],["Inheritance","Callum's disability followed lack of oxygen at birth; Jade's scans normal","Not inherited; risk to baby not raised by Callum's cause"],["Who raises the baby","'They'll dump it on me'","Pre-birth assessment and adapted support for the couple; not her duty"],["The exposing","Two episodes, both under stress, adult victim, no contact","Specialist assessment and stress work; police liaison"],["Carer strain","Night shifts, tearful, no sleep, no help in 26 years","Carer's assessment, GP review, benefits advice"]]},"key":"The request is a cry for help. Refuse the operation clearly and early, then answer each fear underneath it with a fact and an action, and make sure Patricia leaves with support of her own.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-t-48":{"name":"Not back to the wing yet: handover, risk and a safe plan","why":"Turns a pressured corridor request into a safe, defensible plan built on facts.","frame":["Handover (SBAR)","Risk to others","Risk to self","Differential","Placement and transfer"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the forensic psychiatry doctors. Are you Kiran, looking after Mr Mulvey?","Could you give me a quick handover: how he's been since arriving, observations, any incidents, and drug test results?","Then I'll share my thinking and we'll agree the plan, including the wing question."],"hear":"\"Sure. Honestly, I think it's GHB, and I need the bed.\""},{"h":"Handover from nurse","say":["How has he slept and eaten?","What is he saying about the voices now? Anything about being controlled or thoughts being read?","Observations, incidents, and what drug test was done?"],"hear":"\"Shaky the first night, settled now. Still hears a voice warning him. Asked if officers read his thoughts. Urine negative.\"","mark":"Persisting psychotic symptoms identified"},{"h":"Risk to others","say":["He's here for assault. Has he talked about harming anyone, or named anyone?","Voices to defend himself, feeling controlled, urges and past violence: that's a combination I take seriously."],"hear":"\"He told the GP he gets urges. No names. He's been no trouble.\"","mark":"High risk to others identified"},{"h":"Risk to self","say":["Any self-harm, low mood or talk of dying?","Any debts or bullying on the wing?"],"hear":"\"He said he'd rather die than be attacked. Word is he owes someone.\"","mark":"Vulnerability and self-harm risk identified"},{"h":"Differential","say":["Most likely this is psychosis; drugs could be part of it.","Withdrawal usually brings sweats and confusion within days; his voices started weeks before.","Honestly, nobody has done a full assessment yet, so we can't be sure."],"hear":"\"GHB withdrawal can make people psychotic, though.\"","mark":"Psychosis likely; substances possible"},{"h":"Initial management","say":["I'll see him myself this afternoon.","Bloods, ECG and a repeat urine screen today, and regular observations.","No antipsychotic until I've assessed him; promethazine as needed if distressed."],"hear":"\"We can do the bloods. Are you starting anything?\""},{"h":"Wing and transfer","say":["I know the bed pressure is real. But he stays here until I've reassessed him.","If it's psychosis needing treatment, he'll need transfer to hospital; we can't treat him against his will in prison.","Transfer needs reports from two doctors and a Ministry of Justice warrant; the aim is 28 days."],"hear":"\"The bed manager won't like it.\"","mark":"Remains on healthcare unit"},{"h":"Hand over and close","say":["Please share the risk with wing and security on a need-to-know basis, and consider an ACCT.","If he's aggressive, confused or unwell, call the emergency response and me.","Let me recap, then I'll document it and speak to the bed manager myself."],"hear":"\"Makes sense. I'll get the bloods done now.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Handover","Shaky night one, settled; voices persist; thought-reading questions; urine negative","Withdrawal likely passed; psychotic symptoms persist"],["Risk to others","Assault offence; voices to defend himself; feels controlled; urges, no named target","High risk on a wing; stays on healthcare"],["Risk to self","'Rather die than be attacked'; possible drug debt","Vulnerable; consider ACCT"],["Differential","Symptoms began weeks before the move, after a witnessed assault","Probable psychosis; substances and trauma possible contributors"],["Placement","Bed pressure; no full assessment yet","Not back to the wing; transfer if psychosis needs treatment"]]},"key":"Two calm days on a staffed unit are not a risk assessment. Respect the nurse's view and the bed pressure, but decide on risk: no wing return until he has been properly assessed.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-t-49":{"name":"Name it with his own words: mania, bipolar disorder and a plan he accepts","why":"Turns a fast, reluctant man into a partner in a safe plan at home.","frame":["Sleep and energy","Spending and plans","Lithium story","Practical risk","Treatment and setting"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Rhys Llewellyn?","Thanks for making time, Rhys. Your GP and parents are worried, and I'd like your side of it.","We'll be as quick as we can. Then we'll agree what happens next."],"hear":"\"Fine. I've never felt better. You'd be great in my video, actually.\""},{"h":"Current picture","say":["That's kind, but I'll stick to being your doctor today. How have you been feeling?","How many hours are you sleeping? Do you feel tired?","What have you spent, and who's going to be in the video?"],"hear":"\"Two hours. Four grand on kit. I stop people in cafés; some look at me like I'm mad.\"","mark":"Manic symptoms identified"},{"h":"Lithium story","say":["What made you stop lithium?","Did you stop it all at once?"],"hear":"\"It made me shake and feel flat. I binned them two months ago.\"","mark":"Abrupt discontinuation identified"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Any alcohol, drugs, or relationships you wouldn't usually have?","Are you driving? Has anyone taken advantage of your money?","Any special powers, people against you, or thoughts of harming anyone?"],"hear":"\"I drive fast, maybe. No drink. I'm just very talented.\"","mark":"Driving, debt and work risks identified"},{"h":"Diagnosis explained","say":["Little sleep without tiredness, huge energy, big spending and racing plans: that's mania, like two years ago.","A second episode confirms bipolar disorder, a long-term tendency to highs and lows.","Stopping lithium suddenly makes a high more likely."],"hear":"\"So it's all just illness? That's gutting.\""},{"h":"Treatment options","say":["To settle this now: quetiapine at night, built up over a few days. Drowsiness and weight gain are the main downsides.","If you want less sedation, aripiprazole is an option.","Lithium is the best long-term protection; the tremor can often be eased."],"hear":"\"Every three months for bloods I could live with. Maybe later.\""},{"h":"His concerns","say":["I'd rather you didn't need hospital. Could you stay with your parents, with the home team visiting daily?","Please don't drive while you're high, and tell the DVLA. Could your dad hold your cards?","If it gets worse, hospital comes back on the table."],"hear":"\"Fine. The cards I get. The driving's annoying.\"","mark":"Home treatment agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["What will you tell your parents tonight?","If you feel worse, or anyone's worried, ring the crisis line or 111, option 2.","I'll see you in a week."],"hear":"\"I'm high again, it's bipolar, quetiapine tonight, staying with them, no driving.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Sleep and energy","Two hours' sleep, not tired; gym twice a day; working late","Core manic features"],["Spending and plans","£4,000 on equipment; strangers recruited; 'a famous band will definitely want it'","Grandiosity and disinhibition; financial risk"],["Lithium story","Stopped abruptly two months ago for tremor and flatness","Likely trigger; side effects to address before restarting"],["Practical risk","Driving fast; manager concerned; no drugs or alcohol","Stop driving, DVLA, cards to father, fit note"],["Treatment and setting","Accepts tablets; fears hospital; can stay with parents","Antipsychotic now, home treatment team, admission as fallback"]]},"key":"Name mania using his own evidence, not your authority. Once he accepts treatment and has support, stop pressing for admission and make the home plan concrete: which team visits, whose house he stays at, which relative keeps the cards.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[7,8]]},"c-t-50":{"name":"Agree what's true, then the menu: the serotonin doubt and a plan","why":"Turns a sceptical colleague's doubt into an honest, shared plan he will follow.","frame":["The serotonin question","Switch","Augment","rTMS","Psychological therapy","Return to work"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dr Farhan Qureshi?","Thanks, Farhan. I understand you've been reading about the serotonin debate and have real doubts about tablets.","That's fair to raise. Could we look at how you are now, then go through the evidence and options together?"],"hear":"\"Fine. But I've read the papers.\""},{"h":"His depression now","say":["How did this start?","How are your sleep, appetite, energy and concentration? What do you still enjoy?","How much are you drinking?"],"hear":"\"Since my wife left. Waking at four, eating little, can't read a paper. Wine most nights.\"","mark":"Moderately severe depressive episode identified"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Has it ever felt like life isn't worth carrying on with?","Any plans, or thoughts of using anything from work?","What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think the kids would cope without me. No plans. The children keep me going.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts without plan identified"},{"h":"Medicines so far","say":["Which antidepressants, what dose, how long, and every day?","What side effects have you had?"],"hear":"\"Sertraline, nothing. Paroxetine made me darker. Mirtazapine: I can't ejaculate properly.\"","mark":"Three adequate trials and sexual side effect identified"},{"h":"The serotonin question","say":["You're right: the neat low-serotonin story hasn't held up.","Whether antidepressants work is a separate question, and large trials show they help many people.","They probably work through slower changes in how the brain processes emotion."],"hear":"\"So you don't know how they work.\""},{"h":"Options and switch","say":["I'd suggest switching to vortioxetine: it works differently and has fewer sexual side effects.","Agomelatine is another choice; if a full dose fails, adding lithium or aripiprazole, or a course of rTMS.","CBT alongside: structured work on how thoughts like 'I've failed' drive mood, about sixteen sessions."],"hear":"\"Vortioxetine and CBT. I'll give it a fair go.\"","mark":"Shared treatment choice made"},{"h":"Return to work","say":["An occupational health referral for a phased return, starting without on-call.","Practitioner Health, so your care sits outside your own hospital.","You don't need to tell the GMC just for treated depression; follow occupational health advice."],"hear":"\"That's a relief.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Can you tell me the plan in your words?","Keep only small supplies at home, and cut back the wine.","If those thoughts get stronger: 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, or 999. Review in two weeks."],"hear":"\"Taper mirtazapine, start vortioxetine, CBT, OH and Practitioner Health. Two weeks.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Option","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The serotonin question","Read that the theory is disproved; three medicines failed","Agree on the theory; separate mechanism from effectiveness"],["Switch","Mirtazapine: ejaculatory problems, weight gain, partial sleep benefit","Vortioxetine first; agomelatine as an alternative"],["Augment","Three adequate trials without response","Lithium or aripiprazole if the switch fails at a full dose"],["rTMS","Asked about magnetic stimulation; wants to return to work","Safe, NICE-supported; daily sessions may clash with work"],["Psychological therapy","Counselling 'felt like chatting'","High-intensity CBT combined with medication"],["Return to work","Off three weeks, keen to go back, fears the GMC","OH phased return, Practitioner Health, no automatic GMC report"]]},"key":"Don't defend the serotonin theory. Agree what is true, separate how a medicine works from whether it works, then give the menu, each option with how it works and whether it suits him.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-52":{"name":"Refeeding syndrome for a worried colleague: name, mechanism, today, prevention","why":"Turns a frightened nurse's phone call into a clear explanation and a same-day medical plan that keeps feeding going safely.","frame":["Cause: starvation then rapid intake","Mechanism: insulin surge and salt shift","Her two symptoms","Danger","Today and prevention"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and agree urgency","say":["I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Thanks for flagging this; it's worth worrying about.","Let me check a few facts, then I'll explain and we'll agree what happens today."],"hear":"\"Her hands and feet are puffy and she says swallowing is hard.\""},{"h":"Clarify the picture","say":["Three kilos in a week, after her mum increased her meals, and phosphate a bit low a week ago?","Right now, is she breathless, faint, confused, or getting chest pain or palpitations?"],"hear":"\"Yes. She's tired and weak, but not breathless that I saw.\"","mark":"Key facts confirmed, no immediate collapse"},{"h":"Name it and its cause","say":["I think this is refeeding syndrome. It happens when someone who's been starved starts eating a lot very quickly.","Her body has been running on fat and muscle. When lots of food arrived, insulin surged and drove phosphate, potassium and magnesium from the bloodstream into the cells.","It also burns through her thiamine, one of the B vitamins."],"hear":"\"So the low phosphate was the start of it.\"","mark":"Diagnosis and mechanism understood"},{"h":"Why her symptoms","say":["Insulin makes the kidneys hold salt and water, so fluid settles in her hands and feet.","Starving thins the heart muscle, so that extra fluid can strain it.","Low phosphate weakens muscles, including swallowing and breathing muscles."],"hear":"\"I didn't realise it was about her heart.\""},{"h":"How dangerous it is","say":["If those salts fall further, the heart can beat irregularly or fail, breathing muscles can tire, and some people have fits.","Caught early, it's very treatable."],"hear":"\"That's frightening.\""},{"h":"Today's plan","say":["She needs to be seen by the paediatric team today. I'll ring them now.","They'll do a heart tracing, urgent bloods, replace the salts, and start thiamine and vitamins.","Feeding doesn't stop. The dietitian will set a steady plan and build it up, with bloods every day."],"hear":"\"So she doesn't stop eating. Just slower and watched.\"","mark":"Same-day plan agreed"},{"h":"Prevention and her mum","say":["Next time intake goes up, bloods first, vitamins, a gradual build-up and close checks.","Her mum acted out of love. The ward can show her what a safe pace looks like."],"hear":"\"She'll feel terrible. I'll make sure she knows it's not her fault.\""},{"h":"Hand over and close","say":["Can you tell me back what you'll do now?","If she gets chest pain, palpitations, faints, is breathless or confused before she's seen, call 999.","I'll tell the consultant, and please debrief with your supervisor today."],"hear":"\"Ring mum, get her to the assessment unit, 999 if worse, write it up.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Element","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Rapid intake","Mother pushed big meals; 3 kg in 7 days","Intake rose too fast; gain is mostly fluid"],["Low phosphate","Mildly low a week ago","Early sign; likely lower now"],["Swollen hands and feet","New pitting puffiness","Salt and water retention; possible heart strain"],["Swallowing difficulty","Finds swallowing hard and tiring","Phosphate-related muscle weakness; choking risk"],["Current state","Tired and weak, no reported chest pain","Same-day assessment, 999 if red flags"]]},"key":"Treat the electrolytes, not the food: refeeding syndrome is managed by slowing and monitoring intake, not stopping it. Say 'today' and name who acts.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-53":{"name":"Relapse or PTSD? The change, the event, the three clusters, then psychosis","why":"Separates a new, treatable PTSD from a psychotic relapse in a guarded man, and explains it in his own terms.","frame":["Trauma and reminder","Re-experiencing","Avoidance","Sense of current threat","Psychosis screen"],"steps":[{"h":"Open gently","say":["I'm Dr Chinonso. Your family have been worried you're not going out much.","I'd like to hear it from you. We can go at your pace."],"hear":"\"They worry about everything.\""},{"h":"Recent changes","say":["What's made going out hard lately?","When did that start, and what else has changed: sleep, temper?"],"hear":"\"A few months. I just don't want to go out. Everything winds me up.\"","mark":"Recent change and onset clarified"},{"h":"The trauma event","say":["Can you tell me how you came into hospital last time?","At that moment, what did you think was going to happen?","What set the memories off again?"],"hear":"\"Police had me face down on the grass. I thought I'd die. Then a cop show had an arrest like that.\"","mark":"Trauma and reminder identified"},{"h":"Post-traumatic symptoms","say":["Do you get pictures or feelings that put you right back there? Nightmares?","Do you avoid anything because it reminds you?","Do you feel on edge, jumpy, or check the street a lot?"],"hear":"\"Sirens and my chest goes tight like the knee's on me. I don't watch TV. I jump at everything.\"","mark":"Re-experiencing, avoidance and hyperarousal identified"},{"h":"Psychosis check","say":["Have you heard voices, or felt watched or plotted against, like before?","Are you taking your olanzapine every night? Any cannabis?"],"hear":"\"No voices. It's not like before. I take it every night. I've stopped the weed.\"","mark":"Psychotic relapse excluded"},{"h":"Mood and risk","say":["How's your mood, appetite and motivation? How do you see yourself?","Any thoughts of killing yourself, or of self-harm?","You sound angry with the police. Any thoughts of harming them, or anyone at home?"],"hear":"\"Low and fed up. I don't blame myself, I blame them. I wouldn't hurt anyone.\"","mark":"Risk assessed"},{"h":"Impact and supports","say":["How is this affecting home and friends?","Who's on your side at the moment?"],"hear":"\"Rows with Dad. My brother still checks on me.\""},{"h":"Explain and close","say":["Your schizophrenia looks settled. This is post-traumatic stress disorder, a reaction to that arrest.","A talking therapy for trauma works well; keep the olanzapine as it is.","Tell me in your words what we've said, and here's who to call if things get worse."],"hear":"\"So it's not me going mad. It's the arrest. And there's therapy.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Trauma and reminder","Face-down restraint, thought he'd die; TV arrest scene 3 months ago","Qualifying trauma; delayed onset after a cue"],["Re-experiencing","Flashbacks with chest tightness, smell of grass; nightmares","Reliving in the present"],["Avoidance","No TV, films, games; won't go out","Avoidance of reminders"],["Current threat","Jumpy, sirens, checks the street, poor sleep","Hypervigilance and startle"],["Psychosis screen","No voices or persecutory ideas; takes olanzapine; no cannabis","Schizophrenia in remission, not relapse"]]},"key":"Ask how he came into hospital last time. The trauma is often the admission itself; screen psychosis once, then follow the trauma.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5],[6,7,8]]},"c-t-54":{"name":"Formulation for a relative: the loss, the circle, the strengths, then alcohol and clozapine","why":"Gives a grieving sister a story that makes sense of her brother's decline, and the specific dangers she needs to know.","frame":["Predisposing","Precipitating","Perpetuating","Protective","Alcohol and clozapine"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, consent, condolences","say":["I'm Dr Chinonso. Martin has agreed we can talk openly.","I'm so sorry about your parents."],"hear":"\"It's been a horrible year.\""},{"h":"What changed and what she sees","say":["What were your parents doing for Martin day to day?","Since then, how is his mood, and how much and how often is he drinking?","How often has he missed clozapine, and when did he last take it?"],"hear":"\"Mum did his tablets, meals, everything. Dad took him out. Now bottles everywhere, two weeks of tablets spare.\"","mark":"Loss of care and missed clozapine identified"},{"h":"The gaps and her worries","say":["Is he shaky in the mornings? Does he smoke, or use anything else?","Any fits, heart or bowel problems? Has he talked about wishing you weren't here?","What worries you most?"],"hear":"\"Shaky till he's had one. Twenty a day. He said 'what's the point' once. I'm scared he'll end up like before.\"","mark":"Possible dependence and risk identified"},{"h":"The formulation","say":["Martin has an illness that has always made him vulnerable, and he relied on your parents for structure.","Losing both of them took away his routine, his company and his tablet prompts.","He's drinking to cope with grief and fear, missing clozapine, so the paranoia returns and keeps him in: a vicious circle."],"hear":"\"So it's all connected.\"","mark":"Formulation understood"},{"h":"Strengths","say":["He's been well for years on clozapine, he still talks to you, and he let us speak today.","Those are real strengths, and we can build on them."],"hear":"\"He does still answer my calls.\""},{"h":"Alcohol and his body","say":["Short term: drowsiness, falls, accidents, choking, low blood pressure.","Long term: liver damage, pancreatitis, heart and blood pressure problems, memory damage and some cancers."],"hear":"\"Could he be addicted?\""},{"h":"Alcohol and his mind","say":["Drink lowers mood and raises anxiety, and can bring dependence: morning shakes, needing more.","It makes paranoia worse, makes tablets easier to forget, and raises the risk of suicide."],"hear":"\"So it could make him relapse.\""},{"h":"Clozapine gaps and risk","say":["With clozapine, alcohol adds sedation, low blood pressure and fit risk, and worsens constipation.","After 48 hours off, he must restart low. Please don't let him take his old dose.","The spare tablets are a danger in themselves; let's get them back to the pharmacy."],"hear":"\"I had no idea. I'll take them back.\"","mark":"Clozapine safety understood"},{"h":"Plan and close","say":["His care coordinator will visit within two days, check his tablets, bloods and drinking, and restart clozapine safely.","If you explained this to your husband tonight, what would you say?","There's a carers' assessment for you, and here are the crisis numbers."],"hear":"\"Grief, drink and missed tablets in a circle. No old dose. Team visits this week.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Formulation","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Predisposing","Treatment-resistant schizophrenia; lived with parents since his twenties","Vulnerable to stress; depended on others for structure"],["Precipitating","Both parents died within six months","Grief plus sudden loss of care and routine"],["Perpetuating","Drinking, missed clozapine, alone, paranoid","Vicious circle driving early relapse"],["Protective","Years stable on clozapine; Leah; agreed to share","Foundations to rebuild on"],["Clozapine risk","Two weeks spare, smokes 20 a day, constipated","Retitrate, remove stockpile, watch fits and bowels"]]},"key":"Don't just list alcohol's harms: tie grief, lost care, drinking and missed clozapine into one circle, then give the 48-hour rule and the stockpile risk plainly.","objmap":[[1,2],[2,3],[4,5],[6,7],[8,9]]},"c-t-55":{"name":"Psychotic depression: safety, the belief, the mood beneath it, then the plan","why":"Shows that a frightening persecutory belief grew out of severe depression, finds the hidden risks, and secures safe treatment.","frame":["The belief and real danger","Depressive syndrome","Mood-congruent psychotic features","Past episodes and treatment","Risk and insight"],"steps":[{"h":"Make her safe","say":["I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. You're safe here.","You did the right thing coming. Could you tell me what's been happening?"],"hear":"\"I didn't know where else to go.\""},{"h":"Fear about her husband","say":["What makes you think Liam wants to hurt you? Has he said or done anything?","Why would he want to?","I ask everyone this: has he ever hit, threatened or controlled you?"],"hear":"\"He's never touched me. He's kind. But I'm useless, so he must want me gone.\"","mark":"Belief explored; real abuse excluded"},{"h":"Depressive symptoms","say":["When did you start feeling low, and what came first?","How are your sleep, appetite, energy and concentration? Do you enjoy anything?","How do you see yourself? Do you feel guilty?"],"hear":"\"Two months. Waking at four first. I can't do anything. I've ruined everything.\"","mark":"Severe depressive syndrome identified"},{"h":"Psychotic features","say":["Has food tasted or smelled different lately?","Any voices when nobody's there, or visions? Any sense of being controlled from outside?","Do you feel something terrible has happened to your body or your money?"],"hear":"\"His cooking tastes bitter. He's putting something in it. No voices.\"","mark":"Mood-congruent delusion and gustatory hallucination identified"},{"h":"Past and treatment","say":["Have you been like this before? Were you ever in hospital?","What medicines did you take, and what happened to them? Is the citalopram helping?","Have you ever had times of feeling very high? Any alcohol or drugs?"],"hear":"\"In hospital three years ago. Two tablets. I stopped them when we moved. The new one does nothing.\""},{"h":"Risk","say":["When did you last eat or drink?","Have you thought of killing yourself, or planned anything?","Have you had any thoughts of harming Liam, perhaps to protect yourself?"],"hear":"\"Two days, just water. I wouldn't do anything, but I deserve to die. I'd never hurt him.\"","mark":"Self-neglect and passive death wish identified"},{"h":"Mental state and insight","say":["Can you tell me where we are and today's date?","Do you think any of this could be an illness?"],"hear":"\"Hospital. Friday. Maybe I am ill. I don't know.\""},{"h":"Explain and close","say":["I think you have a severe depression, so heavy it's changing how you see things, including Liam and your food.","It's an illness, not your fault, and very treatable.","I'd like you to come into hospital today to start a stronger treatment and eat again safely."],"hear":"\"Can't I just go home?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The belief","Liam will kill her because she's useless; no threats or violence","Mood-congruent persecutory delusion"],["Depression","Two months, 4am waking first, anhedonia, weight loss, guilt","Severe depressive episode"],["Perception","Bitter taste in his food; no voices","Gustatory hallucination"],["Past","Admission 3 years ago, two medicines stopped; citalopram no help","Recurrent, severe illness; inadequate treatment"],["Risk","Not eaten 2 days; 'deserves to die'; no plan; no harm to husband","High self-neglect and suicide risk; admit"]]},"key":"Ask what came first. Low mood before the belief, with guilt-laden content, makes it psychotic depression. Then ask about food: the hallucination and the self-neglect both hide there.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,5],[4],[6],[7,8]]},"c-t-56":{"name":"Teach to her need: why it matters, what to ask, what to do","why":"Turns a nervous student into someone who can assess Mr Desai safely and present a joined-up plan.","frame":["Her learning needs","Why depression after a heart attack matters","What to assess","Medicines and safety","Therapy, rehab, smoking and the team","Presenting and supervision"],"steps":[{"h":"Find her goals and what she knows","say":["What do you know about Mr Desai so far?","What does your consultant want from you?","Anything about seeing him that worries you?"],"hear":"\"I've read the referral. I have to present him, and I don't get the cardiac rehab part.\"","mark":"Learning needs identified"},{"h":"His story in one breath","say":["Years of moderate depression, well on sertraline, discharged to his GP nine months ago.","He stopped it because he felt well. Then a heart attack, and now low mood, poor sleep and appetite.","He's off his heart tablets, missing rehab and still smoking."],"hear":"\"So it's probably a relapse after stopping the sertraline?\"","mark":"Relapse after stopping treatment recognised"},{"h":"Why it matters for his heart","say":["About one in five people get depressed after a heart attack.","It works both ways: depression is a heart risk factor, and a heart attack can trigger depression.","Untreated, he's less likely to take tablets or do rehab, and his risk of another event and of dying goes up."],"hear":"\"Oh, so fixing his mood is part of fixing his heart.\"","mark":"Two-way link and prognosis explained"},{"h":"What to assess next week","say":["Cover core symptoms, sleep, appetite, energy and hopelessness, and when it started relative to the heart attack.","Ask about suicide plainly, and whether skipping his heart tablets is on purpose.","Ask about alcohol, smoking, what he believes about his heart, and work and family."],"hear":"\"I'm worried about asking about suicide.\"","mark":"Assessment and risk points set"},{"h":"Medicines: what's safe and what to avoid","say":["Sertraline has the best safety evidence after a heart attack, and it worked for him before.","SSRIs add bleeding risk with antiplatelets, so think about stomach protection, and check sodium.","Avoid tricyclics; be cautious with citalopram because of the QT interval."],"hear":"\"Sertraline, check sodium, watch for bleeding, no tricyclics.\"","mark":"Safe prescribing explained"},{"h":"Therapy, rehab, smoking and the team","say":["CBT, usually 8 to 16 sessions, can be tied to rehab: activity diaries, tablet charts.","We'd speak to the cardiac rehab nurse about a fresh start, maybe at home.","Stop smoking service with varenicline or NRT; a dietitian; occupational therapy and occupational health about work."],"hear":"\"What does the OT actually do for him?\"","mark":"Wider team and lifestyle covered"},{"h":"How to present him","say":["Present: who he is, the problem, history, mental state, risk, then a short formulation and plan.","Keep your notes anonymised and off your phone.","Give me the plan back in two or three sentences."],"hear":"\"Relapse of depression after stopping sertraline, made worse by the MI; restart sertraline, CBT linked to rehab, help to stop smoking.\"","mark":"Understanding checked"},{"h":"Supervision and close","say":["I'll be in the next room; call me if he talks about suicide or anything worries you.","Have a look at NICE CG91 on depression alongside physical illness.","We'll debrief after clinic. Any questions now?"],"hear":"\"That's really helpful. Thank you.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What she needs to hear","What it means for Mr Desai"],"rows":[["Why it matters","About 1 in 5; two-way link; worse adherence and survival","Treating mood is part of his cardiac care"],["What to assess","Symptoms, suicide, deliberate missed tablets, alcohol, beliefs","Risk may hide in non-adherence"],["Medicines","Sertraline first; bleeding with antiplatelets; sodium; no tricyclics","Restart what worked, safely"],["Therapy and rehab","CBT linked to rehab; home-based option","One joined-up plan"],["Smoking and team","Varenicline or NRT; dietitian; OT; work","Secondary prevention shared by the team"]]},"key":"Teach to her goal, not your knowledge. Tie every fact to Mr Desai, and finish with her presenting the plan back, so you know she can use it.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-57":{"name":"Autism or psychosis? What's new against his baseline","why":"Pins down a relapse of schizophrenia in an autistic man, without confusing autistic traits with psychosis.","frame":["Adapted communication","Trigger and timeline","Delusions and conviction","Voices and thought interference","New versus baseline","Insight and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Adapt and open","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jordan Ashworth?","I will ask some questions. You can take your time to answer.","What happened before the police brought you here?"],"hear":"\"A man was following me. So I shouted. Then the police came.\"","mark":"Incident clarified"},{"h":"Changes and timeline","say":["What has changed for you in the last few months?","Are you still taking risperidone? When did you stop? Why?","How are you sleeping?"],"hear":"\"The day centre closed. I stopped the tablets three months ago. The agents said to stop.\"","mark":"Trigger and non-adherence identified"},{"h":"Beliefs and conviction","say":["Tell me about the agents.","Do you get messages from the TV or radio?","Is anyone trying to harm you? How sure are you, from 0 to 100?"],"hear":"\"I am a special agent. The TV gives me coded instructions. Strangers follow me. I am 100 percent sure.\"","mark":"Grandiose, referential and persecutory delusions identified"},{"h":"Voices and thought interference","say":["Do you hear people talking when nobody is with you? Are they talking to you, or talking about you?","Do they tell you to do things?","Can other people know your thoughts without you saying them? Can anyone put thoughts in, or take them out?"],"hear":"\"The agents talk to me. They say wait. People can hear my thoughts.\"","mark":"Second-person voices and thought broadcasting identified"},{"h":"New versus baseline","say":["What do you usually like doing? Is that the same now?","Did you think about agents before this year?","Has anything outside you made you move or feel things?"],"hear":"\"I like trains. Always trains. The agents started after the summer.\"","mark":"Lifelong interest separated from new beliefs"},{"h":"Mood and substances","say":["How is your mood? Low, normal or very happy?","Do you drink alcohol or use cannabis or other drugs?"],"hear":"\"Normal. I do not drink. No drugs.\"","mark":"Mood and substance causes screened"},{"h":"Insight and risk","say":["Could this be an illness coming back?","If someone follows you again, what will you do? Do you want to hurt anyone? Yourself?","If you leave now, where will you go?"],"hear":"\"I am not ill. I would shout again. I don't want to hurt myself. I'd go home for the mission.\"","mark":"Insight absent; risks to others and absconding identified"},{"h":"Explain next steps clearly","say":["I think your illness has come back because the tablets stopped.","I want you to stay in hospital tonight and restart risperidone.","I will talk to your mum. Do you have questions?"],"hear":"\"I don't want to stay. Can I have a quiet room?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Trigger and timeline","Day centre shut, tablets stopped 3 months ago, beliefs 2 months","Relapse after loss of routine and stopping treatment"],["Delusions","Special agent, TV codes, strangers following, 100% sure","Grandiose, referential and persecutory delusions"],["Voices and thoughts","Agents talk to him; others hear his thoughts","Second-person voices; thought broadcasting"],["New versus baseline","Trains lifelong; agents new","Psychosis, not a special interest"],["Insight and risk","Not ill; would shout again; wants to leave","No insight; risk to others and absconding"]]},"key":"Every finding must be compared with his usual self. A lifelong interest is autism; a new belief held with total conviction, with voices, is psychosis. Ask him and his family what is new.","objmap":[[1],[1,2],[3,4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-58":{"name":"Fear of judgement: situations, body, behaviour, causes","why":"Builds a clear diagnosis of social anxiety disorder and a cause map that points straight to CBT.","frame":["Feared situations and fear of judgement","Body symptoms","Avoidance and safety behaviours","Differentials and risk","Causes and maintaining factors"],"steps":[{"h":"Open warmly","say":["Thanks for coming, Aaron. Congratulations on the wedding. I gather it's also causing a lot of worry.","Could you tell me what goes through your mind when you picture the day?"],"hear":"\"Standing up, everyone looking at me. I'll go red and freeze.\"","mark":"Performance fear identified"},{"h":"The fear and its range","say":["What do you fear people will think?","Does this happen elsewhere: parties, eating in front of people, phone calls, meetings at work?","How long has it been like this?"],"hear":"\"That I'm pathetic. Parties, eating out, phone calls. Since school.\"","mark":"Fear of negative judgement identified"},{"h":"Body symptoms","say":["What happens in your body at the time?","Do you blush, shake or sweat? Does it ever become a panic attack?"],"hear":"\"Dry mouth, sweating, heart racing, I go bright red. Sometimes I feel I'll panic.\"","mark":"Autonomic symptoms identified"},{"h":"Avoidance, safety behaviours, impact","say":["What do you do to get through, or to avoid it?","Some people have a drink first. Do you?","How has it affected work, friends and Chloe?"],"hear":"\"I make excuses and leave early. Two pints before. Work took me off circle time.\"","mark":"Safety behaviours and impact identified"},{"h":"Causes and what keeps it going","say":["When did you first notice it? What was school like?","Was anyone in your family anxious or shy? What was home like?","What happens to the fear when you leave early?"],"hear":"\"I was teased for blushing when reading aloud. Mum's a worrier. Leaving makes it go away.\"","mark":"Predisposing, precipitating and maintaining factors identified"},{"h":"Differentials and risk","say":["Do you get panic attacks out of the blue, when nobody's around?","Do you avoid places like shops or buses even alone?","How's your mood been? Any thoughts life isn't worth living?"],"hear":"\"Only around people. I'm fine alone. Fed up, not depressed. No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Panic, agoraphobia, depression and suicide excluded"},{"h":"Check personality and substance","say":["Do you have close friends you feel at ease with?","Any other drinking, or drugs?"],"hear":"\"Two good mates and Chloe. Just the pints before things.\"","mark":"Avoidant personality and substance misuse less likely"},{"h":"Explain and plan","say":["This is social anxiety disorder. It's common and very treatable.","The best treatment is a specific CBT, and we can set wedding goals in it.","Can you tell me back what we've said?"],"hear":"\"So it's a real thing, and CBT can help before the wedding.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Fear of judgement","'They'll think I'm pathetic'; speeches, parties, eating, phone","Generalised social anxiety"],["Body","Blushing, sweating, dry mouth, racing heart","Visible symptoms that feed the fear"],["Avoidance and safety behaviours","Leaves early, excuses, two pints first, no eye contact","Maintaining factors"],["Differentials","No unprovoked panic, no agoraphobia, not depressed, close friends","Social anxiety disorder, not panic, depression or avoidant PD"],["Causes","Shy child, anxious mother, teased for blushing","Temperament, family and early learning"]]},"key":"Diagnosis is only half the task. Map the causes and, above all, what keeps it going: avoidance, safety behaviours and pre-event drinking. That is what CBT targets.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,4],[4,7],[6,7],[5,8]]},"c-t-59":{"name":"Can Shane refuse? The four tests for one decision","why":"Gives a lawful, defensible capacity outcome and a safe plan, without pressure.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use or weigh","Communicate","Impairment and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Purpose without pressure","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shane Mulholland?","I'm here to understand your decision and make sure it's yours to make.","Could you tell me what the surgeons have told you about your neck?"],"hear":"\"Yeah. Are you here to make me have the operation?\""},{"h":"Brief history and context","say":["What happened just before you cut yourself?","What did you want to happen? Did you want to die?","What's your diagnosis, and how long are you serving?"],"hear":"\"Told again no date for the DBT wing. I was fed up, not trying to die. EUPD. Two years for ABH.\"","mark":"Non-suicidal self-harm after transfer delay identified"},{"h":"Understanding","say":["What did the surgeons say they want to do, and why?","What might happen if it isn't closed in theatre?"],"hear":"\"Close it under general, stay a few nights. If not, it might get infected, heal slow, scar badly, maybe not close.\"","mark":"Understanding demonstrated"},{"h":"Retain: check it sticks","say":["One more point: it could also bleed.","In your words, what are the main risks if it isn't closed in theatre?"],"hear":"\"Infection, slow healing, a bad scar, it might not close, it might bleed.\"","mark":"Retention demonstrated"},{"h":"Use or weigh","say":["What matters most to you in this decision?","How do you weigh the scar against going back today?","What would you do if it got infected?"],"hear":"\"The DBT wing. If I'm away too long I might lose my place. A scar's nothing. Dressings and antibiotics, and I'd come back if it went bad.\"","mark":"Weighing demonstrated"},{"h":"Communicate, and rule out impairment","say":["So what have you decided?","How's your mood been over the last few weeks? Any voices, or feeling people are against you?","Any drink or drugs today?"],"hear":"\"I'm going back. Mood's fine, just frustrated. No voices. Nothing, it's prison.\"","mark":"Clear choice; no depression, psychosis or intoxication"},{"h":"Risk of further self-harm","say":["Any thoughts of hurting yourself now, or of ending your life?","What might make you do it again?","What helps when the frustration builds?"],"hear":"\"Not now. If they keep messing me about, maybe. Talking to the wing nurse helps.\"","mark":"Ongoing but not immediate risk identified"},{"h":"Outcome, advice and plan","say":["You understand, remember and weigh the information, and your choice is clear, so it's your decision.","My advice is still to have it closed; I'll ask whether they can do it under local anaesthetic today.","I'll tell prison healthcare, ask about the transfer date, and you can come back any time."],"hear":"\"Local's fine if I can go back tonight.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Test","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","GA closure, few nights; infection, slow healing, scar, failure to close","Understands the nature, purpose and consequences"],["Retain","Repeated the risks accurately minutes later","Retains long enough to decide"],["Use or weigh","DBT transfer matters more than a scar; plan for complications","Weighs in line with his own values"],["Communicate","Clear, consistent refusal","Can communicate"],["Impairment and risk","No low mood, psychosis or intoxication; self-harm risk ongoing","Has capacity; needs a safety plan"]]},"key":"The weighing test is about whether he can use the information, not whether you agree with the outcome. Ask why the transfer matters more than the scar; a reasoned answer that fits his values is capacity.","objmap":[[1],[2],[3,4,5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-t-62":{"name":"Voices about a colleague: the commands, the risk, then the plans","why":"Pins down command voices and risk to a named person, then turns them into treatment and a safety plan he owns.","frame":["Voices and commands","Beliefs about Gary","Risk to others","Risk to self and protective factors","Management plan","Safety plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","Thanks for coming at short notice, Tom. Your GP said things have been tough since that work argument.","I'd like to hear what's been happening, check everyone's safety, and plan together. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Okay.\""},{"h":"The voices","say":["When did things change after the argument?","Tell me about the voices: how many, whose, and what exactly do they say?","Do they tell you to do things? How hard is it not to?"],"hear":"\"A week after. Two men. They say Gary's poisoning my tea and I should deal with him.\"","mark":"Command hallucinations identified"},{"h":"Other symptoms","say":["How sure are you that Gary is doing that? Anyone else against you?","How are sleep, mood and temper? Any drink or drugs?","Have you been taking the quetiapine every night?"],"hear":"\"I'm pretty sure. I take a flask now. Sleeping three hours. Missed a few tablets for early shifts.\"","mark":"Persecutory belief and missed doses identified"},{"h":"Risk to others","say":["Have you had thoughts of harming Gary? What have you pictured?","Have you planned anything, carried anything, or gone looking for him?","Have you ever hurt anyone before, well or unwell?"],"hear":"\"I've thought about hitting him in the canteen. No plan, nothing on me. I pushed my brother when I was ill before.\"","mark":"Violent thoughts without plan identified"},{"h":"Risk to self","say":["Any thoughts of self-harm or wishing you were dead?","Are you managing to eat and look after yourself?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. Ola makes sure I eat.\""},{"h":"Protective factors","say":["What helps you stay well? Who's on your side?","How do you feel about getting help from us now?"],"hear":"\"Ola. I want to stay well; I've done six years. I came here because I'm frightened.\"","mark":"Protective factors identified"},{"h":"Management plan","say":["I think this is your illness coming back, and we can treat it quickly.","Let's restart the quetiapine every night; my consultant may raise the dose, and we can help your sleep for a short time.","The home treatment team will call round daily. If things get worse, hospital is the safe option."],"hear":"\"Okay, as long as it's not hospital now.\""},{"h":"Safety plan","say":["I'd like you off work for two weeks; I'll give you a fit note.","What are your warning signs, and who do you call if the voices push you?","If I ever thought Gary was in serious danger, I may need to share that. I'd try to tell you first."],"hear":"\"Voices louder, no sleep. I'd ring the crisis line and tell Ola.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: tablets every night, crisis team tomorrow, off work, and you'll call if the voices push you to act.","Can you tell me the plan back in your words? Shall we bring Ola in now?"],"hear":"\"Tablets, crisis team, stay home, ring if it gets bad. Yes, bring her in.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Voices and commands","Two male voices, daily: 'deal with him'","Command hallucinations: relapse of schizophrenia"],["Beliefs about Gary","Tea being poisoned; brings own flask","Persecutory belief about a named person"],["Risk to others","Imagined hitting him; no plan or weapon; pushed brother before","Moderate risk, reduced by distance and treatment"],["Risk to self and protective factors","Not suicidal; partner; help-seeking","Low risk to self; strong engagement to build on"],["Plan","Missed doses; agrees home treatment and leave","Restart medication, daily crisis team, off work, written plan"]]},"key":"Get the exact words of the voices and separate thought from intent, plan and preparation. A named target with daily contact makes distance from work part of the treatment, not an optional extra.","objmap":[[2,3],[2,3],[4,5,6],[7],[8,9]]},"c-t-63":{"name":"Seeing the dead husband: grief, depression or psychosis?","why":"Separates normal grief from depression and psychosis and gives her a clear, reassuring explanation.","frame":["The loss","The perceptions","Grief reactions","Depression and psychosis screen","Risk","Formulation"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette Quinn?","Thank you for coming, Bernie. I'm very sorry about Frank.","Your GP mentioned some experiences since he died. Could you tell me about Frank and how things have been?"],"hear":"\"We were married twenty-nine years. It still doesn't feel real.\""},{"h":"The loss","say":["What happened when Frank died? Was there any warning?","What was the funeral like, and how were things between you before?"],"hear":"\"He had chest pain, said it was indigestion. He collapsed in the hall and died in A&E that night.\"","mark":"Sudden, unexpected death identified"},{"h":"Seeing Frank","say":["Tell me what happens when you see him. Where, when, and for how long?","At the time, do you know he isn't really there? How do you feel when it happens?","Anything else: hearing him, smelling him, sensing him near?"],"hear":"\"In his chair, out of the corner of my eye, then he's gone. On waking he's at the end of the bed. I hear him in the kitchen. I know it's not real. It's comforting.\"","mark":"Grief-related and hypnopompic perceptions with insight identified"},{"h":"Grief reactions","say":["How did you feel in the first weeks, and how has it changed?","Do you find yourself feeling angry or guilty about anything?","How are your sleep and appetite?"],"hear":"\"Numb at first. Then furious with myself for not making him see the GP. I cry every day. I sleep badly.\"","mark":"Grief reactions identified"},{"h":"Depression screen","say":["Is your mood low all the time, or does it lift at times?","Can you still enjoy anything? How do you feel about yourself and the future?","How are you managing at work and at home?"],"hear":"\"I laugh with my granddaughter. I'm back at work. I don't feel worthless, just lost without him.\"","mark":"Depressive episode excluded"},{"h":"Psychosis and other screen","say":["Have you heard voices talking about you, or felt people are against you?","How much are you drinking? Any tablets or medicines?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. A glass of wine at the weekend.\"","mark":"Psychosis excluded"},{"h":"Supports and history","say":["Who's been around for you?","Have you lost anyone close before, or had mental health problems in the past?"],"hear":"\"My son and daughter are close by. My mum died ten years ago. Never seen anyone before.\""},{"h":"Risk","say":["Some people say they'd like to be with the person they've lost. Have you felt that?","Have you thought about taking your own life, or planned anything?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I wish I was with him. I'd never do anything; the grandchildren need me.\"","mark":"Passive wish without plans identified"},{"h":"Formulation and close","say":["You're not going mad. Seeing or hearing Frank is common in grief, and you know it isn't real; that's the key difference from mental illness.","Because his death was so sudden, there was no chance to prepare, which often makes grief stronger and more raw.","Grief comes in waves that usually ease over time. Cruse can support you, and I'll see you again in six weeks.","What will you take away from today, in your words?"],"hear":"\"So it's normal, and it's grief. I'll ring Cruse.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The loss","Sudden heart attack, no goodbye, 3 months ago","Sudden death: grief likely to be more intense"],["The perceptions","Brief glimpses, on waking, voice, aftershave; full insight; comforting","Grief-related and hypnopompic perceptions, not psychosis"],["Grief reactions","Numbness, anger, guilt, daily crying, poor sleep","Typical grief reactions"],["Depression and psychosis screen","Mood lifts, works, self-esteem intact; no delusions","No depressive episode or psychotic illness"],["Risk","Wish to be with him, no plans, grandchildren","Passive wish; low current risk with safety advice"]]},"key":"Insight is the hinge. Brief perceptions of the dead person that she knows are not real, felt as comforting, are grief; a fixed belief that he is really there, or voices commenting on her, are not.","objmap":[[2,4],[3],[5,6],[7,8],[9]]},"c-t-64":{"name":"Flat after rehab: find the cause, then match the help to her goals","why":"Turns 'she stays in her room' into named negative symptoms and a small, specific plan she has chosen.","frame":["Negative symptoms","Other causes","Psychological options","Social options","Her goals"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shanice Morrow?","Thanks for seeing me, Shanice. How have you found the move here?","Staff mentioned you've mostly been staying in your room. Could we talk about that and what might help?"],"hear":"\"If you want.\""},{"h":"Her experience","say":["Talk me through yesterday, from when you woke up.","Is there anything you enjoy, even a bit? Anything you look forward to?","What stops you going downstairs?"],"hear":"\"Stayed in bed till eleven. Food's nice, I suppose. Don't look forward to anything. The others are loud.\"","mark":"Avolition, anhedonia and asociality identified"},{"h":"Rule out other causes","say":["Is your mood low and sad, or more flat and empty?","Are you sleepy in the day? Any constipation, dribbling or weight change?","Have the old worries or voices come back at all?"],"hear":"\"Flat. Not sad. Sleepy every afternoon. No, nothing like before.\"","mark":"Depression and positive symptoms excluded; sedation identified"},{"h":"Explain negative symptoms","say":["What you describe has a name: negative symptoms. They're part of the illness, like the beliefs were.","They're not laziness. They can improve, especially with the right activity and support."],"hear":"\"So it's not just me being useless?\""},{"h":"Psychological options","say":["Arts therapy is a group where you make art with a therapist; it's recommended for exactly these symptoms.","CBT for psychosis, about sixteen sessions, can help with thoughts like 'I'll only fail' and build a routine.","If you'd like, family sessions with your sister can help her support you."],"hear":"\"The art one sounds okay. I worry I'd look stupid in a group.\"","mark":"Defeatist beliefs identified"},{"h":"Social options","say":["The OT can help you plan a week with small, doable steps.","The recovery college runs art courses; there are walking groups and, later, help to find work."],"hear":"\"I used to paint all the time.\""},{"h":"Her goals","say":["What would you like to be doing in six months?","What could be a first step this week?"],"hear":"\"Painting again. Seeing Kerry more. Maybe one class.\"","mark":"Personal goals identified"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: OT to plan your week, an art class with a support worker, a referral for arts therapy and CBT, and I'll check your clozapine level.","What will you tell Kerry about today? Can staff help you get to the first class?"],"hear":"\"That I'm going to try art again. Yes, okay.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Negative symptoms","In bed till 11, no drive, looks forward to nothing, avoids residents","Avolition, anticipatory anhedonia, asociality"],["Other causes","Flat not sad, sleepy afternoons, no voices or beliefs","No depressive or psychotic relapse; possible clozapine sedation"],["Psychological options","Worries about looking stupid; 'I'll fail'","Arts therapy and CBTp to target defeatist beliefs"],["Social options","Used to paint; misses structure of rehab","OT timetable, recovery college art course"],["Her goals","Paint again, see Kerry more","Anchor every option to these"]]},"key":"Exclude the fixable mimics first: depression, sedation and residual psychosis. Then offer named options linked to what she wants; a list of therapies without a reason she cares about rarely leads to attendance.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-65":{"name":"Low after mania: name it, explain the misses, offer real options","why":"Gives him a clear diagnosis, makes sense of why treatment stalled, and lets him choose a plan that respects his priorities.","frame":["Current depression and risk","The diagnosis","Why treatment stalled","Medication options","Psychological and lifestyle options"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jordan Asante?","Thanks for seeing me, Jordan. I hear things have been tough since the high spell settled.","Could we talk about what's been happening, what the diagnosis means, and options to help?"],"hear":"\"Yeah, okay.\""},{"h":"Current picture","say":["How's your mood been since the high settled?","How are your sleep, energy, appetite and concentration?","Do you ever feel life isn't worth it? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"Empty. I sleep twelve hours and still feel wiped. Sometimes I think everyone'd be better off without me. I'd never act on it, though.\"","mark":"Bipolar depression with passive death wishes identified"},{"h":"The diagnosis","say":["What did the team tell you about why you came in?","A spell of mania followed by a low like this tells us it's bipolar disorder: the same illness, swinging both ways.","Why sertraline hasn't helped: antidepressants on their own work less well in bipolar lows, and risperidone mainly treats the highs."],"hear":"\"So that's why nothing's worked. Will it keep happening?\"","mark":"Diagnosis explained"},{"h":"Medication options","say":["First, I'd suggest tapering the sertraline.","Lamotrigine treats bipolar lows and doesn't usually cause weight gain. It's started slowly over six weeks because of a rare serious rash.","Lithium is the best long-term protection; it needs regular blood tests, mostly at the start. Quetiapine also works well, but you've been worried about weight."],"hear":"\"Lamotrigine sounds better. What about the blood tests for lithium?\""},{"h":"Psychological options","say":["Talking therapy such as CBT for bipolar depression, about sixteen sessions, can help with the hopeless thoughts.","A bipolar education group and a mood diary help you spot early signs of highs and lows."],"hear":"\"I'd do the group.\""},{"h":"Lifestyle and social","say":["Regular sleep is one of the strongest protectors against relapse; drugs and heavy drinking are the biggest triggers.","You'll need to tell the DVLA and not drive until they say so. We can plan a phased return to work."],"hear":"\"I'm worried about facing them at work.\""},{"h":"Risk","say":["If those thoughts about everyone being better off get stronger, who would you tell?","What would warn you that a high or low was coming back?"],"hear":"\"I'd tell Amara or the nurses. Not sleeping is the first sign of a high.\"","mark":"Relapse signs and crisis plan agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: bipolar disorder, currently in a low. Taper sertraline, start lamotrigine, think about lithium for the long term, and join the education group.","Can you tell me in a sentence what we've agreed? I'll give you leaflets, and we can talk with Amara at the next review."],"hear":"\"Bipolar, coming off sertraline, starting lamotrigine slowly, and I'll read about lithium.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Current depression and risk","Empty, sleeps 12 h, guilt about the high, passive death wishes","Moderate to severe bipolar depression; needs a crisis plan"],["The diagnosis","Two weeks of elation, no sleep, spending, then a rapid low","Bipolar I disorder, current depressive episode"],["Why treatment stalled","Sertraline 150 mg for 3 weeks, little change; risperidone stopped the high","Antidepressant alone is a poor fit; risperidone is antimanic"],["Medication options","Fears weight gain","Lamotrigine first; lithium for prevention; quetiapine if he accepts weight risk"],["Psychological and lifestyle options","Embarrassed, worried about work","CBT, psychoeducation, sleep routine, DVLA, phased return"]]},"key":"Explain why the current tablets failed before offering new ones. 'Antidepressants alone suit bipolar lows poorly; risperidone treats highs' makes the new plan make sense and builds trust in it.","objmap":[[2,7],[3],[3,4],[4,5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-66":{"name":"Three kilos too fast: find the cause, act today","why":"Turns a worried nurse's report into a named diagnosis and a same-day medical plan, without blame.","frame":["Symptoms","Eating and weight","Observations and bloods","Mechanism and danger","Same-day plan and prevention"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ruth from the eating disorder team?","I understand you're worried about Chloe's physical health and a sudden weight jump.","Could you take me through what you've seen, then we'll make a plan together?"],"hear":"\"Three kilos in a week. It doesn't feel right.\""},{"h":"Her symptoms","say":["What did Chloe tell you, in her own words?","How is she today?","Any swelling, breathlessness, palpitations, chest pain, dizziness or confusion?"],"hear":"\"Weak, tired, sick, blurry vision. Today puffy ankles, puffed on stairs, heart racing last night.\"","mark":"Refeeding symptoms identified"},{"h":"Eating and weight","say":["How did her weight change before last week?","Do you know why it jumped? Did anything change in her eating?","Any vomiting, laxatives, or drinking lots of water before weigh-ins?"],"hear":"\"About half a kilo a week. New plan ten days ago; she eats all of it and extra. No purging.\"","mark":"Rapid increase in intake identified"},{"h":"Observations and bloods","say":["What were her pulse, BP and temperature? Lying and standing?","Has she had bloods or an ECG since the plan started?","Who knew about your visit, and when?"],"hear":"\"90/60, pulse 60; 100/70 today. No bloods. I only told the team this morning.\"","mark":"No monitoring since refeeding began"},{"h":"Likely cause","say":["I think Chloe has refeeding syndrome, and it needs action today.","Eating more raises insulin, which pulls phosphate, potassium and magnesium into her cells and uses up thiamine; her body also holds on to water.","Very low salts can upset the heart rhythm and cause fits or heart failure."],"hear":"\"So the swelling is fluid, not real weight?\"","mark":"Refeeding syndrome named"},{"h":"Risk","say":["Her BMI is in the MEED amber range and she has palpitations, so she's not one for routine review.","Has she mentioned self-harm, or feeling hopeless?","Is anyone at home with her?"],"hear":"\"No, she's motivated. She lives with a flatmate.\"","mark":"High physical risk; no self-harm"},{"h":"Immediate plan","say":["I'll ring Chloe and the medical team now: she needs assessment today where results come back today.","They'll do lying and standing BP, temperature, an ECG, and U&Es, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, liver tests and a blood count.","Thiamine and vitamin B start now; salts are replaced as needed. She keeps eating at a steady, set amount; the dietitian adjusts it.","Then half to one kilo a week, with bloods daily until stable."],"hear":"\"So keep her eating, just not too fast.\""},{"h":"Hand over and close","say":["So: likely refeeding syndrome; seen today; tests, thiamine, salts and a steadier plan.","Could you run back through what happens next and who does what?","I'll tell the consultant, GP and dietitian. From now on, abnormal obs go to the duty doctor the same day."],"hear":"\"You ring Chloe and medics, I write up the visit and recheck tomorrow.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Symptoms","Weak, nauseous, blurred vision, ankle swelling, palpitations","Low salts, low thiamine and fluid retention"],["Eating and weight","New plan 10 days ago, eating extra; 3 kg in a week","Intake rose too fast; gain is mostly fluid"],["Observations and bloods","BP 90/60 then 100/70, pulse 60; no bloods or ECG","Unmonitored refeeding; better BP does not rule it out"],["Mechanism and danger","Starved stores, insulin surge","Phosphate, potassium, magnesium fall: arrhythmia, heart failure, fits"],["Same-day plan and prevention","Medics today, thiamine, salts, steady feeding","Treat now, then 0.5-1 kg a week with monitoring"]]},"key":"Explaining refeeding well is not enough: the plan must name a place and a time. Same-day assessment where results return today, every named test, thiamine, and feeding continued at a steady amount.","objmap":[[2,3,4],[5],[6],[7],[7,8]]},"c-t-67":{"name":"Starting ADHD medication: checks, first choice, safe use","why":"Gets a newly diagnosed adult onto the right medicine safely, with support that makes it work.","frame":["Goals and what he has tried","Pre-medication checks","First-line medicine and titration","Side effects, monitoring and rules","Non-drug support"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kwame Asante?","Good to meet you, Kwame. You've already had your diagnosis, so today is about what we can do to help.","Let's hear what you've read and what you hope for, then I'll go through the options."],"hear":"\"Great, I've read a bit online.\""},{"h":"His goals and concerns","say":["What would you most like to change?","What have you already tried at uni or at home, and did it help?","Anything you've read that worries you about tablets?"],"hear":"\"I read a page five times. Short study blocks helped a bit. I don't want to be a zombie.\"","mark":"Ongoing impairment despite adjustments"},{"h":"Before medication","say":["Any heart problems, fainting or chest pain when you exercise?","Any relative with heart disease, or a sudden death at a young age?","Any tics or fits, other medicines, alcohol or drugs?"],"hear":"\"No heart stuff. Hay fever tablets. A few drinks at weekends.\"","mark":"No barrier to a stimulant"},{"h":"First-line medication","say":["The first choice is lisdexamfetamine, a stimulant. It boosts dopamine and noradrenaline, which helps you hold attention.","Start 30 mg each morning, go up 20 mg a week if needed, to a maximum of 70 mg.","It works from the first days. At the right dose you feel like yourself, just focused."],"hear":"\"So I'd know fairly quickly.\""},{"h":"Side effects and monitoring","say":["Common effects: less appetite, poorer sleep, headache, feeling edgy. Most ease within weeks.","We check BP, pulse and weight at each change, then every six months.","Chest pain or a racing heart: stop it and get help."],"hear":"\"Mostly mild, then.\""},{"h":"Rules and alternatives","say":["It's a controlled drug: a month at a time, no early scripts, never share or sell it.","If ADHD or the medicine affects your driving, tell the DVLA.","If it doesn't suit you after six weeks, methylphenidate next, or atomoxetine, a non-stimulant."],"hear":"\"A mate once gave me one before an essay.\"","mark":"Diversion risk discussed"},{"h":"Non-drug help","say":["The ADHD nurse will help you understand how ADHD affects study.","CBT for ADHD, about twelve sessions, works on planning and putting things off.","Register with the disability service for extra time and deadline flexibility."],"hear":"\"Extra time would be massive.\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So: lisdexamfetamine 30 mg mornings, stepping up weekly, checks at each step, plus practical support.","Can you tell me how you'll take it and what we'll check?","Phone review in two weeks, clinic in four; your GP takes over once you're settled. Here's a leaflet."],"hear":"\"Every morning, up each week, BP and weight checked. Never share it.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Goals and what he has tried","Failed assessments; short study blocks helped a little","Impairment persists after adjustments: medication indicated"],["Pre-medication checks","No cardiac history or family sudden death; BP 118/74","Safe to start a stimulant; no ECG needed"],["First-line medicine and titration","Keen, worried about personality change","Lisdexamfetamine 30 mg, +20 mg weekly, max 70 mg"],["Side effects, monitoring and rules","Once took a friend's tablet","Discuss diversion; Schedule 2 rules; checks at each change"],["Non-drug support","Final year, deadlines slip","Psychoeducation, CBT for ADHD, DSA and adjustments"]]},"key":"A good plan names the medicine; an excellent one gives the numbers (start, step, maximum), ties every dose change to BP, pulse and weight, and pairs the tablet with study support.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-t-71":{"name":"What's underneath? Last night, the pattern, then what it isn't","why":"Turns a guarded frequent attender into an evidenced working diagnosis, a current risk picture and a plan she owns.","frame":["Self-harm and its function","Emotions and anger","Relationships and fear of being left","Self-image and emptiness","Impulsivity and substances","What it isn't"],"steps":[{"h":"Open without judgement","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Keira Ashdown?","Not at all. I'm glad you rang for help last night, and I'm glad you're medically okay.","I want to hear what life has been like lately. Could we talk for about twenty minutes?"],"hear":"\"Fine. Everyone here's sick of me anyway.\""},{"h":"Last night's overdose","say":["Can you walk me through last night, from the end of your shift?","How many tablets, had you been drinking, and what did you want to happen?","What made you ring for help? How do you feel about being alive now?"],"hear":"\"Reece accused me of flirting. I'd had a few vodkas. I took twelve and wanted it all to stop, then rang 999 because of my sister.\"","mark":"Interpersonal trigger identified"},{"h":"Self-harm pattern","say":["The notes mention cuts to your arms. Can you tell me about that?","How often, and what does it do for you?","Has there been an overdose before?"],"hear":"\"Razor, about once a week. It lets the pressure out. Six ibuprofen last year.\"","mark":"Repeated self-harm for relief identified"},{"h":"Emotions and relationships","say":["When your mood changes, how fast is it, and how long before it settles?","How often do you lose your temper? In between, do you feel empty inside?","What are relationships usually like for you? How do you feel if someone might leave?"],"hear":"\"It flips in minutes if a mate doesn't text back. I lose it a lot. He's perfect, then an idiot. I'm terrified of being left.\"","mark":"Emotional instability and fear of being left identified"},{"h":"Self-image and impulsivity","say":["How would you describe yourself? Does that change with who you're with?","What have you done on the spur of the moment and regretted? Give me an example.","What do you drink in a typical week, and which drugs do you use?"],"hear":"\"I'm whoever people want. I blew a week's tips on clothes, walked out of three jobs. Vodka most nights after work, coke some weekends.\"","mark":"Unstable self-image and impulsivity identified"},{"h":"What it isn't","say":["Have you had two weeks or more of feeling low every day? Any days of feeling high and needing little sleep?","Have you heard voices when no one's there? Under stress, do things feel unreal, or do people seem against you?","Any nightmares or flashbacks? Any worries about food or weight?"],"hear":"\"Highs last an hour. No voices. Sometimes I watch myself from outside, or think people are talking about me, but it's gone by the next day.\"","mark":"Mood, psychotic, trauma and eating disorders excluded"},{"h":"Background, gently","say":["Can I ask about growing up? Only share what you're comfortable with.","Thank you. We don't need to go further today.","Has anyone helped before, your GP or a counsellor? Any trouble with the police?"],"hear":"\"Dad hit me and shouted, Mum drank. I left at sixteen. I don't want to go into it.\"","mark":"Childhood adversity identified"},{"h":"Risk now","say":["Right now, do you have any thoughts about ending your life?","Are there more tablets at home? If you and Reece row again, what might you do?","Has Reece ever hurt or frightened you? Who helps when things are bad?"],"hear":"\"No thoughts now. There's another box in the bathroom. He grabbed my wrist once. My sister Lily and my flatmate help.\"","mark":"Ongoing risk and protective factors identified"},{"h":"Name it, give hope, agree the plan","say":["What you've described since your teens fits a pattern called emotionally unstable personality disorder, sometimes called borderline.","You're not broken. It's recognised, it usually improves, and therapy like DBT helps most; there's no tablet for it.","Tell me in your words what we've said. Then let's write a crisis plan, get Mia to hold the tablets, and I'll refer you and write to your GP."],"hear":"\"So it's a known thing and therapy helps. Mia can take the box.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Self-harm and its function","Weekly cutting for relief; two overdoses after rows","Recurrent self-harm used to regulate feelings"],["Emotions and anger","Flips in minutes after a missed text; smashed her phone","Emotional instability and intense anger, not a mood episode"],["Relationships and fear of being left","Perfect, then an idiot; terrified of being left","Unstable, intense relationships with frantic fear of abandonment"],["Self-image and emptiness","'I'm whoever people want'; empty, useless","Unstable identity and persistent emptiness"],["Impulsivity and substances","Spending sprees, quitting jobs, vodka binges, cocaine","Impulsivity in several areas"],["What it isn't","Highs last an hour; no voices; detachment passes within a day","No depressive or hypomanic episode, no psychosis; transient stress-linked symptoms fit EUPD"]]},"key":"Ask how long the lows and highs last and what sets them off. Minutes to hours after someone lets her down is emotional instability; a fortnight of low mood or days of elation with little sleep is a mood episode.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5],[6],[7,8],[9]]},"c-u-01":{"name":"Psychosis or depression? The four-part screen","why":"Pins down a first-episode psychosis, rules psychotic depression in or out, and lands a safe plan in ten minutes.","frame":["Content of the belief","Thought interference and voices","Mood screen","Background risk factors","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Callum Adeyemi?","Your friends were worried you haven't been eating, so tell me, in your words, how things have been.","There's no rush at all. We have about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Something's not right. I just don't know what.\""},{"h":"Eating and self-care (1 min)","say":["When did you last have a proper meal? And something to drink?","What makes it hard to eat?","Have your clothes got looser? Are you managing to wash and sleep?"],"hear":"\"Bits of bread. I drink tap water. The food does something to me.\"","mark":"Self-neglect quantified"},{"h":"Unusual experiences (1.5 min)","say":["You said something's not right. What's been happening?","How do you know people have been in your room?","Who could it be, and why you? How sure are you, out of ten?"],"hear":"\"They come at night. The shadows are in a different place in the morning. Ten out of ten.\"","mark":"Systematised persecutory belief identified"},{"h":"Thoughts and perceptions (1.25 min)","say":["You said the food does something to you. Tell me more about that.","Do those thoughts feel like your own, or like they've been put there?","When people are under this much strain, some hear voices when no one's about. Has that happened?"],"hear":"\"They're not mine. I don't hear voices. I'm not mad.\"","mark":"Possible thought insertion identified"},{"h":"Mood screen (1 min)","say":["How's your mood been, on a scale of 0 to 10?","Do you still enjoy anything? How are your sleep and energy?","Do you feel you've done something to deserve this?"],"hear":"\"Mood's okay. Sleep's fine. I haven't done anything wrong.\"","mark":"Psychotic depression unlikely"},{"h":"Background (1 min)","say":["Do you smoke cannabis or use anything else? How much, how often?","Have you seen a doctor about your mental health before?","Has anyone in the family had problems like this?"],"hear":"\"Weed at weekends. My uncle was in a mental hospital for years.\"","mark":"Risk factors for psychosis identified"},{"h":"Risk (1.25 min)","say":["This sounds awful. Do you ever feel like getting back at these people?","Have things ever got so bad you've thought of ending your life?","Have you told anyone? Do you feel safe going back to the flat?"],"hear":"\"I rang the police. There are too many of them to fight. I don't want to die.\"","mark":"Risk to self and others assessed"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["Let me check I've got it right. Is that a fair summary?","I think your mind has been under a lot of strain, in a way that changes how things seem. It's treatable, and there's a team for exactly this.","Tonight we'll check your bloods and heart, and I'll talk to my consultant about where you'd feel safest. Can I tell your friends the plan?"],"hear":"\"OK. As long as someone checks my body.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Belief","Night intruders, memory 'glove', shadows as proof, 10/10","Systematised persecutory delusion"],["Thought interference","Food puts thoughts in that are not his","Probable thought insertion"],["Mood","'Okay', sleeping, no guilt","Against psychotic depression"],["Background","Weekend cannabis, uncle admitted for years","Raised risk of primary psychosis"],["Risk","Barely eating, pulse 104, suspicious of flatmates","Self-neglect and vulnerability: act tonight"]]},"key":"Good residents find the belief. Excellent ones score its certainty, ask whether the inserted thoughts feel alien, do a three-question mood screen to rule out psychotic depression, and treat weeks of poor eating as a physical emergency.","objmap":[[3],[4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-u-02":{"name":"Health anxiety in ten minutes: fear, cycle, explain, plan","why":"Confirms health anxiety quickly, rules out what it isn't, and leaves her with an explanation she believes and a plan she can start today.","frame":["The feared illness","Checking and reassurance","Trigger","What it isn't","The cycle explained"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (0.75 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette Kowalski?","Thank you for coming. Your GP tells me you've been very worried about your health, and the tests haven't given answers.","We have about ten minutes. Tell me what has worried you most, then we'll talk about how we can help."],"hear":"\"Yes. I don't know why I've been sent here.\""},{"h":"Her health fears (1.25 min)","say":["What are you most afraid it might be?","What did you notice, and what did the liver tests show?","Part of you may wonder if this is worry. How likely does that feel, out of ten?"],"hear":"\"My skin looks yellow. Liver, maybe cancer. The tests were fine, but tests miss things.\"","mark":"Current fear and certainty recorded"},{"h":"Pattern over time (1 min)","say":["When did health worries first start?","Was anything happening in your life then?","What other illnesses have you feared?"],"hear":"\"About six years. After Mum died of pancreatic cancer.\"","mark":"Trigger identified"},{"h":"Behaviours and impact (1 min)","say":["How many times a day do you check your skin or eyes?","How long do you spend reading about symptoms?","How often have you seen the GP or been to A&E this year? How long does relief from a normal result last?"],"hear":"\"Ten times a day. Two hours most nights. Every week at the GP. Relief lasts a few hours.\"","mark":"Maintaining behaviours quantified"},{"h":"Exclude other causes and risk (1.25 min)","say":["What's your mood been like? Are you still enjoying things?","Do you get other worries or thoughts you can't push away, not about health?","Has it ever got so bad you felt life wasn't worth living?"],"hear":"\"It gets me down, but I enjoy my family. Nothing like that.\"","mark":"Depression, OCD and delusional belief excluded"},{"h":"Explain the diagnosis (1.75 min)","say":["This has a name: health anxiety. Some worry about health is normal and useful. Here it has taken over.","Look at your pattern: you notice a sensation, fear the worst, check or search, feel relief, and then the worry returns stronger.","Losing your mum to cancer makes this very understandable. Could you put into your own words how the cycle works for you?"],"hear":"\"So the checking keeps it going? That does make sense.\"","mark":"Teach-back achieved"},{"h":"Plan (1.75 min)","say":["The treatment that works best is CBT for health anxiety: 12 to 16 weekly sessions. You can refer yourself to Talking Therapies today.","I'll ask Dr Ellison for one named GP and planned appointments, and no new tests unless there's something new a doctor can see.","Meanwhile, keep a diary of urges to check, and try checking just once a day."],"hear":"\"No more tests? That frightens me a bit.\"","mark":"Plan agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min)","say":["So: health anxiety, CBT, one GP, fewer checks. Does that feel doable?","I'll send you a copy of my letter and see you in eight weeks.","If your mood drops, see your GP that week or call 111 and choose option 2."],"hear":"\"Yes. Thank you for not making me feel silly.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Feared illness","Yellow skin, liver cancer; before that bowel and bone","Shifting disease fears: health anxiety"],["Certainty","'Might be worry', 6/10","Not fixed: not delusional"],["Behaviours","Mirror 10x/day, 2 h online, weekly GP, A&E x3","The maintaining cycle; treatment targets"],["Trigger","Mother's death from pancreatic cancer","Understandable onset; jaundice fear mirrors it"],["Mood and risk","Fed up, enjoys family, no suicidal thoughts","No depressive episode; low risk"]]},"key":"Good residents name health anxiety. Excellent ones use her own numbers to show the cycle, check the belief is not fixed, and change the system around her: one GP, planned visits, no tests without new signs.","objmap":[[2],[4],[3,5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-u-03":{"name":"Mental state in mania: align, observe, elicit, present","why":"Turns a loud, restless man in A&E into a clear mental state, a risk list and a lawful plan in ten minutes.","frame":["Appearance and behaviour","Speech","Mood and affect","Thought form and content","Insight and risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Marcus Lindqvist?","Thanks for seeing me. I understand you want your records; I'd like to help with that.","Can we sit for about ten minutes first so I understand what's been going on?"],"hear":"\"Finally, someone sensible! Ten minutes, then.\"","mark":"Engaged and seated"},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour (1 min)","say":["Why do you need the records today?","(Observe: clothes, pacing, eye contact, distraction by noises, overfamiliarity.)","Shall we both sit down while you tell me?"],"hear":"\"They prove I've been chosen. Sorry, what's that noise? Anyway, the app...\"","mark":"Agitation and distractibility observed"},{"h":"Speech and mood (1 min)","say":["(Note rate, volume, pressure and how hard he is to interrupt.)","How are you feeling in yourself, from 0 to 10?","Do you get irritable with people at the moment?"],"hear":"\"Ten! Never better. People are slow, that's all.\"","mark":"Elated, irritable affect"},{"h":"Thought form and content (1.5 min)","say":["Tell me about the app. Who chose you, and how do you know?","How sure are you, out of ten?","Is anyone trying to stop you, or take it from you?"],"hear":"\"The NHS chose me. Ten out of ten. Someone at the trust is hiding my records to steal it.\"","mark":"Grandiose and persecutory ideas identified"},{"h":"Perceptions and cognition (1 min)","say":["Do you ever hear voices or get messages that others don't?","Do you know where we are and today's date?","Can you count backwards from 20 for me?"],"hear":"\"No voices. Hospital, Saturday. Twenty, nineteen... look, this is pointless.\"","mark":"No perceptual disturbance; attention poor"},{"h":"Insight and risk (1.5 min)","say":["How much sleep have you had this week? How much have you spent?","How did you get here? Any alcohol, cocaine or other drugs?","Has anyone made you so angry you felt like hurting them? Any risky sex, or thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"No sleep for three nights, don't need it. About four grand. Drove. A few beers. I'd never hurt anyone.\"","mark":"Risk profile recorded"},{"h":"Close with him (1 min)","say":["Thank you. I'm worried your mind is running too fast, and you're not sleeping.","I'd like some blood tests and for my senior to see you. Please don't drive today.","For the records, the hospital records team will process a request; I'll get you the form."],"hear":"\"Fine, but quickly. I've got investors waiting.\""},{"h":"Present findings (1.5 min)","say":["Marcus is 34, not known to us. Bright suit, pacing, distractible; rapid pressured speech with flight of ideas; elated and irritable.","Grandiose delusion of being chosen to run a national health app, with persecutory ideas about the trust. No voices. Oriented, poor attention, no insight.","No sleep for three nights, £4,000 spent, drove here. Probable manic episode with psychotic features. Bloods and drug screen please; no driving; I'm calling my consultant, and if he leaves, we'll need an MHA assessment."],"hear":"\"Clear. I'll send the bloods and tell the nurse in charge.\"","mark":"Structured presentation delivered"}],"table":{"cols":["MSE domain","What you saw or heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Appearance and behaviour","Bright suit, pacing, distractible, overfamiliar","Psychomotor agitation, disinhibition"],["Speech","Rapid, loud, hard to interrupt","Pressure of speech"],["Mood and affect","'10/10', snaps when interrupted","Elated and irritable, labile"],["Thought","Chosen to run a national app; trust hiding records","Grandiose delusion with persecutory ideas"],["Risk","No sleep 3 nights, £4,000 spent, drove here","Financial, driving and conflict risk"]]},"key":"Good residents spot mania. Excellent ones describe it with examples, check drugs and organic causes in a first episode, name money and driving as risks, and know Section 5(2) cannot hold him in A&E.","objmap":[[1,2],[2,3],[4,5],[6],[8]]},"c-u-04":{"name":"Mental state in severe depression: wait, observe, name the delusions, act today","why":"Gets a slowed, frightened man to speak, finds the psychotic content and puts his physical risk at the top of the plan.","frame":["Physical state","Mood and biological symptoms","Depressive delusions","Hallucinations","Risk to life"],"steps":[{"h":"Open slowly (1.5 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Stuart Halloran?","Thank you, Stuart. I'm sorry things have been so hard at home.","I'd like to understand how you're feeling. Take as long as you need with each answer."],"hear":"\"(Long pause) ...Yes. Oh dear.\"","mark":"Engagement despite retardation"},{"h":"Appearance and physical state (1 min)","say":["(Observe: clothes, lips, skin, movement, eye contact, posture.)","When did you last have something to drink? And to eat?","Would you have a sip of water with me now?"],"hear":"\"I don't know. There's no point.\"","mark":"Dehydration and self-neglect observed"},{"h":"Speech, mood and biological symptoms (1.25 min)","say":["How's your mood been, from 0 to 10?","Is there anything at all you still enjoy?","How are you sleeping? Is it worse at a particular time of day?"],"hear":"\"Nought. Nothing. I don't sleep. Mornings are worst.\"","mark":"Severe depressive syndrome"},{"h":"Thought content (1.5 min)","say":["What goes through your mind, sitting in the chair?","Do you feel you've done something wrong? Is there anything wrong with your body?","How are things with money at home?"],"hear":"\"I ruined my family. There's no money. My insides have rotted away.\"","mark":"Guilt, poverty and nihilistic delusions identified"},{"h":"Perceptions and cognition (1 min)","say":["Sometimes when people feel this low, they hear voices. Has that happened?","What does it say? Is it talking to you or about you? Does it tell you to do anything?","Do you know where you are today?"],"hear":"\"A voice. It says I'm worthless. Hospital.\"","mark":"Second-person derogatory voice identified"},{"h":"Risk and insight (1.25 min)","say":["Have you thought of ending your life?","Have you stopped eating and drinking so that you would die?","Why did you stop your tablets? Do you think treatment could help?"],"hear":"\"I'm already dead. Nothing can help a dead man.\"","mark":"High risk to life recorded"},{"h":"Close with him (0.75 min)","say":["Thank you, Stuart. I know it feels hopeless, but this kind of depression does get better, and you've recovered before.","A nurse will bring you drinks and stay close. We'll do some blood tests today."],"hear":"\"(Small nod.)\""},{"h":"Present to the consultant (1.5 min)","say":["Stuart is 54, admitted from HTT. Unkempt, dry, retarded, long response latency, mood 0 out of 10.","Nihilistic, guilt and poverty delusions and a second-person derogatory voice. Says he is already dead; not eating or drinking.","Severe depression with psychotic features, high physical risk. I've sent bloods, started fluid and food charts and NEWS2, and held ramipril. Could we discuss urgent ECT and his capacity today?"],"hear":"\"Good. I'll see him straight after the ward round.\"","mark":"Structured presentation with same-day actions"}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you saw or heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Physical state","Dry lips, soiled clothes, unknown last drink","Dehydration, self-neglect: act today"],["Mood","'Nought', no enjoyment, mornings worst","Severe depressive episode"],["Delusions","Insides rotted, ruined family, no money","Nihilistic, guilt and poverty delusions"],["Perception","Voice says he's worthless","Mood-congruent second-person hallucination"],["Risk","'Already dead', no point eating","High risk of death: ECT discussion"]]},"key":"Good residents diagnose psychotic depression. Excellent ones wait out the silences, name each depressive delusion, read 'I'm already dead' as high risk, and lead their presentation with fluids, kidneys and ECT.","objmap":[[1,3],[2,3],[4,5],[6],[8]]},"c-u-05":{"name":"Relapse after a switch: voices, beliefs, medicine, named risk","why":"Confirms a relapse, finds the reason in the medicine, and protects the person he is angry with, in ten minutes.","frame":["Observed behaviour","Voices and thought interference","Persecutory belief","The switch","Risk to the named person"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Patrick Osei?","Thanks for staying to talk. The hostel staff were worried about you.","Then help me understand how things have been for you this week. We have about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Yes. They made me come. They're worried about nothing.\""},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour (1 min)","say":["(Observe: clothes, restlessness, pauses, glances to one side.)","I noticed you stopped and looked over there. Did you hear something?","You seem fidgety. Is it hard to sit still?"],"hear":"\"Maybe. It's personal. I can't keep my legs still since the new tablet.\"","mark":"Responding to voices; possible akathisia"},{"h":"Perceptions (1.5 min)","say":["How many voices? Men or women? Do you know them?","Are they speaking to you, or about you? Do they comment on what you're doing?","Do they ever tell you to do things?"],"hear":"\"Two men. They talk about me. 'Look at him eating.' No orders.\"","mark":"Third-person commentary identified"},{"h":"Thought content and interference (1.5 min)","say":["The staff said you're worried about the food. What's been happening?","How do you know? How sure are you, out of ten?","Can anyone put thoughts in, take them out, or read them? Does anything control your body?"],"hear":"\"The manager's poisoning it. Nine out of ten. They pull thoughts out of my head.\"","mark":"Persecutory delusion and thought withdrawal identified"},{"h":"Medication and triggers (1 min)","say":["When did the tablets change? How many doses have you missed this week?","Any cannabis or other drugs? How's your sleep?","What do you make of the new tablet?"],"hear":"\"Three weeks ago. Missed four or five. One joint at the weekend. It makes me jittery.\"","mark":"Switch, non-adherence and akathisia recorded"},{"h":"Cognition, insight and risk (1.25 min)","say":["If the manager carries on, what might you do? Have you thought of hurting him?","Any thoughts of hurting yourself? Are you eating enough?","Do you think you might be getting unwell again?"],"hear":"\"He'll be sorry. I haven't planned anything. I just eat crisps. Maybe I'm getting ill.\"","mark":"Risk to named person identified"},{"h":"Close with him (1 min)","say":["Thank you, Patrick. I think you're becoming unwell again, and the switch may be part of it.","I'll talk to my consultant about going back to what worked for you.","I need to let the hostel know you've been worried about the manager, so everyone stays safe."],"hear":"\"OK. As long as I don't lose my room.\""},{"h":"Present to the consultant (1.5 min)","say":["Patrick, 38, known schizophrenia. Relapse three weeks after switching risperidone to aripiprazole 10 mg, with akathisia and four or five missed doses.","Two male third-person voices with running commentary, no commands; thought withdrawal; poisoning belief about the hostel manager, 9/10, angry, no plan. No suicidal thoughts; eating poorly.","I suggest restarting risperidone, home treatment with supervised doses if you agree, the hostel told about the manager, and the care coordinator updated tomorrow."],"hear":"\"Agreed. Risperidone 2 mg tonight, stop the aripiprazole.\"","mark":"Structured presentation with risk plan"}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you saw or heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Behaviour","Pausing, glancing at the corner, legs restless","Responding to voices; possible akathisia"],["Voices","Two men, talk about him, comment, no orders","Third-person commentary: first-rank"],["Thought","Manager poisoning food 9/10; thoughts pulled out","Persecutory delusion; thought withdrawal"],["Medication","Switched 3 weeks ago, missed 4-5 doses, jittery","Relapse linked to switch and non-adherence"],["Risk","'He'll be sorry', no plan; eating only crisps","Risk to named person; self-neglect"]]},"key":"Good residents confirm the relapse. Excellent ones find why: a switch, akathisia and missed doses. They turn anger at a named person into a direct question and a specific protective action.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[4],[5],[6,8]]},"c-u-06":{"name":"Lithium in ten minutes: why, how, what to watch, what next","why":"Gives him a clear, honest picture so he can choose lithium safely and knows exactly how to stay safe on it.","frame":["Why lithium for him","Before starting","Dose and levels","Side effects and toxicity","Long-term monitoring and stopping"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (45 sec)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ewan Castellano?","Good to see you looking well, Ewan. I hear the olanzapine's caused weight gain and tiredness.","Let's talk about lithium as an option and go through your questions. We have about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Two stone. I hate it.\"","mark":"Concern acknowledged"},{"h":"His view (1 min)","say":["What have you heard about lithium so far?","What matters most to you in a medicine?","Anything that worries you about it?"],"hear":"\"Staying out of hospital. But I read it's toxic, and I hate needles.\"","mark":"Priority and worries identified"},{"h":"Why lithium (1 min)","say":["Lithium has the best evidence of any medicine for preventing manic and depressive relapses.","It also lowers the risk of suicide, which no other mood stabiliser has shown as clearly.","Quetiapine and valproate didn't hold you, so it's the natural next step."],"hear":"\"So why wasn't I offered it first?\"","mark":"Rationale given"},{"h":"Starting checks (1 min)","say":["Before starting we check your kidneys, thyroid and calcium, a blood count, and your weight.","If there's any heart risk, an ECG too.","Do you take anything else, even ibuprofen from the chemist?"],"hear":"\"Just ibuprofen now and then for my back.\"","mark":"Baseline tests and NSAID use identified"},{"h":"Starting and levels (1 min)","say":["You'd start on 400 mg at night, always the same brand.","A week later you have a blood test 12 hours after the dose, so the next morning.","We aim for 0.6 to 0.8, adjusting the dose weekly until it's steady."],"hear":"\"So a blood test every week at first?\"","mark":"Dose, level timing and target stated"},{"h":"Side effects and toxicity (2 min)","say":["Common early effects: thirst, passing more urine, a fine shake of the hands, a metallic taste. Some people gain weight.","Too high a level shows as vomiting, diarrhoea, a coarse shake, unsteadiness or muddled thinking. Stop it and get a level that day.","Swap ibuprofen for paracetamol, and drink more in hot weather, when exercising or if you're ill."],"hear":"\"So if I get a stomach bug I need to be careful?\"","mark":"Toxicity and interactions explained"},{"h":"Long-term monitoring and stopping (1 min 15 sec)","say":["Once steady, levels go to three-monthly through year one, then six-monthly.","Kidneys, thyroid, calcium and weight every six months, because lithium can affect them over years.","Never stop suddenly: that can bring on mania. Olanzapine comes down slowly only once lithium is working."],"hear":"\"OK. And my partner and I want kids. Is that a problem?\"","mark":"Monitoring and stopping risks explained"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min 45 sec)","say":["In a man, lithium isn't known to affect a baby he fathers.","Can you tell me the warning signs and what you'd do?","Here's the booklet, record book and alert card. Shall we do your baseline bloods today and decide together in two weeks?"],"hear":"\"Sickness, diarrhoea, bad shake, wobbly: stop and get checked. Yes, bloods today.\"","mark":"Teach-back correct; next step agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Lithium topic","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Priority","Stay out of hospital; hates the weight","Lithium fits his goal; be honest that it can add weight too"],["Worries","'Toxic'; blood tests; time off work","Explain levels as the safety net; early-morning bloods"],["Interactions","Ibuprofen for back pain","Switch to paracetamol; tell every pharmacist"],["Family plans","Wants children with his partner","No known risk through fathers; reassure honestly"],["Transition","Wants olanzapine stopped now","Lithium therapeutic first, then taper over about 4 weeks"]]},"key":"A good explanation lists side effects. An excellent one turns them into actions he can take: which signs mean stop and get a level today, which tablets to avoid, and why lithium is never stopped suddenly.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5],[6],[7],[8]]},"c-u-07":{"name":"Is it BDD? Concern, conviction, behaviours, risk","why":"Gets the diagnosis, its severity and the specific BDD risks in ten minutes without ever endorsing the defect.","frame":["The perceived defect","Conviction and insight","Checking and hiding","Impact and surgery","Differentials","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (45 sec)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Adeel Rashid?","Thanks for coming, Adeel. Mr Hale, the surgeon, asked me to see you. It sounds like your nose causes you a lot of distress.","Could you tell me about it, and how it affects your days? We have about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Yeah. I don't really know why I'm here. It ruins everything.\"","mark":"Distress acknowledged"},{"h":"The concern itself (1 min 15 sec)","say":["What exactly do you see when you look at it?","When did it start, and how many hours a day does it take up?","Out of 100, how sure are you it looks that way? Could you be wrong?"],"hear":"\"Since I was 15. Four or five hours a day. 90%... my friends say it's fine, but they're being kind.\"","mark":"Preoccupation and conviction measured"},{"h":"Checking and hiding (1 min)","say":["What do you do to check it, or to hide it?","Do you compare yourself with others, online or in person?","Do you ask people whether it looks OK?"],"hear":"\"I check it on my phone camera all day. I cover it in photos. I tape it at night.\"","mark":"Safety behaviours identified"},{"h":"Impact on life (1 min)","say":["What has this stopped you doing?","How is it affecting work and seeing friends?"],"hear":"\"I stopped the gym. I've asked to work in the stockroom so customers don't stare.\"","mark":"Functional impairment identified"},{"h":"Surgery and procedures (45 sec)","say":["How many surgeons have you seen, and has anyone done anything to your nose?","What would change for you if the operation happened?"],"hear":"\"Three said no. If it was fixed, I'd finally have a life.\"","mark":"Surgical seeking and expectations recorded"},{"h":"Mood and other conditions (1 min 15 sec)","say":["What's your mood like lately? Are you still getting pleasure from things?","Any other thoughts or habits you can't stop, not about your looks? Any worries about weight, eating or muscles?","Ever hear voices, or feel people are plotting about you? Alcohol or drugs?"],"hear":"\"Low for a few months. No to the rest. A few drinks at weekends.\"","mark":"Depressive symptoms identified; OCD, eating, psychosis screened"},{"h":"Risk (1 min 30 sec)","say":["Has it ever got so bad that you've felt life isn't worth it? Any plans?","Have you ever thought of doing something to your nose yourself, or buying a treatment online?","Have you ever been so angry with a surgeon that you thought of getting back at them?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think what's the point if it can't be fixed. No plan. I've looked at filler pens online.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts and DIY risk identified"},{"h":"Background (45 sec)","say":["Were you ever teased about your looks growing up?","Who do you have around you? Have you had any help for this before?"],"hear":"\"They called me 'beak' at school. My sister's great. Never seen anyone.\"","mark":"Background and supports recorded"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min 45 sec)","say":["What you describe sounds like body dysmorphic disorder. It's common, it's not vanity, and talking therapy and medicine help a lot.","Please don't buy or try anything on your nose yourself. If things feel unbearable, call Samaritans on 116 123 or NHS 111 option 2.","Can I write to Mr Hale and your GP? My consultant will join us now, and we'll book you back in two weeks."],"hear":"\"OK. I'm not promising anything, but I'll come back.\"","mark":"Likely BDD named; follow-up and crisis route agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["BDD area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Preoccupation","Nose since 15; 4-5 hours a day","Core feature, clearly beyond ordinary concern"],["Conviction","90% sure; others 'being kind'","Poor insight: still BDD, affects severity"],["Behaviours","Camera checking, covering, taping, comparing","Repetitive behaviours: the targets for exposure work"],["Impact","No gym, stockroom shifts, avoids dates","Moderate to severe impairment"],["Risk","Passive suicidal thoughts; filler pens online","Needs a crisis plan and explicit DIY advice"]]},"key":"A good history confirms BDD. An excellent one puts a number on conviction, asks about DIY and online procedures by name, and never agrees or argues about the nose.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5],[6],[7],[9]]},"c-u-08":{"name":"The unspoken side effect: name it, place it, rule out the rest, choose together","why":"Turns a vague request to stop into a specific, treatable problem and a plan that keeps her well.","frame":["Desire","Arousal","Orgasm","Other causes","Options and safe stopping"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (45 sec)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shona Gallagher?","Thanks for coming, Shona. Dr Kaur said you'd like to stop sertraline. I'm keen to understand why.","Some side effects are awkward to talk about. Everything here is confidential, and nothing will shock me. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Okay. It's a bit embarrassing.\"","mark":"Confidentiality given; awkwardness normalised"},{"h":"Her side effects (1 min 45 sec)","say":["Many people get sexual side effects on these tablets. Is it something like that?","Is it your interest in sex, becoming aroused, or reaching orgasm?","When did it start? Is it the same when you're on your own?"],"hear":"\"I can't finish, or it takes ages. It started about a week in. Same on my own.\"","mark":"Orgasm and arousal problem identified"},{"h":"Impact on her (45 sec)","say":["How was your sex life before the depression, and during it?","How is this affecting you and your boyfriend?"],"hear":"\"It was fine before. Now he thinks I've gone off him.\"","mark":"Timeline and relationship impact recorded"},{"h":"Mood now (1 min)","say":["How's your mood compared with a month ago?","Have you been depressed before?","Have you had thoughts of self-harm or wishing you weren't here, lately?"],"hear":"\"Much better. Once before, at 22. No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Good response; no suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Other causes (1 min 15 sec)","say":["Any contraception, or plans for pregnancy?","How much alcohol, and any drugs? Any physical health problems?","Has anything sexual ever happened that you didn't want?"],"hear":"\"The pill for five years. A few glasses at weekends. No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Other causes screened"},{"h":"Options for her (1 min 30 sec)","say":["You don't have to choose between your mood and your sex life.","Option one: lower to 50 mg and see if the side effect eases while your mood holds.","Option two: switch to a medicine with fewer sexual effects, such as mirtazapine, which can cause sleepiness and weight gain. Waiting it out is a third, but this often doesn't settle."],"hear":"\"I'd rather try the lower dose first.\"","mark":"Options given with trade-offs"},{"h":"Stopping safely (45 sec)","say":["Please don't stop suddenly: you may feel dizzy, irritable or get electric-shock feelings.","And stopping a month in risks the depression coming back. We usually continue at least six months after you're well."],"hear":"\"I was going to stop this weekend, to be honest.\"","mark":"Discontinuation and relapse risk explained"},{"h":"Shared decision (45 sec)","say":["So we'll reduce to 50 mg from tomorrow and review in four weeks.","What would you like me to say in my letter to Dr Kaur?"],"hear":"\"You can say it's a sexual side effect. That's fine.\"","mark":"Plan and GP letter wording agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min 30 sec)","say":["Can you tell me the plan in your words?","If your mood drops or dark thoughts come, ring your GP the same day, NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123.","Here's a leaflet on sertraline. I'll see you in four weeks."],"hear":"\"Fifty milligrams, don't stop suddenly, back in four weeks. Thank you.\"","mark":"Teach-back correct; safety net given"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Desire","Low while depressed, now returning","Depression, not the drug, drove this part"],["Arousal and orgasm","Slow arousal, orgasm delayed or absent, since week 1, alone too","SSRI-induced sexual dysfunction"],["Mood","Much brighter; no suicidal thoughts","Good response: protect it"],["Other causes","Pill unchanged for 5 years, low alcohol, well","Drug is the likeliest cause"],["Her wish","Stop this weekend","Risk of discontinuation and relapse: offer dose reduction"]]},"key":"A good doctor finds the sexual side effect. An excellent one separates desire, arousal and orgasm on a timeline, rules out other causes, and gives her a real choice that keeps the antidepressant working.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,5],[6,7],[8,9]]},"c-u-09":{"name":"Is it adult ADHD? The triad, the timeline, the mimics","why":"Reaches a defensible initial diagnosis in ten minutes and tells her what happens next.","frame":["Inattention","Hyperactivity","Impulsivity","Onset before 12","Mimics","Impulsivity risks"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (45 sec)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Imogen Faulkner?","Thank you for coming, Imogen. Occupational health mentioned work has been tough since your promotion.","Could you tell me what's been happening, in your words? We have about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Yes. Imogen. That's an understatement.\"","mark":"Her account invited"},{"h":"Current difficulties (1 min 45 sec)","say":["Do you lose focus, lose things or leave tasks half-done? Can you give me an example?","Do you find it hard to sit through meetings, or catch yourself interrupting?","Do you make snap decisions you later regret?"],"hear":"\"I can't type for more than ten minutes. I lose my keys every day. People say I talk over them.\"","mark":"Inattention, hyperactivity and impulsivity identified"},{"h":"Why now (45 sec)","say":["How did you manage as a writer that you can't as an editor?","What tricks or routines kept you on track?"],"hear":"\"I worked at home, pacing, dictating, all on adrenaline. Now it's meetings and managing people.\"","mark":"Loss of compensating structure identified"},{"h":"Since childhood (1 min 15 sec)","say":["What were you like at primary school? What did your reports say?","Was it the same at home?","Is there someone who knew you then, and do you still have any reports?"],"hear":"\"'Bright but daydreams'. Always in detention for calling out. Mum's kept every report.\"","mark":"Onset before 12, two settings, collateral source named"},{"h":"Impact across life (45 sec)","say":["How does this affect home, money and relationships?","Any problems with driving?"],"hear":"\"Late fees on bills, my partner gets frustrated, and two speeding fines.\"","mark":"Impairment across settings"},{"h":"Other explanations (1 min 30 sec)","say":["How's your mood? Do you still enjoy things?","Ever had a spell of days feeling unusually high, needing much less sleep?","Any worry, trouble sleeping, alcohol or drugs? Any difficulty reading people or very fixed routines?"],"hear":"\"Mood's fine. Never high like that. I drink a little now; more in my twenties. No to the rest.\"","mark":"Depression, bipolar, anxiety, ASD and substances screened"},{"h":"Risk (45 sec)","say":["With all this pressure, has it ever pushed you towards hurting yourself, or feeling you'd rather not be here?","Any impulsive spending or debts?"],"hear":"\"No, never. I buy things on a whim, but nothing serious.\"","mark":"No suicidal thoughts; impulsivity risks noted"},{"h":"Background (45 sec)","say":["Anyone in the family with similar difficulties?","Any heart problems, fainting, or family members who died suddenly young?"],"hear":"\"My brother was just like me. No heart problems.\"","mark":"Family history and cardiac screen recorded"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min 45 sec)","say":["This fits adult ADHD: lifelong, from childhood, and unmasked when the structure changed. I can't confirm it today.","Next: a short questionnaire today, a referral for a full specialist assessment with your mum's account and your reports, and, with your consent, a letter to occupational health about adjustments.","What will you tell your partner about today?"],"hear":"\"That it might be ADHD, there's a proper assessment, and work can help meanwhile.\"","mark":"Initial impression and next steps understood"}],"table":{"cols":["ADHD domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Inattention","Can't type >10 min, loses keys, unfinished tasks","Core inattentive symptoms"],["Hyperactivity and impulsivity","Restless in meetings, interrupts, whims","Combined presentation likely"],["Onset","'Bright but daydreams', detentions, before 12, home too","Childhood onset, two settings"],["Mimics","Mood fine, no high spells, little alcohol","Depression, bipolar and substances unlikely"],["Impulsivity risks","Two speeding fines, impulse buys","Driving advice; watch finances"]]},"key":"A good history lists current symptoms. An excellent one proves they started before 12, shows they are constant rather than episodic, and names a collateral source, so the referral can be accepted first time.","objmap":[[2],[4],[6],[7,8],[9]]},"c-u-10":{"name":"After an overdose: before, during, after, now, and the plan","why":"Builds a full picture of the act and her current risk in ten minutes and leaves her with a plan she helped write.","frame":["Before","During","After","Now: thoughts and mental state","Needs and protective factors","Safety plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (45 sec)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Chloe Matthews?","Thanks for seeing me, Chloe. I know you've had a hard time. Is now an okay moment to talk?","First I'd like to hear what happened; then we can work out together how to keep you safe. Is that alright? It'll take about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Yeah. I suppose.\"","mark":"Consent and privacy established"},{"h":"Before the overdose (1 min 15 sec)","say":["Can you take me through yesterday, from when things started to go wrong?","When did the thought of taking tablets first come? Where did you get them?","Did you leave a note or message, or do anything so you wouldn't be found?"],"hear":"\"We had a row. I bought them on the way home. No note. Ryan was out.\"","mark":"Impulsive; no note or final acts"},{"h":"The overdose itself (1 min)","say":["How many did you take, over how long? Anything else, like alcohol or other tablets?","What were you expecting the tablets to do? Were you hoping to die?"],"hear":"\"All 32, over an hour, with a bottle of wine. I wanted everything to stop. Maybe to die.\"","mark":"Ambivalent intent; alcohol involved"},{"h":"After the overdose (45 sec)","say":["What happened next? How did you get here?","How do you feel now about still being here?"],"hear":"\"I texted my sister. She rang 999. I'm embarrassed. Glad, I think.\"","mark":"Help-seeking; some relief"},{"h":"Mental state and history (1 min 30 sec)","say":["How's your mood been over the last few months? Sleep, enjoyment, energy?","Have you ever hurt yourself before, or seen anyone for your mental health?","How much do you drink usually? Any drugs? Any experiences like hearing voices?"],"hear":"\"Low since the summer. I cut myself at 16. About three bottles of wine a week. No voices.\"","mark":"Depressive symptoms and alcohol use identified"},{"h":"Risk factors now (1 min 15 sec)","say":["Right now, are you having thoughts about ending your life? Any plans?","Do you feel safe at home? Has Ryan ever hurt or frightened you?","Are there tablets at home? What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"Not now. He shouts, but he's never hurt me. There's more paracetamol in the cupboard. My nephew keeps me going.\"","mark":"No current intent; access to means; protective factor"},{"h":"Formulation (30 sec)","say":["From what you've said, the overdose came in a moment of overwhelming upset, on top of months of feeling low and drinking more.","So the priorities are your mood, the drinking and the stress at home."],"hear":"\"That's fair. I've not been right for ages.\"","mark":"Needs-based formulation shared"},{"h":"Plan together (1 min 45 sec)","say":["You'll stay until the drip finishes and the bloods are fine; then you can go home with a plan.","Let's write your safety plan: warning signs, what helps, who you'll call. Could your sister clear the tablets and stay a few nights?","The crisis team will contact you within three days, and I'll write to your GP today about treatment for your mood."],"hear":"\"OK. Kirsty will. I'd like help with my mood.\"","mark":"Safety plan, means removal and follow-up agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min 15 sec)","say":["Can you tell me what you'd do if those thoughts came back tonight?","Samaritans on 116 123 any time, NHS 111 option 2, or 999 if you might act on them.","I'll let the A&E doctor and nurse know the plan."],"hear":"\"Text Kirsty, ring Samaritans, or 999 if it's bad.\"","mark":"Teach-back correct; handover to A&E"}],"table":{"cols":["Phase","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Before","Row, bought 32 tablets on the way home, no note","Impulsive act, no final acts"],["During","All 32 over an hour, with wine; 'maybe to die'","Ambivalent intent; alcohol lowered restraint"],["After","Texted sister; embarrassed, partly glad","Help-seeking; some relief"],["Now","Low 4 months, more drinking, no plans, tablets at home","Untreated depression; access to means"],["Protective","Sister, nephew, job","Build these into the safety plan"]]},"key":"A good assessment gets the facts of the overdose. An excellent one links them to her ongoing needs, asks about home safety privately, removes the means with a named person, and replaces a risk label with a plan.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,6],[5,6],[7],[8,9]]},"c-u-12":{"name":"Before, during, after: building the seizure story","why":"A precise, time-ordered account of one spell plus a witness view separates temporal lobe seizures from dissociation and panic.","frame":["Aura (before)","Ictal features (during)","Post-ictal state (after)","Pattern and triggers","Risk factors","Differentials and impact"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (under 1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in this clinic. Are you Funmilayo Bankole?","Thanks for coming, Funmi. Your GP mentioned some unusual episodes. They sound unsettling.","Could you walk me through one, from the very start?"],"hear":"\"They really are. I wasn't sure why I'd see a psychiatrist.\""},{"h":"Describe an episode (1.5 min)","say":["What's the very first thing you notice?","Any feeling rising from your stomach, or a smell or taste that isn't there?","Any sudden familiarity, as if it's happened before, or a wave of fear?"],"hear":"\"A rising feeling in my stomach, then I smell burning rubber. Then I'm watching myself from above.\"","mark":"Aura identified"},{"h":"During the episode (1 min)","say":["Can you hear people and answer them during it?","What has your flatmate seen you do with your face or hands?","Do you ever fall, stiffen or shake all over?"],"hear":"\"She says I stare and smack my lips and don't answer for about a minute.\"","mark":"Automatisms with impaired awareness identified"},{"h":"Afterwards (under 1 min)","say":["How do you feel straight afterwards?","How long until you feel yourself again?","Do you remember the whole thing?"],"hear":"\"Confused and wiped out for about twenty minutes. I lose bits of it.\"","mark":"Post-ictal confusion identified"},{"h":"Pattern (under 1 min)","say":["How often do they happen now, compared with when they started?","Does anything bring them on: poor sleep, alcohol, stress, missed meals?"],"hear":"\"Twice a month now. Worse when I'm tired or I've been drinking.\"","mark":"Triggers identified"},{"h":"Background (under 1 min)","say":["Did you ever have fits as a baby or toddler, maybe with a fever?","Any serious head injury, meningitis or brain infection?","Anyone in the family with epilepsy? Any medicines or recreational drugs?"],"hear":"\"Mum says I had fits with a temperature when I was two. Nothing else.\"","mark":"Risk factor: febrile convulsions"},{"h":"Other causes (under 1 min)","say":["Do you ever feel detached or unreal for hours at a time, without the smell?","Any panic, racing heart or headaches with them?"],"hear":"\"No, it's only ever in these spells.\"","mark":"Depersonalisation disorder and panic less likely"},{"h":"Risk and impact (1 min)","say":["How has this affected work and going out?","And your mood lately? Any thoughts of wishing you weren't here?","Do you drive, swim or work at heights?"],"hear":"\"I've cut my hours and stopped going out. Low, but never like that. I drive sometimes.\"","mark":"Low mood, no suicidal thoughts; drives"},{"h":"Summary and close (2.5 min)","say":["I think these are small seizures starting in the temporal lobe, a type of epilepsy. You're not going mad, and nothing points to a tumour.","I'm referring you urgently to neurology for a brain wave test and an MRI scan, with some bloods and a heart tracing.","Until then, please don't drive and tell the DVLA; showers not baths, and no swimming alone.","Could you sum up what we've agreed? Could your flatmate film the next one?"],"hear":"\"So it's epilepsy, I stop driving, and I get tests. That's actually a relief.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Phase","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Before","Rising stomach feeling, smell of burning rubber","Aura: focal seizure in the temporal lobe"],["During","Out-of-body, unreal; stares, lip smacking, unresponsive","Impaired awareness with automatisms"],["After","Confused and exhausted 20 minutes, memory gaps","Post-ictal state: not panic or dissociation"],["Pattern","Twice a month, worse with poor sleep and alcohol","Typical seizure triggers"],["Background","Febrile convulsions at 2","Recognised risk factor"]]},"key":"Depersonalisation is a symptom, not a diagnosis. A smell aura, lip smacking and confusion afterwards make it a seizure: always get the witness account and ask about driving.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,5],[6],[7,8],[9]]},"c-u-15":{"name":"Sudden visual loss: story, red flags, pupils, then the fundus","why":"Gives the eye team clean findings and keeps an open mind, while noticing her mood and risk.","frame":["Onset and nature","Red flags for organic causes","Stress, mood and risk","Acuity, pupils, colour","Fundoscopy"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (under 1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working alongside the medical team. Are you Leila Haddad?","I'm sorry, that sounds really worrying. I'd like to hear exactly what happened, then look into the back of your eyes with a light. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Okay. Nobody has looked yet.\""},{"h":"Visual loss story (1.25 min)","say":["Take me back to Monday morning. What did you notice?","Is it completely dark in that eye, or blurred, patchy, or like a curtain?","Does it hurt to move the eye? Any flashes or floaters before it went?"],"hear":"\"I woke and it was black. No pain, no flashes. It hasn't changed.\"","mark":"Sudden painless total loss"},{"h":"Linked symptoms (under 1 min)","say":["Any headache, weakness, numbness, tingling, or trouble with speech?","Have you ever had blurred vision or numbness before that went away?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that ever.\"","mark":"No neurological red flags"},{"h":"Background and stress (1.25 min)","say":["Contact lenses, diabetes, blood pressure, any medicines?","What's been going on in your life over the last few weeks?","And your mood? Any moments of feeling life isn't worth it?"],"hear":"\"I lost my job last week. Sometimes I think I'd rather not wake up. I'd never do anything.\"","mark":"Recent stress; passive death wishes, no plans"},{"h":"Preparation (1.5 min)","say":["I'll check how well each eye sees, covering one at a time.","Now I'll shine a light in each eye, then swing it from one to the other.","Can you read the numbers in these coloured dots with your right eye?"],"hear":"\"I can't see anything with the left. The right is fine.\"","mark":"L no light perception, no RAPD"},{"h":"Fundoscopy (2.25 min)","say":["I'll dim the lights. Please look at that spot on the far wall and keep your eye still, even when I come close.","I'm checking the red glow from a distance, then coming in to look at the nerve at the back of your eye.","Now the blood vessels, top and bottom, both sides. Last, look straight into the light for a second."],"hear":"\"The light's very bright.\"","mark":"Left disc, vessels and macula normal"},{"h":"Compare eyes (1 min)","say":["Now the right eye, the same way: keep looking at the spot.","That one looks healthy too."],"hear":"\"So what did you see?\"","mark":"Right fundus normal"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["The back of both eyes looks healthy, and your pupils react normally. That's reassuring.","The eye specialists still need to look with special equipment, so I'm making sure they see you today. Please don't drive for now.","I'll come back tomorrow to see how you're coping. If you get eye pain, a headache, or the other eye changes, tell the nurse straight away."],"hear":"\"Thank you for actually looking.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Onset and nature","Woke with total, painless left loss; unchanged","Needs same-day eye assessment"],["Red flags","No eye pain, flashes, headache or neurology","Optic neuritis and detachment less likely"],["Pupils","Equal, normal reactions, no RAPD","Positive sign against optic nerve damage"],["Fundi","Normal red reflex, discs, vessels, maculae both eyes","No retinal or disc cause seen"],["Mood and risk","Job loss; passive death wishes, no plans","Liaison follow-up and safety plan"]]},"key":"Normal findings are not 'nothing wrong'. Record them precisely, note the pupils as a positive sign, keep the label open until the eye team see her, and don't forget her mood.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-u-22":{"name":"Every domain in ten minutes: a targeted bedside cognitive check","why":"Shows which domains are impaired and which are spared, quickly and fairly, so the team can choose the right next tests.","frame":["Orientation and attention","Memory: registration and delayed recall","Language","Visuospatial","Executive"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, purpose and consent (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mr Lau?","Thanks, Kenneth. I'd like to do some memory and thinking tasks with you, about ten minutes.","Some will feel easy, some harder; that's normal. Do you have your glasses and hearing aid? Shall we start?"],"hear":"\"Like a test? ... Go on then. Glasses are here; my hearing's fine.\"","mark":"Consent and senses checked"},{"h":"Brief history (1 min)","say":["What have you noticed, and since when?","Things from long ago, like opening the restaurant: still clear?","How's your mood? How much do you drink? Is English your everyday language, and when did you leave school?"],"hear":"\"Six months. Names, birthdays. Old things are clear as day. Mood's fine, just fed up with it. A couple of glasses a week. English always; left school at sixteen.\"","mark":"Onset, long-term memory, mood, alcohol, language"},{"h":"Orientation and attention (1.25 min)","say":["What's the day, date, month, year and season?","What's the name of this building, and which floor are we on?","Take 7 away from 100, then keep taking 7 away until I say stop."],"hear":"\"Thursday the 5th of November, 2026, autumn. The building? Oh... it's new to me. 93, 86, 79, 73... no, 72, 65.\"","mark":"Time oriented; place not known; attention largely intact"},{"h":"Registration (0.75 min)","say":["I'll say three words. Repeat them, then keep them in mind: I'll ask again later.","River, candle, garden."],"hear":"\"River... candle... garden.\"","mark":"3/3 registered"},{"h":"Language (1.5 min)","say":["What's this called? And this?","Please say after me: no ifs, ands or buts.","Take this paper in your right hand, fold it in half and put it on the table. Then write two sentences about your restaurant."],"hear":"\"Watch. Pen. ... Writing? Give me a moment... I'm all in a muddle with this.\"","mark":"Naming, repetition, command intact; writing effortful"},{"h":"Visuospatial and executive (2.25 min)","say":["Put the numbers in this clock and set the hands to ten past eleven.","Now tell me as many words as you can that start with F in one minute. No names of people or places."],"hear":"\"(Draws a correct clock.) Fish, fork, fire, father, fun, fast... flower, food.\"","mark":"Clock correct; fluency 8 words"},{"h":"Delayed recall (0.75 min)","say":["What were the three words I asked you to remember?","One was something you'd find in a house that gives light."],"hear":"\"Oh dear. They've gone. ... A candle?\"","mark":"0/3 free recall; 1/3 with cue"},{"h":"Feedback and close (1.5 min)","say":["How do you feel it went?","Some parts went well: the clock, the sums, the words. Remembering the words and knowing where we are were harder. That's worth looking into, not a diagnosis.","Next: blood tests, a brain scan, and, with your permission, a chat with your wife. We'll see you both with the results. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"So what happens now? Is something wrong with my brain? ... All right. She can come in.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Orientation and attention","Date correct; did not know the building; serial 7s 4/5","Place disorientation; attention preserved"],["Memory","3/3 registered; 0/3 free recall; 1/3 with cue","Encoding or storage deficit: hippocampal pattern"],["Language","Naming, repetition, command intact; writing slow and muddled","Mild writing difficulty; recheck at full assessment"],["Visuospatial","Correct clock at ten past eleven","Parietal function preserved"],["Executive","8 F-words in a minute, no rule breaks","Fluency in the lower range; no clear frontal pattern"]]},"key":"Good is covering every domain. Excellent is ordering the tests so recall follows real distraction, using a cue to tell storage from retrieval failure, and recording what could lower the scores.","objmap":[[1],[2],[3,4,5,6,7],[7,8],[8]]},"c-u-23":{"name":"Before, during, after: overdose risk in a young person in ten minutes","why":"Separates the intent of the act from her risk now, and turns that into a plan that keeps her safe tonight.","frame":["Before: trigger and planning","During: method, intent, lethality","After: help-seeking and view now","Mood and self-harm","Current risk and protective factors"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and confidentiality (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Amira?","I know you've told your story a few times already tonight. I'm sorry; it's a lot.","This stays between us, unless I'm worried you're not safe. Could we talk for about ten minutes, just us, about what happened?"],"hear":"\"Yeah... Okay.\"","mark":"Confidentiality limits stated; seen alone"},{"h":"Before and during (2 min)","say":["What was happening just before?","How many did you take, what time, and where were they from? Was it planned or sudden?","What did you want to happen? Did you think that many could kill you? Was anyone with you; did you leave a note?"],"hear":"\"They posted stuff about me again. I took 16 from the kitchen box at seven. I didn't plan it. I wanted it to end. I thought it'd be enough. I was alone. No note.\"","mark":"High intent, impulsive, believed lethal"},{"h":"After (1 min)","say":["What did you do after you'd taken them?","How did your parents find out?","How do you feel now about still being here?"],"hear":"\"I went to bed hoping I wouldn't wake. Then I felt sick and got scared, and told Mum. I regret it now, because of them.\"","mark":"Help-seeking; regret mainly for parents"},{"h":"What led up to it (1.25 min)","say":["Tell me about the posts. How long has it been going on?","How are school and friends otherwise? And things at home?","Is anyone, at home, school or online, hurting you or making you do things you don't want?"],"hear":"\"Three months. Same girls. Home's fine, my parents are nice. No one's doing anything else.\"","mark":"Bullying trigger; no other safeguarding disclosure"},{"h":"Mood and mental state (1.5 min)","say":["How's your mood been over those months?","Sleep, appetite, concentration? Things you used to enjoy?","How do you see the future? Any drinking or drugs? Any voices or odd experiences?"],"hear":"\"Low. Can't get to sleep, not hungry, can't focus. I stopped piano and sketching. It'll never stop. No drink or drugs, nothing weird like that.\"","mark":"Probable moderate depressive episode; hopeless"},{"h":"Self-harm and current risk (1.25 min)","say":["Have you ever hurt yourself any other way, or tried before?","Right now, are you having thoughts about ending your life? Any plan?","If you went home, what tablets or sharp things could you get to?"],"hear":"\"I scratch my arm with a pen on bad days. Never tried before. No plan. Sometimes I still wish I wasn't here. There's always tablets in the kitchen.\"","mark":"Ongoing self-harm; passive thoughts; access at home"},{"h":"Protective factors (1 min)","say":["What's kept you going through this?","What would you like to happen next?","Would you accept help from the CAMHS team?"],"hear":"\"Mum and Dad. I want to do my GCSEs. Yeah, I'd try.\"","mark":"Protective factors named"},{"h":"Summary and plan (1 min)","say":["You took the tablets wanting it to end, after months of feeling low because of the bullying, and now you want help. Have I got that right?","Tonight you'll stay on the children's ward for treatment; tomorrow CAMHS will see you and your parents.","I'd like to tell your parents about the plan and ask them to lock away medicines. Is there anything you don't want me to share?"],"hear":"\"Do I have to stay here? ... Okay. Just don't tell them about my arm yet.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Phase","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Before","Unplanned, after hurtful posts; no alcohol","Impulsive act with a clear, ongoing trigger"],["During","16 tablets, alone, wanted to die, thought it would be fatal","High suicidal intent"],["After","Went to bed hoping not to wake; told parents when sick; regrets for them","Some ambivalence; regret is not full safety"],["Mood and self-harm","Three months of depressive symptoms, hopeless; arm scratching","Probable moderate depression; ongoing self-harm"],["Now and protective","Fleeting thoughts, no plan; tablets at home; parents, GCSEs","Risk raised; supervised overnight stay and CAMHS review"]]},"key":"Good is finding intent. Excellent is separating the high intent of the act from her risk now, asking about the self-harm nobody else found, and naming access to tablets at home before she leaves.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4,5,6],[6,7],[8]]},"c-u-24":{"name":"The four abilities, then the cause: capacity to refuse surgery in ten minutes","why":"Gives him every chance to decide, shows exactly which ability fails and why, and leads to a lawful decision today.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use or weigh","Communicate","The causal link to his learning disability"],"steps":[{"h":"Open, plain words (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Callum?","I know you want to get home. I'm here to talk about the hip operation and what you think about it. It'll take about ten minutes.","That's okay to say. I'll use simple words, and stop me if anything is confusing."],"hear":"\"Yes. Can I go home now? ... I don't want it.\"","mark":"Purpose explained; support offered"},{"h":"His understanding (1 min)","say":["What happened yesterday?","What have the bone doctors told you about your hip?","What would the operation do?"],"hear":"\"I fell off my bike. They said it's broken and they want to cut my leg. Something about the blood.\"","mark":"Partial understanding"},{"h":"Fill the gaps with a picture (1.5 min)","say":["This is your hip. The top of the thigh bone has snapped here. Blood is like food for the bone; the break can cut it off.","The operation joins the bone with metal while you're asleep. It can cause pain, bleeding or infection.","Without it, the bone may not join, and the top may die. Can you tell me back what I've said?"],"hear":"\"The bone's broke, the blood feeds it, the operation joins it, and if I don't, it might die.\"","mark":"Understands with support"},{"h":"Weigh: his reasons, faith and mum (1.5 min)","say":["What makes you say no?","What are the good things and the bad things about having it, and about not having it?","Who's looking after your mum? If she had help, would that change things?"],"hear":"\"God will fix it. It already hurts less. Mum's on her own; I need to get back. Nothing bad can happen.\"","mark":"Cannot weigh: uses analgesia as proof; denies any risk"},{"h":"Retain: come back to it (1 min)","say":["Callum, earlier I showed you this picture. Can you tell me again what happens if the bone isn't joined?","Take your time; here's the picture."],"hear":"\"Erm... I don't know. It'll be fine.\"","mark":"Not retained after a few minutes, even with the picture"},{"h":"Communicate and pressure (0.5 min)","say":["So what's your decision about the operation?","Has anyone told you what you should choose?"],"hear":"\"No. Nobody told me. I just don't want it.\"","mark":"Clear, consistent; no outside pressure"},{"h":"The causal link (0.75 min)","say":["Do you sometimes find new things hard to hold on to?","Who usually helps you with big decisions?"],"hear":"\"Yeah, Mum helps me with letters and the doctor's.\"","mark":"Impairment causes the failure to retain and weigh"},{"h":"Explain the outcome (1.5 min)","say":["Callum, I can see how much your faith and your mum matter to you, so I'll be honest about what I've found.","You understood the picture really well. But a few minutes later it had gone, and you said nothing bad can happen. So this decision is too hard to make on your own today.","The bone doctors will now decide what's best for you, and they must listen to you, your mum and what your faith means to you."],"hear":"\"So can I go home now, then?\"","mark":"Outcome explained plainly"},{"h":"Close (1.25 min)","say":["Not yet; your hip needs looking after first. A social worker is getting help for your mum today.","A nurse who helps people with learning disabilities will come, and the chaplain can pray with you.","What would you like the bone doctors to know about you?"],"hear":"\"Tell them I'm scared. And I want to pray first.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Ability","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","Told back the break, blood supply, operation and risk with a picture","Met with support"],["Retain","Five minutes later: 'I don't know, it'll be fine'","Not met, despite the same picture"],["Use or weigh","'Nothing bad can happen'; less pain means healing","Not met: cannot hold the risks in mind"],["Communicate","Clear, repeated 'no'; no one pressuring him","Met"],["Causal link","New information slips; mum helps with decisions","Learning disability causes the failures: lacks capacity for this decision"]]},"key":"Good is testing all four abilities. Excellent is showing that the failure is in holding and using the risks, not in his faith, and removing the worry about his mother before you decide.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4,5,6],[4,7],[8,9]]},"c-u-25":{"name":"Limits first, then ages, acts, images and access: risk to children in ten minutes","why":"Gets the full disclosure safely, sizes the risk to named and unnamed children and to him, and leads to honest, lawful action tonight.","frame":["Confidentiality and its limits","Nature of the interest","Escalation: recent events and images","Access to children","Suicide risk and protective factors"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and confidentiality (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Stuart Pembridge?","We keep what you say confidential, with one exception: if someone, especially a child, might be at risk, I'd have to share it.","You've come in for a reason, and that took courage. We've got about ten minutes. What's been happening?"],"hear":"\"Yes. Is this private? ... Right. Okay.\"","mark":"Limits stated before disclosure"},{"h":"His concern and why now (1 min)","say":["What's brought you in tonight?","Why tonight, rather than another night?"],"hear":"\"I've got feelings about kids. Young girls. And last week something happened and I'm scared I can't control it.\"","mark":"Sexual urges towards children disclosed"},{"h":"Nature of the interest (1 min)","say":["What ages are the girls you're drawn to?","How long have you been aware of this? Have you been attracted to adults too?","What goes through your mind when these feelings come?"],"hear":"\"Under twelve. As long as I can remember. I've had girlfriends, but it never lasted. I know it's wrong.\"","mark":"Lifelong interest in pre-pubertal girls; knows it is wrong"},{"h":"Recent events (1.25 min)","say":["Tell me what happened last week.","Did you touch her in any way? Do you think she noticed anything?","What's been happening in your mind since?"],"hear":"\"I babysat my mate's girl, she's eight. Four hours. I got aroused on the sofa. I didn't touch her. I keep thinking about her now.\"","mark":"Escalation: arousal alone with a child; fantasies since"},{"h":"Images and offending (1 min)","say":["Some people with these feelings look at images of children online. Has that happened?","Do you keep any? Where?","Any police involvement in the past, or have you touched a child before?"],"hear":"\"Yes. For a few years. They're on a memory stick at home. Never been arrested. Never touched anyone.\"","mark":"Indecent images held; no known contact offence"},{"h":"Mental state (1.25 min)","say":["How have you been in yourself? Sleep, mood?","Any thoughts of self-harm or of ending your life?","Any alcohol or drugs? Anything unusual like hearing voices?"],"hear":"\"Anxious. Can't sleep for worrying I'll be arrested. No thoughts like that. I don't drink or do drugs.\"","mark":"Anxious; no suicidal thoughts now; no substances or psychosis"},{"h":"Access and protective factors (1 min)","say":["At work, are you ever alone with children?","Are you due to babysit again, or see her?","What's stopped you acting on these feelings so far?"],"hear":"\"I'm the caretaker; I'm around kids all day, sometimes on my own in the hall. My mate asked me again for Saturday. I know it's wrong, and I came here.\"","mark":"Access through work and babysitting; protective: help-seeking, insight"},{"h":"Safeguarding steps (1.5 min)","say":["Stuart, you came here to stop harm happening, and I'll be honest with you about what has to happen next.","Because of the images and your contact with children, I must inform the police and children's services tonight, and your school's safeguarding route tomorrow.","Please don't babysit on Saturday or be alone with any child. Don't destroy the stick."],"hear":"\"What happens now? Are you going to call the police? ... I knew it. I suppose I did.\"","mark":"Duty to share explained; no contact with children agreed"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min)","say":["After hearing this, how are you feeling? Any thoughts about ending your life now?","Help for you: a specialist service, and Stop It Now on 0808 1000 900. If you feel unsafe, call 999 or Samaritans on 116 123.","The crisis team will call you tomorrow. What questions do you have?"],"hear":"\"Scared. But not like that. I'll ring them.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Nature of interest","Girls under 12, lifelong; adult relationships short-lived","Probable paedophilic disorder"],["Recent events","Aroused alone with an 8-year-old; fantasies since; asked to babysit again","Escalating risk to a named child"],["Images","Indecent images on a memory stick for years","Serious crime; police must be told"],["Access","Primary school caretaker; babysitting","Wider risk; LADO and children's services"],["Self and protective","Anxious, no suicidal thoughts; came for help; knows it is wrong","Suicide risk to recheck after disclosure; engagement is the main protective factor"]]},"key":"Good is getting the disclosure. Excellent is stating the limits before he speaks, asking plainly about touching, images and access at work, and checking suicide risk again after telling him about the police.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,4],[4,5,7],[6,7],[8,9]]},"c-u-26":{"name":"Erotomania in A&E: belief, pursuit, force","why":"Settles him, proves the belief is delusional, and measures how close he is to acting on it.","frame":["Safe start","The belief","Fixity","Pursuit","Force and risk","Rule-outs"],"steps":[{"h":"Safe start (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dominic Arrieta?","I'm sorry about the long wait. I can see you're frustrated. Can we sit down and talk?","I'd like about ten minutes with you. Tell me about Dr Fenwick, and why it matters so much tonight."],"hear":"\"I just want to see her. She'll come if she knows I'm here.\"","mark":"Agitation acknowledged; no promise made"},{"h":"The relationship (1.5 min)","say":["How did you first meet her?","What happened that showed you how she felt?","How often do you think about her?"],"hear":"\"She treated my asthma. She blinked twice and raised her eyebrows. That was the sign.\"","mark":"Erotomanic belief identified"},{"h":"How fixed is it (1 min)","say":["On a scale of nought to a hundred, how certain are you?","Have you spoken to her since that day?","What did it mean when she didn't accept your friend request?"],"hear":"\"A hundred. She can't show it at work, so she's keeping it quiet.\"","mark":"Fixed despite contrary evidence"},{"h":"Pursuit (1.5 min)","say":["How many times have you come back to A&E hoping to see her?","Have you looked her up online, found where she lives, written to her or waited for her?","What were you planning to do tonight if you saw her?"],"hear":"\"I've got two tickets to Venice for tomorrow morning. I'll take her home to pack.\"","mark":"Active pursuit and imminent plan identified"},{"h":"Mental state screen (1 min)","say":["How's your mood been? Any times you've felt on top of the world, or needed little sleep?","Any voices, or feeling people are watching or plotting against you?","What about alcohol, and any drugs?"],"hear":"\"Mood's good, apart from tonight. No voices. I hardly drink.\"","mark":"Mania, schizophrenia and substances screened"},{"h":"Force and risk (1.5 min)","say":["If staff or security stop you seeing her tonight, what would you do?","Have you ever hurt anyone, or carried anything you could use as a weapon?","If this falls through, could you see yourself doing anything to hurt yourself?"],"hear":"\"I'd go through them. I do MMA. I'd never hurt myself.\"","mark":"Threat of force to staff identified"},{"h":"Background (1 min)","say":["Have you felt this way about anyone before? How did it end?","Any trouble with the police, or any mental health help in the past?"],"hear":"\"No, never. This is the real thing. Never been in trouble.\""},{"h":"Close and act (1.5 min)","say":["Thank you for telling me all this, Dominic. She won't be coming down, and I'd like you to stay while I speak with my senior.","[To the nurse in charge] Security outside, no one alone with him, police if he leaves. I'm calling the consultant for a Mental Health Act assessment and asking the clinical lead to warn Dr Fenwick."],"hear":"\"[Nurse] Understood. Security's already on the corridor.\"","mark":"Safety plan handed over"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The belief","A blink and raised eyebrows proved her love","Delusional perception of tiny signals: erotomania"],["Fixity","Ignored friend request means secrecy","Contrary evidence absorbed; over three months"],["Pursuit","Repeat A&E visits, online search, flights for two","Escalating and invested; imminent plan"],["Force","Would use force if stopped; MMA trained","High, immediate risk to staff and to her"],["Rule-outs","Normal mood, no voices, no drugs","Delusional disorder more likely than mania or schizophrenia"]]},"key":"A good doctor diagnoses erotomania. An excellent one asks what he plans next and what he would do if stopped, then acts before leaving the room: security, consultant, and a recorded warning to her.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5,6],[8]]},"c-u-27":{"name":"Fire-setting: before, during, after, and the meaning","why":"Finds the reason for the fire, shows whether it is a pattern, and measures the chance of the next one.","frame":["Trigger and context","Before: tension","During and after: relief and arousal","Past fires","Rule-outs","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Hands first, then confidentiality (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Rhiannon?","The burns team say they're minor and should heal well. I'd like about ten minutes to understand how today happened.","This is private between us; I'd only share if someone faced real danger, and I'd tell you first."],"hear":"\"Okay. I just don't want to get arrested.\"","mark":"Confidentiality and its limit explained"},{"h":"Today's events (1.5 min)","say":["Take me through today, from this afternoon.","What happened after you saw him?","What did you drink, and is that usual for you?"],"hear":"\"I saw my ex kissing someone. I went home, drank half a bottle of wine, and I just had this urge.\"","mark":"Trigger and alcohol identified"},{"h":"The fire: before, during, after (1.5 min)","say":["How did you feel just before you lit it?","And when it caught?","What did you do next? How did you feel watching the fire engines arrive?"],"hear":"\"Tense, then this huge relief. Watching the engines I felt excited, then scared.\"","mark":"Tension-relief and arousal identified"},{"h":"Past fires (1 min)","say":["Has a fire happened before, even a small one?","When did fire first interest you?","Do you carry a lighter or matches?"],"hear":"\"A few small ones, always when I'm stressed. I've loved fire since I was little. I keep a lighter, though I don't smoke.\"","mark":"Recurring pattern identified"},{"h":"Rule out other causes (1.5 min)","say":["Was the fire meant to hurt anyone, get back at anyone, or hurt yourself?","Any voices, or very high or very low moods lately?","Any drugs? Any trouble with reading, learning or school?"],"hear":"\"No. I'd never want to hurt anyone. No voices. Just stressed.\"","mark":"Revenge, self-harm, psychosis and mood excluded"},{"h":"Stress and support (1 min)","say":["What's been piling up lately?","Who do you have around you?"],"hear":"\"Coursework, and my dad's got cancer. My friends don't know.\""},{"h":"Risk (1 min)","say":["Who else lives in the house? What could have happened to them?","If the urge came back tonight, what would you do?","Any urge to hurt yourself, or thoughts of suicide?"],"hear":"\"Four of us. I hadn't let myself think about it. No, I wouldn't hurt myself.\"","mark":"Risk to others and self assessed"},{"h":"Summary and plan (1.5 min)","say":["What you describe, tension that eases only when you set a fire, sounds like pyromania. It's treatable with a specific kind of talking therapy.","For now: could you leave the lighter with me, and avoid alcohol until we've got help in place?","I'll write to your GP for a psychology referral. If the urge comes back, call 111 and choose option 2."],"hear":"\"Okay. I can do that.\"","mark":"Safety plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Stage","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Trigger","Saw her ex with someone; half a bottle of wine","Stressor plus disinhibition, not the cause"],["Before","Tension building for weeks","Classic pre-fire arousal"],["During and after","Relief, then excitement at the engines","Pattern typical of pyromania"],["Past","Small fires since childhood; carries a lighter","Recurrent and ongoing: higher recidivism"],["Meaning","No revenge, no self-harm, no gain","Tension relief: treat with targeted CBT"]]},"key":"A good doctor asks why she lit the fire. An excellent one maps her feelings before, during and after, asks about the fire engines and the lighter in her pocket, and leaves with that lighter handed over.","objmap":[[1],[3],[4],[2,5],[7,8]]},"c-u-28":{"name":"Missed methadone: verify, history, protect","why":"Gets the full substance picture, treats withdrawal safely, and stops one missed dose becoming a relapse or an overdose.","frame":["Methadone details","Withdrawal now","Opioid history","Other substances","Complications and risk","Verified plan"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and acknowledge (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Lee Gartside?","I can see you're feeling rough. Let's sort out what's happened and what we can safely do.","That sounds awful. Can I take about ten minutes of questions to work out the best plan?"],"hear":"\"Go on. But I need it today.\""},{"h":"Today's problem (1 min)","say":["When did you last take your methadone?","What symptoms have you got right now?","[Score COWS: pulse, sweating, restlessness, pupils, aches, runny nose, gut, tremor, yawning, anxiety, goosebumps]"],"hear":"\"Sunday lunchtime. Runny nose, sweats, stomach cramps, feel sick.\"","mark":"Withdrawal confirmed"},{"h":"Methadone details (1.5 min)","say":["What dose are you on, and how long have you been on it?","Who prescribes it, which pharmacy, and do they watch you take it?","Any missed doses before this? Any heroin or other opioids on top?"],"hear":"\"60 mil, two years. Drug service. I pick it up daily and take it home. Haven't touched heroin.\"","mark":"Methadone details recorded"},{"h":"Opioid history (1.5 min)","say":["How did heroin start for you, and how did you take it at its worst?","How much were you spending, and how did you pay for it?","Have you ever shared needles or other kit?"],"hear":"\"Smoked it at 22, then injected daily. £150 a day. Shared once or twice.\"","mark":"Injecting history identified"},{"h":"Other substances (1.5 min)","say":["These are routine questions I ask everyone. How much do you drink in a day, and what?","Cigarettes? Cannabis? Any tablets like diazepam or pregabalin, from a doctor or not?","Crack, cocaine, speed, ketamine, spice or anything else?"],"hear":"\"Two cans of lager a night. Forty fags. Used to smoke weed, not now. Nothing else.\"","mark":"All substance groups screened"},{"h":"Complications (1 min)","say":["Have you ever overdosed, by accident or on purpose?","When were you last tested for hepatitis and HIV?","Any abscesses, clots or chest problems? How's your mood?"],"hear":"\"Never overdosed. Got tested when I started the script. Mood's okay.\""},{"h":"Risk (1 min)","say":["If you don't get methadone today, what do you think you'll do?","Do you drive?","Any thoughts of hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"I'll end up scoring, I know it. Don't drive. No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Relapse and overdose risk identified"},{"h":"Verified plan and close (1.5 min)","say":["I can't give methadone on anyone's word alone, because the wrong dose can stop someone breathing. I'll ring your pharmacy's out-of-hours line and the drug service now.","Meanwhile I'll get you something for the cramps, sickness and diarrhoea. If we can't confirm, we can give a small dose and recheck you in an hour.","Before you go you'll get a naloxone kit, and we'll tell your pharmacy what you had so tomorrow's dose is right. What will you do if the urge to use gets strong?"],"hear":"\"Fair enough. I'll ring my keyworker tomorrow and I won't use alone.\"","mark":"Safe plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Methadone","60 mg daily, unsupervised, 2 years; last dose 26 h ago","Verify before dosing; one missed day"],["Withdrawal","Runny nose, sweats, cramps, nausea; COWS 14","Moderate withdrawal: treat now"],["Opioid history","Injected daily, shared needles, £150/day","BBV testing and hep B status needed"],["Other substances","2 cans/night, 40 cigarettes, past cannabis","Alcohol adds overdose risk; smoking support"],["Risk","Will buy heroin if not helped; no past overdoses","Relapse with lower tolerance: naloxone and a plan"]]},"key":"A good doctor takes a thorough drug history. An excellent one verifies the dose before giving a milligram, tells the pharmacy what was given, and sends him home with naloxone and a plan for tomorrow.","objmap":[[2,3],[5],[4,6],[2,7],[8]]},"c-u-29":{"name":"Is IPT right for him? Finding the focus area","why":"Links his depression to what changed in his life, so the therapy targets the real cause.","frame":["Grief","Role dispute","Role transition","Interpersonal deficits"],"steps":[{"h":"Open (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Professor Iyer?","Thank you, Rohan. I understand the CBT didn't help as much as you'd hoped, and your GP mentioned another talking therapy.","I'll explain it, but first, in the next ten minutes or so, could I hear how you've been and what's been happening in your life?"],"hear":"\"Of course. I'd never heard of IPT.\""},{"h":"Mood now (1 min)","say":["How's your mood been over the last two weeks?","Sleep, appetite, energy, concentration, enjoyment?","[Repeat PHQ-9 or read today's score]"],"hear":"\"Flat, mostly. Poor sleep. Nothing much interests me. The form came to 14.\"","mark":"Moderate depression confirmed"},{"h":"Safety (0.5 min)","say":["Have you felt life isn't worth carrying on with, or thought of hurting yourself?","How much do you drink?"],"hear":"\"No, never. A glass of wine at weekends.\"","mark":"Safe for outpatient therapy"},{"h":"Life and relationships (1.5 min)","say":["When did this start, and what was happening then?","What was CBT like for you?","How are things at home?"],"hear":"\"After I retired. I miss the department. My wife and I keep arguing about how I fill my days. CBT felt abstract.\"","mark":"Role transition identified; role dispute identified"},{"h":"What IPT is (1 min)","say":["IPT looks at how mood and relationships affect each other, especially around big changes.","It stays in the here and now, and it's time-limited.","CBT wasn't a wrong choice: both are recommended, and CBT is usually tried first. It just didn't fit what's driving this for you."],"hear":"\"So why wasn't I offered it first?\""},{"h":"The four areas: which fit (1.5 min)","say":["Have you lost anyone close in the last few years?","Before retiring, did you have people you felt close to? Do you find it easy to make friends?","So: retiring is a big role change, and the arguments at home are a dispute about roles. Does that fit?"],"hear":"\"No recent deaths. I had lots of friends at work. Yes, that fits exactly.\"","mark":"Grief and interpersonal deficits excluded"},{"h":"Suitability (1 min)","say":["You've linked your mood to retiring yourself, which is exactly what IPT builds on.","Could you come weekly for about four months, and do some work between sessions?","What would you most like to be different by the end?"],"hear":"\"Yes, I can commit to that. I'd like to feel useful again, and stop the rows.\"","mark":"Suitable and motivated"},{"h":"Practicalities (1 min)","say":["It's 12 to 16 weekly sessions of about 50 minutes with a trained IPT therapist.","First we map your relationships and agree the focus, then work on it, then plan for setbacks.","The wait is about eight weeks; your GP will check in monthly. You could bring your wife to one session if you'd like."],"hear":"\"That sounds manageable.\""},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["Shall we go ahead with IPT, focusing on retirement and how things are at home?","How would you sum up IPT, so I know I've explained it clearly?","If you ever have thoughts of hurting yourself, call 111 and choose option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123."],"hear":"\"Weekly sessions on how retiring changed things, and the rows. I'll take the leaflet.\"","mark":"Shared decision and safety net"}],"table":{"cols":["IPT area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Grief","No recent bereavement","Not a focus"],["Role transition","Mood fell after retiring; misses colleagues and students","Main focus"],["Role dispute","Arguments with his wife about how he spends his days","Second strand: renegotiate roles"],["Interpersonal deficits","Lifelong good relationships; fewer since retiring","Part of the transition, not a separate focus"]]},"key":"Finding two areas does not mean treating both equally. Agree one main focus, here role transition, and let the dispute with his wife be worked on through it.","objmap":[[2,3],[4,6],[5,8],[5],[7,9]]},"c-u-30":{"name":"Anorexia on the medical ward: intake, compensation, body, risk","why":"Pins down the eating disorder and its medical danger in one short conversation, and turns it into a plan the medics can act on.","frame":["Intake and rules","Compensatory behaviours","Body image and fear of gain","Physical risk","Mood and suicide risk","Insight and capacity"],"steps":[{"h":"Open (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors who visit the wards. Are you Tamsin Bello?","Thank you for seeing me, Tamsin. The medical team asked me to come because they're worried about your weight and your tummy pain.","I'd just like about ten minutes to understand how things have been with food and how you're feeling. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"I suppose. I'm fine, really.\""},{"h":"Eating now (1.5 min)","say":["Could you take me through yesterday, from when you woke up?","Do you count calories, or have rules about what you can eat?","How much do you drink, and what?"],"hear":"\"An apple and black coffee, mostly. I keep it under 500. Lots of water.\"","mark":"Severe restriction identified"},{"h":"Weight and compensations (1.5 min)","say":["What was your highest weight, and how fast has it come down?","Do you ever make yourself sick, or use laxatives or diet pills? How many?","How much exercise do you do in a day?"],"hear":"\"I've lost about 12 kilos this year. I walk three hours a day. And I take laxatives every night.\"","mark":"Purging and over-exercise identified"},{"h":"Beliefs and body image (1 min)","say":["How do you feel about your body now?","What would happen if you put on weight?","How often do you weigh or check yourself?"],"hear":"\"I feel huge. Gaining would be the worst thing. I weigh myself every morning.\"","mark":"Fear of weight gain identified"},{"h":"Physical symptoms (1 min)","say":["Have you felt dizzy, fainted, or had a racing heart or chest pain?","Do you struggle to get up from a chair or climb stairs?","When was your last period?"],"hear":"\"Dizzy when I stand up. Stairs are hard. No period for a year.\"","mark":"Physical red flags identified"},{"h":"Mood and background (1 min)","say":["How's your mood been?","When did the eating changes start? Has anyone helped before?","Do you drink alcohol or use drugs?"],"hear":"\"Low. It started at university. I've never told anyone. No drink or drugs.\""},{"h":"Suicide and self-harm (1 min)","say":["Have you had thoughts of wishing you weren't here?","Have you made any plans, or ever hurt yourself?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I wish I wouldn't wake up. No plans. I've never hurt myself.\"","mark":"Passive death wish identified"},{"h":"Insight and capacity (1 min)","say":["How do you make sense of what's happening with food?","Your potassium was fixed by a drip and will drop again. What do you understand about the risk to your heart?","Would you be willing to stay while we make eating safe?"],"hear":"\"I just want to go home. But... okay, I'll stay for now.\"","mark":"Agrees to stay"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min)","say":["I think you have an eating disorder called anorexia, and your body is under real strain.","A dietitian will make a plan with you, you'll have daily blood tests, and the eating disorder team will see you.","Could you tell me what will happen next? And would you like your family to know?"],"hear":"\"A meal plan, blood tests, and the eating disorder people. You can tell my mum.\"","mark":"Plan understood"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Intake","An apple and black coffee a day","Severe restriction"],["Compensation","Nightly laxatives; three hours' walking","Binge-purge pattern; explains hypokalaemia"],["Body image","Feels huge at BMI 14; daily weighing","Core anorexic cognition"],["Physical","Dizzy on standing, weak on stairs, no periods","High medical risk; refeeding risk"],["Mood and risk","Low mood; wishes she wouldn't wake up","Passive suicidal ideation: daily review"]]},"key":"A good doctor diagnoses anorexia. An excellent one finds the laxatives that explain the potassium, asks the death-wish question, and leaves the medics a refeeding plan that avoids both refeeding syndrome and underfeeding.","objmap":[[2,3],[3,4],[5],[6,7],[8,9]]},"c-u-31":{"name":"Briefing a nurse on refeeding: SBAR, the why, the numbers, the call-now list","why":"Gives the nurse a safe, lawful plan he understands and can act on alone, in ten minutes.","frame":["Situation and background","Legal frame","Refeeding mechanism","Feed, vitamins and tube checks","Monitoring and escalation","Behaviour and safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the liaison psychiatry team. Are you Callum, Imogen's nurse today?","I've just seen her. Have you got about ten minutes to go through her plan?","What do you know so far, and have you nursed NG feeds before?"],"hear":"\"Sure. I've done NG feeds, but never for anorexia.\""},{"h":"Situation and background (1 min)","say":["She's 22 with severe anorexia. Her BMI is 13 despite months on the eating disorder unit.","She's here because last time she was fed, she developed refeeding syndrome and needed intensive care.","So she's in the highest risk group, and the first week is the danger period."],"hear":"\"I didn't know she'd been in ITU.\"","mark":"Very high refeeding risk shared"},{"h":"Legal frame (1 min)","say":["She's detained under the Mental Health Act and lacks capacity to consent to this treatment.","Feeding counts as treatment for her anorexia under Section 63, so we can feed her without consent.","But we explain every step and ask for her help each time, and any holding is a last resort, recorded."],"hear":"\"OK. So it's legal, but we still try to do it with her.\"","mark":"Legal authority stated"},{"h":"What refeeding syndrome is (1 min)","say":["When you starve, your body runs low on phosphate, potassium and magnesium.","When food comes back, insulin rises and drags those minerals out of the bloodstream, and the levels plummet within days.","That can stop the heart, cause fits, weakness and confusion. That's why the start is so small."],"hear":"\"That makes sense. I wondered why the rate was so low.\"","mark":"Mechanism explained"},{"h":"Feeding plan (1 min)","say":["The dietitian's starting rate stays as written. It only goes up on her advice, over about a week.","Thiamine, vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin go in before the first feed at noon, and then for ten days.","Check tube position before every feed: aspirate pH 5.5 or below. No aspirate, or higher, don't feed and call."],"hear":"\"Thiamine's due at half eleven. I'll make sure it goes first.\"","mark":"Vitamins and tube checks agreed"},{"h":"Monitoring (1.5 min)","say":["Bloods daily for the first week: phosphate, potassium, magnesium, glucose and kidney function.","An ECG today, and again if her minerals fall. NEWS2 at least every four hours, capillary glucose, and a strict fluid chart.","Weigh her as the plan says, in a gown, same scales."],"hear":"\"Daily bloods, ECG, four-hourly obs, fluid chart. Got it.\"","mark":"Monitoring plan given"},{"h":"Warning signs and escalation (1 min)","say":["Call the medical doctor straight away for palpitations, chest pain, new confusion, a fit, sudden swelling or breathlessness.","Same for a phosphate under 0.5, or any big drop in potassium or magnesium.","Then let me know. Out of hours, it's the on-call medical registrar and the duty psychiatrist."],"hear":"\"So ankles swelling up on day three isn't just 'a bit of fluid'?\"","mark":"Call-now triggers named"},{"h":"Behaviour and safety (1.5 min)","say":["She may pull the tube, turn the pump down, hide feed or exercise. That's the illness, not naughtiness.","A mental health nurse stays with her one-to-one, especially during feeds and for an hour after.","If the tube comes out: stop the feed, keep her safe, call the doctor, and don't re-pass it until we agree a plan. If she leaves the ward, she's absent without leave: tell the nurse in charge and call the on-call psychiatrist."],"hear":"\"I'm not keen on holding her down.\"","mark":"Tube-out and AWOL plan agreed"},{"h":"Teach-back and close (1 min)","say":["That's a fair worry. Calm talking first; holding only with trained staff, as a last resort.","Can you tell me back the things that mean you call straight away?","I'll write this in the notes and come back at four. Here's my bleep."],"hear":"\"Palpitations, chest pain, confusion, a fit, swelling or breathlessness, and phosphate under 0.5.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you said","What it means for Callum"],"rows":[["Risk","BMI 13, past refeeding syndrome in ICU","Highest risk: the first week is critical"],["Legal","Detained, lacks capacity, Section 63","Feed lawfully; explain, record any holding"],["Feed and vitamins","Low start, thiamine first, pH 5.5 or below","Never speed up; check before every feed"],["Monitoring","Daily bloods, ECG, NEWS2, fluids, glucose","Trends spot trouble before symptoms"],["Escalation","Named symptoms, phosphate under 0.5","Call the medical doctor now, then psychiatry"],["Behaviour","Tube pulling, tampering, leaving","One-to-one; stop, call, no re-pass without plan"]]},"key":"A good briefing lists the rules. An excellent one explains why the feed is small, gives numbers he can act on alone, and names exactly what to do when the tube comes out or she walks off.","objmap":[[4,5],[5,6],[7],[3,8],[9]]},"c-u-32":{"name":"Methadone in pregnancy: check, inform, offer a real choice, plan","why":"Keeps her stable and engaged by answering her fears honestly and agreeing a safe, shared plan.","frame":["Drug history and stability","Pregnancy and blood-borne viruses","Mood and risk","Options for methadone","Baby after birth"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with the perinatal team. Are you Jodie?","Congratulations. I hear you've stopped smoking and drinking already: that's brilliant.","I know you're worried about the methadone. Can we spend about ten minutes on where you're at, then your options?"],"hear":"\"Thanks. I just want to do it right.\""},{"h":"Drug history (1 min)","say":["You're on 30 mg a day from the pharmacy. Any heroin or anything else on top, even once?","When did you last inject? Do you get cravings, and what do you do?","What were things like before the methadone?"],"hear":"\"Nothing for four years. Cravings sometimes, I ring my keyworker.\"","mark":"Stable, no on-top use"},{"h":"Pregnancy and health (1 min)","say":["Have you booked with a midwife yet? Are you taking folic acid?","Your notes mention hepatitis C. Was it treated, and what did they say?","Have you had recent tests for HIV and hepatitis B?"],"hear":"\"I had the tablets two years ago. They said it's gone.\"","mark":"Hepatitis C treated; booking next week"},{"h":"Mood and risk (1 min)","say":["How's your mood been since you found out? You had a low patch in your twenties.","Any thoughts of self-harm or of using again to cope?","I ask everyone this: does anyone at home ever hurt or frighten you?"],"hear":"\"Worried, not low. Dan's good to me.\"","mark":"No depression, no abuse"},{"h":"Methadone and the baby (1.5 min)","say":["Methadone isn't known to cause birth defects, and staying on a steady dose is usually safest.","Stopping suddenly can bring on withdrawal, which can harm the pregnancy, and cravings that pull people back to heroin.","After a break your tolerance drops, so a relapse carries a real overdose risk."],"hear":"\"So coming off could be worse than staying on?\"","mark":"Continuing is safest"},{"h":"Options (1 min)","say":["Option one, which I'd recommend: stay on 30 mg, and review it later in pregnancy.","Option two, if you still want to: cut down slowly, usually from about four months, with your drug service, stopping if cravings come back.","Late in pregnancy you may need a little more, because your body clears it faster. It's your decision."],"hear":"\"I'll stay on it. I don't want to risk using.\""},{"h":"After birth (1.5 min)","say":["Some babies show withdrawal: crying, poor feeding, sneezing. They'll be watched in hospital for several days, and it's very treatable.","Breastfeeding is encouraged, and treated hepatitis C isn't a reason to avoid it.","Social care will probably be part of planning before birth. Honestly, it's about support; doing well on methadone isn't a reason to remove a baby."],"hear":"\"That's what I was most scared of.\"","mark":"Neonatal withdrawal and social care explained"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["So: stay on 30 mg, see the specialist midwife, booking bloods, folic acid, and a naloxone kit and locked box at home.","Just so I can check I've explained it clearly: why are we keeping the methadone going?","I'll write to your GP, prescriber and midwife. Here's a leaflet to take home, and I'll see you in four weeks. If you use or feel low, call your keyworker, 111 option 2, or 999 in an emergency."],"hear":"\"Stopping could make me use again, and that's worse for the baby.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Stability","30 mg, 4 years, no on-top use","Strong position: keep it steady"],["Health","Hep C treated, booking next week","Confirm HCV RNA; specialist midwife"],["Mood and risk","Anxious and guilty, no abuse","Validate; recheck after birth"],["Her wish","Wants to stop all medication","Explain risks; offer slow reduction only if stable"],["After birth","Fears withdrawal and social care","Honest about observation, treatment and support"]]},"key":"A good answer says 'stay on methadone'. An excellent one first honours her wish, explains why stopping is riskier, offers a genuine slow-reduction choice, and answers the social care fear honestly.","objmap":[[2,3],[5,6],[7],[4,8],[8]]},"c-u-33":{"name":"Low after a baby: timeline, bond, differentials, risk to all","why":"Reaches a confident diagnosis of postnatal depression and a safe risk picture for mother and both children in ten minutes.","frame":["Timeline and depressive symptoms","Bond and care of the baby","Differentials: blues, psychosis, OCD, physical","Vulnerability: past and family history","Risk to self and both children"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ffion Dempsey?","Please don't apologise. Having a new baby is a lot, and lots of mums feel low.","Can we take about ten minutes so I can understand how things have been since Noah arrived?"],"hear":"\"I just don't feel how I should.\""},{"h":"Mood since birth (1.5 min)","say":["When did you first notice it, and has it been there most days since?","Can you still enjoy anything, even time with Alfie?","When Noah sleeps, can you sleep? How's your appetite, energy and concentration?"],"hear":"\"About two weeks after. I cry every day. I lie awake even when he's asleep.\"","mark":"Depressive episode, postpartum onset"},{"h":"Bonding and the baby (1 min)","say":["What's it like when you hold him?","How are you managing his feeds and care?","Some mums get frightening thoughts that pop up about the baby being hurt. Has anything like that happened?"],"hear":"\"I feel nothing. I do everything for him, but it's like he's someone else's.\"","mark":"Detachment; care maintained"},{"h":"Pregnancy and birth (1 min)","say":["How was the pregnancy and the birth?","Did you lose much blood? How's the feeding going?","How's Noah's health?"],"hear":"\"Long labour, they used a ventouse. I lost a lot of blood and I'm on iron.\""},{"h":"Exclude other causes (1 min)","say":["Have you been hearing or seeing things other people can't, or felt something strange was going on?","Any times you've felt on top of the world, racing with energy, barely needing sleep?","Do you check or clean over and over to stop something bad happening?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. I check he's breathing once or twice a night.\"","mark":"No psychosis, mania or OCD"},{"h":"Background (1 min)","say":["How were you after Alfie was born?","Has anyone in your family been unwell after having a baby?","How much do you drink? Who helps at home? And I ask everyone: does anyone ever hurt or frighten you?"],"hear":"\"I was low for a couple of months after Alfie. Mum was the same after my brother.\"","mark":"Previous postnatal low mood; family history"},{"h":"Risk (2 min)","say":["When you feel this low, do you ever think life isn't worth living, or that they'd be better off without you?","Have you made any plans, or ever harmed yourself? What keeps you going?","Have you had any thoughts of harming Noah or Alfie, or been unable to look after them?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think they'd be better off. But I'd never do anything, because of the boys.\"","mark":"Passive thoughts, no plan; no risk to children"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["What you're describing sounds like postnatal depression. It's common, it's not your fault, and it gets better with help.","I'd like to talk to my consultant today and refer you to the perinatal team, who specialise in this.","If you ever feel you can't keep yourself or the boys safe, call 111 option 2, or 999. Can we share that plan with Rhys?"],"hear":"\"Yes. I'm relieved it has a name.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Timeline","Started at two weeks, lasted a month","Not baby blues: postnatal depression"],["Symptoms","Low, tearful, anhedonia, poor sleep and appetite, guilt","Moderate depressive episode"],["Bond","Feels nothing holding Noah; cares for him well","Red flag for urgent perinatal referral"],["Differentials","No voices, highs or compulsions; blood loss","Psychosis and OCD unlikely; check FBC, TFTs"],["Risk","Passive thoughts, no plan; no harm to children","Moderate; crisis plan; discuss with consultant"]]},"key":"A good history finds the depression. An excellent one also pins the timeline against baby blues, separates unwanted thoughts from intent, asks about both children, and treats detachment from the baby as a reason to act this week.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-u-34":{"name":"Mental state and risk in suspected postpartum psychosis","why":"Captures the full mental state and the real risks in ten minutes so the right emergency plan starts today.","frame":["Appearance, behaviour and speech","Mood","Thought content: misidentification and persecution","Perception","Cognition and insight","Risk: baby, others, self"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors in the hospital. Are you Mrs Raghunathan?","The midwives asked me to see how you are after a really hard few days. I'm here to help, not to take anything away.","Could we talk for about ten minutes? Tell me what's been happening since your daughter was born."],"hear":"\"Everyone keeps saying that. Who sent you?\""},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour (1 min)","say":["You seem very on edge, and you keep looking at the vent. What's frightening you?","You're speaking quite fast. Are your thoughts racing?"],"hear":"\"I have to listen. I have to keep watch.\"","mark":"Agitated, guarded, pressured"},{"h":"Speech and mood (1 min)","say":["How would you describe your mood?","How have you slept since the birth? Are you eating?"],"hear":"\"Petrified. I haven't slept since they took her away. I can't eat.\"","mark":"Fear, no sleep, no appetite"},{"h":"Thought content (1.5 min)","say":["Tell me about your daughter. How do you feel about the baby here?","What makes you sure she isn't yours? Who do you think did this, and why?","On a scale of nought to a hundred, how certain are you?"],"hear":"\"A midwife swapped her. They think I'm a bad mother. A hundred percent.\"","mark":"Misidentification and persecutory delusions"},{"h":"Perception (1 min)","say":["Have you heard people talking when nobody's in the room?","What do they say? Are they there now?","Anything else you've heard, seen or felt that seemed strange? Anyone putting thoughts in your head?"],"hear":"\"They whisper through the walls: bad mother. Last night my real baby was crying in the vent.\"","mark":"Auditory hallucinations"},{"h":"Cognition and insight (1 min)","say":["What's today's date, and where are we?","Can you go through the months in reverse order, starting with December?","Do you think the birth might have made you unwell? Would you take treatment?"],"hear":"\"Wednesday. The maternity ward. I'm not ill. I don't need anything.\"","mark":"Oriented; insight absent"},{"h":"Risk (2 min)","say":["What are you planning to do about your real baby?","When you say 'any means', could someone get hurt? Would you hurt someone if they stopped you?","Have you had thoughts of harming the baby here with you, or yourself?"],"hear":"\"Once I can walk, I'll get her back. If someone gets hurt, so be it.\"","mark":"High risk to others; neglect risk to daughter"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["Thank you for being so honest. I can see how frightened you are, and how much you love your daughter.","I'm worried you're very unwell after the birth. I need you to stay here, with a nurse with you, and I'm calling my consultant now.","The aim is treatment today, ideally in a unit where you and your daughter can stay together."],"hear":"\"Are you going to lock me up again?\""}],"table":{"cols":["MSE domain","What you heard or saw","What it means"],"rows":[["Appearance and speech","Restless, guarded, fast whispered speech","Agitation; possible mood component"],["Mood","'Petrified', no sleep, not eating","Mixed affective picture"],["Thought content","Midwife swapped her baby; midwives plotting","Misidentification and persecutory delusions"],["Perception","Whispers through walls; baby crying in vent","Auditory hallucinations"],["Cognition and insight","Oriented, attention intact; 'I'm not ill'","Delirium less likely; no insight"],["Risk","Will get 'real' baby by any means","High risk to others, other babies, daughter"]]},"key":"A good assessment finds the delusion. An excellent one also tests cognition for delirium, asks what 'by any means' would mean, and protects other babies on the ward as well as her own.","objmap":[[2,3],[3,4,5],[6],[7],[8]]},"c-u-35":{"name":"Morbid jealousy in custody: the belief, the psychosis, the knife, everyone at risk","why":"Turns a tense custody interview into a clear risk formulation and a safe, lawful plan in ten minutes.","frame":["Last night: intent and trigger","The jealous belief and checking","Psychopathology: voices, mood, alcohol","The knife and current intent","Risk to each person","Insight"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pryce?","The officers were worried about you overnight, so I've come to hear your side. We've got about ten minutes.","Before we start, one thing. Most of what we discuss stays within the care team. But if I think someone could be seriously hurt, I may have to pass that on. Is that OK?"],"hear":"\"Fine, whatever. Why am I seeing a shrink?\""},{"h":"Last night (1 min)","say":["Tell me what happened last night.","Where were you going, and what made you go at that moment?","What did you plan to do when you got there?"],"hear":"\"Going to catch them. The voice said go now.\"","mark":"Acting on the belief; command"},{"h":"Jealousy beliefs (1.5 min)","say":["How did you first come to believe Kerry was having an affair?","What's made you sure? How sure, out of a hundred?","What have you done to check? Her phone, her clothes, following her?"],"hear":"\"New perfume, his car in our street. I check her phone, I wait in the car park. A hundred percent.\"","mark":"Fixed delusion with checking"},{"h":"Other psychopathology (1.5 min)","say":["Have you heard voices when no one's there? What do they say? Did they start before or after you suspected her?","Have you smelled anything on her others might not?","How's your mood and sleep? How much are you drinking, when was your last drink, any shakes? Anything else, like cocaine?"],"hear":"\"A man's voice, before all this. I smell his aftershave on her. Six to eight cans a night.\"","mark":"Voices first; olfactory; heavy drinking"},{"h":"Background and forensic (1 min)","say":["Have you ever hurt Kerry, or anyone, or come close?","Any police trouble before? Do you have access to other weapons?","Who's at home? Where's your daughter now?"],"hear":"\"I grabbed her wrist once, over the phone. Ellie's at my mum's.\"","mark":"Domestic abuse; child at home"},{"h":"Risk to wife and others (2 min)","say":["The police found a knife. What was it for? And if he'd laughed or refused?","Could Kerry, or anyone who stepped in, have been hurt? What will you do when you leave here?","Has it got so bad you've thought about killing yourself? Or about hurting Kerry and then yourself?"],"hear":"\"To make him admit it. Can't say what I'd have done. I still need to have it out with them.\"","mark":"High risk: weapon, ongoing intent"},{"h":"Insight (1 min)","say":["Could there be any other explanation for the perfume or the car?","Stress, drink or illness can make people hear and believe things. Could that be happening to you?"],"hear":"\"No. There's nothing else it could be.\"","mark":"No insight"},{"h":"Summary and close (1 min)","say":["Thank you for being honest. I think you're unwell, and I'm worried for your safety and other people's.","I'm not advising release home. I'm asking for an assessment for hospital today, and I'll talk to my consultant.","As I said at the start, I have to tell the police about the risk so Kerry can be kept safe. Please don't drive until you're well."],"hear":"\"So what are you going to do with me?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The belief","Eight months, perfume, a parked car, 100% sure","Delusional jealousy on trivial evidence"],["Behaviour","Checks phone and underwear, waits in car park","Stalking; escalating towards confrontation"],["Psychopathology","Voices first, command last night, smells aftershave","Psychotic illness, alcohol contributing"],["The knife","'To make him admit it'; can't rule out using it","Planned weapon use: high risk to rival"],["Others at risk","Wife present, daughter at home, past wrist grab","Wife and child at risk; safeguarding, MARAC"],["Insight and intent","No alternative view; still plans to confront","Not safe to release; MHA assessment"]]},"key":"A good assessment confirms the delusion. An excellent one asks about the knife until the honest answer comes, considers everyone who could be hurt, and tells him plainly who will be told.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4,7],[2,6],[5,6],[8]]},"c-u-36":{"name":"Ten minutes at the door: food, beliefs, voices, risk, insight","why":"Captures the evidence of relapse and the main risks without losing his trust, so the team can act today.","frame":["Appearance and behaviour","Persecutory beliefs and conviction","Reference, interference and passivity","Hallucinations in all senses","Risk and insight"],"steps":[{"h":"Open at the door and observe (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Kofi Mensah?","Thanks for letting me in, Kofi. I won't take long. Staff say you've been keeping to yourself and missing meals.","I'd like to understand how things are for you. Is that alright?"],"hear":"\"Okay.\" (He stays near the door, avoids eye contact, glances up at the vent.)","mark":"Weight loss, poor self-care, guarded, listening behaviour noted"},{"h":"Since leaving hospital (1 min)","say":["How did things go when you first moved in here?","What changed for you around three weeks ago?"],"hear":"\"It was fine till the last injection. It really hurt, and then I knew what they were doing.\"","mark":"Change dated to the painful depot"},{"h":"Food and the depot (1.5 min)","say":["Staff say you're mostly eating takeaways. What's wrong with the food here?","What do you think the injection was being used for?","Out of a hundred, how certain do you feel? Is there any other way of seeing it?"],"hear":"\"They put something in the kitchen food. The injection was to control me. A hundred per cent.\"","mark":"Persecutory delusion, conviction 100%; possible delusional perception"},{"h":"Reference, interference and passivity (1.25 min)","say":["Do you feel people here talk about you, or send you messages?","Can anyone put thoughts in your head, take them out, or know what you're thinking?","Do you ever feel something outside controls your body or actions?"],"hear":"\"I hear them through the wall, talking about me. They know what I think when they look at me.\"","mark":"Delusion of reference; possible thought broadcast"},{"h":"Voices and other senses (1.25 min)","say":["Do you ever hear a voice when no one is around? Is it inside your head or outside? What does it say?","Does it ever tell you to do things, like hurt someone or yourself?","Has food or drink tasted or smelt strange? Any strange feelings on your skin?"],"hear":"\"One man's voice. Says don't let the nurse in. Nothing about hurting. Food smells normal, I just don't trust it.\"","mark":"Second-person auditory hallucination, warning content"},{"h":"Mood, sleep and drive (1 min)","say":["How's your mood been? Any times you feel hopeless?","How are you sleeping? What do you do with your days?"],"hear":"\"Mood's alright. I can't sleep. I stay in here mostly.\"","mark":"No depressive syndrome; poor sleep; withdrawal"},{"h":"Risk and insight (1.25 min)","say":["With all this going on, has suicide crossed your mind at all?","Have you thought of hurting anyone here, to protect yourself? Are you planning to move out?","Do you think any of this could be your illness coming back?"],"hear":"\"No, I wouldn't. I'm saving up for my own place. I'm not ill, just tired.\"","mark":"No harm intent; plans to leave; insight absent"},{"h":"Summary, options and next contact (1.5 min)","say":["So you don't feel safe with the food or the injection, and you're sleeping badly. Have I got that right?","If not the injection, would you consider tablets from a sealed pack, and sealed food here?","I'll talk to Dr Baines and your care coordinator today. Can the same nurse come tomorrow at 11? Here's the crisis line."],"hear":"\"Tablets, maybe. Just her, not loads of people.\"","mark":"Option agreed; next contact dated"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Appearance and behaviour","Thin, unshaven, glances at the vent","Self-neglect; likely hallucinating during interview"],["Persecutory beliefs","Food tampered with; depot used to control him; 100% sure","Fixed persecutory delusion; possible delusional perception"],["Reference and interference","Staff talk about him; know his thoughts","Delusion of reference; possible thought broadcast"],["Hallucinations","Male voice warning him off the nurse; no taste or smell changes","Second-person auditory hallucination driving disengagement"],["Risk and insight","No harm intent; saving to move out; 'not ill'","Self-neglect and disengagement risk high; insight absent"]]},"key":"A good MSE lists symptoms. An excellent one links each belief to behaviour and risk: the food belief explains the weight loss, the voice explains the closed door, and the moving plan explains the urgency.","objmap":[[3,4],[4,5],[7],[7,8],[8]]},"c-u-37":{"name":"Third overdose: the act, intent now, pattern, then the decision","why":"Gives a defensible, shared decision about where she is safest tonight, based on the history rather than a score.","frame":["The act and its intent","Current thoughts of dying","The pattern of self-harm","Mood, alcohol and background","Supports and the decision"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and check fitness (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with A&E. Are you Leah Cartwright?","Thanks for talking to me, Leah. How are you feeling physically this morning?","I'd like to understand what led to last night, and how you're feeling now. It'll take about ten minutes. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Tired. Groggy. Okay, I suppose.\""},{"h":"This overdose (1.75 min)","say":["Take me back to yesterday. What was happening?","How many tablets, and whose were they? Anything else, like paracetamol or drink?","Did you plan it, wait until you were alone, or leave a note? How were you found?"],"hear":"\"He left. I took about twenty of Mum's diazepam with wine, when my flatmate went out. She found me.\"","mark":"Planned, isolated, alcohol involved"},{"h":"Intent and now (1.25 min)","say":["When you took them, what did you hope would happen?","How do you feel about waking up here?","Are you having thoughts of dying right now? Any plan?"],"hear":"\"I wanted it to stop. I wish I hadn't woken up. I still think about it.\"","mark":"High intent; ongoing ideation"},{"h":"The earlier overdoses (1 min)","say":["What happened the other two times?","Have you hurt yourself in other ways, like cutting? When did that start?"],"hear":"\"Both after rows. I've cut since I was fourteen. Last time was two weeks ago.\"","mark":"Escalating frequency; long-standing self-harm"},{"h":"Mood, alcohol and background (1.5 min)","say":["How's your mood been, sleep and eating?","How much are you drinking? Any drugs?","Has anything happened to you in the past that still affects you? You don't have to give details."],"hear":"\"Low. Empty. A bottle of wine most nights. Some stuff happened when I was younger.\"","mark":"Low mood, alcohol misuse, possible trauma"},{"h":"Supports and reasons to live (0.75 min)","say":["Who's around you? Who knows you're here?","What's kept you going, even a little?"],"hear":"\"My mum. My cat. Work, sort of.\"","mark":"Protective factors identified"},{"h":"Risk formulation and decision (1.75 min)","say":["Leah, I'm worried: three overdoses in six weeks, you wish you hadn't woken, and you're still thinking about it.","I think you need more help than going home alone tonight. I'd suggest a short stay on the ward, informally, with a clear plan.","What do you think?"],"hear":"\"So can I go home, or what? ... If it's short, okay.\"","mark":"Informal admission agreed"},{"h":"Close (1 min)","say":["Can I ask your mum to remove the diazepam from the house?","You'll stay here with a nurse until the bed's ready. I'll tell the A&E team and your GP.","If you change your mind about staying, tell us first. We would need to talk again, and a fuller assessment may be needed."],"hear":"\"Yeah, tell Mum. Okay.\"","mark":"Means restriction and handover named"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The act","About 20 diazepam with wine, waited till alone, found by flatmate","Planned, isolated, high-lethality method for her"],["Intent now","Wishes she hadn't woken; still thinks of dying","Current, high risk"],["Pattern","Three overdoses in six weeks; cutting since 14","Escalating; strongest predictor of further harm"],["Mood and alcohol","Low, empty, wine most nights, past trauma","Possible depressive episode, alcohol misuse, emotional instability"],["Supports","Mother, cat, work","Real but cannot supervise her tonight"]]},"key":"Good assessors judge each overdose. Excellent ones judge the trajectory: rising frequency, a planned act, regret at surviving and ongoing thoughts outweigh 'it was after a row'.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-u-38":{"name":"Calm, count, check: the ten-minute DTs assessment","why":"Separates withdrawal delirium from psychosis and other causes, and gets life-saving treatment started on a surgical ward.","frame":["Alcohol and timing","Orientation and attention","Perceptions and beliefs","Autonomic signs","Diagnosis and handover"],"steps":[{"h":"Reassure and orientate (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Desmond Clarke?","You're safe, Desmond. You're in hospital, after your fall. I'm a doctor here to help.","Can you tell me what you're seeing? Then I'll ask a few questions."],"hear":"\"Who are you? Get them off the wall!\""},{"h":"Perceptions (1.25 min)","say":["What exactly can you see on the wall? Are they moving?","Can you feel anything on your skin? Hear anything?","Is there anyone here you feel wants to hurt you?"],"hear":"\"Spiders, big ones. They're on my arms. The nurses are whispering about me.\"","mark":"Visual and tactile hallucinations; persecutory ideas"},{"h":"Orientation and attention (1.25 min)","say":["Do you know where we are? What day is it today?","Could you go through the months backwards for me, starting at December?"],"hear":"\"The depot? Monday... December, November, October... er, June?\"","mark":"Disorientated; inattentive"},{"h":"Alcohol history (1.5 min)","say":["How much do you usually drink? What do you drink?","When did you last have a drink?","Have you had the shakes, seen things, or had a fit when you've stopped before?"],"hear":"\"Whisky. A bottle every couple of days. Last one the night before I fell. Shaky in the mornings.\"","mark":"Dependence; last drink about 60 hours ago"},{"h":"Mood, thought and insight (1 min)","say":["How are you feeling in yourself right now?","Do you think something is wrong with you?"],"hear":"\"Terrified. I just want to go home.\"","mark":"Fear; insight absent"},{"h":"Physical signs (1.25 min)","say":["Can I hold out your hands for a moment? And look at your eyes, following my finger?","I'll check your pulse and the chart."],"hear":"(Coarse tremor, sweating, pulse 112, temperature 37.8; no nystagmus.)","mark":"Autonomic overactivity"},{"h":"Explain to him (1 min)","say":["Desmond, your body is reacting to suddenly going without alcohol. It's called withdrawal, and it can make people see things.","We'll give you medicine to calm it and vitamins through a drip. A nurse will stay close."],"hear":"\"Make it stop. Okay.\""},{"h":"Handover to the surgical team (1.5 min)","say":["This is delirium tremens: last drink about 60 hours ago, visual hallucinations, inattention, pulse 112, temp 37.8.","Please start CIWA-Ar with symptom-triggered lorazepam, IV Pabrinex before glucose, magnesium, phosphate and a septic screen.","I've also considered infection, a subdural, low glucose and Wernicke's: please check a capillary glucose now.","Escalate to your registrar and outreach if NEWS rises or he has a fit. I'll review tomorrow and document now."],"hear":"\"Got it. Should we repeat the CT?\"","mark":"Plan and escalation handed over"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Alcohol and timing","Whisky, bottle every two days; last drink ~60 hours ago","Dependence; classic window for DTs"],["Orientation and attention","Wrong place and day; months backwards fails","Delirium, not a primary psychosis"],["Perceptions and beliefs","Spiders on wall and arms; nurses whispering","Visual and tactile hallucinations, persecutory ideas"],["Autonomic signs","Tremor, sweating, pulse 112, temp 37.8","Withdrawal; also screen for infection"],["Bloods","Raised MCV, GGT, ALP; mild CRP rise","Chronic heavy drinking; check chest, urine, wound"]]},"key":"Good assessors spot withdrawal. Excellent ones prove delirium with an attention test, give thiamine before glucose, adjust the benzodiazepine for age and liver, and still exclude infection and subdural.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5],[6,8],[8]]},"c-u-39":{"name":"Exposure: the act, the pattern, the causes, the risk","why":"Separates illness from paraphilia, grades the risk to children and to him, and gives the police a usable opinion.","frame":["The index act","Sexual history and interests","Illness, organic and substances","Background and access to children","Risk to others and self"],"steps":[{"h":"Open and set limits (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kieran Holt?","I'm here to understand what happened today and how you're doing. It may be hard to talk about.","What you tell me may be shared if children or others are at risk. Is that clear?"],"hear":"\"Okay.\"","mark":"Confidentiality limits understood"},{"h":"Today's incident (1.75 min)","say":["Talk me through today, from the start.","Had you planned to go there? Had you had a drink?","Were you sexually aroused? Did you touch yourself? What did you do afterwards?"],"hear":"\"I'd felt the urge for weeks. I drove there. Their reaction... it did something. I'd had a few cans.\"","mark":"Planned, aroused, alcohol involved"},{"h":"Sexual history and interests (1.5 min)","say":["Has this happened before, caught or not?","Have you ever looked at sexual images of children?","Have you ever touched a child sexually, or had urges to?"],"hear":"\"Twice before. And I've looked at pictures. Young girls. Never touched anyone.\"","mark":"Repeated exhibitionism; child sexual images"},{"h":"Illness, organic and substances (1.25 min)","say":["Any times you've felt high, needed little sleep, or felt unusually confident?","Have you heard voices or felt controlled? Any head injury, fits or memory trouble?","How much do you usually drink? Any drugs or medicines?"],"hear":"\"No. Nothing like that. Four to six cans most nights.\"","mark":"No mania, psychosis or organic features; alcohol misuse"},{"h":"Background and access (1 min)","say":["Any trouble with the police before? Relationships?","Do you have contact with children, at home or through work?"],"hear":"\"No record. On my own. I deliver round schools sometimes.\"","mark":"Access to children via work"},{"h":"Risk to self (1.25 min)","say":["How are you feeling right now, after the arrest?","Has suicide crossed your mind since the arrest? Any plan?"],"hear":"\"Ashamed. I've thought about it. No plan.\"","mark":"Suicidal ideation after arrest"},{"h":"Summary and what happens next (1.25 min)","say":["Kieran, I don't think you have an illness that needs hospital. These urges are a pattern, and they can be treated.","Because children are involved, I'll make a safeguarding referral and share the risk with the police.","I'll tell the custody nurse how low you feel. Here's the Stop It Now information."],"hear":"\"What happens now? Can anyone help me stop this?\""},{"h":"Opinion to the police (1 min)","say":["No mental illness needing admission, and no need for an appropriate adult.","There's a risk of repeat behaviour and escalation; I'm making a children's safeguarding referral.","He has suicidal thoughts: please tell the custody nurse. My written opinion is in the notes."],"hear":"\"Thanks, doctor. That's what we needed.\"","mark":"Clear opinion given"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Index act","Weeks of urges, planned drive, aroused by reaction, drank first","Paraphilic exhibitionism with alcohol disinhibition"],["Pattern","Two earlier episodes; child sexual images","Established, escalating concern"],["Illness and organic","No mania, psychosis, head injury or memory problems","Illness unlikely to explain it"],["Access","Lives alone; delivers near schools","Access to children through work"],["Risk to self","Shame; suicidal thoughts since arrest","Raised risk in custody: tell staff"]]},"key":"Good assessors ask about the incident. Excellent ones ask the uncomfortable questions plainly (arousal, images, contact), name what they will share and why, and protect both the children and the man in front of them.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4,5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-u-40":{"name":"Gender history: identity over time, differentials, safety, pathway","why":"Gives the GP a clear, respectful opinion that supports referral and catches anything else needing help.","frame":["Identity from childhood to now","Social transition","Goals, hormones and fertility","Differentials","Mood and safety"],"steps":[{"h":"Name, pronouns and purpose (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. What name and pronouns would you like me to use?","Thank you, Sophie. Your GP mentioned you'd like to start oestrogen.","I'd like to understand your experience and what you're hoping for. Is that okay? It'll take about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Sophie, and she/her please. Yes, I've waited long enough. Sure.\""},{"h":"Identity from childhood (1.75 min)","say":["When did you first feel your gender didn't match your body?","How did you like to play and dress as a child? What was puberty like?","How do you feel about your body now?"],"hear":"\"Since about six. I loved my sister's clothes. Puberty was horrible: my voice, the hair. I still hate my facial hair.\"","mark":"Onset in childhood; persistent; body distress"},{"h":"Living as herself (1.25 min)","say":["How do you live day to day now? Who knows?","How has work been?"],"hear":"\"Full time as Sophie for two years. Work's fine. My parents won't use my name.\"","mark":"Social transition for two years"},{"h":"Goals, hormones and fertility (1.25 min)","say":["What do you hope oestrogen will do for you? Any thoughts about surgery?","Have you taken or bought any hormones yourself?","Have you thought about having children one day?"],"hear":"\"Breasts, softer skin. Maybe surgery later. I've looked online but not bought any. I hadn't thought about kids.\"","mark":"Clear goals; no self-sourced hormones yet"},{"h":"Differentials (1.5 min)","say":["I ask everyone this: has wearing women's clothes ever been sexually exciting for you?","Do you ever feel something is controlling your body, or that you're being changed by someone else?","Is there one part of your appearance you check or worry about for hours?"],"hear":"\"No, never sexual. Nothing like that. It's not one part, it's being seen as a man.\"","mark":"Fetishistic transvestism, psychosis and BDD unlikely"},{"h":"Mood and safety (1.25 min)","say":["How's your mood been with the wait and your family?","Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or suicide?","Any drink, drugs, or harassment when you're out?"],"hear":"\"Low, honestly. No plans to hurt myself. Some comments on the bus.\"","mark":"Mild low mood; no suicidal intent"},{"h":"Summary, pathway and close (1.75 min)","say":["Sophie, this fits a long-standing gender incongruence, and I don't see anything else explaining it.","I can't prescribe it myself today, but I'll write to your GP to refer you to the Gender Dysphoria Clinic, who start hormones.","Meanwhile: Talking Therapies for your mood, peer support, and please avoid online hormones. If things get worse, call your GP or the crisis line."],"hear":"\"So will you start me on oestrogen today? ... Okay, that makes sense.\"","mark":"Diagnosis and pathway explained"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Identity over time","Girl since six; distressed at puberty; consistent since","Long-standing gender incongruence"],["Social transition","Living as Sophie full time for two years","Settled and sustained"],["Cross-dressing","Never sexually arousing","Fetishistic transvestism unlikely"],["Psychosis and BDD","No control beliefs; no single-feature preoccupation","No better explanation"],["Mood and safety","Low with waits and family; no suicidal plans; looked at online hormones","Treat mood; harm-reduction advice"]]},"key":"Good assessors check the differentials. Excellent ones do it without signalling doubt, then turn the long wait into a plan: mood support, fertility, safe hormones and peer help.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[5],[4,6],[7]]},"c-u-41":{"name":"Is this coercive control? Pattern, risk, name it, safe route","why":"Turns a vague GP worry into a clear account of the abuse, a risk picture, a name she can hold on to and a safe next step.","frame":["Control and isolation","Emotional abuse","Economic abuse","Physical, sexual and strangulation","Risk to her and the children"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: alone, private, limits (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Natalia Kowalski?","Thank you for coming, Natalia. It's just us here, and what you say stays private unless someone is at serious risk.","Is it safe to talk now, and how long have you got?"],"hear":"\"About ten minutes. I have to get my daughter.\"","mark":"Safe to talk; time limit known"},{"h":"Missed appointments (1 min)","say":["Your GP was worried you'd missed appointments. Could you tell me how things are at home?","What made it hard to get to them?","Do you need to ask him before you go out?"],"hear":"\"He cancels them. He says I don't need them.\"","mark":"Control of health care identified"},{"h":"Controlling behaviour (1½ min)","say":["What else does he stop you doing, or make you ask permission for?","Who looks after the money? Do you have your own card?","Does he check your phone, or know where you are?","Do you still see your family and friends?"],"hear":"\"He keeps my card. He reads my messages every night. I'm not allowed to see my sister.\"","mark":"Economic abuse, monitoring and isolation identified"},{"h":"Other abuse (1 min)","say":["Does he say hurtful things about you, or in front of others?","Has he ever pushed, hit or grabbed you, or put his hands round your neck?","Has he ever pressured or forced you into anything sexual?"],"hear":"\"He shouts in my face and punches doors. He's never hit me.\"","mark":"Emotional abuse identified; physical, sexual, strangulation screened"},{"h":"Effect on her (1 min)","say":["How has all this left you feeling?","How are your sleep and appetite?","Do you drink, or use anything, to cope?"],"hear":"\"Low. Jumpy all the time. I've lost weight. I feel stupid.\"","mark":"Low mood and anxiety identified"},{"h":"Risk: her, him, the children (1½ min)","say":["Has it got worse lately? Has he threatened you, or what would happen if you left?","Are you frightened of what he might do? Is there any chance you're pregnant?","Do the children see or hear it? Has he ever hurt them?","Has any of this made you think of taking your own life, or of hurting him?"],"hear":"\"Worse since summer. He says I'd never see the kids. I'd never hurt myself, they keep me going.\"","mark":"Escalation, threats and children exposed; no suicidal intent"},{"h":"Naming the abuse (1¼ min)","say":["What you're describing has a name: coercive control.","He controls your money, your phone, who you see and where you go. That is emotional and economic abuse.","It's a crime in this country, even without hitting. And it is not your fault."],"hear":"\"I didn't think it counted because he doesn't hit me.\"","mark":"Coercive control named"},{"h":"Summary and close (1¼ min)","say":["How can we safely reach you? We'll send nothing to the house.","Here's the free 24-hour helpline: 0808 2000 247. In danger, ring 999, and if you can't speak, press 55.","Can I refer you to a domestic abuse adviser? And because the children hear it, I need to tell our safeguarding team, with you.","Let's meet again in two weeks, at nursery time."],"hear":"\"Weekday mornings, withheld number. Yes, the adviser. I'll save the number as 'Dentist'.\"","mark":"Safe contact agreed; IDVA referral accepted"}],"table":{"cols":["Area of abuse","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Control and isolation","Cancels appointments; banned from seeing her sister; rules about going out","Coercive control: her world has been shrunk"],["Monitoring","Reads messages nightly; location app","Surveillance: plan safe contact with this in mind"],["Economic abuse","Keeps her bank card; checks what she buys","Dependence that makes leaving harder"],["Emotional and threats","Insults, shouting in her face, punching doors, threats about the children","Intimidation and escalation: raises risk"],["Children","Hear shouting; son bedwetting again","Children are victims: safeguarding referral, made openly"]]},"key":"A good doctor asks about hitting. An excellent one maps the whole pattern of control, asks about strangulation and threats, names it as a crime that is not her fault, and leaves with a safe way to reach her.","objmap":[[1],[2,3,4],[5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-u-42":{"name":"Why did she set the fire? ABC, motive, illness screen","why":"Finds the reason for the fire, which gives both the diagnosis and the risk of another one.","frame":["Antecedents","Behaviour","Consequences","Motive","Past fires","Illness and background screen"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening: not police, limits, appropriate adult (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Leah Brennan?","Thanks, Leah. I'm not the police; I'm here to see how you are and whether you need help.","Some things I may need to share with the custody team if they affect your safety or others'. You can stop at any point."],"hear":"\"Right. Is she staying?\" (points at the appropriate adult)","mark":"Role and limits explained"},{"h":"The fire: ABC (1½ min)","say":["What happened in your room today? Tell me however you like.","What were you doing and feeling in the hours before?","What did you set alight, with what, and was anyone with you?","Once it caught, what did you do?"],"hear":"\"I lit the mattress with my lighter. Then I ran out and shouted fire.\"","mark":"ABC complete; alarm raised"},{"h":"Motive (1 min)","say":["When you lit it, what did you want the fire to do?","Were you angry with anyone, or trying to hurt yourself?","How do you feel about it now?"],"hear":"\"A man's voice said the room was poisoned. Fire was the only way to clean it.\"","mark":"Psychotic motive identified"},{"h":"Fire history (½ min)","say":["Have you ever set a fire before, even as a child?","Do you find fire exciting, or does lighting one calm you?"],"hear":"\"Never. I don't even like fire.\"","mark":"No past fires; pyromania unlikely"},{"h":"Mental state (1½ min)","say":["Tell me about the voice. Is it here now? What does it say?","Have you noticed anything else odd: being watched, messages meant for you?","How has your mood and sleep been? Any thoughts about ending your life?","Do you use cannabis, alcohol or anything else?"],"hear":"\"Staff are filming me. The radio talks about me. I haven't slept in three nights. I stopped weed a month ago.\"","mark":"Persecutory beliefs, referential ideas, insomnia"},{"h":"Background (1 min)","say":["Who brought you up? How did you come to be in the hostel?","How was school? Did you need extra help?","Has anything happened to you that still upsets you, or has anyone taken advantage of you at the hostel?"],"hear":"\"Foster care from 13. I left care at 18. School was all right.\"","mark":"Care leaver; no learning difficulty"},{"h":"Risk (1 min)","say":["Is the voice still telling you to burn anything?","If you went back tonight, would you light another fire?","Do you feel safe in yourself right now?"],"hear":"\"It says it's not finished.\"","mark":"High repeat risk while voice active"},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["I think you're unwell rather than bad, Leah. A voice and frightening beliefs drove this.","You need somewhere safe to rest and be treated. Would you come into hospital?","I'll tell the sergeant you're not well enough to be interviewed now, and what happens next."],"hear":"\"Am I going to prison, or what happens to me now?\"","mark":"Diagnosis and next step shared"}],"table":{"cols":["ABC and motive","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Antecedents","Three weeks of feeling filmed, radio messages, no sleep, voice","Psychotic build-up, not a sudden impulse"],["Behaviour","Lit her mattress alone with a lighter, in her own room","Driven by the voice; not aimed at others"],["Consequences","Ran out, shouted 'fire', waited; frightened and sorry now","Concern for others: lowers immediate danger"],["Motive","To 'clean the poison' on a voice's instruction","Command hallucination with persecutory belief"],["Past fires and risk","None before; voice says 'not finished'","Not pyromania; high repeat risk until treated"]]},"key":"A good doctor finds the diagnosis. An excellent one proves the fire came from the illness through the ABC and motive, asks whether the voice still commands fire, and gives custody a clear view on fitness for interview.","objmap":[[1],[2],[3,4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-u-43":{"name":"Is DBT right for her? Brief history, four modules, structure, honest answer","why":"Gives her a clear, honest picture of DBT, a recommendation, and safety while she waits.","frame":["Mindfulness","Distress tolerance","Emotion regulation","Interpersonal effectiveness","Structure and commitment"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (½ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Chloe Mensah?","Thank you for coming, Chloe. I hear you've been reading about DBT. That's a great step.","Can you tell me a bit about yourself first? Then we'll cover DBT and anything you want to ask."],"hear":"\"Yes. I really want to know if it'd work for me.\""},{"h":"Her difficulties and risk (1½ min)","say":["In your words, what's been hardest?","When did you last hurt yourself, and how? Did it need treatment?","Has it ever got so bad you've thought about killing yourself?"],"hear":"\"My moods flip in an hour. I shout at Jordan, then cut my thighs to calm down. Last week. I don't want to die.\"","mark":"Emotional dysregulation, self-harm, no suicidal intent"},{"h":"Past help and options (1 min)","say":["What help have you had before?","Why now?","For difficulties like yours, talking therapies work best, not tablets. DBT is the one with the strongest evidence; another is mentalisation-based therapy."],"hear":"\"Two antidepressants did nothing. Jordan's said he can't take much more.\"","mark":"Therapy first-line stated"},{"h":"What DBT is: the four modules (1½ min)","say":["DBT means accepting yourself as you are, while learning to change what isn't working.","There are four skill sets. Mindfulness: noticing feelings without being swept away. Distress tolerance: getting through a crisis without cutting.","Emotion regulation: understanding and calming your moods. Interpersonal effectiveness: asking for what you need without an argument."],"hear":"\"So the distress one is for when I want to cut?\"","mark":"Four modules explained"},{"h":"Sessions and structure (1½ min)","say":["Usually there's a weekly one-to-one session of about an hour, and a weekly skills group of about two hours.","You fill in a diary card each day: urges, self-harm, spending, skills used.","You can phone your therapist for coaching between sessions. It usually lasts about a year."],"hear":"\"A year? And a group? I'm not sure about the group.\"","mark":"Structure given"},{"h":"Before you start (1 min)","say":["First there's an assessment, to check DBT fits and agree your goals.","You'd be asked to commit to the programme and to working on the self-harm.","Some services start with a short orientation group."],"hear":"\"OK. I can commit to that.\"","mark":"Pre-treatment phase explained"},{"h":"Recommend and the group (1 min)","say":["I'd honestly recommend DBT for you.","I can ask about one-to-one only, but I can't promise it, because the group is a core part.","The group is more like a class: you learn skills, you don't have to share your story."],"hear":"\"Like a class... maybe I could do that.\"","mark":"Clear recommendation; honest about group"},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["Can you tell me back the four skills, in your words?","While you wait: your crisis plan, NHS 111 option 2, and A&E for any serious injury.","I'll refer you to the DBT team today and ask your GP to review the sertraline. Here's a leaflet."],"hear":"\"Mindfulness, getting through a crisis, calming moods, asking for things. Thank you.\"","mark":"Teach-back; crisis plan; referral"}],"table":{"cols":["DBT module","What you heard","What it means for her"],"rows":[["Mindfulness","Moods flip within an hour, swept away","Learn to notice feelings early, before they peak"],["Distress tolerance","Cuts her thighs to calm down","Safer ways to get through the crisis moment"],["Emotion regulation","Low self-esteem, shopping to lift mood","Understand triggers and calm moods without spending"],["Interpersonal effectiveness","Shouts at Jordan; relationship at risk","Ask for needs and handle conflict without blow-ups"],["Structure and commitment","Worried about the group and a year","Honest answer, class-like group, commitment phase first"]]},"key":"A good doctor lists the four modules. An excellent one links each module to her own difficulties, checks last week's self-harm, recommends clearly, and is honest that the group is core without promising one-to-one.","objmap":[[1,2],[3],[4],[5,6],[7,8]]},"c-u-44":{"name":"Whose voice, and why now? Psychopathology, then the drink","why":"Pins down the hallucination, proves clear consciousness, finds the alcohol cause and plans for withdrawal and driving.","frame":["Form of the voice","Content and commands","Clear consciousness","Alcohol and dependence","Withdrawal and Wernicke's"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (½ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pryce?","Thanks, Gareth. I hear something frightening happened at the supermarket today.","That sounds really upsetting. Would you tell me more about it?"],"hear":"\"I keep hearing him. My neighbour.\""},{"h":"The voice (2 min)","say":["Do you hear him through your ears, like me talking now, or inside your head?","What does he say? Does he talk to you, or about you to someone else?","Does he ever tell you to do things?","When did it start, how often, and was he actually there when you heard him?"],"hear":"\"Clear as day. 'You're a drunk, a failure.' All week. He's away in Spain, though.\"","mark":"Real-sounding voice, speaks to him, insulting, gives no orders"},{"h":"Other symptoms and consciousness (1 min)","say":["Have you seen things others can't, or felt things crawling on your skin?","Any sense of people plotting against you, or thoughts being put into your head?","What day and month is it, and what is this place? Now say the months in reverse, starting at December."],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. Friday, November. Hospital. December, November, October...\"","mark":"Clear consciousness; no other psychotic features"},{"h":"Alcohol history (1½ min)","say":["How much do you drink in a usual day, and what?","When was your last drink?","Do you wake up shaky or sweaty? Do you drink in the morning to settle it?","Have you tried to cut down? What happened?"],"hear":"\"A bottle of vodka a day, three years. Had some at seven this morning. I shake till I drink.\"","mark":"28 units/day; dependence; last drink 07:00"},{"h":"Physical and other causes (1 min)","say":["Ever had a fit, or the shakes so bad you saw things?","Any head injury, blackouts or epilepsy? Any drugs?","Have you been unsteady on your feet, or had double vision? How's your eating?"],"hear":"\"No fits. No drugs. I don't really eat much now.\"","mark":"No seizure history; poor nutrition"},{"h":"Background (1 min)","say":["Has anyone in the family had a mental illness?","Have you ever seen a psychiatrist before?","What does Sian think is going on?"],"hear":"\"No. Never. She says it's the drink.\"","mark":"No past or family psychiatric history"},{"h":"Risk (1 min)","say":["Have you thought about having it out with him?","Has any of this made you want to hurt yourself?","Did you drive today? You drive for work: when did you last drive after drinking?"],"hear":"\"I was going to go round. Sian drove today. I do drive for work.\"","mark":"Risk to neighbour and driving identified"},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["You're not going mad. This is called alcoholic hallucinosis: heavy drinking causing voices while you're fully awake.","Stopping at your level needs medication and monitoring, so I'd like you to stay in, with vitamin injections and a reducing medicine.","You mustn't drive until this is sorted, and you need to tell the DVLA. Can I speak to Sian too?"],"hear":"\"So I'm not going mad? What can you do about the voice?\"","mark":"Cause explained; admission, no driving, consent for collateral"}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Form of the voice","Through his ears, as clear as speech, neighbour's voice","True auditory hallucination"],["Content and commands","'Drunk, failure', to him, no instructions","Second person, abusive, no commands: typical of alcohol"],["Consciousness","Oriented, months backwards correct, no visions","Clear consciousness: not delirium tremens now"],["Alcohol","28 units a day for 3 years; morning shakes; last drink 07:00","Dependence; withdrawal window opening tonight"],["Risk","Wanted to confront neighbour; drives for work","Agree no confrontation; stop driving; DVLA"]]},"key":"A good doctor finds the drink. An excellent one proves clear consciousness with an attention test, times the last drink to plan for seizures, gives thiamine before glucose, and tells a delivery driver to stop driving.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5],[7,8]]},"c-u-45":{"name":"Is IPT right for her? Depression, risk, the four areas","why":"Confirms the depression suits IPT and finds the specific relationship problems the therapy will target.","frame":["Grief","Role disputes","Role transitions","Interpersonal deficits","Suitability and motivation"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (½ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Fiona MacLeod?","Thanks for coming, Fiona. I hear you'd like to try a talking treatment.","Could we talk about how you've been feeling first, then see which therapy fits you best?"],"hear":"\"I would, yes.\""},{"h":"Depressive symptoms (2 min)","say":["How's your mood been, and for how long?","What have you stopped enjoying? How are sleep, appetite, energy and concentration?","Do you blame yourself for things? Your questionnaire score is 14, which fits moderate depression.","Have you ever had spells of feeling unusually high, or heard or seen things others don't?"],"hear":"\"Four months. I've stopped choir. I wake at five. No highs, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Moderate, non-psychotic, non-bipolar depression"},{"h":"Risk (1 min)","say":["Has it ever felt as if life isn't worth carrying on with?","Any thoughts of self-harm or plans?","What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"No, never. The kids keep me going.\"","mark":"No suicidal thoughts; protective factors"},{"h":"Grief (½ min)","say":["Has anyone close to you died in the last few years?","How did you cope with that?"],"hear":"\"My dad, six years ago. It was hard but I got through it.\"","mark":"Grief considered: not a focus"},{"h":"Role disputes (1 min)","say":["Who are the important people in your life? How are things with them?","Are there any disagreements that keep coming back?","What were you hoping would happen?"],"hear":"\"Iain and I argue about Mum. He thinks it's my job because she's my mother.\"","mark":"Role dispute identified"},{"h":"Role transitions (1 min)","say":["What changed in your life around when this started?","What did you lose, and what's new?","How do you feel about the change?"],"hear":"\"Mum moved in after her stroke. I cut my hours. I resent it, then feel terrible.\"","mark":"Role transition identified"},{"h":"Isolation and suitability (1½ min)","say":["Before your mum moved in, were friendships easy for you to make and keep?","Do you see a link between how you feel and what's happened with Mum and Iain?","Could you come every week for about four months? Who would sit with Mum?"],"hear":"\"I had lots of friends, I've just stopped going out. Yes, I see the link. My sister could do Tuesdays.\"","mark":"No interpersonal deficit; motivated; can attend"},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["I think IPT suits you well. Two areas stand out: the change to being Mum's carer, and the strain with Iain.","It's usually 12 to 16 weekly hour-long sessions: mapping relationships first, working on those two areas, then planning ahead.","You can refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies today. Carry on the sertraline, and if things get worse, call your GP or NHS 111 option 2."],"hear":"\"So would this IPT suit me, and what does it involve?\" then \"That makes sense. I'll refer myself tonight.\"","mark":"Suitability explained; focus agreed; referral"}],"table":{"cols":["IPT area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Grief","Father died six years ago; grieved and moved on","Not a focus"],["Role disputes","Repeated arguments with Iain over Mum's care; feels unsupported","Focus area: unmet expectations to work through"],["Role transitions","Became Mum's carer after her stroke; cut work hours; dropped choir","Main focus: onset of depression tied to this change"],["Interpersonal deficits","Good friendships before; withdrawn only since low","Not a focus: withdrawal is a symptom"],["Suitability","PHQ-9 14, no psychosis or highs, motivated, sister covers Tuesdays","Suitable for IPT as an outpatient"]]},"key":"Finding several areas does not mean treating them all. Map all four, then agree one or two focus areas that clearly link to when her mood dropped.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-u-46":{"name":"Is this BDD? Concern, conviction, rituals, risk","why":"Pins down body dysmorphic disorder, its insight level and its risks in ten minutes, without ever debating her appearance.","frame":["The concern and time lost","Conviction and insight","Rituals and avoidance","Surgery and self-surgery","Look-alike diagnoses","Suicide risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (¾ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the clinic psychiatrists. Are you Amira Haddad?","Thank you for coming, Amira. Your GP said you've been very worried about your eyes.","That sounds really distressing. Could you tell me how it affects you day to day? We have about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Everyone can see it. I can't stop thinking about it.\""},{"h":"The concern (1¼ min)","say":["What exactly is it about your eyes that bothers you? Anything else about how you look?","When did you first notice it?","Adding it all up, how many hours a day is it on your mind? And how upsetting is it, out of 10?"],"hear":"\"They're too wide apart and one's lower. Since I was about fifteen. Four hours a day, easily. Eight out of ten.\"","mark":"Preoccupation with a perceived defect: ~4 h/day"},{"h":"Conviction and insight (1 min)","say":["How sure are you that your eyes look the way you describe?","If someone tells you they're fine, how do you explain that to yourself?","Is it possible you see them differently from how others do?"],"hear":"\"I know they're wrong. But... I suppose it could be me seeing it worse than it is.\"","mark":"Fair insight: overvalued idea"},{"h":"Rituals and avoidance (1¼ min)","say":["How often do you check them, in mirrors, windows or your phone camera?","What do you do to hide or change how they look?","Do you compare yourself with others, or ask people if your eyes look all right?"],"hear":"\"Thirty, forty times a day. I wear these glasses everywhere. I ask my sister constantly.\"","mark":"Checking, camouflage, comparing, reassurance-seeking"},{"h":"Impact (¾ min)","say":["How has this affected your work?","And relationships, going out, being photographed?"],"hear":"\"I asked to move off the front desk. I stopped dating. I won't be in photos.\"","mark":"Moderate impairment: work and social life"},{"h":"Cosmetic procedures (1 min)","say":["Have you seen anyone about changing your eyes? What did they say?","Are you planning anything now, and how would you pay?","What would you do if no surgeon would operate? Have you ever thought about trying something yourself?"],"hear":"\"Two surgeons, privately. I'm saving. I've watched videos of people doing things themselves. I haven't tried.\"","mark":"Surgery-seeking; self-surgery thoughts, no plan"},{"h":"Look-alike diagnoses (1½ min)","say":["How's your mood been? Sleep, appetite, enjoyment?","Leaving your eyes aside, do social situations make you anxious in other ways? Any other habits or thoughts you can't stop?","Any worries about your weight or shape? Does anything make you feel watched, or receive special messages?"],"hear":"\"I'm low because of my eyes, but I sleep and eat fine. It's only about how I look. No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"Depression, social anxiety, OCD, eating disorder and psychosis screened"},{"h":"Risk (1 min)","say":["When things are at their worst, have you felt life isn't worth living?","Have you thought of ending your life, made any plans or ever tried?","Do you drink or use anything to cope?"],"hear":"\"Most weeks I think, what's the point. But I'd never do anything. My sister needs me. I drink a bit at weekends.\"","mark":"Passive suicidal thoughts, no plan or intent"},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["So: hours a day worrying about your eyes, checking and hiding them, and it's cost you work and dating.","This has a name, body dysmorphic disorder. It's treatable, mainly with a specific kind of CBT, and sometimes medicine. Surgery usually doesn't help it.","Could you tell me back what we've agreed? If things get worse, call NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123."],"hear":"\"So it's a real thing. I'll try the therapy. And hold off on surgery for now.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The concern","Eyes too wide and uneven since 15; 4 h/day; 8/10","Marked preoccupation with an unseen defect"],["Conviction","Sure, but can accept she may see it worse","Fair insight: overvalued idea, not a delusion"],["Rituals","30–40 checks a day, tinted glasses, asks her sister","Core BDD behaviours: targets for ERP"],["Surgery and risk","Two surgeons, saving; DIY videos; passive suicidal thoughts","Low-moderate chronic risk; needs a safety plan"],["Look-alikes","Mood secondary, no other social fears, no weight or psychotic features","BDD is the primary diagnosis"]]},"key":"A good doctor notes the surgery visits. An excellent one asks what she will do if every surgeon says no, and rates her insight with one question rather than an argument.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5],[6,8],[7,9]]},"c-u-48":{"name":"Why not better, then one clear next step","why":"Finds fixable reasons for non-response before recommending a single, monitored next step with therapy and a clear fallback.","frame":["Response so far","Reasons for non-response","Risk","One recommended step","Therapy","If it fails"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (½ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the clinic psychiatrists. Are you Jeremy Pearce?","Thanks for coming, Jerry. Your GP has explained the medicines you've tried.","Can I check how you are and what matters to you, then go through the options? We've got about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Please.\""},{"h":"Where he is now (1¼ min)","say":["What's got better since the venlafaxine, and what hasn't?","How's your energy, sleep, appetite and concentration?","Is there anything you still get pleasure from?"],"hear":"\"Mood's a notch better. Still exhausted, can't focus at work, haven't been on my bike in months.\"","mark":"Partial response"},{"h":"Reasons for non-response (1¼ min)","say":["How often do you take it? Any side effects?","Have you noticed feeling the cold, constipation, dry skin, or weight changes?","Any heart trouble, high blood pressure or fits, you or the family? How much do you drink?"],"hear":"\"Every day. Bit sweaty. Actually, I'm always cold, and I've put on weight. Dad had thyroid trouble. Few beers a week.\"","mark":"Possible hypothyroidism"},{"h":"Risk (¾ min)","say":["When you're at your lowest, have you felt life isn't worth living?","Any thoughts about ending your life, or plans?"],"hear":"\"No. Never that. I've got the kids.\"","mark":"No suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"His circumstances (¾ min)","say":["What matters most to you in treatment?","Any big stresses at the moment? Have you ever had talking therapy?"],"hear":"\"Staying at work. Not putting on more weight. Never had therapy.\""},{"h":"Medication: one recommendation (1¾ min)","say":["First, a blood test today for your thyroid, kidneys and blood count. If your thyroid is low, treating it may lift a lot of this.","If it's normal, my recommendation is to increase venlafaxine to 225 mg, because you've already had some benefit.","I'll check your blood pressure today and again in two weeks. Sweating may increase. Never stop it suddenly."],"hear":"\"Makes sense. So not a new tablet?\"","mark":"One clear next step agreed"},{"h":"Therapy (1 min)","say":["Tablets work best alongside talking therapy. I'd like to refer you for CBT, about 16 to 20 sessions.","Sessions can be early or by video, so you can keep working."],"hear":"\"I could do early mornings.\""},{"h":"If still stuck (1 min)","say":["If that doesn't work after four to six weeks, the next step is adding a second medicine, like aripiprazole or lithium.","I'd avoid mirtazapine first, given your weight worry.","Treatments like ECT or rTMS are for much later, if several steps fail."],"hear":"\"Good to know there's a plan B.\""},{"h":"Summary and close (1¼ min)","say":["Could you sum up what we've agreed?","I'll see you in four weeks. If suicidal thoughts ever come, call NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123."],"hear":"\"Blood test, then 225 if it's normal, BP check, CBT. Plan B if not.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Response","Mood a bit better; energy, interest, focus not","Partial response to venlafaxine 150 mg"],["Adherence and side effects","Daily; mild sweating","Dose increase is tolerable"],["Physical","Cold, constipated, dry skin, weight up; father hypothyroid","Check TFTs before changing anything else"],["Risk","No suicidal thoughts; children protective","Outpatient plan is safe"],["Preferences","Keep working; avoid weight gain; never had therapy","Raise venlafaxine, add CBT; avoid mirtazapine first"]]},"key":"A good doctor lists the options. An excellent one finds the thyroid clue first, then gives one recommendation that builds on the partial response, with its monitoring, and keeps the rest as a clear fallback.","objmap":[[3,6],[4],[6],[7,8],[9]]},"c-u-49":{"name":"The love belief: belief, actions, rejection, weapons","why":"Keeps everyone safe while mapping the belief and the behaviours that predict harm, and turns that into immediate protective action.","frame":["Safety and boundaries","The belief and its evidence","Knowledge of her and actions","Response to rejection","Weapons and violence history","Mental state and substances"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening and safety (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the on-call psychiatrists. Could I take your name?","Thank you, Lewis. I can see you're upset about not getting to see someone. Shall we sit somewhere quieter? My colleague will join us.","Before we start: I'll keep what you say confidential, except if I think someone might come to harm."],"hear":"\"Fine. But I'm not leaving.\"","mark":"Safe room, colleague, confidentiality limits"},{"h":"How it began (1½ min)","say":["That's okay. Could you tell me about this nurse and how you met?","What happened that made you feel this way?","How much of the day is she on your mind?"],"hear":"\"In A&E. I thought I was dying. She held my hand and smiled at me like nobody ever has.\""},{"h":"The belief (1 min)","say":["How do you know she feels the same?","Could there be another explanation, for example that she was being kind as part of her job?"],"hear":"\"No. She meant it. I know.\"","mark":"Fixed belief: probable erotomanic delusion"},{"h":"Knowledge and actions (1¼ min)","say":["What do you know about her: her full name, shifts, where she lives?","Have you tried to see her before, waited for her, followed her, or messaged her?","Does she have a boyfriend or husband, as far as you know?"],"hear":"\"Hannah. I know she works lates. I waited outside the unit twice. Found her on Instagram.\"","mark":"Approach behaviour: waiting, online searching"},{"h":"Response to rejection and weapons (1½ min)","say":["What will you do if she doesn't want to see you?","What do you mean by 'whatever it takes'? Are you thinking of hurting her or anyone?","Do you have a knife or any weapon on you now, or at home?"],"hear":"\"People will have to notice me. I've got a knife at home. I'm not stupid enough to bring it.\"","mark":"Veiled threat; weapon access"},{"h":"Mental state screen and history (1¼ min)","say":["How's your mood been? Any voices, or feeling people are against you?","Do you drink or use drugs?","Any police record? Have you ever hurt someone? Felt like this about someone before?"],"hear":"\"Mood's fine. No voices. A bit of weed. Got a caution for a fight when I was nineteen.\"","mark":"Static risk: past violence; substances"},{"h":"Honest close (1 min)","say":["I can't arrange for you to see her, Lewis, or give you her details.","I am worried about how all-consuming this has become, and I'd like my consultant to see you tonight. Will you wait here with us?"],"hear":"\"So you're not going to help me. (pause) Fine. I'll wait.\""},{"h":"Act on the risk (1½ min, after leaving the room)","say":["To security: please stay with him; he says he has a knife at home but not on him.","To the consultant: probable erotomanic delusion, approach behaviour, veiled threat, weapon at home. I've called the police; can you advise on an MHA assessment?","To the nurse's manager: please keep Hannah away from reception and arrange an escort tonight. I'll complete a Datix."],"hear":"\"Police are on their way. I'll come in now.\"","mark":"Risk graded high; protective actions taken"}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The belief","A smile in A&E means she loves him; no doubt","Fixed erotomanic belief"],["Knowledge and actions","Knows her name and shift; waited twice; online searching","Active pursuit: raised risk"],["Rejection","'People will have to notice me', 'whatever it takes'","Veiled threat linked to rejection"],["Weapons and history","Knife at home; caution for affray at 19; cannabis","Static and dynamic risk factors present"],["Mental state","Irritable; no other psychotic or mood symptoms","Isolated delusion: delusional disorder likely"]]},"key":"A good doctor asks if he would hurt her. An excellent one asks what he will do when she says no, what weapons he can reach, and then warns the nurse and calls the police before writing a word.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4],[5],[7,8]]},"c-u-50":{"name":"Name the dependence, negotiate the taper","why":"Confirms dependence quickly, then turns her reluctance into a slow, written, shared plan she can stick to.","frame":["Her lorazepam story","Dependence features","Risk and other substances","Why reduce","How the taper works","Her fears and follow-up"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (¾ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the clinic psychiatrists. Are you Natalie Osei?","It is, Natalie. Your new GP asked us for advice. Nothing will change today without us agreeing it together.","That's understandable. Could you tell me how the lorazepam started and how it's going now? We've got about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Okay. I'm already nervous.\""},{"h":"Lorazepam story (1 min)","say":["What was happening when it started? Did you try anything else first?","How do you take it now, across the day? Has the dose changed?"],"hear":"\"A crisis at work four years ago. The GP just kept repeating it. 1 mg four times a day.\""},{"h":"Signs of dependence (1¼ min)","say":["What happens if a dose is late?","Do you find yourself craving it, or watching the clock?","Does it work as well as it did? Ever taken extra or run short?"],"hear":"\"I shake and sweat. I count the hours. It doesn't do as much now; I've run short once.\"","mark":"Withdrawal, craving, tolerance: dependence"},{"h":"Mood, risk and substances (1 min)","say":["How's your mood and anxiety at the moment? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","Do you drink? Any other tablets, painkillers or sleeping pills? Any health problems, like fits?"],"hear":"\"Mood's fine, anxiety's mostly okay. Just the odd glass of wine. No other tablets.\"","mark":"Low risk; no other sedatives"},{"h":"Why reduce (1¼ min)","say":["The shaking when a dose is late tells me your body has become dependent. That isn't your fault.","Over time it does less for anxiety, and it can cause tiredness, poor memory and concentration, and accidents, especially with alcohol or driving.","Many people feel clearer and less anxious once they're off it."],"hear":"\"Why can't I just stay on it, though?\""},{"h":"How the taper works (2 min)","say":["We'd switch you to diazepam, a longer-acting cousin, over about a week: 10 mg for each 1 mg of lorazepam.","Then we'd cut by small amounts every two to four weeks, at your pace. It usually takes several months.","Never stop suddenly, avoid alcohol, and don't drive if you feel drowsy. One prescriber, weekly scripts, and a written plan."],"hear":"\"Would I have to go into hospital?\"","mark":"Taper agreed"},{"h":"Her fears (1¼ min)","say":["No hospital. You're well and supported, so home is the best place to do this.","If anxiety comes back, we'll treat it with CBT and, if needed, a non-addictive medicine like sertraline.","You can pause any step that feels too much."],"hear":"\"If I can pause, I'll try it.\""},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["Can you tell me the plan back in your words?","I'll write to your GP today and refer you for CBT. I'll see you in two weeks.","If you ever have a fit or feel confused, call 999."],"hear":"\"Diazepam over a week, small cuts every few weeks, no stopping suddenly, review in two weeks.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Start and course","Work crisis 4 years ago; repeated without review; 1 mg QDS","Long-term use without a plan"],["Dependence","Shakes and sweats if late; craving; less effect; ran short","Physical and psychological dependence"],["Risk","Mood fine; no self-harm; little alcohol; no other sedatives","Safe for a community taper"],["Her fears","Anxiety returning; busy life; hospital","Control the pace; CBT; reassure no admission"],["Plan","Diazepam 40 mg/day, small steps every 2-4 weeks","Written, shared, single prescriber, reviewed"]]},"key":"A good doctor gives a taper. An excellent one keeps the history short, names dependence from her own words, and hands her control of the pace, which is what makes the plan last.","objmap":[[2,3],[4],[5],[6],[7,8]]},"c-u-52":{"name":"Why has he relapsed? Symptoms, then the four usual causes","why":"Confirms the relapse, finds the reasons in his own words, and builds a plan he will actually follow.","frame":["Current symptoms","Adherence","Substances","Stress and physical triggers","Past pattern and insight","Risk"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (½ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Callum Reid?","Thanks for coming in, Callum. Leah was worried you've been hearing voices again, so we brought your appointment forward.","I'd like to hear how things have been, and then think together about what may have set this off. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Fine. She worries a lot.\""},{"h":"Current symptoms (2 min)","say":["Tell me about the voices. When did they come back?","How many are there, where do they seem to come from, and what do they say?","Do they ever tell you to do things? Have you felt watched or that people mean you harm?","Any sense that your thoughts or actions are being controlled? How are your mood and sleep?"],"hear":"\"About a month. Two blokes, outside, taking the mick and saying I'm being watched. They don't tell me to do anything.\"","mark":"Relapse symptoms confirmed, no commands"},{"h":"Tablets and adherence (1½ min)","say":["Most people miss tablets sometimes. How many nights a week would you say you miss?","What is it about the olanzapine that puts you off?","How long has that been going on?"],"hear":"\"I skip it on work nights; I can't get up for five. And I've put on a stone.\"","mark":"Partial adherence identified"},{"h":"Substances (1 min)","say":["Have you been using cannabis or anything else lately?","How much, and has that changed recently?","Any alcohol or other drugs?"],"hear":"\"A new lad at work smokes skunk. I've been having a few joints most evenings for a couple of months.\"","mark":"Cannabis restarted"},{"h":"Stress and physical triggers (1 min)","say":["What else has changed in the last couple of months: work, money, home?","Any illness, new medicines, or change in how much you smoke?"],"hear":"\"They moved me to early shifts. Money's tight. Smoking's the same.\"","mark":"Shift stress and sleep loss"},{"h":"Past pattern and insight (1 min)","say":["Last time you were unwell, what happened first?","Does any of this feel like then?","What do you make of the voices yourself?"],"hear":"\"Last time I stopped my tablets and was smoking weed. I suppose it does feel a bit like that.\"","mark":"Partial insight; same pattern"},{"h":"Risk (1½ min)","say":["Have the voices made you feel like hurting yourself, or ending your life?","Have you felt like harming anyone, at work or at home, or carried anything to protect yourself?","Are you still driving at the moment?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. I drive to work.\"","mark":"Low current risk; driving to address"},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["I think the illness is starting to come back. Missed doses, the skunk and the early shifts have probably added up.","Could you take the olanzapine at 7 pm every night from tonight, so you're not groggy at five? We'll look at weight-friendlier options too.","Your care coordinator will see you this week. No driving while the voices are here. If things get worse, ring the duty line, or 111 option 2 at night.","What will you tell Leah about today?"],"hear":"\"Seven o'clock I can do. I'll tell her we've got a plan.\"","mark":"Relapse and causes explained; plan agreed"}],"table":{"cols":["Possible cause","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Symptoms","Two mocking voices for a month; feels watched; no commands","Early relapse; low immediate risk"],["Adherence","Skips olanzapine on work nights: grogginess, weight","Main driver; fix timing and side effects"],["Substances","Skunk most evenings for two months","Second driver; offer drug service and MI"],["Stress","New early shifts, money worries, poor sleep","Adds load; consider occupational health"],["Past pattern","Last relapse: stopped tablets plus cannabis","Same pattern; write down early warning signs"]]},"key":"Finding that he missed doses is good. Finding why (grogginess before early shifts, weight gain) and building the plan around that reason is what stops the next relapse.","objmap":[[2],[3,4],[5,6],[7],[8]]},"c-u-53":{"name":"Jealousy and a weapon: the belief, the intent, the history","why":"Gets the facts that decide risk tonight, especially intent with the knife, and turns them into protective action.","frame":["The jealous belief","Intent with the knife","Past violence to partners","Other risks: alcohol, mood, suicide","Children and weapons","Protective factors"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening and confidentiality (1 min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working with the custody team. Are you Gareth Holloway?","Thanks for seeing me, Gareth. Today sounds like it's been a lot. I'm here to understand what's been going on for you.","What we discuss is confidential, unless I'm concerned someone could get hurt."],"hear":"\"Yeah. Why do I need a psychiatrist? My wife is the problem, not me.\""},{"h":"Today's events and the knife (1½ min)","say":["Tell me what happened today, from your side.","What were you going to do when you got there? Why did you take the knife?","If he'd laughed at you, or Donna denied it, what would have happened?"],"hear":"\"I took it to scare him off. If she'd lied to my face... I don't know.\"","mark":"Intent with weapon: cannot rule out use"},{"h":"The jealous belief (1½ min)","say":["What makes you sure about the affair? How long have you thought this?","What have you done to check: her phone, following her, asking her?","Could there be another explanation for any of it?"],"hear":"\"Two years. His car's been on our street. I check her phone, smell her clothes, time her. I followed her twice. I'm not wrong.\"","mark":"Fixed jealous belief with checking"},{"h":"Violence and threats (1½ min)","say":["When you've argued about this, has it ever got physical?","Have you ever threatened her or him?","Has anything like this happened with a previous partner?"],"hear":"\"I pushed her against the wall once. My ex at 31... I grabbed her. I got a caution.\"","mark":"Past violence to partners"},{"h":"Other symptoms and alcohol (1 min)","say":["Do you ever hear voices or notice things others don't?","How have your mood and sleep been?","How much are you drinking at the moment, roughly?"],"hear":"\"No voices. I'm low, sleeping three hours. Four or five cans a night, none today.\"","mark":"Low mood, more drinking"},{"h":"History and protective factors (1 min)","say":["Any other trouble with the police: fights, weapons?","Who lives at home? Who matters most to you?","Would you accept help for how you're feeling?"],"hear":"\"Pub fights in my twenties. Our daughter Ellie's 14. I'd do anything for her.\"","mark":"Child at home; daughter protective"},{"h":"Current risk (1 min)","say":["What do you plan to do when you leave here?","Are there knives, tools or any guns at home?","With all this, have you thought of ending your life?"],"hear":"\"Get the truth out of her. Work blades, no guns. Sometimes I think they'd be better off without me.\"","mark":"Ongoing intent; passive suicidal thoughts"},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["I can hear how tormented you've been, Gareth. I'm also worried someone could be badly hurt.","I'm going to tell the custody sergeant about that risk, and ask for a further assessment, possibly under the Mental Health Act.","Please don't contact Donna or her manager. The custody nurse will keep a close eye on you tonight."],"hear":"\"So that's it? I'm the one locked up.\"","mark":"Risk named, sharing explained"}],"table":{"cols":["Risk area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Belief","Two years, fixed, trivial evidence; checks, follows, interrogates","Probable delusional jealousy; escalating"],["Intent","Took knife 'to scare him'; can't say he wouldn't use it","High, imminent risk to Donna and manager"],["History","Pushed Donna; grabbed ex at 31; pub fights","Strongest predictor: past partner violence"],["Current state","Low, 3 h sleep, more drinking, no insight","Raises both violence and suicide risk"],["Others","Ellie, 14, at home; work blades","Safeguarding referral; remove weapons"]]},"key":"A good assessment confirms morbid jealousy. An excellent one pins down intent with the knife, links it to past partner violence, and tells him honestly who will be informed and why.","objmap":[[1],[2,7],[3],[4,5,6,7],[8]]},"c-u-54":{"name":"Postpartum psychosis: timeline, psychosis, body, family, two risks","why":"Reaches the diagnosis safely, excludes physical causes, and keeps both mother and baby safe.","frame":["Timeline since birth","Psychotic and manic features","Physical causes","Family history","Risk to herself","Risk to Leo"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening and Leo first (¾ min)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Amara Whitfield?","Leo is just next door with the children's nurses, and he's being looked after.","Your health visitor was worried about you both today. Could you tell me how things have been since he was born?"],"hear":"\"Where's Leo? Is he safe? ... Okay.\""},{"h":"Since the birth (1¼ min)","say":["How did the birth go? Any problems afterwards?","When did things start to feel different? Was it sudden or gradual?","How much have you slept in the last few nights?"],"hear":"\"Forceps, but fine. I was fine for four weeks, then everything sped up. I haven't really slept for four nights.\"","mark":"Rapid onset after a well period"},{"h":"Psychotic symptoms (2 min)","say":["The health visitor said Leo hasn't been feeding much. Is there a reason he doesn't need feeding?","Have you heard anyone speaking when nobody's there? What does she say?","Do you feel anyone is against you or Leo? Do you ever feel muddled about the time or where you are?"],"hear":"\"He's pure light; he doesn't need milk. A lady's voice tells me to keep him from food. The health visitor wants to take him.\"","mark":"Delusion about baby, voice, persecutory idea"},{"h":"Mood (1 min)","say":["How's your mood been? Up, down, or changing a lot?","Do your thoughts feel faster than usual? How is your energy?"],"hear":"\"Amazing, then I cry. My head's buzzing. I clean at night.\"","mark":"Manic features, lability"},{"h":"Physical causes and background (1½ min)","say":["Any fever, pain, bleeding, headaches or problems with your eyesight?","Any alcohol, drugs or new medicines?","Has anyone in your family been unwell after having a baby, or had bipolar disorder? Have you ever been unwell like this before?"],"hear":"\"No fever, just paracetamol. My mum was in hospital after she had me. Never me.\"","mark":"Strong family history"},{"h":"Risk to herself (1 min)","say":["Have you been eating and drinking yourself?","Have you had thoughts of self-harm or that life isn't worth living?"],"hear":"\"I don't need food either. No, I'm blessed.\"","mark":"Self-neglect"},{"h":"Risk to Leo (1 min)","say":["Sometimes new mums have frightening thoughts about their baby. Have you had any thoughts of harming Leo?","If the voice told you to do something else to him, would you do it?","What would you do if we asked you to stay here today?"],"hear":"\"Never. But if she was right, I'd have to. I want to take him home.\"","mark":"Would act on voice; absconding risk"},{"h":"Summary and close (1½ min)","say":["I think you're unwell with an illness that can happen after having a baby, called postpartum psychosis. It's serious, and very treatable.","We want to keep you and Leo together and safe, in a mother and baby unit. Until you're better, someone will always be with you when you're with him.","We'll do some blood tests and checks now. Kwame's on his way; can I talk to him too?"],"hear":"\"Am I going to lose him? ... Okay, you can talk to Kwame.\"","mark":"Diagnosis and plan explained"}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Timeline","Well four weeks, then rapid change; 4 nights' little sleep","Typical postpartum onset"],["Psychosis","Leo is 'pure light', voice says withhold food, health visitor a threat","Postpartum psychosis"],["Mood","Elated then tearful, racing thoughts, cleaning at night","Manic or mixed picture"],["Physical and family","No fever or drugs; mother admitted after birth","Physical screen still needed; high familial risk"],["Risk","Not feeding Leo; would follow voice; wants to leave","High risk to Leo; supervised care, MBU"]]},"key":"The danger is not usually a wish to harm the baby, but acting on a delusion. Ask whether she would follow the voice, and keep mother and baby together, safely, in a mother and baby unit.","objmap":[[1,2],[3,4,5],[5],[6,7],[8]]},"c-u-56":{"name":"Focused MSE and risk in postpartum psychosis: one finding per domain","why":"Gets a complete, defensible mental state and a clear risk picture in ten minutes, without losing a wary mother.","frame":["Appearance and behaviour","Speech and mood","Thought content","Perception","Cognition and insight","Risk to baby, self and others"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Leanne Barker?","Congratulations on Ellis. I know you're keen to go home. The midwives asked me to check how you're doing.","Could we talk for about ten minutes about how you've been feeling since he arrived?"],"hear":"\"If it's quick. I'm fine, he's the one who matters.\""},{"h":"Appearance and behaviour","say":["Tell me about Ellis. What's he like?","You seem a bit restless. How's your body feeling?","Have you been able to hold him or feed him since he was born?"],"hear":"\"He doesn't need feeding like other babies. I can't sit still, there's too much to do.\"","mark":"Restless, unkempt, not feeding Ellis"},{"h":"Speech and mood","say":["How would you describe your mood right now?","How much sleep have you had since the birth?","Do your thoughts feel faster than usual, or hard to keep up with?"],"hear":"\"Amazing! Never better. I don't need sleep.\" (then suddenly tearful)","mark":"Elated, labile mood with pressured speech"},{"h":"Thought content","say":["You told the midwives he's special. What makes him special?","How sure are you about that? Is there any other way to see it?","Has anything made you uneasy about the staff here?"],"hear":"\"God sent him to heal the world. I've never been surer. The nurses want to keep him from me.\"","mark":"Grandiose religious delusion; persecutory ideas about staff"},{"h":"Perception","say":["When people are under huge strain, some hear voices that nobody else hears. Has that been happening to you?","What does the voice say? Does it ever tell you to do things?","Any visions or strange smells or tastes?"],"hear":"\"A voice tells me he's chosen. It doesn't tell me to do anything.\"","mark":"Second-person voice confirming the delusion; no command"},{"h":"Cognition and insight","say":["What's today's date, and where are we right now?","How do you make sense of what's been happening to you?","Would you accept help if the doctors felt you needed it?"],"hear":"\"Thursday, the maternity ward. There's nothing wrong with me.\"","mark":"Oriented, distractible; no insight"},{"h":"Risk","say":["What would happen if Ellis didn't have milk for another day?","Have you had any thoughts that Ellis might come to harm, or that you might harm him?","Has it crossed your mind to hurt yourself or end your life?","If the nurses tried to stop you leaving, what would you do?"],"hear":"\"God will look after him. I'd never hurt him or me. I'd just walk out.\"","mark":"High neglect and absconding risk; no stated intent to harm"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Leanne, you love Ellis, and you've had almost no sleep. I think you're unwell with an illness that can follow birth, and it gets better with treatment.","I'd like you both to stay here today. The midwives will help with Ellis's feeds, and I'm calling the specialist perinatal team now.","Can we call your partner so he can come in? I'll be back to see you this afternoon."],"hear":"\"I still think I'm fine. But he can come in.\""}],"table":{"cols":["MSE domain","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Appearance and behaviour","Dishevelled, restless, ignores the cot","Overactivity; neglect of Ellis"],["Speech and mood","Rapid, pressured; elated then tearful; no sleep","Manic or mixed picture"],["Thought content","Ellis sent by God; staff want to take him","Grandiose delusion; persecutory ideas"],["Perception","Voice says he is chosen","Auditory hallucination congruent with delusion"],["Cognition and insight","Oriented; distractible; 'nothing wrong'","Delirium less likely; no insight"],["Risk","Won't feed him; would walk out","High risk to Ellis by neglect; high absconding risk"]]},"key":"Good residents ask about harm to the baby. Excellent ones see that a mother with no intent to harm can still endanger her baby through neglect, and make sure he is fed and supervised before leaving.","objmap":[[2,3,4,5,6],[6],[7],[8],[8]]},"c-u-57":{"name":"Teenage overdose: before, during, after, why, and now","why":"Gives a clear account of intent, the causes behind it and today's risk in ten minutes, so discharge is safe.","frame":["The overdose","After the overdose","Why now","Mood and safeguarding","Current risk and protection"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Chloe?","I'm glad you're physically better. I'd like to understand what happened. We'll take about ten minutes.","Our chat is kept private, except if I'm worried you or someone else isn't safe. Then I'd have to share it."],"hear":"\"Fine.\""},{"h":"The overdose","say":["Can you take me through that day, from the start?","Where did you get the tablets, and how long had it been on your mind?","Where were you, was there a note, and what did you expect would happen?"],"hear":"\"I bought them from two shops after school. I'd thought about it for weeks. I wanted everything to stop.\"","mark":"Planned, alone, intent to die"},{"h":"After the overdose","say":["What happened after you took them?","How do you feel now about being alive?"],"hear":"\"I messaged my friend an hour later. I'm sort of relieved, but I still feel rubbish.\"","mark":"Ambivalence; relief but low"},{"h":"Why now","say":["What's been happening at school?","When did it start, and has it got worse?","Does it carry on online? Does anyone at home or school know?"],"hear":"\"Girls in my year share edited pictures of me in a group chat. It's been months. Mum doesn't know.\"","mark":"Escalating bullying with online spread; hidden from mum"},{"h":"Mood and mental health","say":["How's your mood been over the last few months?","How are your sleep and eating? Do you still enjoy anything?","Have you ever hurt yourself before? Do you drink or use any drugs?"],"hear":"\"Low most days. I don't sleep and I skip lunch so I don't have to face them. Never cut. Don't drink.\"","mark":"Depressive symptoms; no prior self-harm or substances"},{"h":"Safeguarding","say":["Were any of the pictures sexual, or has anyone asked you for pictures?","Has anyone ever hurt you or made you feel unsafe at home?"],"hear":"\"No, they just make me look stupid. Home's fine.\"","mark":"No sexual images or abuse disclosed"},{"h":"Protective factors and current risk","say":["Who or what keeps you going?","Are you having any thoughts about ending your life now? Any plans?","What might make those thoughts stronger, and what would you do if they came back tonight?"],"hear":"\"My friend Amara and my art. No plans now. If school's the same, I don't know.\"","mark":"Protective factors present; risk tied to return to school"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["So, months of bullying that followed you home, low mood, then a planned overdose. I'm glad you messaged Amara.","Before you go home: a safety plan with you and mum, medicines locked away, school's safeguarding lead told, and CAMHS seeing you within two days.","What would you like me to tell mum, and what should I leave for you to tell her?"],"hear":"\"You can tell her about the bullying. Not the pictures yet.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["The overdose","~80 tablets, two shops, alone, intended to die","Planned, high-lethality act: high intent"],["After","Messaged friend; relieved but low","Ambivalence; risk still present"],["Why now","Months of bullying; images in group chat","Main cause; no escape at home"],["Mood","Low, poor sleep, skipping meals","Probable depressive episode"],["Protection","Best friend, art, relief","Things to build the safety plan on"]]},"key":"Good residents ask whether she still wants to die. Excellent ones find what would make the thoughts return, here going back to the same school, and build the plan around changing that before discharge.","objmap":[[1],[2,3],[4,5],[5,6,7],[8]]},"c-u-58":{"name":"Delusion or overvalued idea? Source, culture, conviction, consequences, screen","why":"Tells a culturally shared conviction apart from psychosis, respectfully, in ten minutes.","frame":["Origin of the belief","Cultural fit","Conviction","Effect on life and risk","Psychotic screen"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the psychiatry team. Are you Neil Corrigan?","Thanks for coming. Your GP said you had concerns about the blood tubes. I'd really like to understand your view. It'll take about ten minutes.","I'm not here to laugh. Could you tell me how it started?"],"hear":"\"Most people laugh. I don't see why I'm here.\""},{"h":"The belief and its origin","say":["Where did you first come across the idea about barcodes?","Is this something your church teaches? Do your family share it?","I'm not familiar with all of Revelation. Can you help me understand?"],"hear":"\"Our study group has talked about it for years. My wife agrees. Lots of the church do.\"","mark":"Culturally shared belief"},{"h":"Fixity and reasoning","say":["Blood tubes help diagnose illness. Is there any chance not every barcode is linked to evil?","How much of the day do you spend thinking about it?","When people disagree, how do you feel?"],"hear":"\"I suppose I might be wrong about the tubes. It bothers me more than I'd like. I don't argue with people.\"","mark":"Conviction not absolute"},{"h":"Effect on life and risk","say":["With barcodes everywhere, how do you manage day to day?","Has it ever stopped you, or your children, getting medical care?","Have you ever got into a confrontation over it?"],"hear":"\"I avoid them where I can. I skipped my bloods. My daughter's had her jabs.\"","mark":"Missed own care; no risk to child or others"},{"h":"Psychotic screen","say":["Do you ever see signs meant for you on TV, radio or in the paper?","Have you heard voices when no one was around?","Have you felt someone else was controlling your thoughts or actions, or inserting or removing thoughts?"],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that.\"","mark":"No psychotic features"},{"h":"Mood, substances and background","say":["How's your mood been, and your sleep?","Do you drink, or use any drugs?","Any mental health problems in the past, or in the family?"],"hear":"\"Mood's fine. Pint on a Sunday. Never been unwell.\"","mark":"No mood disorder or substances"},{"h":"Diagnostic impression","say":["Neil, I see no sign of a mental illness. This is a firmly held belief that comes from your faith.","What tells me that: it's shared by your church, you can see other views, and you've none of the other experiences that come with psychosis.","It does have a cost: you missed your blood test. I'd like to help with that."],"hear":"\"So I'm not mad. Good. But I'm not touching those tubes.\"","mark":"Overvalued idea, not psychosis"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["I'll write to your GP and suggest the nurse labels the tube after you've left the room.","If anything new happens, like voices or the worries spreading, your GP can send you straight back.","How would you sum up, in your words, what we've agreed?"],"hear":"\"No illness, GP sorts the label, and I come back if things change.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Feature","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Origin","Years of Bible study on Revelation","Understandable development"],["Cultural fit","Wife and church members share it","Culturally congruent"],["Conviction","'I might be wrong about the tubes'","Not held with absolute certainty"],["Effect and risk","Avoids barcodes; skipped his bloods","Overvalued: real cost to his health"],["Psychotic screen","No voices, passivity, references","No psychotic illness"]]},"key":"Good residents check whether the belief is shared. Excellent ones also test conviction gently, ask what it costs him, and leave with the blood test problem solved, not just a label.","objmap":[[2],[3],[5,6],[4],[7,8]]},"c-u-59":{"name":"Perinatal low mood: both periods, both children, three risks","why":"Pins down perinatal depression and its severity, and grades risk to mother, baby at home and pregnancy, in ten minutes.","frame":["Mood symptoms","Since Isha's birth","This pregnancy","Bipolar and psychosis screen","Risk to herself","Risk to the children"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the psychiatry team, working with the perinatal service. Are you Priya Lawson?","Thank you for coming. Your GP mentioned you've been feeling low, with Isha at home and a new pregnancy.","I'd like about ten minutes to understand how you've been, and how to support you. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Yes. It's a lot.\""},{"h":"Mood symptoms","say":["How's your mood been, and when did it start?","What about sleep when Isha is asleep, your appetite and concentration?","How do you see yourself these days, and what lies ahead?"],"hear":"\"Low for months. I cry every day. I lie awake even when she's asleep. I feel like a bad mother.\"","mark":"Moderate depressive symptoms with guilt and hopelessness"},{"h":"Since Isha's birth","say":["How was Isha's birth? Any problems afterwards?","How do you feel when you're with her?","Do you worry something is wrong with her?"],"hear":"\"Emergency caesarean, a day in special care. I love her but feel flat. I keep thinking she isn't gaining weight.\"","mark":"Bonding flat; health worries about Isha"},{"h":"This pregnancy","say":["How did you feel when you found out?","Have you seen the midwife yet? Are you taking folic acid?"],"hear":"\"It wasn't planned. I felt sick about it. I've booked with the midwife.\"","mark":"Unplanned; antenatal care in place"},{"h":"Background","say":["Have you had low mood before, or any mental health problems in the family?","Do you drink alcohol? Who helps you at home?"],"hear":"\"My mum had depression after I was born. No drinking. My husband works long hours; Mum helps.\"","mark":"Family history of postnatal depression"},{"h":"Bipolar and psychosis screen","say":["Have there been times you felt very high, or full of energy with little sleep?","When the health visitor says Isha's weight is fine, can you believe her?","Have you heard voices, or felt you'd done something terrible that deserved punishment?"],"hear":"\"Never high. I believe her for a bit, then worry again. No voices.\"","mark":"No bipolar or psychotic features"},{"h":"Risk to herself","say":["When things are this hard, some people feel life isn't worth living. Have you?","Have you thought about how you might end your life, or made any plans?","What keeps you going?"],"hear":"\"Sometimes I think it'd be easier not to wake up. No plans. I'd never leave Isha.\"","mark":"Passive death wish; no plan; protective factors"},{"h":"Risk to the children","say":["Many mums get frightening thoughts about their baby being harmed. Have you had any?","Have you ever felt like hurting Isha, or been unable to look after her?"],"hear":"\"No, never. I just worry I'm not doing it right.\"","mark":"No risk to Isha; pregnancy cared for"},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["Priya, I think you have depression that's been building since Isha was born. It's common, it isn't your fault, and it's treatable.","We can talk about talking therapy and a medicine that's used in pregnancy. With your agreement I'll let your midwife, health visitor and GP know.","If things get worse, call NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123, or 999 in an emergency. I'll see you in two weeks."],"hear":"\"Is there anything I can take while I'm pregnant?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Mood symptoms","Tearful daily, poor sleep, guilt, hopeless","Moderate depressive episode"],["Since Isha's birth","Emergency caesarean; bonding flat; weight worries","Postnatal onset; anxious, not delusional, worry"],["This pregnancy","Unplanned; booked with midwife","Stressor; antenatal care in place"],["Bipolar and psychosis","No elation, no voices; mum had PND","Unipolar; family risk factor"],["Risk","Passive wish not to wake; no harm to Isha","Self: moderate, needs crisis plan; children: low"]]},"key":"Good residents ask about suicide. Excellent ones ask about each child separately, normalise intrusive thoughts so mothers feel safe to answer, and screen for bipolar before talking about antidepressants.","objmap":[[2],[3,4,5],[6],[7,8],[9]]},"c-u-60":{"name":"Explaining CBT: his cycle, the tools, the practicalities","why":"Makes CBT feel personal and doable, so he accepts referral and knows what to expect, in ten minutes.","frame":["Situation","Thoughts","Feelings and body","Behaviour","Breaking the cycle"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the psychiatry team. Are you Martin Ellery?","Thanks for coming in. Your GP suggested a talking treatment called CBT could help.","We have about ten minutes. Let's start with how things have been, then I'll explain CBT and answer your questions."],"hear":"\"Fine. I'm not sure what it involves.\""},{"h":"His difficulties","say":["What went through your mind when you told your team?","What have you stopped doing since?","Have you felt like this before? What were your parents like when you were growing up?"],"hear":"\"I felt I'd betrayed them. I've stopped seeing anyone. At university I was low when I left my brothers at home; my parents were strict.\"","mark":"Guilt thought, withdrawal and early pattern identified"},{"h":"What CBT is","say":["CBT looks at how what happens, what we think, how we feel and what we do all feed each other.","Let me draw yours: the redundancies, then the thought 'I betrayed them', then guilt and exhaustion, then you pull away.","Pulling away means less contact and less achievement, which feeds the thought that you're failing. That's the loop."],"hear":"\"That's exactly what it's like.\"","mark":"Personal cycle drawn"},{"h":"How it helps him","say":["First, small planned activities, like one call to a friend this week, to lift energy and mood.","Then catching thoughts like 'I betrayed them' in a diary and testing them: what's the evidence, what would you say to a colleague?","Later, looking at the pattern of feeling responsible for everyone, which you've had since protecting your brothers."],"hear":"\"So it's practical. Not just chatting.\"","mark":"Techniques linked to his problems"},{"h":"Structure of therapy","say":["It's usually 12 to 20 weekly sessions, about an hour each, with a trained CBT therapist.","It focuses on now, not deep digging into childhood.","There's homework between sessions; that's where most of the change happens."],"hear":"\"Do I have to go through my childhood?\""},{"h":"Evidence and expectations","say":["In research, CBT helps depression roughly as much as tablets, and the gains tend to last longer.","Early on it can feel harder, because you're facing painful thoughts. That usually eases.","Please carry on with the mirtazapine. I'll ask your GP to review the dose."],"hear":"\"Should I stop the tablets if I'm doing therapy?\""},{"h":"Concerns and questions","say":["What worries you about starting?","Therapy is confidential. Work only hears what you agree to.","What else would you like to know?"],"hear":"\"Will my employer find out?\""},{"h":"Summary and close","say":["In your words, how might CBT help you?","You can self-refer to NHS Talking Therapies today; here's the link and a leaflet.","If your mood gets worse, see your GP the same day or call NHS 111 option 2."],"hear":"\"It helps me catch the guilty thoughts and get back to doing things. I'll refer myself tonight.\""}],"table":{"cols":["CBT part","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Situation","Told team about redundancies","The trigger"],["Thoughts","'I betrayed them'; 'I'm failing'","Automatic negative thoughts; over-responsibility"],["Feelings and body","Guilt, low, drained","Depressive symptoms"],["Behaviour","Stopped seeing friends; off work","Withdrawal keeps the cycle going"],["Breaking the cycle","Curious but unsure about 'talking'","Start with activity, then thought records"]]},"key":"Good residents describe CBT correctly. Excellent ones draw the patient's own cycle in his words, link it to why he is vulnerable, and leave him with one concrete first step.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4],[5,6,7],[8]]},"c-u-61":{"name":"Family therapy for a doubting father: fear, pattern, format, stress, yes or no","why":"Turns a frustrated, defensive parent into a willing participant by starting with his fear and ending with a direct yes or no.","frame":["His fear","The pattern at home","What family therapy is","Format and numbers","Stress and relapse","Willingness"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (30 s)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists, working with the early intervention service. Are you Mr Owen Pritchard?","Thanks for coming, Mr Pritchard. Ffion has agreed that we can share information with you about her care.","I'd like to hear your worries and then talk about family therapy. Is that okay?"],"hear":"\"Fine. Owen's fine. I just want something to change.\""},{"h":"His concerns (1.5 min)","say":["What's worrying you most about Ffion at the moment?","What makes that feel so frightening for you?","That sounds very hard to live with."],"hear":"\"She's been off school six weeks. My brother had this and never worked again.\"","mark":"Fear for her future identified; family history driving it"},{"h":"Impact at home (1.5 min)","say":["What happens when you try to get her out of her room?","And what does Ffion do on the days after a row? What sort of things do you say to her when it gets heated?","Has anyone ever been pushed or hurt during the arguments? How is your son coping?"],"hear":"\"She won't take her tablets after a row. I've called her lazy. Nobody's ever been hurt, though.\"","mark":"Rows linked to refused medication; critical comments; no violence"},{"h":"What family therapy is (1 min)","say":["Family therapy treats the family as a team, where what one person does affects everyone else.","Nobody is put on trial. The aim is to understand the illness together and find ways of responding that help Ffion.","You have a big part to play in her recovery."],"hear":"\"So it's not just about Ffion, then.\""},{"h":"How it works (1 min)","say":["A trained therapist from our team meets you, your wife and Ffion if she can manage it.","It's usually ten or more sessions, spread across three months to a year, often fortnightly, in clinic or sometimes at home.","Everyone gets a turn to talk, and the therapist keeps it fair."],"hear":"\"Ten sessions? I work shifts.\"","mark":"Format explained with numbers"},{"h":"How it helps (1.5 min)","say":["Sessions cover what psychosis is, why she seems to lack drive, her early warning signs and a plan if she gets worse.","Stress at home, whether it's raised voices or doing everything for her, makes relapse more likely. Both come from love.","Families who do this work see fewer relapses and steadier medication taking."],"hear":"\"So my wife and I are both doing it, in a way.\"","mark":"Stress and relapse explained without blame"},{"h":"His expectations (1.5 min)","say":["Change usually comes in small steps: one outing she chooses, not a full week back at school.","Would you be willing to come? And how do you think your wife and Ffion would feel?","We start with whoever can come and keep inviting the others."],"hear":"\"As long as nobody's pointing the finger at me, yes. Carys will come too.\"","mark":"Willing to attend"},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["So you're frightened for her future, the rows are adding stress for everyone, and family sessions can help you work as one team.","What will you say to Carys when you get home?","I'll refer you to our family intervention team today and arrange a carer's assessment for you both.","If Ffion stops her tablets for days, talks about harming herself, or seems much worse, ring the team, or NHS 111 option 2 out of hours."],"hear":"\"That it's about all of us, not blame. About ten sessions.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Area","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["His fear","Brother never recovered; Ffion off school six weeks","Love and fear drive his pushing; start there"],["Pattern at home","He pushes, wife steps in, rows follow","Two loving styles clashing: a target for therapy"],["Ffion's response","Refuses tablets, stays in her room after rows","Family stress is directly affecting her treatment"],["What he says","Has called her lazy","Reads negative symptoms as choice: needs psychoeducation"],["Safety","No violence; son keeping away","No safeguarding referral now; whole family affected"],["Willingness","Will come if not blamed; wife likely","Suitable: refer to family intervention today"]]},"key":"A good explanation gives the format. An excellent one names both criticism and over-protection as stress that comes from love, so neither parent is blamed, and then asks directly: will you come?","objmap":[[2,3],[4,5,6],[6],[7,8],[8]]},"c-u-62":{"name":"Supported capacity for sertraline: inform in chunks, then test four abilities","why":"Gives Liam the best chance to decide for himself, and leaves written evidence for each ability.","frame":["Understand","Retain","Use or weigh","Communicate","Support given"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (30 s)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Liam Fairweather?","Thanks for coming, Liam. Is it okay if your support worker stays with us?","Today I want to hear about your mood and talk about a tablet that might help. I'll use simple words."],"hear":"\"Okay. Jade can stay.\""},{"h":"Brief history (2 min)","say":["How have you been feeling? What do you call that feeling?","How's your sleep and eating? What have you stopped doing that you used to like?","When did it start? Did anything happen then? And how have the sessions with the psychologist been?","Do you ever think about hurting yourself?"],"hear":"\"The sad feeling. Since Nan died. I stopped drama. Talking helps a bit. I don't want to hurt myself.\"","mark":"Depressive symptoms, trigger and partial response to psychology established; no self-harm thoughts"},{"h":"Explain sertraline (1.5 min)","say":["Do you know anything about the tablet already?","It's called sertraline. It can help the sad feeling. One small tablet every morning with breakfast.","It takes a few weeks to work. In the first days a few people feel queasy or get a headache. That usually passes. Don't stop it suddenly."],"hear":"\"Jade said it's a happy tablet.\""},{"h":"Understand (1 min)","say":["What is this tablet for? Tell me your way.","How would you take it?","Can you point to the picture of the side effect we talked about?"],"hear":"\"It's for the sad feeling. One in the morning with my breakfast.\"","mark":"Understanding shown in his own words"},{"h":"Retain and weigh (1.5 min)","say":["After a short chat about drama group: what was the tablet called again, and what might happen at first?","What good things might happen if you take it?","What might happen if you don't take it?"],"hear":"\"Sertraline. Might feel sick. I might go back to drama. If I don't, I might stay sad.\"","mark":"Retains after a pause and weighs benefit, risk and not taking it"},{"h":"Communicate (30 s)","say":["So what have you decided about the tablet?","What's your reason?"],"hear":"\"I want to try it. I want to feel better.\"","mark":"Clear decision with a reason"},{"h":"His concerns (1 min)","say":["Is there anything worrying you about it?","It won't change who you are. It may help you feel more like yourself.","You can keep seeing the psychologist too. It's both, not one or the other."],"hear":"\"Will it make me weird?\""},{"h":"Outcome and close (1.5 min)","say":["You understood it, remembered it, thought about the good and bad bits, and told me your choice. That means you can decide this yourself.","We'll start a small dose for a week, then a bit more. A nurse will see you in two weeks.","If you feel sadder, or start wanting to hurt yourself, let Jade know straight away. Can you tell me what you'll do?","Here's the easy-read leaflet to keep. I'll write to your GP."],"hear":"\"Tell Jade if I feel worse. See the nurse in two weeks.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Ability","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Understand","'It's for the sad feeling, one in the morning'","Grasps purpose and how to take it"],["Retain","Recalled name and feeling sick after a pause","Holds information long enough to decide"],["Use or weigh","'Might feel sick, but I might go back to drama'","Links benefit, risk and not taking it to his life"],["Communicate","'I want to try it'","Clear choice with a reason"],["Support given","Plain words, chunks, pictures, Jade present","All practicable steps taken: outcome is valid"]]},"key":"A good assessment tests four abilities. An excellent one supports him first, tests with open questions and a pause, and writes down his own words as evidence for each ability.","objmap":[[2],[3],[4,5,6],[7,8],[8]]},"c-u-63":{"name":"Clozapine at home: why, before, start, bloods, side effects, rules","why":"Gives consent-quality information in ten minutes, so she knows exactly what will happen and what to act on.","frame":["Why clozapine","Before starting","Starting at home","Blood monitoring","Side effects and red flags","Practical rules"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (30 s)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Aisha Rahman?","Thanks for coming in, Aisha. Your care coordinator tells me you're now thinking about clozapine. That's a big decision, and a brave one.","Let's start with how things are now, then I'll talk you through clozapine step by step."],"hear":"\"I just want the voices to stop.\""},{"h":"How things are now (1 min)","say":["What are the voices like at the moment? And the neighbours?","How often do you manage the olanzapine? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","What have you heard about clozapine, and what worries you most?"],"hear":"\"Every evening, calling me useless. I take every tablet. I read it can kill your white cells.\"","mark":"Persisting symptoms with good adherence; no self-harm thoughts; fear of blood side effects"},{"h":"Why clozapine (1 min)","say":["Two different antipsychotics at good doses haven't settled the voices. That's called treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","Clozapine is the one medicine that works best in this situation. Around half of people improve, sometimes a lot.","It can take a few months to show its full effect."],"hear":"\"So it's not just another tablet like the others?\"","mark":"Rationale understood"},{"h":"Before starting (1 min)","say":["First we'd check bloods, including your white cells, sugar, cholesterol and heart markers, plus an ECG, your weight and blood pressure.","We register you with a clozapine monitoring service. The pharmacy can only give it out with a safe blood result.","Do you smoke? How many a day?"],"hear":"\"About fifteen a day.\""},{"h":"Starting at home (1 min)","say":["You'd start with a tiny dose, 12.5 mg, and go up slowly over two to three weeks, so your body gets used to it.","The Home Treatment Team comes every day for the first two weeks and takes your pulse, temperature and blood pressure.","We bring the olanzapine down gradually as the clozapine goes up."],"hear":"\"So I don't have to come into hospital?\""},{"h":"Blood tests (1 min)","say":["Clozapine can lower white blood cells, about 3 in 100 people, and seriously in under 1 in 100.","So bloods are weekly for 18 weeks, every two weeks until a year, then monthly. That catches any drop early.","Any sore throat, temperature or flu-like feeling: ring us that day for a blood test."],"hear":"\"Weekly? I hate needles.\"","mark":"Blood schedule and infection red flag understood"},{"h":"Side effects (2 min)","say":["Common ones: sleepiness, so take most of it at night; drooling at night, which a tablet can help; a fast pulse; dizziness on standing; weight gain.","Constipation is common and can become dangerous. You'll take a laxative from the start. If your bowels haven't opened for three days, tell us.","Rarely it inflames the heart, mostly in the first two months. Chest pain, breathlessness or feeling feverish and unwell: ring us that day, or 999 if severe.","At higher doses it can cause fits. We go slowly and check levels."],"hear":"\"That's a lot. But the team will be checking?\"","mark":"Common and serious side effects with red flags"},{"h":"Practical rules (1 min)","say":["If you miss more than two days of doses, don't restart on your own: we start again from the low dose.","Don't change your smoking without telling us. Stopping can push levels up, so we'd check a level.","No driving while you feel drowsy, and if a pregnancy is possible, plan it with us first."],"hear":"\"Two days, smoking, tell you. Got it.\""},{"h":"Summary and close (1.5 min)","say":["So: bloods and ECG first, a slow start at home with daily visits, weekly bloods, and a laxative from day one.","Can you tell me the three things that mean you ring us the same day?","Here's the leaflet and the HTT number. Out of hours, the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2.","Shall I book your baseline bloods today?"],"hear":"\"Sore throat or fever, chest pain, and no poo for three days. Yes, book them.\""}],"table":{"cols":["Stage","What you heard","What it means"],"rows":[["Why clozapine","Two drugs, full adherence, voices persist","Treatment resistance: clozapine indicated"],["Before starting","Smokes 15 a day; 9 kg gained on olanzapine","Baseline metabolic tests; smoking affects dose"],["Starting at home","Prefers home; mother lives with her","HTT daily checks; mother can spot side effects"],["Blood monitoring","Fears white cells; hates needles","Explain numbers; finger-prick option"],["Side effects","Worried about weight and bowels","Laxative from day 1; weight plan"],["Practical rules","Repeats 48 hours and smoking rule","Ready to consent and start"]]},"key":"A good explanation lists side effects. An excellent one turns them into three same-day red flags (infection signs, chest symptoms, no bowel opening for 3 days) and checks she can say them back.","objmap":[[2],[3,5],[4,6],[7],[8,9]]},"c-u-65":{"name":"Lower limb neuro check: look, tone, power, reflexes, coordination, sensation","why":"Answers 'is there a deficit, and what pattern?' in ten minutes, separating peripheral nerve from spinal cord.","frame":["Inspection","Tone","Power (MRC)","Reflexes and plantars","Coordination and gait","Sensation"],"steps":[{"h":"Opening (45 s)","say":["Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Joanne Fletcher?","Thanks, Jo. The nurses say your legs feel heavy and numb. I'd like to check the nerves and muscles in both legs. Could you change into shorts, and would you like a chaperone?","Any pain in your hips, knees or ankles before I start? It takes about ten minutes."],"hear":"\"Okay, yes please. No pain, they just feel like lead.\""},{"h":"General look (45 s)","say":["I'm looking at your feet, nails and skin, and feeling for the pulses on top of your feet.","Just lie back and relax your legs on the couch."],"hear":"\"My feet are always cold.\"","mark":"Pulses present; dry skin; no joint deformity"},{"h":"Inspection (30 s)","say":["I'm looking for any muscle thinning, twitching, scars or sores, comparing both sides.","Any cuts on your feet you haven't felt?"],"hear":"\"Not that I know of.\"","mark":"No wasting, fasciculation, deformity or ulcers"},{"h":"Tone (45 s)","say":["Let your legs go floppy. I'll roll them side to side.","Now I'll lift behind your knee quickly, then press your foot up sharply to check for jerking."],"hear":"\"Like this?\"","mark":"Tone normal; no clonus"},{"h":"Power (1.5 min)","say":["Lift your leg up and stop me pushing it down. Now push down into the bed.","Bend your knee; keep it bent while I try to pull it straight. Now kick out against my hand.","Push your foot down like a pedal. Now pull your foot up towards your nose. Point your big toe up against my finger."],"hear":"\"That one's harder, the toes.\"","mark":"Ankle dorsiflexion 4+/5, big toe extension 4/5, both sides; rest 5/5"},{"h":"Reflexes (1 min)","say":["I'll tap just below your kneecap, then at the back of your ankle.","Now hook your fingers together and pull hard when I say. Pull.","I'm stroking the sole of your foot. It may tickle."],"hear":"\"I didn't feel the ankle one move.\"","mark":"Knee reflexes reduced; ankle reflexes absent with reinforcement; plantars flexor"},{"h":"Coordination and gait (1.5 min)","say":["Put your heel on the other knee and run it down your shin, then back up. Now the other side.","Walk to the door and back normally. Now heel to toe, like a tightrope. I'm right beside you.","Feet together, arms by your side, eyes open. Now close your eyes. I won't let you fall."],"hear":"\"Whoa, I'm going as soon as I shut my eyes.\"","mark":"Heel-shin normal; broad-based gait; cannot heel-toe; Romberg positive"},{"h":"Sensation (2 min)","say":["Here's the cotton wool on your chest so you know what normal feels like. Eyes closed: say 'now' whenever you feel it touch your feet and legs.","Now a blunt and a sharp point: tell me sharp or blunt, and when it changes as I move up.","This tuning fork buzzes. Tell me when it stops. If you can't feel it at your toe, I'll move up to your ankle and knee.","Eyes closed: is your big toe moving up or down?"],"hear":"\"Can't feel it on my toes. It comes back about halfway up my shin.\"","mark":"Stocking loss to mid-shin; vibration absent to ankle; JPS impaired at toes; no sensory level"},{"h":"Summary and close (75 s)","say":["Thank you, Jo. Let's get you covered up.","The nerves to your feet aren't carrying feeling well, and your ankles are a little weak, on both sides. That's why your legs feel heavy and you trip, especially in the dark. There's no sign of a spine problem.","One quick safety check: any trouble with your bladder or bowels, or numbness between your legs?","We'll do bloods, start vitamin B1 today, ask the medical team for a nerve test, and get physio to help your balance."],"hear":"\"No, nothing like that. So it's fixable?\""}],"table":{"cols":["Part of the assessment","What you found","What it means"],"rows":[["Inspection and tone","No wasting or fasciculation; tone normal, no clonus","No upper motor neurone or anterior horn signs"],["Power","Ankle and big toe extension 4 to 4+/5 both sides","Distal, symmetrical weakness"],["Reflexes","Knees reduced, ankles absent with reinforcement; plantars flexor","Lower motor neurone, length-dependent"],["Coordination and gait","Heel-shin fine; Romberg positive; cannot heel-toe","Sensory ataxia, not cerebellar"],["Sensation","Stocking loss to mid-shin; vibration and JPS lost at toes","Peripheral sensorimotor neuropathy; no sensory level"]]},"key":"A good assessment finds the signs. An excellent one compares sides throughout, uses reinforcement and maps sensation upward, then states the pattern in one line: peripheral neuropathy, no cord signs.","objmap":[[1],[4,5],[6],[7,8],[9]]}});
